{"row_id": "q003", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15490, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00325481.2017.1319730", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "title": "Reviewing treatments and outcomes in the evolving landscape of ulcerative colitis", "date": "2017-07-04", "text": "The first-line therapy for patients with active, mild-to-moderate forms of distal UC is rectal 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) therapy [Citation22,Citation35]. ... Rectal corticosteroids are also recommended for patients with mild-to-moderate distal UC [Citation22], but as second-line therapy in patients refractory to rectal 5-ASAs [Citation35]. ... The three TNF-α antagonists currently approved by the US Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of patients with moderate-to-severe UC are infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab [Citation84]."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development. Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists. ... |JAK Inhibitor|Oncostellae (OST-122)|Oral gut-restricted JAK3/TYK2/ARK5 inhibitor|NCT04353791|Phase 1b/2a|Completed Dec 2022; development not pursued further| |JAK Inhibitor|Ritlecitinib (PF-06651600)|Oral selective JAK3/TEC family kinase inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; prioritized for alopecia areata| ... |TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027| |TYK2 Inhibitor|Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647)|Selective TYK2 inhibitor|NCT04209556|Phase 2b|Withdrawn with zero enrollment due to portfolio re-prioritization| ... |α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027| ... |IL-23R antagonist|Icotrokinra|First-in-class targeted oral peptide blocking IL-23 receptor|NCT06049017 (ANTHEM-UC)|Phase 2b|Met primary endpoint; 63.5% response (highest dose) vs 27% placebo| ... |TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029| ... |RIPK1 inhibitor|Eclitasertib (SAR443122)|Highly potent selective oral RIPK1 kinase inhibitor|NCT05588843 (RESOLUTE)|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2026| ... |MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|Tilpisertib (GS-4875)|Oral MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|NCT04130919, NCT06029972 (PALEKONA)|Phase 2|First study terminated Feb 2021; PALEKONA ongoing, estimated completion 2027| ... |Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination. |Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026| |Combination|Infliximab + Ustekinumab|TNF antagonist + IL-12/23 p40 antibody|NCT06453317|Phase 2|Started Feb 2025; estimated completion June 2028| ... Brepocitinib (PF-06700841) is an oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor evaluated alongside ritlecitinib in the same VIBRATO Phase 2b trial (NCT02958865) for moderate-to-severe UC with identical inclusion criteria of patients aged 18–75 years having total MS ≥6, RBS ≥1, and MES ≥2 (38). ... Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to ..."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://practicalgastro.com/2019/04/10/the-ibd-therapeutic-pipeline-is-primed-to-produce/", "domain": "practicalgastro.com", "title": "The IBD Therapeutic Pipeline Is Primed to Produce", "date": "2019-04-10", "text": "Several other IL23 specific inhibitors are rapidly entering clinical trials for CD and UC, including mirikizumab (LY3074828) (Eli Lilly), tildrakizumab (Sun Pharma), and guselkumab (Janssen).^9^ Of the three, mirikizumab is farthest along in development with two phase II clinical trials recruiting, one for active CD (SERENITY) and another for moderate to severe UC (NCT02891226, NCT02589665 respectively). ... Tofacitinib (Xeljanz) (Pfizer) is a pan-JAK inhibitor with a preference for JAK1 and JAK3 that has demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of ulcerative colitis.^33^ As a result, Tofacitinib was recently approved in the United States and Europe for moderate to severe active ulcerative colitis (UC) based on phase II and phase III trials. ... Upadacitinib (ABT-494) (AbbVie) is a more potent JAK1-selective inhibitor that is currently being investigated for use in Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and psoriatic arthritis.^42^ CELEST (NCT02365649) was a phase II, randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled study investigating upadacitinib for patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development. Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists. Tumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A pathway inhibitors demonstrate particularly robust efficacy in early trials, with clinical remission rates exceeding 25% compared to less than 2% for placebo. ... |PSGL-1 agonist|Leiolizumab|Tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1|NCT06109441|Phase 2a|Started Dec 2023; estimated completion Nov 2026| ... |TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029| ... |Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026| ... Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to assess its safety and efficacy in participants with moderate to severe UC. ... The same MORF-057 molecule is being evaluated in a double-blind, Phase 2b placebo-controlled RCT (EMERALD-2, NCT05611671) with 282 participants meeting similar inclusion criteria but specifically requiring inadequate response to aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, or advanced UC therapies, measuring clinical remission using mMS at 12 weeks as primary endpoint. ... Tralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC. ... Rosnilimab is a novel PD-1 agonist IgG1 monoclonal antibody designed to reduce T cell proliferation and inflammatory cytokine secretion by selectively depleting PD-1high expressing T follicular helper, T peripheral helper, and T effector cells while sparing PD-1low cells. ... CU104 is a new oral investigational drug that specifically inhibits IL1β, IL-6, and TNFα (69). It is being studied in a double-blind, placebo-controlled, multi-center Phase 2 RCT (NCT05907330) designed to evaluate its efficacy and safety in patients with moderate to severe UC. ... RO7837195 is a p40xTL1A bispecific antibody developed through a Roche-Pfizer collaboration that simultaneously targets IL-12/IL-23 (via the p40 subunit) and TL1A pathways, currently being evaluated in the Phase IIb SUNCREST trial (NCT06979336) which employs a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled ..."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/16/1/2/6390052", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "date": "2022-01-28", "text": "We suggest the use of vedolizumab rather than adalimumab for the induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis [weak recommendation, low level of evidence]"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://atlasbyauro.com/trials/condition/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "atlasbyauro.com", "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - 1,154 clinical trials", "date": null, "text": "|Mirikizumab Administered at the Same Time as Tirzepatide in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis and Obesity or Overweight: Phase 3b Study (NCT06937086)|Eli Lilly and Company|2025-06-26|"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4609145/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Mild to Moderate Ulcerative Colitis", "date": null, "text": "Biologic therapy for patients with UC includes the anti-TNF agents infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab and the integrin inhibitor vedolizumab (Figure 4). ... ^31^ A second integrin inhibitor, vedolizumab, was developed that is directed against the integrin α~4~β~7~. ... Moreover, vedolizumab was beneficial regardless of the patient’s treatment history, whether that included prior anti-TNF failure, prior anti-TNF loss of response, or no previous anti-TNF therapy.^32^ Vedolizumab, which is administered via intravenous infusion, received FDA approval in 2014 for patients with previously treated moderately to severely active UC. ... A therapy that was designed to meet these needs is budesonide foam, which recently received FDA approval for the induction of remission in patients with mild to moderate distal UC extending up to 40 cm from the anal verge.^1^ This area includes the rectum, which spans the first 10 cm to 15 cm, as well as the sigmoid colon, which spans the next 25 cm to 30 cm. ... Several investigational integrin inhibitors are being evaluated, including etrolizumab, which targets the integrin β7, and AMG 181, a gut-specific anti-α~4~β~7~ antibody. Etrolizumab demonstrated clinical efficacy in a randomized, controlled, phase 2 trial in patients with moderate to severe UC not responding to conventional therapy. ... Several Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are being evaluated in the treatment of UC. The pan-JAK inhibitor tofacitinib, which has been shown to affect multiple cytokines, demonstrated efficacy as induction therapy in patients with moderate to severe UC.^10^ Tofacitinib is currently being evaluated in phase 3 trials for induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately to severely active UC.^11^^,^^12^ Potential safety concerns with tofacitinib include significant immunosuppression, which is associated with a small increased risk of infection.^10^"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/7/jjag066/8726617", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "date": "2026-07-07", "text": "Among the most promising alternatives to infliximab and calcineurin inhibitors in ASUC are JAKi^195^. ... To date, the main therapeutic third-line options may include one of the three following sequences: cyclosporine followed by infliximab, infliximab followed by cyclosporine, or JAKi [upadacitinib or tofacitinib] following failure of either previous agent."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "merckmanuals.com", "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - Gastroenterology - Merck Manuals", "date": "2026-03-06", "text": "Suppositories are effective for more distal disease and are usually preferred by patients. ... Patients with inflammation proximal to the sigmoid colon or left-sided disease unresponsive to topical agents should receive a biologic agent (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, guselkumab, risankizumab, mirikizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab) with or without an immunomodulator medication (azathioprine, methotrexate, 6-mercaptopurine) (1, 4, 5). ... Finally, in some patients, a trial of the small-molecule Janus kinase inhibitors (tofacitinib or upadacitinib) or the sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulator ozanimod or etrasimod can be considered. ... - Treat mild disease with 5-ASA by rectum and, for proximal disease, by mouth. - Treat extensive or severe disease with high-dose glucocorticoids, immunomodulator therapy (eg, azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine), biologics (eg, adalimumab, infliximab, vedolizumab), tofacitinib, upadacitinib, or ozanimod."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/article/what-are-the-key-players-in-the-ulcerative-colitis-treatment-market", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "What are the key players in the ulcerative colitis treatment ...", "date": "2025-03-11", "text": "Small molecule inhibitors, such as tofacitinib and upadacitinib, have further expanded the therapeutic horizon by acting on Janus kinases (JAKs) to modulate inflammatory signaling cascades. ... The landscape of the UC treatment market involves several major multinational pharmaceutical companies, as well as emerging biopharmaceutical firms with pipeline candidates and novel delivery technologies. ... With products such as tofacitinib, Pfizer has been influential in pioneering the use of oral JAK inhibitors, which have provided an additional treatment option for patients who have failed biologic therapies or cannot tolerate them. ... remains a notable competitor with a wide range of generic and innovative treatment options, including biosimilars that have an impact on pricing and market share for UC therapies. • Additionally, companies such as Merck & Co. and Shire (now part of Takeda following its acquisition) have contributed to the richer pipeline of UC treatments, often focusing on both existing biologics and emerging novel compounds. ... Other companies highlighted in later reports include Johnson & Johnson’s emphasis on advanced biologics, Bausch Health Companies Inc. with its product line for rectal therapies (e.g., UCERIS® budesonide rectal foam, which is becoming significant in the treatment of mild to moderate distal UC). Emerging players such as Janssen Biotech Inc. (notably with its FDA approval of STELARA [ustekinumab] for UC) are also key contributors, marking advances in targeting interleukin pathways to achieve better management outcomes. In addition, pipeline-focused companies like Palatin Technologies are developing next-generation small-molecule candidates such as PL8177 for oral colon delivery and effective targeting of the melanocortin-1 receptor, opening up new therapeutic modalities for UC patients. ... • Additionally, newer candidates in development such as filgotinib (a JAK1 preferential inhibitor) and emerging agents like cobitolimod (a toll-like receptor 9 agonist) are expanding the therapeutic armamentarium."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10647, "cached_input_tokens": 10644, "output_tokens": 233}, "timing": {"search_ms": 705.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 4053.7, "total_ms": 4759.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 4053.7, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "RpoB’s mechanism or mechanistic class is not stated, so same-class competing agents for distal ulcerative colitis cannot be identified.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["RpoB distal ulcerative colitis mechanism class competitors"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q002", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15711, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "date": "2025-08-14", "text": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain. ... Herein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%). AZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes. ... An anti-TcdB monoclonal antibody (mAb), Zinplava (bezlotoxumab), was approved in 2016 and recently withdrawn from the market by the sponsor [5]. ... We recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16]. ... AZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*)."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "date": "2025-11-03", "text": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells. ... AZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab. In a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab. Furthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit. ... Bezlotoxumab (Zinplava), an FDA-approved mAb targeting TcdB, was effective in reducing recurrence but has recently been discontinued. ... Bezlotoxumab (Zinplava), a TcdB-neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb), was FDA-approved to prevent recurrent CDI in high-risk patients, but its production was discontinued in January 2025 [16,17]. ... We previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26]. ... These results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice. ... In conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains. By blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes. ... AZD5148 is currently being evaluated in a Phase I clinical trial for safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics in healthy adults (NCT06469151)."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "date": null, "text": "AZD5148 anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C.diff recurrence ... AZD5148 C. difficile mAb - Prevention of Recurrence (US) ... APRICUS ... Clostridium difficile AZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb) Trial Population Patients Design Endpoints Status Phase IIb PRISM NCT07285213 Adults with qualifying C. difficile infection episode 230 • Randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled • AZD5148 or placebo • Primary endpoint: efficacy • Secondary endpoint: safety, PK • FPCD: Q4 2025 • Data anticipated: >2027 Phase I NCT06469151 Healthy volunteers 84 • Randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation • Cohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo • Cohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo • Cohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m., Chinese participants) or ... • ... • ... • ... • ... • FPCD: Q2 2024 • ... • Data readout: Q1 2026"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/h1q2/H1-and-Q2-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "date": null, "text": "AZD5148 anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C.diff recurrence ... Clostridium difficile AZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb) Trial Population Patients Design Endpoints Status Phase IIb PRISM NCT07285213 Adults with qualifying C. difficile infection episode 230 • Randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled • AZD5148 or placebo • Primary endpoint: efficacy • Secondary endpoint: safety, PK • FPCD: Q4 2025 • Data anticipated: >2027 Phase I NCT06469151 Healthy volunteers 84 • Randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation • Cohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo • Cohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo • Cohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m., Chinese participants) or placebo • Cohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo • Cohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo • Cohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or placebo • Cohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo • Primary endpoint: safety • Secondary endpoint: PK parameters • FPCD: Q2 2024 • LPCD: Q4 2024 • Data readout: Q1 2026 131"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "title": "Pipeline", "date": null, "text": "### Phase II - ** AZD0120 ** *multiple myeloma* #### AZD0120 - Phase II Close - ** AZD0305 ** *relapsed/refractory multiple myeloma* #### AZD0305 - Phase II Close - ** AZD3470 ... ### Phase II - ** AZD1163 ** *rheumatoid arthritis* #### AZD1163 - Phase II Close - **Mechanism:** anti-PAD2/4 bispecific antibody - **Area under investigation:** rheumatoid arthritis - **Molecule size:** Large molecule - ** ... ### Phase II - ** AZD0292 ** *bronchiectasis* #### AZD0292 - Phase II Close - ** AZD5148 ** *reduction of C. diff recurrence* #### AZD5148 - Phase II Close - **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb - **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence - **Molecule size:** Large molecule - **"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "date": "2026-06-15", "text": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31]. The anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33]. AZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34]. TcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37]. The two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain. Therefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14]. In contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity. A recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%). AZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33]. In addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39]. AZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41]. ... TcdB, as previously discussed, is a well-characterized protein that has been extensively validated as a therapeutic target for mAb development, making it a good choice for testing novel mAb development methodologies."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "date": "2025-08-12", "text": "This is supported by clinical evidence that: (1) hospital outbreaks have been caused by TcdA-negative and TcdB-positive *C. difficile* strains^7,8^; (2) higher antibody titers against TcdB (but not TcdA) correlate with a lower risk of recurrent infection^7,9^; and (3) neutralizing TcdB with bezlotoxumab reduces the risk of recurrent infections and adverse outcomes, while treatment with an anti-TcdA neutralizing mAb does not^10^. ... |GSK|GSK2904545A|TcdA, TcdB DD and CROPS (VPI 10463)|Recombinant fusion protein (F2 antigen)|AS01|N/A|2024, Phase 1|Ongoing| ... Bezlotoxumab binds to two epitopes in the TcdB CROPS, located near the ‘hinge’ region (Figure 2B). ... Another well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^. ... - The neutralizing mAb Zinplava^™^ (bezlotoxumab) was discontinued in January 2025; however, a promising antibody candidate, AZD5148 (formerly PA41), is currently in early clinical trials (NCT06469151)."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33039", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "title": "Ozmosi | AZD-5148 Drug Profile", "date": "2025-01-23", "text": "Anti-toxin B neutralizing mAb AZD5148 provides protection in a Clostridioides difficile gnotobiotic piglet model. ... Mechanisms of Action: TcdB Inhibitor ... FDA Designation: * Approval Status: Not Approved Approved Countries: None ... Company: AstraZeneca ... ###### Active Clinical Trial Count: 3 ... Phase 1: Clostridium Infections|Healthy Volunteers |Trial|Phase|Trial Status|Disease|Primary Completion Date|Probability of Success|Latest Trial Update Date|Data Updated| ... |D8820C00005|P1|Active, not recruiting|Clostridium Infections|2025-12-29|69%|2025-01-23|Primary Endpoints|Study Completion Date|Treatments|Trial Status| |jRCT2071240071|P1|Not yet recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2026-01-12| |D8820C00002|P1|Active, not recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2025-11-07|69%|2025-01-22|"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gg/study/NCT07285213", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gg", "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody (NCT07285213)", "date": null, "text": "RecruitingNCT07285213PRISMUpdated Aug 12, 2026 # Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody A Phase 2 interventional study of AZD5148 and Placebo in Clostridioides Difficile Infection, sponsored by AstraZeneca. Recruiting at 116 sites in 14 countries. Open to participants aged 18 Years and older. Per ClinicalTrials.gov, last updated 2026-08-12. Sponsored by **AstraZeneca** · Phase 2, Interventional, and Prevention - **Phase** Phase 2 - **Study type** Interventional - **Enrollment** 230 - **Allocation** Randomized - **Ages** 18 Years and older ... The purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in Individuals 18 years of age and above. ... Approximately 230 participants will be enrolled and randomized 1:1 to receive a single dose of either AZD5148 or placebo (normal saline). Route of administration (intramuscular or intravenous push) will be according to the Investigator's choice. Stratification will be based on geographical region. ... Participant must be ≥ 18 years of age at the time of signing the informed consent, capable of giving signed informed consent. Participants with a qualifying C. difficile infection episode at the time of providing informed consent defined by: - History of 3 or more unformed stools (Bristol stool scale 6 or 7) in ≤ 24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days, (Note: Diarrhea is not required to be present on the day of IMP administration) and - Positive local C. difficile toxin test (eg, immune assay or CCNA) on a stool sample collected during this episode, as part of routine clinical care (Note: Toxin testing, where not part of routine clinical care, may be conducted as a pre- screening activity; if this occurs, participant consent is required), and ... ## Study design - **Phase** Phase 2 - **Primary purpose** Prevention - **Allocation** Randomized - **Intervention model** Parallel assignment ... - **Enrollment** 230 participants (estimated) ### Study arms - Experimental AZD5148: dose A Participants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push. Drug: AZD5148 - Placebo comparator Placebo Participants will receive a single dose of placebo via intramuscular or intravenous push. ... ### Primary outcomes 1. First occurrence of recurrence of C difficile infection Recurrence of C difficile infection (rCDI) occurring after initial clinical cure (ICC) of the qualifying C difficile infection (CDI). CDI is defined as a history of diarrhea (\\>=3 unformed stools, ie, type 6 or 7 stool on Bristol Stool Scale in \\<=24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days) accompanied by a positive stool test for C difficile toxin. ... Immunogenicity is evaluated through AZD5148 anti-drug antibody responses over time in serum from participants who receive AZD5148. ... ## Key details - **Study ID** NCT07285213 - **Lead sponsor** AstraZeneca ... Dec 16, ..."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d6acfad0-c39d-44cd-90d2-d4edff505376", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "date": null, "text": "Despite these advancements—and the approval of numerous mAbs for other indications—few anti-bacterial mAbs have been approved for clinical use, which is limited to obiltoxaximab (Anthim) and raxibacumab (Abthrax) for treatment of *Bacillus anthracis* toxin exposure, and bezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) for *Clostridioides difficile* infection of the gastrointestinal tract [11,12,13]. ... Medarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23]. Two fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24]. ... Bezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) was approved as a single intravenous infusion of 10 mg/kg in the USA and European Union for prevention of rCDI in individuals at high risk for CDI and receiving antibacterial treatment [23]. ... AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31]. The anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33]. AZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34]. ... In contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity. A recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%). AZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33]. ... AZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41]."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11002, "cached_input_tokens": 10999, "output_tokens": 460}, "timing": {"search_ms": 632.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 6994.8, "total_ms": 7627.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 6994.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "Whether participants must receive standard-of-care antibacterial therapy (fidaxomicin, vancomycin, or metronidazole), and whether its planned duration must be 10–25 days at the time of IMP administration.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["AZD5148 PRISM NCT07285213 antibacterial therapy 10–25 days"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q004", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "anchors": ["MT501"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 16448, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.spandidos-publications.com/10.3892/ijmm.2026.5961", "domain": "spandidos-publications.com", "title": "Inflammatory bowel disease treatment: Mechanisms ...", "date": "2026-10-01", "text": "The mechanism of action primarily involves inhibiting natural killer cell activity, suppressing the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways of arachidonic acid metabolism, thereby decreasing prostaglandin and leukotriene levels, inhibiting th ... Methotrexate (MTX) is a dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor that exerts therapeutic effects by inhibiting cell proliferation and modulating immune function (79). ... Monoclonal antibodies targeting TNF-α (infliximab and adalimumab) are used in IBD management (82), decreasing inflammation, promoting mucosal healing and decreasing the need for surgery (83). TNF-α serves a key role in intestinal inflammation during IBD, promoting the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, increasing intestinal permeability, activating immune cells and facilitating inflammatory cell migration (84). Mechanistically, anti-TNF-α neutralizes soluble and transmembrane TNF-α, decreases NF-κB activation (85) and diminishes downstream cytokine cascades (IL-1β, IL-6 and IL-12/23) (86). ... Vedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, exerting selective immunosuppression by inhibiting lymphocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa (93). α4β7 integrin serves a key role in the trafficking of gut-specific lymphocytes. It binds the intestinal mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1), thereby mediating lymphocyte migration into intestinal tissue (94). ... Ustekinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the p40 subunit common to IL-12 and IL-23, indicated for the treatment of moderate-to-severe CD and UC (100). ... Ustekinumab inhibits IL-12- and IL-23-mediated signaling and cytokine cascades by blocking the binding of IL-12 and IL-23 to the IL-12Rβ1 receptor protein on target cell surfaces (102). ... JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib and filgotinib) block JAK-STAT signaling downstream of multiple cytokine receptors (including IL-2, IL-4, IL-6, IL-7 and IL-12/23), producing bro ad immunomodulation (121). ... S1P receptor modulators (such as etrasimod and ozelamod) bind S1P receptors on lymphocyte surfaces, functionally inhibiting lymphocyte migration out of lymphoid tissue. ... TYK2 is a member of the JAK family, primarily mediating the signaling of IL-12, IL-23 and type I interferon (136). Selective TYK2 inhibitors (such as deucravacitinib) achieve selective inhibition through allosteric mechanisms, theoretically avoiding inhibition of JAK1/2/3 and improving safety (137)."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/477311c534e0406d8c27a73fc39e6074", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "MT-501 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "date": null, "text": "|Drug Type Small molecule drug| ... |Therapeutic Areas Immune System DiseasesDigestive System Disorders| |Active Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn Disease| ... |Crohn Disease|Phase 2|United States|Mirador Therapeutics, Inc.|26 Jun 2025|"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-inflammatory-bowel-diseases-5-2025-6303d", "domain": "withpower.com", "title": "MT-501 for Inflammatory Bowel Disease (ASCEND-IBD Trial)", "date": "2021-10-09", "text": "Researchers are excited about MT-501 because it offers a novel approach to treating Inflammatory Bowel Disease, including Ulcerative Colitis and Crohn's Disease. Unlike traditional treatments, which often rely on suppressing the immune system, MT-501 targets specific inflammatory pathways, potentially reducing side effects and increasing effectiveness. This precision in targeting inflammation may lead to faster symptom relief and improved long-term outcomes for patients. ... Studies have shown that MT-501 may benefit people with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including Crohn's Disease and Ulcerative Colitis. Early findings suggest that MT-501 can reduce gut swelling, potentially leading to fewer symptoms. Research indicates that the treatment targets specific body parts responsible for this swelling, possibly offering better disease control than some current treatments."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/inflammatory-bowel-diseases-global-competitive-landscape-report-2026/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "date": "2026-05-14", "text": "Notably, 2025-2026 has produced an exceptional wave of approvals including Janssen’s oral peptide IL-23 antagonist Icotrokinra (March 2026), Lilly’s Mirikizumab (Omvoh) for Crohn’s expansion, and multiple Chinese-developed JAK and IL-23 mAb approvals. Specifically, the late-stage IBD landscape is bifurcated between injectable biologics — AbbVie’s Skyrizi (Risankizumab) and Rinvoq (Upadacitinib), Janssen’s Stelara (Ustekinumab) and Tremfya (Guselkumab), Takeda’s Entyvio (Vedolizumab), Pfizer’s Etrasimod (Velsipity), and BMS’s Zeposia (Ozanimod) — and the emerging oral biologic and JAK1-selective wave. Moreover, sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) modulators have established a third oral mechanism alongside JAK and TYK2 inhibitors. However, the strategic outlook is increasingly defined by oral peptide IL-23 antagonists and selective JAK1 inhibitors rather than next-generation TNF or anti-integrin biologics. ... Furthermore, microbiome therapeutics, mesenchymal stem cell therapy, and IL-23 / TL1A combination biology extend the long-term modality horizon. ... Across the 200 sampled IBD programs, small molecules dominate with 114 entries (oral JAK, TYK2, S1P modulators), followed by 31 monoclonal antibodies, 6 herbal medicines, 4 mesenchymal stem cell therapies, 3 synthetic peptides, 3 cyclic peptides, 3 bispecific antibodies, 3 probiotics, and 3 polymers. Notably, the strong small-molecule presence reflects oral therapy ambitions across multiple mechanisms."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://assetmemo.bio/drug/mt-501", "domain": "assetmemo.bio", "title": "MT-501 - AssetMemo", "date": null, "text": "Phase 1 / first-in-human studies establish safety, tolerability, and dosing — they are not designed to demonstrate efficacy, so no evidence signal is assigned yet."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "date": "2023-09-08", "text": "Most immunomodulators showed a late onset of action, requiring a differentiated initial strategy to control the disease. New therapeutic perspectives emerged with biological therapy, initially with anti-TNF, followed by anti-integrins and anti-interleukins. Despite the different mechanisms of action, there are similarities between the general rates of effectiveness. These similar results were also evidenced in JAK inhibitors and S1p modulators, the last therapeutic classes approved for the treatment of IBD. ... The first approved monoclonal antibodies were TNF-alpha antagonists (anti-TNF), followed by integrin antagonists (anti-integrins) and more recently interleukin antagonists (anti-IL). ... The first medication directed at the treatment of IBD was sulfasalazine (SSZ), which consists of binding sulphapyridine (active metabolite responsible for most adverse events related to the use of SSZ) and the 5-ASA portion, which is responsible for the anti-inflammatory action [6]. ... Action of salicylates and glucocorticoids in IBD. 5-ASA acts on pro-inflammatory factors such as molecular adhesion, Natural Killer cell activation, intestinal permeability, nuclear factor kappa B activation, IL-1β, IL-2, Interferon gamma, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes. ... Three classes of biological therapies are currently used in the treatment of IBD: (I) anti-TNF, (II) anti-integrins, and (III) anti-interleukins [6,35]. ... Anti-integrins are molecules expressed by leukocytes that bind to adhesion molecules (CAM) and allow the trafficking of these cells [85]. ... JAK blockade occurs in pairs involving five different inhibition scenarios interrupting STAT-mediated extracellular-nuclear communication. ... Tofacitinib was the first small molecule released for the treatment of UC, it is a molecule that acts on all JAK, mainly on JAK 1 and 3. ... S1p modulators act as functional antagonists in lymphocytic receptors, inhibiting lymphocytes dependent on this receptor from leaving secondary lymph nodes for peripheral blood, reducing the circulating number of these cells."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "date": "2024-12-05", "text": "We highlight promising therapies that target known mechanisms, including selective Janus kinase inhibitors, anti-adhesion molecules, tumor necrosis factor inhibitors, and IL-23 selective inhibitors. In addition, we delve into novel therapeutic strategies such as sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators, miR-124 upregulators, anti-fractalkine (CX3CL1), anti-TL1A, peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma agonists, TGFBRI/ALK5 inhibitors, anti-CCR9 agents, and other innovative small molecules, as well as combination therapies. ... Some of these drugs target well-known pathways, like JAK inhibitors and IL-23 blockers, while others focus on newer areas, such as specific receptors or molecules involved in inflammation. ... |Selective Inhibitors of IL-23|Guselkumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV and SC|Phase III|Active|NCT03466411|2030-06-30|✔|✘|✔|✘|✘|✘| ... Brazikumab (MEDI2070) is a human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds exclusively to the p19 subunit of IL-23. ... Mirikizumab, a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody targeting the p19 component of IL-23, offers both IV and SC administration options. ... Upon activation, this initiates a cascade of signaling events that regulate various biological processes, including lymphocyte migration, endothelial cell permeability, angiogenesis, cell differentiation, proliferation, survival, and apoptosis.^32^ S1P receptors regulate the influx of leukocytes from primary and secondary lymphoid organs into the intestinal wall, thereby contributing to the maintenance of intestinal inflammation. ^33,34^ Modulating this pathway holds the potential to impede lymphocyte migration into the gut, a critical factor in chronic inflammation, thereby reducing inflammation and minimizing tissue damage.^35^ Oral small molecules that can modulate S1P receptors have been developed, offering advantages over biologic agents such as ease of administration and rapid action. ... Etrasimod exhibits high affinity for S1P1,4,5 receptors with minimal impact on S1P3 and no activity on S1P2. ... ^43^ Blocking the activation of this pathway halts the activity of several chemokines involved in mediating inflammation. Several pan-JAK inhibitors and selective JAK inhibitors, including the newer TYK2 inhibitors, are in various stages of development for IBD. ... New small molecules targeting the JAK pathway inhibit critical cytokines in rheumatic diseases and IBD. ... LMT503 is a small molecule designed to treat IBD by modulating macrophage polarization.^146^ Macrophages play a crucial role in immune response and tissue homeostasis by switching between pro-inflammatory (M1) and anti-inflammatory (M2) states. LMT503 reprograms macrophage metabolism, shifting them from an M1-like inflammatory state to an M2-like anti-inflammatory state. In vitro studies showed that LMT503 suppresses M1 markers (e.g., iNOS) and enhances M2 markers (e.g., Arg-1)."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://marketintelo.com/report/inflammatory-bowel-disease-drugs-market", "domain": "marketintelo.com", "title": "Competitor Landscape", "date": "2025-08-24", "text": "The regulatory approval of next-generation biologics targeting novel inflammatory pathways - including JAK inhibitors, integrin antagonists, and S1P receptor modulators - has substantially broadened the therapeutic arsenal. ... The therapeutic landscape for inflammatory bowel disease has undergone revolutionary transformation through emergence of mechanistically distinct biologic classes offering superior efficacy and safety profiles compared to conventional small-molecule immunosuppressants. JAK-STAT inhibitors including tofacitinib and upadacitinib demonstrate oral bioavailability combined with targeted intracellular signaling inhibition, achieving clinical remission rates of 48-56% in moderate-to-severe disease compared to 22-28% with conventional therapies. Integrin antagonists such as vedolizumab provide selective gut-homing lymphocyte targeting, substantially reducing systemic immunosuppression and opportunistic infection risk. Sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators represent an emerging frontier, with ozanimod demonstrating 41% remission rates while preserving systemic immunity. Furthermore, dual-mechanism agents combining TNF inhibition with IL-23 pathway targeting are entering late-stage development, with interim data suggesting remission rates exceeding 58-62% in treatment-naive cohorts. ... Biologic therapies including TNF inhibitors, integrin antagonists, IL-12/IL-23 inhibitors, and JAK inhibitors represent the most rapidly expanding therapeutic category, driven by superior efficacy profiles enabling disease remission in 45-65% of patients compared to 20-35% with conventional small-molecule therapies. ... Integrin antagonist vedolizumab commands 18.7% of biologic market revenues, offering gut-selective targeting advantages particularly appealing to patients with treatment-limiting infections or malignancy concerns. ... Patients presenting with pure colonic ulcerative colitis frequently receive integrin antagonists or JAK inhibitors as first-line biologic therapy, leveraging gut-selective mechanisms minimizing systemic immunosuppression."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://s29.q4cdn.com/642306244/files/doc_downloads/2025/Publications/Danese-2025.pdf", "domain": "s29.q4cdn.com", "title": "Understanding the therapeutic toolkit for inflammatory bowel disease", "date": null, "text": "Subsequent advancements led to the development of new biologic agents with distinct mechanisms of action: vedolizumab, an anti-integrin α4β7 agent29,30; ustekinumab, which inhibits IL-12 and IL-2331,32; and risankizumab and mirikizumab, which specifically target ... and tailored lifestyle modifications to holistically address individual patient needs. • Current treatment options for IBD span a range of agents, including aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, biologic agents (anti-tumour necrosis factor agents, anti-integrins, anti-interleukins), and small molecules such as JAK inhibitors and S1P modulators. • Emerging therapies for IBD, including anti-IL-23, JAK inhibitors, TL1A inhibitors and RIPK1 inhibitors, offer targeted approaches to mitigate inflammation, expanding options for patients with IBD. ... The mechanisms of action of these drugs are illustrated in Fig. 1. ... Aminosalicylates are a class of drugs that includes sulfasalazine and 5-ASA. ... Cyclosporine A and tacrolimus are calcineurin inhibitors that work by blocking the nuclear translocation of the transcription factor NFAT, thereby ­preventing the initiation of T cell cytokine transcription76. ... Many IBD treatments focus on inhibiting pro-inflammatory cytokines, which is particularly evident with anti-tumour necrosis factor (TNF) agents (such as infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab pegol and golimumab), which are approved, and investigational therapies (such as V565 and tulinercept). Other therapies target specific interleukins, including: lutikizumab (targets IL-1α/β); aldesleukin and efavaleukin alfa (targets IL-2); PF-04236921 (inhibits IL-6); and olamkicept (targets IL-6 receptor). Additionally, lusvertikimab antagonizes the IL-7 receptor and GSK1070806 inhibits IL-18. ... Anti-TNF therapies bind to both soluble and transmembrane TNF, inhib- iting its interaction with receptors and therefore regulating various signalling pathways involved in IBD pathophysiology105,106."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33772", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "title": "Ozmosi | MT-501 Drug Profile", "date": "2025-06-19", "text": "Mechanisms of Action: Unknown Novel Mechanism: No Modality: Small Molecule"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10525, "cached_input_tokens": 10522, "output_tokens": 600}, "timing": {"search_ms": 1375.6, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 8666.9, "total_ms": 10042.5}, "luna_wall_ms": 8666.9, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "MT501’s mechanism or mechanistic class is needed to identify competing agents in the same class; the evidence does not state it. It also does not establish which named Crohn’s disease agents, if any, share MT501’s class or address the moderately to severely active CDAI and SES-CD population.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["MT-501 mechanism target Crohn’s disease", "MT-501 same-class competitors Crohn’s disease"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 2, "next_queries_subject_first": 2}}
{"row_id": "q001", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 19040, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/static-files/47cbb9ba-f8c5-43e5-9235-01ca63f91f25", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "title": "VTX002 UEGW 2024", "date": null, "text": "Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data ... • The efficacy and safety of tamuzimod as induction therapy was previously demonstrated in a phase 2, multicentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (NCT05156125)1 ... Open-label extension – Tamuzimod 60 mg up to ~ 2 years ... Key eligibility criteria: • Moderately to severely active UC • No/insufficient response, loss of response, and/or intolerance to conventional or advanced therapies (≤ 2 biologics with different mechanisms of action or 1 biologic + a Janus kinase inhibitor) Primary endpoint: clinical remission (MMS stool frequency (SF) subscore ≤ 1, rectal bleeding (RB) = 0, endoscopic subscore (ES) ≤ 1) at Week 13 in patients with baseline MMS 5-9 Long-term extension: clinical responders (≥ 2-point and ≥ 30% decrease from baseline in MMS, and ≥ 1-point decrease from baseline in RB or an absolute RB ≤ 1) at Week 13 were eligible for maintenance therapy with previously assigned treatment for up to 39 weeks ... • Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks • High rates of both clinical and endoscopic remission observed during tamuzimod induction and maintenance therapy potentially a result of rapid and sustained absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) reductions • 58.9% (tamuzimod 30 mg) and 71.5% (tamuzimod 60 mg) ALC decrease from baseline at Week 52"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "date": null, "text": "Tamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study. Moreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile. The selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1]. ... “The drug was very safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese. ... “Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of tamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded Prof. Danese. ... The infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/news-releases/news-release-details/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-phase-2-trial/", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase ...", "date": "2024-10-15", "text": "## Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024 ... ### Late-breaking abstract highlights high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission among LTE completers at Week 52 SAN DIEGO, Oct. 15, 2024 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Ventyx Biosciences, Inc. (Nasdaq: VTYX) (“Ventyx”), a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on advancing novel oral therapies that address a broad range of inflammatory diseases with significant unmet medical need, today announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria. “We are excited to present the new long-term extension data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at UEG Week,” said Raju Mohan, PhD, Chief Executive Officer. “These 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of our S1P1R modulator tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis, with a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy. We believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies for UC.” ... **Title: **“Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data”"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "date": null, "text": "Individuals who completed the U-ACHIEVE maintenance study were subsequently eligible for inclusion in the efficacy population of this long-term extension study. Patients in clinical remission per adapted Mayo score at week 52 of the maintenance study could continue their double-masked treatment upon entering the long-term extension study. ... In the long-term extension-only safety analysis, we assessed data from 467 patients, representing 1027·9 patient-years of continuous long-term extension exposure on a consistent upadacitinib dose. Treatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg. Event rates of serious treatment-emergent adverse events were 11·7 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 12·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg. The most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster. Three treatment-emergent adverse events leading to death were reported in the long-term extension-only safety population.This interim analysis supports the positive long-term risk–benefit profile for upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg among patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.AbbVie. ... Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo. Most adverse events were mild or moderate. The most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]). No adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences. ... The safety of mirikizumab in Crohn’s disease was consistent with its known favourable profile.Mirikizumab was safe and effective as induction and maintenance treatment for patients with moderately-to-severely active Crohn's disease who had intolerance, inadequate response, or loss of response to standard therapy.Eli Lilly and Company."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "date": null, "text": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns. ... ## Sphingosine 1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators as an Induction and Maintenance Therapy for Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials. (PubMed, Dig Dis Sci) - \"S1PR modulators demonstrate robust efficacy during the induction phase across all key clinical and tissue-based outcomes, with more modest or variable effects during maintenance. Their safety profile remains acceptable, supporting their role as a promising therapeutic option for UC.\" ... ## Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial. (PubMed, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol) - P2 | \"Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\""}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/884b63886644433694ee6557e3324413", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Ulcerative colitis, active severe - Drugs, Targets, Patents - Synapse", "date": null, "text": "Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo. Most adverse events were mild or moderate. The most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]). No adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.INTERPRETATIONInduction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGVentyx Biosciences. ... The overall safety profile was favourable and consistent with that of guselkumab in approved indications. In the induction study, adverse events were reported by 49% of patients in both groups (208 of 421 guselkumab-treated patients and 138 of 280 placebo-treated patients), serious adverse events were reported by 3% (12 of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 7% (20 of 280) of placebo-treated patients, and adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation were reported by 2% (seven of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 4% (11 of 280) of placebo-treated patients. In the maintenance study, adverse event rates were similar among groups, and the most frequently reported adverse events in all groups were ulcerative colitis, COVID-19, and arthralgia. No active tuberculosis, anaphylaxis, serum sickness, or clinically important hepatic disorders were reported in either study.INTERPRETATIONGuselkumab was effective and safe as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGJanssen Research and Development."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12650479/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Sphingosine-1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators for the ... - PMC", "date": "2025-10-29", "text": "They also appear to have a favourable safety profile, although awareness of potential cardiovascular and ophthalmological side-effects is crucial for safe use. ... ELEVATE-UC 12 consisted of a 12-week induction with a subsequent optional open-label extension (OLE), whilst ELEVATE-UC 52 involved a 12-week induction, at which point non-responders could enter an OLE, followed by 40-week maintenance programme [33]. ... Across both ELEVATE studies, there were nine cases (1.7%) of bradycardia reported [33]. ... Macular oedema occurred in three patients, two of which were in the etrasimod group of either study (one of which had pre-existing uveitis). ... Tamuzimod is a selective S1PRM that acts on S1PR1 and has completed phase two trials in UC. These trials demonstrated that tamuzimod at both 30 mg (*p* = 0.041) and 60 mg (*p* = 0.018) was associated with significantly higher rates of remission at 13 weeks compared to placebo [3]. There were no cardiovascular-related AEs reported and no cases of macular oedema. Phase 3 trials are awaited. ... All cardiac abnormalities, including hypertensive crisis, developed within 48 h of treatment initiation in the clinical trials."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "date": "2024-10-15", "text": "## Key Insights - Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy. - The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies. - High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease. Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease. The results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator. The long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect. ... He also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options. ... The Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period. ... - Sustained clinical remission rates - Continued endoscopic remission - A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/cf939ce88f9b42f9b400ce70eaf89a7a", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Ulcerative colitis, active moderate - Drugs, Targets, Patents", "date": null, "text": "Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo. Most adverse events were mild or moderate. The most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]). No adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.INTERPRETATIONInduction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGVentyx Biosciences. ... The overall safety profile was favourable and consistent with that of guselkumab in approved indications. In the induction study, adverse events were reported by 49% of patients in both groups (208 of 421 guselkumab-treated patients and 138 of 280 placebo-treated patients), serious adverse events were reported by 3% (12 of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 7% (20 of 280) of placebo-treated patients, and adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation were reported by 2% (seven of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 4% (11 of 280) of placebo-treated patients. In the maintenance study, adverse event rates were similar among groups, and the most frequently reported adverse events in all groups were ulcerative colitis, COVID-19, and arthralgia. No active tuberculosis, anaphylaxis, serum sickness, or clinically important hepatic disorders were reported in either study.INTERPRETATIONGuselkumab was effective and safe as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGJanssen Research and Development."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/6f5a2d16ea2a572e/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "date": "2024-10-15", "text": "- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy. - The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies. - High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease. Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease. The results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator. The long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect. ... He also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options. ... The Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period. ... - Sustained clinical remission rates - Continued endoscopic remission - A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10639, "cached_input_tokens": 10636, "output_tokens": 2586}, "timing": {"search_ms": 699.3, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 27224.1, "total_ms": 27923.4}, "luna_wall_ms": 27224.1, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "**UEG Week 2024** In the induction study, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in 33/70 (47%) of patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34/73 (47%) receiving 30 mg, and 24/70 (34%) receiving placebo; most were mild or moderate. The most frequently reported TEAEs (≥5% in any group) were upper respiratory tract infection (60 mg: 6 [9%]; 30 mg: 1 [1%]; placebo: 1 [1%]), anaemia (3 [3%], 4 [5%], and 6 [9%], respectively), and headache (4 [6%], 5 [7%], and 2 [3%]). No atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred. No cardiovascular-related AEs or cases of macular oedema were reported. [E4][E7] Prof. Danese said there were “no major adverse events”; he and Ventyx characterized the safety profile as favourable, with Ventyx describing a potential best-in-disease safety profile among oral UC therapies. [E2][E3]<br><br>In the long-term extension, maintenance with tamuzimod 30 mg or 60 mg was described as well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks. At Week 52, absolute lymphocyte count decreased from baseline by 58.9% with 30 mg and 71.5% with 60 mg. [E1] The 213-participant Phase 2 study’s open-label extension was terminated; Oppilan Pharma Ltd said the decision was not due to safety concerns. [E5]", "confident_score": 0.82, "missing": "Detailed long-term-extension TEAE incidence and types, serious or Grade ≥3 adverse-event rates, treatment discontinuations due to adverse events, and deaths during the extension are not specified. The induction findings do not give serious or Grade ≥3 adverse-event rates or adverse-event discontinuations.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E7"], "next_queries": ["Tamuzimod VTX002 long-term extension 52-week safety adverse events", "Tamuzimod VTX002 serious adverse events Grade 3 ulcerative colitis", "Tamuzimod VTX002 adverse event discontinuations induction trial", "Tamuzimod VTX002 long-term extension deaths adverse events"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 1285, "confident_score": 0.82, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 6, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 6, "cited_domains": ["conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "delta.larvol.com", "ir.ventyxbio.com", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "sciencedirect.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 21, "ungrounded_tokens": ["TEAEs"], "n_ungrounded": 1, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q012", "objective": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab pivotal, registrational, or Phase 3 trial in Unresectable or Metastatic Hepatocellular Carcinoma: planned or initiated status and expected start date", "anchors": ["Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775", "Lenvatinib", "Nofazinlimab", "NCT04194775"], "facet": "catalysts", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 11583, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/f021ad7c4cfbc551/nct04194775-nofazinlimab-lenvatinib-advanced-hepatocellular-carcinoma", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in... | Clinical Trial", "date": "2026-07-30", "text": "NCT04194775Active, not recruitingPhase 3 # A Multi-Center, Double-Blind, Randomized, Phase III Study to Investigate the Efficacy and Safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Combination With Lenvatinib Compared to Placebo in Combination With Lenvatinib as First-Line Therapy in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) CStone Pharmaceuticals125 sites in 6 countries534 enrolledStarted: December 13, 2019Last updated: July 30, 2026 ... - **Phase** Phase 3 - **Status** Active, not recruiting ... - **Enrollment** 534 ... - **Primary Endpoint** ... This is a multi-center, double-blind, randomized, phase III study to investigate the efficacy and safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in combination with lenvatinib and placebo in combination with lenvatinib in the treatment of subjects with no prior systemic treatment and with unresectable advanced hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). ... Results posted to ClinicalTrials.gov on 2026-07-30. ... 534 started · 0 completed ... June 18, 2025 (last year) - **Study Completion** September 30, 2026 (2 days ago) - **Last Verified**"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04194775", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma", "date": "2019-12-10", "text": "Drug: Nofazinlimab (CS1003)+Lenvatinib Drug: Nofazinlimab (CS1003) Placebo+Lenvatinib - CS1003-305 ... Inclusion criteria - Age ≥18 years on the day of signing informed consent-(For Taiwan, the lower limit of age is 20 years). - Subjects with unresectable advanced HCC, that is not eligible for surgery and/or locoregional therapy (Stage B or C based on Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer [BCLC] staging system, and meets either one of the following criteria: 1) histologically or cytologically confirmed diagnosis of HCC, 2) clinically confirmed diagnosis of HCC according to American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases (AASLD) criteria. Patients without cirrhosis require histological confirmation of diagnosis. - With at least one measurable lesion can be assessed - Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status (ECOG PS) 0 or 1. - Life expectancy ≥ 3 months. - Child-Pugh A - No prior systemic treatment for advanced HCC - Subjects with hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection, are willing to continue receiving antiviral treatment while on study. Subjects have adequate organ and marrow function. Female subjects with childbearing potential must have negative serum pregnancy test result at screening. Female subjects with childbearing potential, and male subjects and their female partners with childbearing potential must agree to use an contraceptive method(s) from the day of signing informed consent form (ICF), during the study and till at least 6 months after the last dose of study treatment. Exclusion criteria - Fibrolamellar-HCC, sarcomatoid, cholangiocellular carcinoma or mixed cholangiocarcinoma and HCC. - A prior bleeding event due to esophageal within 6 months or other gastrointestinal bleeding events within 28 days prior to screening. - Malabsorption syndrome or inability to take oral medication due to other causes. - HBV and HCV co-infection. - Investigator evaluates to increase the drug related risk caused by enrolling subjects in trial and taking study drug, or any serious or uncontrolled systematic disease that confound the drug absorption or the study outcome, e,g diabetes mellitus, hypertension, rheumatoid arthritis, major cardiovascular disease and so on. - Surgery or locoregional therapy for palliative purpose within 4 weeks prior to study treatment. - History of other malignancy(ies) in the past 5 years, except for malignant disease treated with curative intent and without active disease. - Known history of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection or acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). - Current or prior use of systemic corticosteroid (> 10 mg/day prednisone or equivalent) or other immunosuppressive medication within 14 days prior to the first dose of study treatment. - History of bone marrow transplantation or organ transplantation. - History of anaphylaxis or hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the investigational product. - Any contraindication of lenvatinib. - Known history of drugs abuse that would interfere with ..."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.cancerindex.io/cancer/liver-epithelial-neoplasm/trials?status=ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING", "domain": "cancerindex.io", "title": "Liver Epithelial Neoplasm", "date": null, "text": "|NCT04770896|A Study of Atezolizumab With Lenvatinib or Sorafenib Versus Lenvatinib or Sorafenib Alone in Hepatocellular Carcinoma Previously Treated With Atezolizumab and Bevacizumab(IMbrave251)|active not recruiting|Phase 3|557|Hoffmann-La RocheINDUSTRY|28|Aug 25, 2026|clinicaltrials| ... |NCT06618300|Streamlined Resin Y90 Radiation Segmentectomy for Small HCC(One&Done)|active not recruiting|—|30|UNC Lineberger Comprehensive Cancer CenterOTHER|1|Aug 13, 2026|clinicaltrials| ... |NCT04194775|A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma|active not recruiting|Phase 3|534|CStone PharmaceuticalsINDUSTRY|6|Jul 30, 2026|clinicaltrials| ... |NCT04523493|Phase III Study of Toripalimab（JS001） Combined With Lenvatinib for Advanced HCC|active not recruiting|Phase 3|530|Shanghai Junshi Bioscience Co., Ltd.OTHER|5|Jul 6, 2026|clinicaltrials|"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT03781934", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "A Study to Evaluate MIV-818 in Patients With Liver Cancer Manifestations", "date": null, "text": "No results posted yet for this study ... This is an open-label, multicentre dose escalation/expansion study to assess safety and tolerability of MIV 818 as either monotherapy or in combination with 1) lenvatinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor used as a standard of care for the treatment of HCC or 2) pembrolizumab, a PD-1 inhibitor. ... ## Timeline & Regulatory - **Start** 2018-09-05 - **Primary Completion** 2025-01-07 - **Completion** 2025-06-16 ... - A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma NCT04194775 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE3 - Lenvatinib in Patients With Previously Treated Advanced Biliary Tract Cancer ... - HAIC Plus Lenvatinib and Toripalimab for Advanced HCC"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://onco.cc/trials/nct04194775/", "domain": "onco.cc", "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in… · NCT04194775 · OnCo", "date": null, "text": "A phase 3 trial of Lenvatinib in hepatocellular carcinoma, run by CStone Pharmaceuticals, active and no longer recruiting. ... A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma is a phase 3 interventional study registered as NCT04194775 by CStone Pharmaceuticals, with 534 participants enrolled, started 2019-12-13 and due to reach its primary completion in 2025-06-18. Interventions recorded: Nofazinlimab (CS1003)+Lenvatinib, Nofazinlimab (CS1003) Placebo+Lenvatinib."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.cancerindex.io/trial/NCT04194775", "domain": "cancerindex.io", "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma", "date": null, "text": "NCT04194775CI-TRIAL-00119920active not recruitingPhase 3Results postedClinicalTrials.gov clinicaltrialsProvenance- **Source** ... ### Dates and enrollment - **Start** Dec 13, 2019 - **Primary completion** Jun 18, 2025 - **Completion** Sep 30, 2026 - **First posted** Dec 11, 2019 - **Last update posted** Jul 30, 2026 - **Results first posted** Jul 30, 2026 - **Enrollment** 534 (actual)"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT05103904", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "Lenvatinib for the Treatment of Recurrent Hepatocellular Carcinoma After Liver Transplant", "date": null, "text": "No results posted yet for this study ... This phase II trial evaluates lenvatinib for the treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) that has come back (recurrent) after a liver transplant. ... ## Eligibility - **Min Age** 18 Years - **Sex** ALL - **Healthy Volunteers** No ## Timeline & Regulatory - **Start** 2022-04-19 - **Primary Completion** 2025-05-09 - **Completion** 2025-05-09 - **FDA Drug** Yes ... NCT03713593 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3 - A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma NCT04194775 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE3"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/bf31aad0266d41688ed754613150c716", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Nofazinlimab - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "date": null, "text": "|NCT04194775 (CS1003-305, ESMO2025)|Phase 3|Hepatocellular Carcinoma First line|534|Nofazinlimab + Lenvatinib|crgoxwqgbt(rzfiobiepn) = wfpwygxeca czvdkshulz (pvclpucluq ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2025| |--|--|--|--|--|--|--|--| |NCT04194775 (CS1003-305, ESMO2025)|Phase 3|Hepatocellular Carcinoma First line|534|Placebo + Lenvatinib|crgoxwqgbt(rzfiobiepn) = cupywigtzp czvdkshulz (pvclpucluq ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2025|"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jjco/article/54/10/1071/7687758", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "Current status of the cost burden of first-line systemic treatment for ...", "date": "2024-10-03", "text": "Thus, there is currently no consensus on the use of atezolizumab plus bevacizumab, durvalumab and tremelimumab for the treatment of advanced HCC. Additionally, many ongoing trials still use TKIs as controls since trials comparing TKIs and ICIs were initiated almost simultaneously, and several trials have not yet reported their results (Table 2). ... | |NCT04194775|Nofazinlimab (anti-PD-1 antibody) plus lenvatinib|Lenvatinib|III|OS|First-line| ... | |NCT03764293|SHR-1210 (anti-PD-1 antibody) plus apatinib (TKI)|Sorafenib|III|OS & PFS|First-line|"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.guidelinecentral.com/clinical-trial/?id=NCT04194775&v=tabular", "domain": "guidelinecentral.com", "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With ...", "date": "2019-12-13", "text": "# A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma - Recruitment Status - ACTIVE, NOT RECRUITING(See Contacts and Locations)Verified July 2025 by CStone Pharmaceuticals ... - NCT04194775 ... - December 10, 2019 ... - July 31, 2025 ... ClinicalTrials.gov processed this data on July 2025. Link to the current ClinicalTrials.gov record . ... |Study Start Date|December 13, 2019| |Actual Primary Completion Date|June 18, 2025| ... |Official Title|A Multi-Center, Double-Blind, Randomized, Phase III Study to Investigate the Efficacy and Safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Combination With Lenvatinib Compared to Placebo in Combination With Lenvatinib as First-Line Therapy in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)| |Brief Summary|This is a multi-center, double-blind, randomized, phase III study to investigate the efficacy and safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in combination with lenvatinib and placebo in combination with lenvatinib in the treatment of subjects with no prior systemic treatment and with unresectable advanced hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). In this study, Nofazinlimab (CS1003) (or placebo) and lenvatinib are both considered as the study treatment while Nofazinlimab (CS1003) (or placebo) is the investigational product of and lenvatinib is selected as the basic treatment for HCC.| ... |Recruitment Status|Active, not recruiting| |--|--| |Anticipated Enrollment|534| |Anticipated Study Completion Date|1w 1d from now| |Anticipated Primary Study Completion Date|June 18, 2025|"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8891, "cached_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1058}, "timing": {"search_ms": 1057.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 13786.4, "total_ms": 14844.1}, "luna_wall_ms": 13786.4, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "The Phase 3 trial NCT04194775 started on December 13, 2019, and was active, not recruiting in its July 30, 2026 update. [E1][E6]", "confident_score": 0.86, "missing": "A separately stated expected start date; the items provide the actual start date, December 13, 2019.", "citations": ["E1", "E6"], "next_queries": ["Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 expected start date"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 128, "confident_score": 0.86, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 2, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 2, "cited_domains": ["cancerindex.io", "trial.medpath.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 7, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q006", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease Moderately to Severely Active Crohn’s Disease Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis with inadequate response, loss of response, or intolerance to ≥1 biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity competing agents in the same mechanistic class as JNJ-4804 Co Antibody Therapy, including Antibody Cocktail Monoclonal Antibody and IL-23 IL23A TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha therapies", "anchors": ["JNJ-4804"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 17861, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.jnj.com/media-center/press-releases/johnson-johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-jnj-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "jnj.com", "title": "JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical ...", "date": "2026-05-05", "text": "## JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials ... **Chicago, Ill., (May 5, 2026) **– Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies. ... In the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated higher clinical remission^b^ rates (50.8%) and endoscopic response^c ^rates (38.1%) versus golimumab (25.4% for clinical remission, 19.8% for endoscopic response) at Week 48. The JNJ-4804 rates were also numerically higher than the rates achieved with guselkumab (42.5% for clinical remission and 33.9% for endoscopic response). In the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with CD who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo. ... In the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated superior clinical remission^d^ rates compared to golimumab, with 41.0% of JNJ-4804-treated patients achieving clinical remission at Week 48 versus golimumab (11.5%), and numerically higher rates than guselkumab (34.0%). ... JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases. JNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.investor.jnj.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Johnson--Johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-JNJ-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease/default.aspx?_sp=75fe9bba-8c8e-4470-a97f-efa1931f0f46", "domain": "investor.jnj.com", "title": "Johnson & Johnson investigational co-antibody therapy JNJ-4804 ...", "date": "2026-05-05", "text": "*JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes* *JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways* ... CHICAGO, May 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn's disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies. ... ** In the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated higher clinical remission^b^ rates (50.8%) and endoscopic response^c ^rates (38.1%) versus golimumab (25.4% for clinical remission, 19.8% for endoscopic response) at Week 48. The JNJ-4804 rates were also numerically higher than the rates achieved with guselkumab (42.5% for clinical remission and 33.9% for endoscopic response). In the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with CD who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo. ... ** In the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated superior clinical remission^d^ rates compared to golimumab, with 41.0% of JNJ-4804-treated patients achieving clinical remission at Week 48 versus golimumab (11.5%), and numerically higher rates than guselkumab (34.0%). ... \"By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach. ... ** JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases. JNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "title": "EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE FIRST CO-ANTIBODY THERAPY, JNJ ...", "date": null, "text": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies Objective: The Phase 2b DUET-CD trial (NCT05242471) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease refractory to systemic therapies In the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy ... In patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose JNJ-4804 exceeded golimumab and guselkumab, with more than additive benefits in high-bar endoscopic and deep remission outcomes ... JNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, demonstrated clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory Crohn’s disease"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.investor.jnj.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Johnson--Johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-JNJ-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease/default.aspx?_sp=649ecbb8-bb9f-4dba-8dc0-824bc2fff7e2", "domain": "investor.jnj.com", "title": "News Details - JNJ Investor Relations", "date": "2026-05-05", "text": "*JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes* *JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways* ... CHICAGO, May 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn's disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies. ... ** In the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated higher clinical remission^b^ rates (50.8%) and endoscopic response^c ^rates (38.1%) versus golimumab (25.4% for clinical remission, 19.8% for endoscopic response) at Week 48. ... \"By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach. ... Based on results from the Phase 2b DUET studies, Johnson & Johnson will be initiating the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active CD as well as the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active UC. ... ** The DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn's disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab. ... ** JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases. JNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection. JNJ-4804 is being studied in the Phase 2b DUET clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease and the Phase 2a AFFINITY study in active psoriatic arthritis (PsA)."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.healio.com/news/gastroenterology/20260521/strikingly-better-coantibody-combination-tops-golimumab-guselkumab-in-refractory-ibd", "domain": "healio.com", "title": "‘Strikingly better’: Co-antibody combination tops golimumab ...", "date": "2026-05-22", "text": "CHICAGO — Combining golimumab and guselkumab into JNJ-78934804, a fixed-dose co-antibody therapy, helped overcome treatment failure in inflammatory bowel disease and exceeded dose-dependent benefits of either drug alone, according to data. ... Two parallel phase 2b trials combining the anti-tumor necrosis factor and anti-interleukin-23 medications in Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis, DUET-CD and DUET-UC, were presented by lead authors at Digestive Disease Week. “Results show that people with Crohn’s disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapies do strikingly better with combination therapy of two drugs with different mechanisms of action, specifically with golimumab, an anti-TNF antibody, and guselkumab, an anti-IL-23 antibody, rather than with treatment with either one alone,” **Bruce E. Sands, MD, MS,** chief of the Dr. Henry D. Janowitz Division of Gastroenterology at Mount Sinai Health System and lead author of the CD study, told Healio. ... “Combining anti-TNF and anti-IL-23 was safe and showed superiority in the toughest to treat UC patients.” ... In the DUET-CD study, 693 adults with Crohn’s disease (57% men) were randomly assigned to receive guselkumab (Tremfya, Janssen), golimumab (Simponi, Janssen), low-, mid- or high-dose JNJ-78934804 (JNJ-4804, Johnson & Johnson) or placebo. ... High-dose JNJ-4804 achieved higher rates of the co-primary endpoints than golimumab, including clinical remission (difference = 25.7; *P* < .001) and endoscopic response (difference = 18.5; nominal *P* < .001), with similar or greater efficacy compared with guselkumab. ... At week 48, high-dose JNJ-4804 demonstrated clinically meaningful improvements in both clinical remission and endoscopic response compared with golimumab (27.3 and 21.2, respectively), guselkumab (21.2 and 11.7) and placebo (39.4 and 35). ... This “fixed-dose combination of golimumab and guselkumab outperformed either one alone” in refractory Crohn’s disease without increased risk for adverse events compared with monotherapy, Sands told Healio."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients-with-moderately-to-sever.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "title": "efficacy and safety of the first co-antibody therapy, jnj- ...", "date": null, "text": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies In the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy This combination was informed by preclinical evidence of complementary and distinct IL-23 and TNF-α activity, with molecular synergy demonstrated in VEGA ... The Phase 2b DUET-UC trial (NCT05242484) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis refractory to systemic therapies ... In patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose JNJ-4804 exceeded the additive effects of golimumab and guselkumab across all key clinical, endoscopic, and histologic-endoscopic endpoints ... JNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, delivered clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory disease ... In the overall population, efficacy of high-dose JNJ-4804 was superior to golimumab and numerically greater than guselkumab"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/j-j-pushes-dual-antibody-ibd-therapy-into-phase-3-despite-mid-stage-fails", "domain": "biospace.com", "title": "J&J pushes dual-antibody IBD therapy into Phase 3 ...", "date": "2026-05-06", "text": "## Johnson & Johnson plans to advance the co-antibody therapy, which combines its IL-23 blocker Tremfya and the TNF-alpha inhibitor Simponi, into late-stage testing for Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis. Johnson & Johnson’s investigational antibody combo failed to significantly improve remission and response in a pair of mid-stage trials of inflammatory bowel diseases, but that isn’t stopping the pharma from forging ahead to pivotal testing. The investigational co-antibody therapy, dubbed JNJ-4804, is a fixed-dose combination of the pharma’s anti-IL-23 therapy Tremfya and TNF-alpha blocker Simponi. Combining both molecules in one therapy “yields a synergistic therapeutic approach” for patients resistant to monotherapies, Esi Lamousé Smith, vice president of J&J’s Gastroenterology Disease Area, said in a Tuesday release. ... In the Phase 2b DUET-CD study, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed significantly higher clinical remission and endoscopic response rates versus monotherapy with Simponi, but not versus Tremfya. ... The mid-stage DUET-UC study, which focused on ulcerative colitis, reached similar outcomes—JNJ-4804 elicited significantly greater clinical remission than Simponi but not Tremfya. Endoscopic improvement, which in this study was set as a secondary endpoint, was “numerically higher” in those treated with the combo therapy versus Tremfya or Simponi alone. ... In these populations, according to the pharma, JNJ-4804 elicited “meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures.” These signals warrant further development, according to J&J, which on Tuesday said it would launch the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD study in Crohn’s disease and DUET ENCORE-UC study in ulcerative colitis."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/inflammatory-bowel-disease-market-to-experience-strong-uptake-during-the-forecast-period-20262036-driven-by-development-of-next-generation-therapies-targeting-il-23-tl1a-and-s1p-pathways--delveinsight-302838594.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease Market to Experience Strong ...", "date": "2026-07-30", "text": "- The promising inflammatory bowel disease therapies in clinical trials include **ABX464 (obefazimod), Tulisokibart (MK-7240), RHB-204, TEV-574 (duvakitug), AGMB-129, Olamkicept, JNJ-4804 Co-antibody Therapy, Zotemtegrast, ATH-063, SPY001, SPY002, SPY003, DB-3Q, Zasocitinib, ABBV-243, ABBV-701, TRX103, XmAb412, ** and others. ... IL-23 inhibitors, including **Mirikizumab (OMVOH), Risankizumab (SKYRIZI), and Guselkumab (TREMFYA)**, are competing alongside well-established TNF inhibitors such as **Adalimumab (HUMIRA) and Infliximab (REMICADE)**. ... - Among the IL-23 inhibitors, **Risankizumab (SKYRIZI) and Guselkumab (TREMFYA)** are emerging as major competitors, each seeking to strengthen its position in the moderate-to-severe IBD market and capitalize on the growing demand for targeted biologic therapies."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "date": "2022-12-01", "text": "|2aNCT03662542|VEGA|Guselkumab (200 mg IV at wks 0,4,8) versus golimumab (200 mg SC at wk 0 then 100 mg SC at wks 2,6,10) versus a combination with guselkumab and golimumab (GUS 200 mg IV + GOL 200 mg SC at wk 0, GOL 100 mg SC at wks2, 6, and 10, and GUS 200 mg IV at wks 4 and 8)|I|*n* = 214| Anti-TNF-naive|12 weeks|- Clinical response at week 12 - Golimumab: 44/72 (61.1%) - Guselkumab: 53/71 (74.6%) - Golimumab + Guselkumab: 59/71 (83.1%); *p* = 0.003 for golimumab and *p* = 0.215 for guselkumab|- Clinical remission, symptomatic remission, endoscopic improvement, normalized fecal calprotectin at week 12: Significant compared to both Gol and Gus - Endoscopic normalization at week 12: Significant compared to Gus - Histologic remission at week 12: Significant compared to Gol|AEs, SAEs, and infecti ... An example of an ongoing dual targeted therapy CT is the phase 2a VEGA study comparing efficacy and safety of a combination induction therapy with guselkumab and GOL (JNJ-78934804) in moderate to severe active UC."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pdf.dfcfw.com/pdf/H3_AP202506171692727016_1.pdf?1750195099000.pdf", "domain": "pdf.dfcfw.com", "title": "IBD治疗领域存在未满足的需求，关注新靶点", "date": null, "text": "◼Guselkumab（Tremfya）：由Jassen/JNJ开发的靶向IL-23p19亚基的全人源IgG1λ抗体，同时其Fc片段可靶向CD64（FcγRI，存在于部分表达IL-23的髓系 抗原呈递细胞表面），先后获批PsO（2017）、PsA（2020）、CD（2024.09）、UC（2025.03）等适应症。 ... ◼Mirikizumab（Omvoh）：由Eli Lilly开发的靶向IL-23p19亚基的人源化IgG4抗体，先后获批UC（2023.10）及CD（2025.01）等适应症。 ... Golimumab + Guselkumab：UC适应症Ph2 VEGA研究，入组未经过TNF-α或IL-23p19抑制剂治疗的中重度UC患者214名，接受联用、GOL或GUS单药治疗， ... JNJ-4804 (Golimumab+ Guselkumab， Ph2b UC/CD)"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11598, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 3984}, "timing": {"search_ms": 519.8, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 41030.6, "total_ms": 41550.4}, "luna_wall_ms": 41030.6, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; a fixed-dose combination of guselkumab and golimumab) is an investigational co-antibody targeting IL-23 and TNF-α, delivered as a single subcutaneous injection. Johnson & Johnson calls it the first and only fixed-dose co-antibody designed to block both pathways. The Phase 2b DUET-CD and DUET-UC studies compared it with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab; the studies are described as randomized, double-blind and dose-ranging. DUET-CD is NCT05242471 and DUET-UC is NCT05242484. Johnson & Johnson said it would initiate the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD and DUET ENCORE-UC trials in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis, respectively. [E1][E2][E3][E4][E6]\n\nIn the reported overall-population results, JNJ-4804 had clinical remission of 50.8% and endoscopic response of 38.1%, versus 25.4% and 19.8% for golimumab and 42.5% and 33.9% for guselkumab at Week 48. A further overall-population clinical-remission result was 41.0% for JNJ-4804, versus 11.5% for golimumab and 34.0% for guselkumab at Week 48. For the highly refractory Crohn’s disease subgroup with inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, Johnson & Johnson reported clinically meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints versus golimumab, guselkumab and placebo. [E1][E2]\n\nIn DUET-CD, 693 adults with Crohn’s disease (57% men) were randomly assigned to guselkumab, golimumab, low-, mid- or high-dose JNJ-78934804, or placebo. High-dose JNJ-4804’s reported differences versus golimumab were 25.7 for clinical remission (P < .001) and 18.5 for endoscopic response (nominal P < .001). At Week 48, reported improvements for clinical remission and endoscopic response, respectively, were 27.3 and 21.2 versus golimumab, 21.2 and 11.7 versus guselkumab, and 39.4 and 35 versus placebo. Healio also reported no increased risk for adverse events versus monotherapy. BioSpace characterized the Crohn’s and ulcerative colitis mid-stage trials as failing to significantly improve remission and response, while also saying high-dose JNJ-4804 significantly improved Crohn’s clinical remission and endoscopic response versus golimumab but not guselkumab; for ulcerative colitis, it said remission was significantly greater than with golimumab but not guselkumab, and endoscopic improvement was numerically higher than with either. [E5][E7]\n\nFor the Phase 2a VEGA study in moderate-to-severe active ulcerative colitis, NCT03662542, 214 anti-TNF-naive patients received guselkumab, golimumab or their combination (JNJ-78934804). The reported regimens were guselkumab 200 mg IV at weeks 0, 4 and 8; golimumab 200 mg SC at week 0 followed by 100 mg SC at weeks 2, 6 and 10; or combination guselkumab 200 mg IV plus golimumab 200 mg SC at week 0, golimumab 100 mg SC at weeks 2, 6 and 10, and guselkumab 200 mg IV at weeks 4 and 8. At week 12, clinical response was 44/72 (61.1%) with golimumab, 53/71 (74.6%) with guselkumab and 59/71 (83.1%) with the combination; p = 0.003 versus golimumab and p = 0.215 versus guselkumab. The source also reports significant combination results versus both monotherapies for clinical remission, symptomatic remission, endoscopic improvement and normalized fecal calprotectin at week 12; endoscopic normalization was significant versus guselkumab, and histologic remission was significant versus golimumab. [E9]\n\nOther named IBD therapies in clinical trials are ABX464 (obefazimod), Tulisokibart (MK-7240), RHB-204, TEV-574 (duvakitug), AGMB-129, Olamkicept, Zotemtegrast, ATH-063, SPY001, SPY002, SPY003, DB-3Q, Zasocitinib, ABBV-243, ABBV-701, TRX103 and XmAb412. The named IL-23 inhibitors are mirikizumab (Omvoh), risankizumab (Skyrizi) and guselkumab (Tremfya); the named TNF inhibitors are adalimumab (Humira) and infliximab (Remicade). The source lists mirikizumab, risankizumab and guselkumab as competing in the moderate-to-severe IBD market alongside TNF inhibitors. Guselkumab is described as targeting IL-23p19 and as approved for Crohn’s disease in 2024.09 and ulcerative colitis in 2025.03; mirikizumab is described as targeting IL-23p19 and as approved for ulcerative colitis in 2023.10 and Crohn’s disease in 2025.01. [E8][E10]\n\nFor the additional IBD therapies listed above, and for adalimumab and infliximab, the available details do not specify each agent’s mechanism, disease subtype, development stage and trial results. The supplied details also do not establish the specific ulcerative-colitis eligibility criterion of inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance to at least one biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity; provide exact DUET-CD/DUET-UC dose amounts, schedules and full enrollment/eligibility details; give the numerical refractory-subgroup results or complete numerical ulcerative-colitis results; or establish whether the stated Phase 3 trials were initiated. [E1][E3][E4][E5][E6][E7][E8][E10]", "confident_score": 0.78, "missing": "For the additional IBD therapies listed above, and for adalimumab and infliximab: each agent’s mechanism, disease subtype, development stage and trial results. The ulcerative-colitis eligibility criterion of inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance to at least one biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity. Exact DUET-CD/DUET-UC dose amounts and schedules, full enrollment and eligibility details, numerical refractory-subgroup results, and complete numerical ulcerative-colitis results. Whether the stated Phase 3 trials were initiated.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8", "E9", "E10"], "next_queries": ["JNJ-4804 DUET-CD DUET-UC doses enrollment eligibility", "JNJ-4804 refractory ulcerative colitis subgroup results", "JNJ-4804 DUET ENCORE-CD DUET ENCORE-UC status", "JNJ-4804 IBD competitors mechanisms trial stage results", "JNJ-4804 UC biologic inadequate response loss response intolerance eligibility"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 4973, "confident_score": 0.78, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 20, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 10, "cited_domains": ["biospace.com", "healio.com", "investor.jnj.com", "jnj.com", "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "pdf.dfcfw.com", "prnewswire.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 95, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q010", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication LY4395089, Eli Lilly & Company, Crohn''s Disease", "anchors": ["LY4395089"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 12771, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/026286e1f04d4c549c14023c3c12d3ed", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "LY-4395089 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "date": null, "text": "Therapeutic Areas Immune System Diseases Digestive System Disorders Active Indication Crohn Disease Crohn's disease, active moderate Crohn's disease, active severe"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/LLY/drug/LY4395089", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "title": "LY4395089 - Crohn Disease | Phase 2 | Eli Lilly and ...", "date": "2026-09-22", "text": "NCT07483099A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's DiseaseCrohn Disease ... |Itraconazole|DRUG|Administered orally| ... ## Competitive Landscape -Crohn's Disease 77 trials (matched to \"Crohn Disease\") Top 11 of 13 competitors |Company|Ticker|Trials|Lead Phase|Drugs| |--|--|--|--|--| |Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970| |Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089| |AbbVie, Inc.|ABBV|14|PHASE3|Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, ABBV-8736| |Takeda Pharmaceutical Co. Ltd. Sponsored ADR|TAK|16|PHASE3|Vedolizumab, Zasocitinib, Darvadstrocel| |Johnson & Johnson|JNJ|13|PHASE3|Ustekinumab, Guselkumab, Golimumab, JNJ-78934804, Icotrokinra| |Merck & Co., Inc.|MRK|2|PHASE3|Tulisokibart| |AstraZeneca PLC|AZN|2|PHASE2|AZD7798| |Disc Medicine, Inc.|IRON|1|PHASE2|DISC-0974| |Spyre Therapeutics, Inc|SYRE|1|PHASE2|SPY001, SPY002, SPY003| |Abivax SA Sponsored ADR|ABVX|1|PHASE2|Obefazimod| |Palisade Bio, Inc.|PALI|1|PHASE1|PALI-2108| ... LY4395089 is an investigational small molecule being studied for the treatment of Crohn's disease. It is currently in Phase 2 clinical development, where it is being evaluated in combination with mirikizumab and compared against mirikizumab alone in adults with Crohn's disease. ... LY4395089 is being developed by Eli Lilly and Company, a biopharmaceutical company listed on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol LLY. The company is conducting clinical trials to evaluate the drug's safety and efficacy in Crohn's disease and in healthy participants. ... LY4395089 is in Phase 2 clinical development for Crohn's disease. A Phase 1 study in healthy participants has been completed. The drug is investigational and has not been approved by regulatory authorities. ... LY4395089 is being studied in a Phase 2 trial, NCT07483099, which is recruiting adults with Crohn's disease across multiple countries including the United States, Belgium, Brazil, Canada, China, Germany, Hungary, Israel, Italy, and Poland. ... Yes, LY4395089 is being tested in combination with mirikizumab in a Phase 2 trial for Crohn's disease. The study compares the combination of LY4395089 and mirikizumab against mirikizumab alone in adult participants with the condition."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/2494549/crohns-disease-pipeline-appears-robust-with-90-key-pharma-companies-actively-working-in-the-domain-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "title": "Crohns' Disease Pipeline Appears Robust With 90+ Key Pharma ...", "date": "2026-06-16", "text": "Around 540 participants will be enrolled in the study at approximately 300 sites worldwide. - ***On June 04, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** announced a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD). This study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07483099", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "title": "Study Details | NCT07483099 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "date": "2026-04-21", "text": "The main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD). This study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks. ... A Phase 2, Multicenter, Randomized, Open-Label, Active-Controlled Study to Investigate LY4395089/Mirikizumab Co-administration Compared With Mirikizumab in Adults With Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease ... Intervention / Treatment ** - Drug: LY4395089 - Drug: Mirikizumab - Intravenous (IV) - Drug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC) ... |Participant Group/Arm **|Active Comparator: Mirikizumab Study Period 1: Mirikizumab administered via IV. Study Period 2: Responders will receive mirikizumab SC.| |Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: LY4395089 and Mirikizumab Co-Administration Study Period 1: Co-administration of LY4395089 administered orally and mirikizumab administered IV.Study Period 2: Responders will stop LY4395089 and receive mirikizumab SC.| ... |Percentage of Participants with Crohn's Disease who Achieve Endoscopic Response|The percentage of participants who achieve endoscopic response at week 12 of Study Period 1.|Baseline, Up to Week 12| ... |Percentage of Participants who Achieve Clinical Remission| |Baseline, Up to Week 12| |Percent Change from Baseline in Fecal Calprotectin| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c639c", "domain": "withpower.com", "title": "Multiple Drugs for Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease", "date": "2024-07-03", "text": "This trial tests new treatments for individuals with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease, conditions that cause inflammation in the digestive tract. The focus is on evaluating the effectiveness and safety of these treatments for those with moderate to severe symptoms. ... For LY4395089, less information is available because it is still under testing and not yet approved by the FDA. This trial is in an early stage (Phase 2), meaning researchers are still assessing its safety and effectiveness. While some safety data exist, further testing is necessary to confirm its safety. ... Researchers are excited about these treatments for ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease because they offer new ways to tackle these conditions. Unlike standard treatments like corticosteroids and immunosuppressants, Mirikizumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets the p19 subunit of IL-23, potentially offering a more targeted approach to inflammation. The co-administration of LY4395089 with Mirikizumab introduces an oral component to the treatment, which could enhance convenience and effectiveness. This combination approach could lead to more personalized and efficient treatment, potentially improving patient outcomes and quality of life. ... Research has shown that mirikizumab, one of the treatments in this trial, effectively treats both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. Studies indicate that about 63% of patients with ulcerative colitis experienced symptom improvement. For Crohn's disease, mirikizumab helped over 80% of patients maintain relief from symptoms like the urgent need to use the bathroom. ... Early results suggest that the combination might be more effective, as LY4395089 outperformed a placebo in other studies. ... The study tests new drugs (LY4395089 and Mirikizumab given by IV or injection) in people with moderate to severe Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis. Participants are randomly assigned to different treatment groups. The study lasts at least 62 weeks. ... LY4395089 is an investigational drug, meaning it is currently being studied in clinical trials and is not yet approved by the FDA for any ..."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/4798717/crohns-disease-clinical-trial-drug-development-pipeline-expands-with-contributions-from-90-key-companies-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "title": "Crohns' Disease Clinical Trial Drug Development Pipeline ...", "date": "2026-09-25", "text": "- ***On September 22, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** initiated a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD). This study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/TAK/drug/Ontamalimab", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "title": "Ontamalimab (Ph 3) - Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Limited", "date": null, "text": "## Target and mechanism Molecular targetMADCAM1 Target classInhibitor ModalityMonoclonal antibody ... |Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970| |Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089| ... Ontamalimab is an investigational monoclonal antibody being developed for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It is designed as a maintenance therapy for these inflammatory bowel diseases. It has not been approved by the FDA and remains in clinical development. ... Ontamalimab targets MADCAM1, also known as mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule 1. It acts as an inhibitor of this target. By binding to MADCAM1, ontamalimab is thought to interfere with the migration of inflammatory cells into the gastrointestinal tract, which may help reduce inflammation in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. ... Ontamalimab is in Phase 3 clinical development. It is an investigational drug and has not been approved by the FDA for any indication."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c750c", "domain": "withpower.com", "title": "LY4395089 + Mirikizumab for Crohn's Disease - WithPower", "date": "2021-10-09", "text": "This trial tests a new combination treatment for individuals with moderate to severe Crohn's disease, a condition that causes inflammation in the digestive tract. Researchers aim to determine if combining LY4395089, an experimental drug, with mirikizumab is more effective than using mirikizumab alone. ... LY4395089 is a novel oral treatment that, when combined with mirikizumab, could provide a more targeted approach to managing inflammation. Mirikizumab, an IL-23 inhibitor, is administered intravenously and then subcutaneously, which might offer more sustained relief for patients who respond well. This combination has the potential to enhance efficacy and improve patient outcomes by leveraging different mechanisms of action and routes of administration. ... Research has shown that mirikizumab effectively treats Crohn's disease. Specifically, studies found that over 92% of patients who were symptom-free remained so after two years of treatment. Additionally, about 60-67% of patients experienced significant symptom improvement. ... LY4395089 reduces inflammation, and its use with mirikizumab may yield even better results by more effectively addressing gut inflammation. ... The study compares the safety and effectiveness of a new drug (LY4395089) given together with mirikizumab versus mirikizumab alone. Treatments are given by IV or under the skin over about 62 weeks. ... The main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together ..."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/trial/706080", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "title": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to ...", "date": "2026-05-04", "text": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and mirikizumab (LY3074828)] given together can help treat Crohn’s disease (CD01 study). ... The CD01 study will test if using 2 investigational medicines together can help treat Crohn’s disease. ... Mirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/find", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "title": "Find Lilly Clinical Trials", "date": "2026-09-23", "text": "### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease to test if different investigati... ENROLLING AGE 18 - 80 years Conditions Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease Investigational Medicines Mirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine Investigational Medicines Mirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine ... ### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and ... ENROLLING AGE 18 - 80 years Conditions Moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease Investigational Medicines Mirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 Investigational Medicines Mirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9880, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 3275}, "timing": {"search_ms": 500.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 35151.8, "total_ms": 35652.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 35151.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "LY4395089 is an oral farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist in Phase 2 for adults with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease. NCT07483099 is a randomized, open-label, active-controlled study comparing LY4395089 co-administered with mirikizumab against mirikizumab alone; the study lasts approximately 62 weeks. Its listed measures include endoscopic response at week 12, clinical remission, and change from baseline in fecal calprotectin through week 12. A Phase 1 study in healthy participants is complete. [E2][E4] Mirikizumab is an IL-23 p19-targeting monoclonal antibody and is the active comparator in NCT07483099. [E4][E5] The Crohn’s disease competitive-landscape listing also names Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, and SAR442970 under Sanofi; Tirzepatide and MORF-057 under Eli Lilly and Company; Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, and ABBV-8736 under AbbVie; Vedolizumab, Zasocitinib, and Darvadstrocel under Takeda; Ustekinumab, Guselkumab, Golimumab, JNJ-78934804, and Icotrokinra under Johnson & Johnson; Tulisokibart under Merck; AZD7798 under AstraZeneca; DISC-0974 under Disc Medicine; SPY001, SPY002, and SPY003 under Spyre Therapeutics; Obefazimod under Abivax; and PALI-2108 under Palisade Bio. [E2] Ontamalimab is also named for Crohn’s disease: it is a Phase 3 monoclonal antibody that inhibits MADCAM1. [E7]", "confident_score": 0.68, "missing": ["Other agents explicitly identified as FXR agonists for Crohn’s disease, or confirmation of which listed agents share LY4395089’s mechanistic class.", "The individual mechanisms, Crohn’s disease stages, and reported results for the landscape-listed agents where these are not stated; LY4395089’s reported trial results; its dose and dosing schedule; and its regulatory designations."], "citations": ["E2", "E4", "E5", "E7"], "next_queries": ["LY4395089 Crohn FXR agonist competing agents", "LY4395089 Crohn trial NCT07483099 results", "LY4395089 Crohn dose schedule NCT07483099", "LY4395089 Crohn regulatory designations", "LY4395089 Crohn landscape agent mechanisms development stages"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 1355, "confident_score": 0.68, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 6, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 4, "cited_domains": ["biopharmawatch.com", "clinicaltrials.gov", "withpower.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 61, "ungrounded_tokens": ["p19-targeting", "The Crohn"], "n_ungrounded": 2, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q013", "objective": "SD-133 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["SD-133"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 5427, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17474124.2024.2322648", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "title": "Full article: Next-generation therapies for pancreatic cancer", "date": "2024-03-03", "text": "For patients with an ECOG PS of 2, GnP is considered in selected patients, and gemcitabine monotherapy is a preferred option for patients with a less favorable performance status [Citation15,Citation16]. ... There continues to be significant interest in adoptive cellular therapy with chimeric antigen receptor T cells (CAT-T) and T cell receptor therapy (TCR-T) in PDAC. Both approaches aim to generate cellular and humoral immunity against selected tumor-associated proteins such as *KRAS* mutant alleles (G12D, G12V), CD133, CD137, EGFR, or mesothelin [Citation148]. A phase I trial of anti-CD133, a cancer stem cell marker highly expressed in PDAC, CAR-T included seven patients with mPDAC and showed that three individuals achieved SD, 2 PR, and 2 patients with POD [Citation149]."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://openurl.ebsco.com/contentitem/doi:10.7150/thno.56737?id=ebsco:doi:10.7150/thno.56737", "domain": "openurl.ebsco.com", "title": "Detail - OpenURL Connection", "date": "2025-05-30", "text": "We were unable to find your item with the information provided. \"Access through your institution\" to search for it on your institution's EBSCOhost experience."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8570383/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Modeling pancreatic cancer in mice for experimental therapeutics", "date": "2021-05-01", "text": "However, the treatment with gemcitabine (100 mg/kg) as adjuvant chemotherapy following the surgery significantly reduce the local recurrence and prolonged the survival of the treated mice [223]."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/12/2/481", "domain": "mdpi.com", "title": "Radioimmunotherapy of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma: A Review of the Current Status of Literature", "date": "2020-02-19", "text": "Clinical studies were limited to investigating β radioimmunotherapy only. Phase I and II trials observed disease control rates of 11.2%–57.9%, with synergistic effects noted for combination therapies."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7031151/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: Treatment hurdles, tumor ...", "date": "2020-02-15", "text": "In addition to mesothelin, other cancer-associated antigens are being tested in ongoing clinical trials as potential targets of CAR-T-based therapeutic regimes (anti-CEA, anti-CD-133, anti-ROR1, anti-WT1) alone or in combination with chemotherapy[60]; and (5) Immune modulating agents targeting the dense pancreatic microenvironment could also exert substantial antitumor activity."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5974576/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Immune therapies in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: Where are we now?", "date": "2018-05-28", "text": "Therefore, the subset of PDAC patients eligible for CPI monotherapy is small. ... Overall, except for MSI-H tumors, PDAC are considered to be resistant to single-agent immune therapy."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8355264/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma in the Era of Precision ...", "date": "2021-02-11", "text": "Devimistat (CPI-613), a novel mitochondrial inhibitor combined with mFOLFIRINOX showed encouraging results in a phase 1 study with newly diagnosed mPDAC. Responses were observed in 11 (61%) out of 18 patients, and included three cases with CR and eight with PR [110]."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.oatext.com/management-of-pancreatic-ductal-adenocarcinoma-pdac-progress-in-the-past-decade-and-challenges-for-the-future.php", "domain": "oatext.com", "title": "Management of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) - OAText", "date": null, "text": "*The Lancet* 389: 1011-1024."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3926968/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pathogenesis of Pancreatic Cancer: Lessons from Animal ...", "date": "2013-10-31", "text": "Confirming that activated KRAS can induce PDAC in adulthood, *iKras* mice develop PanINs when doxycycline administration is delayed until 4–6 weeks of age (Collins *et al. ... More importantly, if doxycycline is subsequently withdrawn to downregulate *Kras^G12D^*, these PanINs disappear: early-stage PanINs appear to redifferentiate into normal exocrine pancreas tissue, while more advanced PanINs undergo apoptosis. And most important, when PDAC is induced by *Tp53* deletion in *iKras* mice, doxycycline withdrawal causes regression of both local and metastatic cancer (Collins *et al. * 2012; Ying *et al.* 2012). ... Indeed, several such agents have proven effective at delaying PanIN progression and PDAC initiation in the hamster and *Kras^LSL^* mouse models, including cyclooxygenase inhibitors (Daniluk *et al.* 2012; Funahashi *et al.* 2007; Takahashi *et al.* 1990), dexamethasone (Rhim *et al.* 2012), and the diabetes drug metformin (Schneider *et al.* 2001), the last of which exhibits anti-inflammatory effects due to inhibition of NFκB (Isoda *et al. ... Inhibiting the Hedgehog signaling pathway in *Kras^LSL^* mice caused stromal regression and enhanced gemcitabine killing of tumor cells, thus extending survival (Olive *et al.* 2009)."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8355264/pdf/nihms-1668451.pdf", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma in the Era of Precision Medicine", "date": null, "text": "radiation plus TLR9 Agonist SD-101"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7531, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 246}, "timing": {"search_ms": 529.3, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 4000.0, "total_ms": 4529.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 4000.0, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "SD-133-specific in vivo single-agent monotherapy data in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models, including the model used and tumour growth inhibition, regression or shrinkage outcomes.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["SD-133 pancreatic cancer animal model monotherapy", "SD-133 PDAC tumour growth inhibition", "SD-133 pancreatic tumour regression mouse", "SD-133 pancreatic cancer xenograft efficacy"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q009", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15444, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8315189/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Antispasmodics for Chronic Abdominal Pain: Analysis of North ...", "date": "2021-05-17", "text": "Anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents inhibit GI smooth muscle contraction, in part, by blocking calcium transport through calcium channels (31); furthermore, these agents decrease colonic motility (32). ... ##### Dicyclomine. In 2 randomized, placebo-controlled studies, dicyclomine improved symptoms of IBS relative to placebo (Table 2) (18,43–49). One study reported no difference in adverse event (AE) rates with dicyclomine vs placebo (43), whereas the other reported that AEs occurred in a greater percentage of patients (69%) receiving dicyclomine 160 mg/d continuously for 2 weeks vs patients receiving placebo (16%; Table 2) (18). ... Hyoscine, also known as scopolamine, is an anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agent and smooth muscle relaxant (20). In 3 studies, hyoscine taken for a duration of 4 weeks to 3 months was more efficacious than placebo at improving IBS symptoms (Table 2) (44–46). ... Dicyclomine, hyoscine, and hyoscyamine are anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents available in the United States. Although placebo-controlled efficacy and safety data related to the use of these antispasmodics in patients with IBS seem favorable, the studies of dicyclomine (18,43) and hyoscine (44–47) identified in this review were published in 1990 or earlier and used different doses, treatment durations, and outcome assessments. ... In summary, data supporting the use of antispasmodics for the treatment of chronic abdominal pain in patients with DGBI, including IBS and FD, are limited. Limited sample size, short duration of therapy, heterogeneity in outcomes, and concerns over potential bias with study design make it difficult to recommend these agents for clinical use, especially when compared with the data sets available from large, randomized, controlled trials that characterize the current US FDA-approved IBS medications."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/crohnscolitis360/article/8/2/otag063/8711826", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "efficacy and safety of hyoscyamine, dicyclomine, and desipramine in ...", "date": "2026-04-22", "text": "In summary, antispasmodic agents and low-dose neuromodulators appear to be effective and generally well tolerated for the management of high bowel frequency and abdominal pain in patients with irritable pouch syndrome."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=a330a08e-0cb6-bc66-e053-2995a90ad9e9&type=display", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Dicyclomine 20mg", "date": null, "text": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride tablets, USP are indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome. ... The recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day. After one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation. If efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued. ... The peripheral effects of dicyclomine hydrochloride are a consequence of their inhibitory effect on muscarinic receptors of the autonomic nervous system. ... The pattern of adverse effects seen with dicyclomine is mostly related to its pharmacological actions at muscarinic receptors [see Clinical Pharmacology (12)] . They are a consequence of the inhibitory effect on muscarinic receptors within the autonomic nervous system. These effects are dose-related and are usually reversible when treatment is discontinued. ... The data described below reflect exposure in controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times a day). ... Dicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent available as: ... Dicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract. Animal studies indicate that this action is achieved via a dual mechanism: - a specific anticholinergic effect (antimuscarinic) at the acetylcholine-receptor sites with approximately 1/8 the milligram potency of atropine ( in vitro, guinea pig ileum); and - a direct effect upon smooth muscle (musculotropic) as evidenced by dicyclomine’s antagonism of bradykinin- and histamine-induced spasms of the isolated guinea pig ileum. ... In controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05)."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3437534/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Pain management in patients with inflammatory bowel disease", "date": null, "text": "As pain from inflammation or partial obstruction usually manifests with intestinal spasms, anticholinergic antispasmodic medications are frequently employed as analgesic agents. ... Of the medications studied by their group, hyoscine (hyoscyamine) was found to be the most efficacious and is the only one available in US markets. Practically speaking, the antispasmodics used most frequently are hyoscyamine and dicyclomine. Unfortunately, though antispasmodics seem to be a reasonable choice for pain management associated with inflammation or partial obstruction, there is no definitive evidence supporting their usage in IBD [Makharia, 2011]. ... Unfortunately, COX-2 inhibitors have not been shown to be beneficial in terms of safety, toxicity, or even anti-inflammatory effect. ... Regardless of the criticisms of COX-2 inhibitors, it is important to note there are no trials specifically investigating the utility of these medications for abdominal pain associated with IBD. ... In addition to standard IBD-specific treatments, medical therapy for pain arising from inflammation or strictures/adhesions could include antispasmodics [i.e. hyoscyamine (hyoscine), dicyclomine (dicycloverine)] as needed."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12116234/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "The Impact of Antispasmodic Use on Abdominal Pain and ...", "date": "2025-04-11", "text": "Patients with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are often prescribed antispasmodics for chronic abdominal pain. Large-scale data regarding efficacy and impact on clinical outcomes are lacking. ... We included 85,859 patients (median age 50; 53.8% female) with IBD; 5,661 used antispasmodics. On follow up, those with antispasmodic use had higher rates of abdominal pain and opioid use (P < 0.001) regardless of baseline abdominal pain or opioid use. ... Patients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of abdominal pain at 1 month, regardless of baseline abdominal pain. Opioid-naïve patients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of opioid use at follow up (1.1% *vs*. 0.2%; P < 0.001). ... Antispasmodic use in patients with IBD was associated with increased abdominal pain and opioid use in opioid-naïve patients. Antispasmodic use was associated with increased likelihood of corticosteroid use, clinic and ER visits and hospitalization. ... Antispasmodic medications, like dicyclomine, have been used to good effect in irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)^6, 7^. ... As such, these medications are recommended by the American Gastroenterological Association for patients with IBD and IBS or functional symptoms^11^. ... We identified patients receiving an antispasmodic prescription (dicyclomine or hyoscyamine) with at least 30-day supply based on RxNorm codes (Supplementary Table1). ... Antispasmodic use was not associated with improvement in abdominal pain or opioid use in patients with IBD. In fact, the converse was observed even after controlling for metrics of IBD severity and baseline characteristics including opioid use and abdominal pain."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/set-ids/40e21575-320f-2ac6-e054-00144ff88e88/spl-doc?hl", "domain": "nctr-crs.fda.gov", "title": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/ ...", "date": null, "text": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome. Dicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome ( ... The peripheral effects of dicyclomine hydrochloride are a consequence of their inhibitory effect on muscarinic receptors of the autonomic nervous system. ... Caution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis. Large doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon ... Dicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent available in the following dosage forms: ... Dicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract. Animal studies indicate that this action is achieved via a dual mechanism: a specific anticholinergic effect (antimuscarinic) at the acetylcholine-receptor sites with approximately 1/8 the milligram potency of atropine ("}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.ibdrelief.com/learn/treatment/medication-for-ibd/antispasmodics-and-ibd", "domain": "ibdrelief.com", "title": "Antispasmodics and IBD", "date": null, "text": "Antispasmodics suppress muscle spasms, helping to reduce stomach pain, cramping and bloating. They are sometimes used in IBD to relieve symptoms. ... Antispasmodic medicines are a type of symptomatic medicine, which suppress muscle spasms. They are used to help ease symptoms such as functional abdominal pain, cramping and bloating you may have as a result of a wide range of conditions, including inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). They can also help to reduce the movement (motility) of your gut. ... People who have IBD, such as Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis, may be advised to take antispasmodic medicines to help to ease pain, discomfort and symptoms whilst other medicines take effect, or if they have symptoms that are not responding to other IBD medicines. ... There are two main types of antispasmodics, antimuscarinics (also called anticholinergics) and smooth muscle relaxants. ... As mentioned earlier, your body sends chemical messages to your gut, telling the muscles to contract. Antimuscarinics block the action of the chemical messenger (neurotransmitter) acetylcholine, reducing the strength of muscle contractions, spasms and cramps. This also reduces gut motility. Examples include: - Dicyclomine (Bentyl) - Hyoscyamine - Hyoscine butylbromide (Buscopan) - Atropine - Propantheline ... Smooth muscle relaxants work directly on the smooth muscle wall of the gut, helping it to relax and relieving pain associated with contractions, cramps and spasms. Examples include: - Mebeverine (Colofac, Duspamen and Duspatalin) - Alverine citrate (Spasmonal) ... Smooth muscle relaxants tend to have fewer side effects so will often be suggested first. Some people seem to respond better to one type of antispasmodic than to another, so if one type does not work for you, it may be worth trying another. ... It is generally recommended that you only take antispasmodics when necessary, stopping if the symptoms settle down. ... Generally smooth muscle relaxant antispasmodics will have fewer, milder side effects than antimuscarinics. ... This will be listed in the patient information leaflets, and includes: - If you have, or might have a bowel obstruction - If you already have gut motility issues - If you have myasthenia gravis, a condition that causes muscle weakness"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "title": "Pharmacology", "date": "2025-04-30", "text": "- Clinical Trials - Phase 0 - 0 - Phase 1 - 0 - Phase 2 - 0 - Phase 3 - 0 - Phase 4 - 0 ... - Mechanism of Action - Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1Antagonist - Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1 ... **Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS. ... Dicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome. ... Dicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome. ... Dicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines. It's duration of action is not especially long as it is usually taken 4 times daily with individual doses of 20-40mg orally or 10-20mg by intramuscular injection. ... Dicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine. Dicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.droracle.ai/articles/856897/what-is-the-most-appropriate-overthecounter-treatment-for-simultaneous", "domain": "droracle.ai", "title": "www.droracle.ai · articles · 856897What is the most appropriate over‑the‑counter treatment for ...", "date": "2026-02-16", "text": "**For simultaneous abdominal cramps and diarrhea, loperamide is the single most effective over-the-counter medication, addressing diarrhea control while providing modest pain relief, though antispasmodics like dicyclomine can be added if cramping persists.** 1 ... If cramping remains bothersome despite loperamide, **add an antispasmodic such as dicyclomine or hyoscine butylbromide (if available in your region).** ### Antispasmodic Evidence ... - Dicyclomine and hyoscine butylbromide are antimuscarinics that reduce intestinal motility and spasm. 1 ... **Stop loperamide immediately and seek medical attention if:** - Diarrhea worsens or is accompanied by fever, severe abdominal pain, or bloody stools—these suggest infection or inflammatory bowel disease requiring antibiotics or other treatment. ... - You have known inflammatory bowel disease during an active flare, as loperamide can precipitate toxic megacolon. 8, 9 ... - **Do not use loperamide chronically without investigating the underlying cause** of diarrhea—conditions like IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, or malabsorption require specific treatment. 8 - **Do not exceed 8 mg daily of OTC loperamide** without medical supervision, as higher doses require monitoring for cardiac effects."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://api.upums.ac.in/pdfs/doc-1712402525891-163237152.pdf", "domain": "api.upums.ac.in", "title": "[PDF] Phase II-(VI) Antispasmodic & antiulcer drugs (cyclopentolate ...", "date": null, "text": "Antispasmodics can be classified in three major subclasses: 1. Anticholinergic / antimuscarinic agents: Examples are • Dicyclomine Hydrochloride • Hyoscyamine Sulfate • Cimetropium Bromide • Otilanium Bromide • Octylonium Bromide • Prifinium Bromide • Darifenacin ... In the United States the antispasmodics dicyclomimne and hyoscyamine are available for inflammatory bowel disease (IBS). cymetropium bromide is a muscarinic compound with strong antispasmodic activity. ... Propantheline bromide (PRO-BANTHINE) is a widely used synthetic nonselective muscarinic receptor antagonist."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10380, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 4128}, "timing": {"search_ms": 534.0, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 41780.0, "total_ms": 42314.0}, "luna_wall_ms": 41780.0, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agent used for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome; its stated actions include antagonism at muscarinic M1, M2 and M3 receptors and a direct effect on smooth muscle. Its label recommends 20 mg four times daily initially, with an increase to 40 mg four times daily after one week unless side effects limit escalation; it should be discontinued if efficacy is not achieved within two weeks or side effects require a dose below 80 mg per day. The DrugBank record lists 0 trials in each of Phase 0, Phase 1, Phase 2, Phase 3 and Phase 4. [E3][E8] In more than 100 patients, 82% receiving dicyclomine at 160 mg daily had a favorable clinical response versus 55% receiving placebo (p<0.05). Two randomized, placebo-controlled IBS studies found symptom improvement; one reported no adverse-event-rate difference, while the other reported adverse events in 69% receiving 160 mg/day continuously for two weeks versus 16% receiving placebo. [E1][E3] For IBD-related pain, dicyclomine (also called dicycloverine) is among the antispasmodics used; a review states there is no definitive evidence supporting antispasmodic use in IBD. [E4] In an IBD cohort of 85,859 patients (median age 50; 53.8% female), 5,661 used antispasmodics; use was associated with higher rates of abdominal pain and opioid use on follow-up, and antispasmodic use was not associated with improvement in abdominal pain or opioid use. Among opioid-naïve patients, opioid use at follow-up was 1.1% versus 0.2% (P<0.001); antispasmodic use was also associated with increased corticosteroid use, clinic and ER visits, and hospitalization. The prescriptions studied were dicyclomine or hyoscyamine. [E5] Named antimuscarinic/anticholinergic agents in the IBD antispasmodic context include hyoscyamine, hyoscine (also called scopolamine in one source), hyoscine butylbromide, atropine, propantheline, cimetropium bromide (also printed as cymetropium bromide), otilanium bromide, octylonium bromide, prifinium bromide and darifenacin. Hyoscine is described as an anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agent and smooth-muscle relaxant; hyoscine and hyoscyamine are mentioned among antispasmodics used for IBD-related pain, and hyoscyamine is included in the IBD prescription cohort. Propantheline is described as a nonselective muscarinic receptor antagonist, and cimetropium as a muscarinic compound with strong antispasmodic activity. [E1][E4][E5][E7][E10]", "confident_score": 0.68, "missing": "Development stage and trial details (registry identifier, enrolment, eligibility, endpoints and results) for the named agents hyoscyamine, hyoscine/hyoscine butylbromide, atropine, propantheline, cimetropium/cymetropium bromide, otilanium bromide, octylonium bromide, prifinium bromide and darifenacin; trial registry identifiers, enrolment and eligibility for dicyclomine; dicyclomine’s regulatory designations; IBD-specific efficacy results for dicyclomine; and direct comparative results between dicyclomine and the named agents.", "citations": ["E1", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E7", "E8", "E10"], "next_queries": ["Dicyclomine IBD trial registry efficacy", "Dicyclomine clinical trial registry enrolment eligibility", "Dicyclomine regulatory designations FDA", "Dicyclomine IBD antimuscarinic comparative study", "Dicyclomine development phase clinical trials"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 2477, "confident_score": 0.68, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 11, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 7, "cited_domains": ["api.upums.ac.in", "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "go.drugbank.com", "ibdrelief.com", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 25, "ungrounded_tokens": ["Named"], "n_ungrounded": 1, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 17904, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "date": "2026-05-28", "text": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity. In part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo. No TEAEs were observed in part 2. In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo. Only 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug. Importantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated. ... In this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3. Notably, some ADA positivity was present at baseline, and most treatment-emergent ADA titers were low. ... In summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity. ... To date, no major safety concerns have emerged; however, immunogenicity and the long-term consequences of dual-pathway immunosuppression will require careful evaluation in ongoing and future studies (11)."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "date": "2025-03-31", "text": "TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1). Most were mild-to-moderate in severity. One AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity). One serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm. ... **Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated. Efficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to the QD and placebo arms, despite the relatively short duration of treatment. ... TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1). Most were mild-to-moderate in severity. One AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity). One serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "date": null, "text": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table 1), 17 patients completed the study Per Protocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6). Reasons for discontinuation: worsening of UC (n=2), study schedule non-compliance (n=2) and consent withdrawal (n=1). ... No clinically significant changes in safety laboratory parameters, dose-limiting toxicities, or events suggestive of pronounced immunosuppression • All but one TEAE were mild to moderate • 1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening UC requiring hospitalization was not considered related to treatment. This TEAE occurred during follow-up in a SOR102 BID patient ... SOR102 was safe and well tolerated. ... SOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-2024-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "title": "PowerPoint-Präsentation", "date": null, "text": "Objectives of Parts 1 and 2: • To evaluate the safety and tolerability of SAD (Part 1) and MD (Part 2) of SOR102 ... SOR102-101 (NCT06080048) is a Phase 1, first in human study that consists of 3 parts. Parts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (HS) age 18-55 years and evaluated single ascending (SAD) and multiple doses (MD) of SOR102, respectively. ... SOR102 is Safe and Well-Tolerated No safety or tolerability concerns noted at single doses up to 3,645mg or multiple doses of 1,215mg BID -No serious adverse events -No clinically significant laboratory, ECG, vitals, or physical exam findings -No subjects discontinued Part 1: One TEAE considered related to study drug (Cohort #1) Part 2: No adverse events Cohort / Dose Event Duration Severity Causality C1 / 135mg Diarrhea ~17 hours Mild Probably related C1 / 135mg Dysmenorrhea ~2.5 days Moderate Not related C1 / 135mg Flatulence ~12 hours Mild Not related C2 / Placebo Headache ~4 hours Mild Not related C2 / Placebo Catheter site pain ~5 hours Moderate Not related C2 / 405mg Dysmenorrhea ~12 hours Moderate Not related TEAE: treatment-emergent adverse event ... Systemic Exposure Of SOR102 And Its Monomers Was Not Observed; Low-Level ADA Signal in 3 of 40 Samples Tested ... No detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum in Parts 1 and 2 ... SOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses. • Systemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity. ... Low levels of intact SOR102 and high levels of active SOR102 monomers in faeces confirm that SOR102 is efficiently cleaved and maintains activity after oral dosing, as designed. ... Low-level ADA positive signal observed in 2 subjects"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg", "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "date": null, "text": "Login to view more data ... Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor of TNF and interleukin-23 in healthy participants and patients with ulcerative colitis: a first-in-human, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 1 trial ... In part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group; mild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache). Only the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related. No TEAEs occurred in part 2. In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo. Two patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation. All other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature. No clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred. ... The favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "date": null, "text": "A novel oral bispecific antibody (SOR102) targeting TNF and IL-23 shows a favourable safety profile with encouraging early efficacy in ulcerative colitis. ... Early disease patients also experienced significantly fewer serious adverse events. ... SOR102 is an oral bispecific antibody targeting TNF and IL-23p19 developed for ulcer ative colitis. This phase 1, double blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluated its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in healthy participants and patients with active ulcerative colitis. Across single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals. In patients with ulcerative colitis treated for 42 days, clinical responses were more frequent with SOR102 than placebo, particularly with twice-daily dosing. Symptomatic remission was achieved in over half of patients receiving twice-daily SOR102, while no placebo-treated patients reached remission. Although one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related. Overall, the favourable safety profile and early signs of clinical bene fit support further clinical development of SOR102 as a potential oral therapy for ulcerative colitis."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/173141909/Safety-and-pharmacokinetics-of-sor102-an-oral-bispecific-inhibitor-of-tnf-and-interleukin-23-in-healthy-participants-an.pdf", "domain": "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific ...", "date": null, "text": "In part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose ... Only the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related. ... In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo. Two patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation. All other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature. No clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred. ... In part 1, any treatment emergent adverse event (TEAE) occurred in three (13%) of 24 healthy participants who received single doses of SOR102 compared with two (25%) of eight patients who received placebo. ... No treatment emergent serious adverse event occurred during parts 1 or 2. In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo (table 3). Two (13%) of 16 patients treated with SOR102 (both in the twice-daily SOR102 group) had TEAEs of worsening ulcerative colitis that led to study discontinuation. All (77%) but one TEAE in patients with at least one TEAE were mild to moderate. One mild TEAE of upper abdominal pain in a patient who received twice-daily SOR102 was considered possibly related to treatment. This patient also had a treatment emergent serious adverse event of worsening ulcerative colitis during the follow-up period that required hospital­ isation, and which led to study withdrawal. This serious adverse event (also considered a severe TEAE) was considered unrelated to study drug."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT06080048", "domain": "ichgcp.net", "title": "A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy Participants and Patients With Ulcerative Colitis", "date": "2024-12-03", "text": "# A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy Participants and Patients With Ulcerative Colitis ... ## A Ph I Randomized Double-Blind First-in-Human Single Ascending Dose & Multiple Dose Study to Investigate the Safety Tolerability and Pharmacokinetics of SOR102 in Healthy Adult Participants and Patient Volunteers With Mild to Severe Ulcerative Colitis SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3). ... #### Status Completed ... #### Enrollment (Actual) 22 #### Phase - Phase 1 ... Key Exclusion Criteria: - Any diagnosis of IBD except for UC. - History of fistula(e), strictures or surgery, known intestinal obstruction, or diagnosis of toxic megacolon. - Concurrent use of any biologic drug. - Prior primary efficacy failure or secondary loss of response to more than one biologic or new small molecule therapy (i.e., JAK inhibitors or S1P receptor modulators) indicated for the treatment of UC. ... |Treatment Emergent Adverse Events following oral dosing Time Frame: Up to 6 weeks in Part 3 or at the time of study discontinuation|After multiple oral doses administered BID or QD, number of subjects with AEs in the SOR102 dose level cohorts compared with the oral placebo group|Up to 6 weeks in Part 3 or at the time of study discontinuation| ... |Incidence of positive ADAs to SOR102 Time Frame: Following 6 weeks of study treatment in Part 3 or at the time of study discontinuation|To assess the incidence of positive ADAs to SOR102.|Following 6 weeks of study treatment in Part 3 or at the time of study discontinuation| ... November 8, 2024 ... #### Other Study ID Numbers - SOR102-101"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "date": "2025-01-17", "text": "In part 3, seven patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 810 mg once-daily, nine to receive the same dose twice-daily, and six to receive placebo. In part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group; mild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache). Only the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related. No TEAEs occurred in part 2. In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo. Two patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation. All other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature. No clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred. ... The favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis. ... • Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients. No unexpected adverse events were observed. ... In Sorriso’s Phase 1b study, six patients received placebo, seven received 810 mg of SOR102 once per day, and nine received 810 mg twice per day."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/DDW-2025-poster.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "title": "PowerPoint Presentation", "date": null, "text": "Data are n (%), mean (SD), or median (min, max); FCAL=fecal calprotectin; CRP=C-reactive protein * One patient received adalimumab, and one patient received mirikizumab, both in clinical trials ... The Phase 1, first-in-human study (SOR102-101; NCT06080048) had 3 parts. Parts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (results presented previously). Here, we present safety and efficacy results for Part 3, a Phase 1b randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in patients with mild to severe UC. ... SOR102 was safe and well tolerated. ... In most samples tested, there were no detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum ... ADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response. ... Efficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to QD and placebo, despite the short treatment duration. ... Patients with a Mayo endoscopy score (ES) of ≥2, a rectal bleeding score (RBS) of ≥1 and stool frequency score (SFS) of ≥1 were randomized 1:1:1 to receive SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo for 6 weeks. ... Most ADA titers were low (<1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response ... Table 2. Treatment Emergent Adverse Events Placebo (N=6) SOR102 810mg QD (N=7) SOR102 810mg BID (N=9) Total (N=22) Total Number of TEAEs 5 2 6 13 Number of patients with ≥1 TEAE (50) 2 (29) 5 (56) 10 (46) ... 12 (55) 1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization 2. Abdominal pain of mild severity ... Treatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 2). Most were mild-to- moderate in severity. One AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity). One unrelated serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10523, "cached_input_tokens": 10520, "output_tokens": 4486}, "timing": {"search_ms": 1344.9, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 46651.1, "total_ms": 47996.0}, "luna_wall_ms": 46651.1, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "In SOR102-101 (NCT06080048), Parts 1–2: among participants receiving SOR102, TEAEs occurred in 3/24 (13%) in Part 1 versus 2/8 (25%) with placebo; the SOR102 events included mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea in one participant, mild flatulence in another, and moderate dysmenorrhoea in a participant receiving 405 mg. Only mild diarrhoea was considered treatment-related. Placebo events were headache and catheter-site pain; the catheter-site pain is listed as moderate in Sorriso’s presentation but mild in another summary. No TEAEs occurred in Part 2, and no serious TEAEs occurred in Parts 1–2. No safety or tolerability concerns were noted at single doses up to 3,645 mg or multiple doses up to 1,215 mg twice daily. [E4][E5][E7] In Part 3, 7/16 (44%) SOR102-treated patients and 3/6 (50%) placebo-treated patients had at least one TEAE. The DDW presentation gives arm-specific rates of 57% with 810 mg twice daily, 29% with 810 mg once daily, and 50% with placebo; its table gives 56% for the twice-daily arm. It lists 13 TEAEs in total: 6 with twice-daily SOR102, 2 with once-daily SOR102, and 5 with placebo. Most TEAEs were mild to moderate. Two patients in the twice-daily group had worsening ulcerative colitis leading to discontinuation; one event was severe and serious, required hospitalization during follow-up, and was considered unrelated to SOR102. One mild upper abdominal pain event in a twice-daily recipient was considered possibly treatment-related. [E1][E2][E7][E10] Across the study, no clinically meaningful effects on ECGs, vital signs, physical examinations or safety laboratory parameters were observed, and no deaths occurred. ADAs were detected in 2/8 (25%) in Part 2 and 6/16 (38%) in Part 3; some positivity was present at baseline and most treatment-emergent titers were low. Sorriso’s presentation separately reports a low-level ADA signal in 3/40 samples and low-level positivity in 2 subjects; the DDW poster says most titers were below 1:240 and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response. [E1][E4][E5][E7][E10]<br>**DDW 2025** The poster reports the Part 3 arm-specific TEAE rates, severity, possibly related abdominal pain, and unrelated serious UC worsening requiring hospitalization as above. [E2][E10]<br>**UEG Week 2025** Of 22 randomized patients, 17 completed per protocol; discontinuations included worsening UC (2), study-schedule non-compliance (2), and consent withdrawal (1). The poster notes no clinically significant safety-laboratory changes, dose-limiting toxicities, or events suggestive of pronounced immunosuppression. [E3]", "confident_score": 0.82, "missing": "SOR102 safety and adverse-event findings in Crohn’s disease; longer-term safety outcomes; aggregate Grade ≥3 event rates beyond the reported severe UC worsening, incidence of adverse events of special interest, and a complete Part 3 event-by-event breakdown.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E7", "E10"], "next_queries": ["SOR102 Crohn's disease safety adverse events", "SOR102 long-term safety follow-up", "SOR102 Part 3 Grade 3 adverse events special interest"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 2603, "confident_score": 0.82, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 15, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 7, "cited_domains": ["eposters.ddw.org", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "sorrisopharma.com", "synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 35, "ungrounded_tokens": ["SOR102-treated", "ECGs"], "n_ungrounded": 2, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 3, "next_queries_subject_first": 3}}
{"row_id": "q008", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15495, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07285213", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody", "date": null, "text": "The purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in Individuals 18 years of age and above. ... AZD5148 Participants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push. ... - **Allocation** RANDOMIZED - **Purpose** PREVENTION - **Masking** QUADRUPLE - **Model** PARALLEL ... ## Timeline & Regulatory - **Start** 2025-12-10 - **Primary Completion** 2027-04-20 - **Completion** 2028-01-18 - **FDA Drug** Yes ## Countries - United States ... - United Kingdom ... NCT03824795 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE2 ... - LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence NCT05330182 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2/PHASE3 - Clostridium Difficile Vaccine 2-Dose Versus 3-Dose Study ... - Study of the Clinical Effectiveness of a Human Monoclonal Antibody to C."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05330182", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "title": "LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence", "date": "2022-04-08", "text": "## LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection RecurrenceClinicalTrials.gov ID ... Drug: LMN-201 Drug: Placebo - CDI02 - CDMRP-PR221885 ( Other Grant/Funding Number ) (OTHER_GRANT: CDMRP) - CDMRP-PR221885 ( Other Grant/Funding Number ) (OTHER_GRANT: CDMRP) ... Inclusion Criteria: - Male or female, aged 18 or older. - Diagnosis of CDI defined as a new or recent history of 3 or more bowel movements per day with a loose or watery consistency (Bristol Stool Scale 5, 6, or 7); a positive stool C. difficile toxin B immunoassay (stool collected no more than 7 days before first dose of LMN-201/placebo), and no other likely explanation for diarrhea. NOTE: Diarrhea is not required to be present on the day of enrollment. - Provision of signed and dated informed consent form. - Scheduled to receive or planning to receive a ≤28-day course of SOC antibiotic therapy for CDI. Participant must have been diagnosed with CDI for 7 or fewer days at time of initial study drug administration. SOC antibiotic therapy is defined as the receipt of oral fidaxomicin or oral metronidazole or oral vancomycin (see Section 8.2.6) - May be on systemic antibiotics for an infection unrelated to the gastrointestinal tract. - Ability to take oral medication and willingness to adhere to the study medication regimen. - Stated willingness and ability to comply with all study procedures and availability for the duration of the study and investigator believes individual will complete the study. - Access to a mobile smartphone. ... Exclusion Criteria: - Fulminant C. difficile colitis. - Admitted or expect to be admitted to an intensive care unit. - Underlying gastrointestinal disorder characterized by diarrhea including but not limited to chronic ulcerative colitis, Crohn's disease, celiac sprue, short bowel syndrome, dumping syndrome following gastrectomy, pancreatic insufficiency, enteric parasitic infection, viral enteritis, bacterial enteritis (salmonella, shigella, ETEC, etc.). - Neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count of < 1000 per microliter for any reason). Current or previous treatment in past 3 months with any therapy likely to influence the outcome of this study, including but not limited to the following: - Bezlotoxumab (Zinplava, Merck & Co.), or another antibody against C. difficile toxin(s) - C. difficile vaccine - SER-109 (Seres Therapeutics) - CP101 (Finch Therapeutics) - VE303 (Vedanta Therapeutics) - Fecal microbiota transplant - Current therapy with oral exchange resins - Protracted exposure to mu-agonist opioids and/or anticholinergic medication prescribed for diarrheal symptoms (unable to stop mu-agonist opioid treatment unless on a stable dose as of onset of diarrhea and no increase in dose planned for the duration of the study.) - Treatment with SOC antibiotic therapy is planned for longer than a 28-day period. - Pregnancy, anticipated pregnancy, or breastfeeding. - Inability or unwillingness to swallow numerous, relatively large capsules containing study drug ..."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/02/lmn-201-achieves-100-initial-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort-of-re-preve-trial", "domain": "lumen.bio", "title": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial", "date": null, "text": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial - Lumen Bioscience # LMN-201 Achieves 100% Initial C. difficile Clinical Cure in Preliminary Cohort of RePreve Trial April 2, 2025 Strong potential to disrupt market for treatment of C. diff infections. RePreve Trial main cohort now recruiting. **Seattle, WA – April 3, 2025** – In a landmark achievement for its pioneering spirulina-based drug development platform, Lumen announced today top-line results from the sentinel cohort of its RePreve Clinical Trial evaluating LMN-201 for Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI). LMN-201 is an oral biologic cocktail, delivered in capsules, intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C. difficile infection. ... **–100% Initial Clinical Cure:** 21 patients completed 7 days of LMN-201 plus standard of care (SoC) antibiotics (metronidazole, vancomycin, or fidaxomicin). All patients (21/21; 95% confidence interval (CI) 85%-100%) achieved initial clinical resolution, significantly outperforming (p<<0.001) the 80% initial clinical cure rate (625/781; 95% CI 77-83%) observed in the active arm of the large MODIFYI and II studies. The RePreve Trial was designed to facilitate comparisons to the MODIFY trial, which targeted the same patient population with the same SoC antibiotics combined with the IV-infused monoclonal antibody, bezlotoxumab. ... Sustained cure rates through Week 4 for SoC alone (MODIFY I/II), SoC plus LMN-201 (RePreve sentinel cohort) and SoC plus bezlotoxumab (MODIFY I/II) were 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56-63%), 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77-100%) and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66-72%), respectively (see enclosed figure). The improvement in sustained cure through Week 4 between LMN-201 and SoC was 35.7% (p<<0.001) and between LMN-201 and bezlotoxumab was 26.3% (p<0.001). **-Marked CDI Recurrence Reduction:** Only 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI 0.24-23%) completing seven days of SoC plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, versus 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI 23-30%) recurrence observed after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II and 87 of 625 (14%; 95% CI 11-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II in the same time period. ... **–LMN-201 Appears to be Safe and Well-Tolerated:** No dose-related severe or serious adverse events were reported. Notably, these sentinel cohort results were achieved with only 7 days of dosing. In the RePreve Trial’s main cohort (Part B), the LMN-201 dosing window will increase from 7 days to ~70 days (until 8 weeks after initial clinical cure). ... It suggests that LMN-201’s tandem mechanism – targeting both the C. diff bacterium and its toxin – can improve outcomes, as it did in preclinical studies.” ... We can now proceed to the placebo-controlled phase with a solid foundation of safety and an encouraging preliminary efficacy signal. We look forward to confirming these results in the larger trial, which is recruiting now.” ... The RePreve Trial has a two-part design. Enrollment ..."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT05330182", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence", "date": null, "text": "No results posted yet for this study ... This is a multisite study to evaluate the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of LMN-201 in participants recently diagnosed with CDI who are scheduled to receive or are receiving SOC antibiotic therapy against C. difficile. ... LMN-201 LMN-201 consists of orally delivered whole, dried, non-viable biomass of spirulina (Arthrospira platensis) grown from 4 separate strains, each of which has been engineered to express one of the following therapeutic proteins: * 3 toxin-binding proteins that bind and inhibit C. difficile toxin B (TcdB), an essential virulence factor for C. difficile * 1 lysozyme-like enzyme that selectively degrades the cell wall of C. difficile and causes rapid destruction of the organism ... - **Allocation** RANDOMIZED - **Purpose** PREVENTION - **Masking** QUADRUPLE - **Model** PARALLEL ## Eligibility - **Min Age** 18 Years - **Sex** ALL - **Healthy Volunteers** No ## Timeline & Regulatory - **Start** 2024-08-29 - **Primary Completion** 2026-08-31 - **Completion** 2026-12-01 - **FDA Drug** Yes ## Countries - United States"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/research/participate/clinical-trials/intervention/C213489?pn=1", "domain": "cancer.gov", "title": "Clinical Trials Using Clostridioides difficile Toxin B-binding proteins ...", "date": "2017-06-13", "text": "# Clinical Trials Using Clostridioides difficile Toxin B-binding proteins/Endolysin Combination LMN-201 Review the clinical trials studying clostridioides difficile toxin b-binding proteins/endolysin combination lmn-201 on this list and use the filters to refine the results by age and location. Some clinical trials are held at NCI-Designated Cancer Centers and may be supported by other organizations. ... Trials 1-1 of 1 - LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence **Status:**Active **Location:**M D Anderson Cancer Center, Houston, Texas"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://deltatrials.com/trial/NCT05330182", "domain": "deltatrials.com", "title": "LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile ... | NCT05330182 ...", "date": "2026-02-01", "text": "Recruiting PHASE2/PHASE3 INTERVENTIONAL 3-arm NCT05330182 # LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence A Phase 2, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study of LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence Sponsor: Congressionally Directed Medical Research Programs Conditions Clostridioides Difficile Infection Interventions LMN-201 Placebo Updated 18 times since 2022 Last updated: Feb 9, 2026 Started: Aug 29, 2024 Primary completion: Jun 1, 2027 Completion: Dec 1, 2027 ... A PHASE2/PHASE3 clinical study on Clostridioides Difficile Infection, this trial is actively recruiting participants. The trial is conducted by Congressionally Directed Medical Research Programs and has accumulated 18 data snapshots since 2024. ... 18 versions recorded 1. Feb 2026 — Present [monthly] Recruiting PHASE2/PHASE3 ... Sponsor contact: - Congressionally Directed Medical Research Programs - Lumen Bioscience, Inc."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4584063/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-2026-fda-updates", "domain": "openpr.com", "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline 2026: FDA Updates,", "date": "2026-07-22", "text": "Replidyne, and others, are developing therapies for the Clostridium Difficile Infections treatment• Emerging Clostridium Difficile Infections therapies in the different phases of clinical trials are- ADS024, DNV3837, Ibezapolstat, LMN-201, Ridinilazole, CP101, SER 109, VE303, MGB-BP-3, CRS3123, and others are expected to have a significant impact on the Clostridium Difficile Infections market in the coming years. • In April 2026, AstraZeneca announced results from a Phase IIb randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study assessing the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for preventing recurrence of Clostridioides difficile Infection in individuals aged 18 years and older.• In April 2026, Vedanta Biosciences announced results from a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III clinical trial evaluating VE303 for the prevention of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection. • In April 2026,Acurx Pharmaceuticals announced results from the Phase II IBZ-PATHFINDER study, an interventional, open-label, single-arm clinical trial evaluating oral Ibezapolstat for the treatment and reduction of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection in patients experiencing multiple recurrent infections.• In February 2026, Lumen Bioscience announced results from a Phase II randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial evaluating LMN-201 for the prevention of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection. ... The main goal of the study is to evaluate safety and tolerability, while secondary efficacy measures will assess mortality, symptom improvement, and recurrence rates.• In April 2025, Lumen Bioscience shared top-line findings from the sentinel cohort of its RePreve Clinical Trial assessing LMN-201 for Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI). LMN-201 is an oral biologic combination therapy, provided in capsule form, designed to be administered alongside and after antibiotics to enhance clinical outcomes in CDI."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=5b13e4fb-1990-4412-b2be-a5b774b0e0c2", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "LMN-201 / Lumen Bio - Larvol Delta", "date": "2024-09-19", "text": "/ Lumen Bio ... LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2/3 | N=375 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc. | Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting Enrollment open • Infectious Disease ... LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence ... - P2/3 | N=375 | Not yet recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc. | Trial completion date: Dec 2025 ➔ Apr 2026 | Trial primary completion date: Apr 2025 ➔ Dec 2025 ... Lumen Bioscience Receives Fast Track Designation from U.S. FDA for LMN-201 ... - \"Lumen Bioscience...announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted Fast Track Designation for LMN-201, an investigational, orally delivered biologic drug to treat and prevent C. difficile infection (CDI).\" ... LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2/3 | N=375 | Not yet recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc. | Trial completion date: Dec 2023 ➔ Dec 2025 | Initiation date: Sep 2022 ➔ Apr 2023 | Trial primary completion date: Sep 2023 ➔ Apr 2025 ... Exploratory Study to Assess Delivery of LMN-201 Components Via Enteric Capsules in the Gut of Individuals With Ostomies (clinicaltrials.gov) - P1 | N=12 | Completed | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc. | Recruiting ➔ Completed | Trial completion date: Jul 2022 ➔ Feb 2022 ... (clinicaltrials.gov) - P1; N=12; Recruiting; Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.; Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting ... - P1; N=12; Not yet recruiting; Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.mayo.edu/research/clinical-trials/diseases-conditions/c-difficile-infection", "domain": "mayo.edu", "title": "C. Difficile Infection Clinical Trials", "date": "2019-06-19", "text": "Difficile infections is able to predict the risk of recurrent infection. - LMN-201 For Prevention Of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence Rochester, MN This is a multisite study to evaluate the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of LMN-201 in participants recently diagnosed with CDI who are scheduled to receive or are receiving SOC antibiotic therapy against C. difficile."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/drug/b7926ed2b4ecf585/lmn-201", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "title": "LMN-201 - MedPath", "date": "2025-04-03", "text": "## Clinical Trials |Title|Posted|Study ID|Phase|Status|Sponsor| |--|--|--|--|--|--| |LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence|2022/04/15|NCT05330182|Phase 2|Recruiting|Lumen Bioscience, Inc.| ... |No FDA approvals found for this drug.|"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10639, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 4564}, "timing": {"search_ms": 532.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 48592.8, "total_ms": 49125.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 48592.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "LMN-201 is an oral biologic cocktail in capsules: non-viable dried biomass of four engineered spirulina strains expressing three proteins that bind and inhibit C. difficile toxin B (TcdB) and one lysozyme-like enzyme that selectively degrades the C. difficile cell wall. It is being studied for prevention of recurrence in NCT05330182, a Phase 2/3 trial; the record lists 375 participants and recruiting status, while another listing calls the study active, not recruiting. The later-dated listing gives a primary completion of June 1, 2027, and completion of December 1, 2027; another listing gives August 31, 2026, and December 1, 2026, respectively. Lumen Bioscience received Fast Track Designation for LMN-201. [E4][E6][E8][E1]\n\nThe trial listing specifies adults aged 18 or older with CDI, including a positive stool toxin B immunoassay collected no more than 7 days before the first dose, and planned or ongoing standard-of-care (SOC) antibiotic therapy for no more than 28 days; diagnosis must have occurred no more than 7 days before initial study-drug administration. SOC options named are oral fidaxomicin, metronidazole, or vancomycin. Exclusions include fulminant colitis, ICU admission or expected admission, specified diarrhea-associated gastrointestinal disorders, and neutropenia below 1,000 per microliter. Prior or current therapies listed as exclusions include bezlotoxumab, another antibody against C. difficile toxins, a C. difficile vaccine, SER-109, CP101, and VE303. [E2]\n\nIn the RePreve sentinel cohort, 21 patients completed 7 days of LMN-201 plus SOC antibiotics (metronidazole, vancomycin, or fidaxomicin); all 21 achieved initial clinical resolution (21/21; 95% CI 85%–100%), versus 80% (625/781; 95% CI 77%–83%) in the active arm of MODIFY I/II (p<<0.001). Week 4 sustained cure was 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77%–100%) for SOC plus LMN-201, 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56%–63%) for SOC alone, and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66%–72%) for SOC plus bezlotoxumab in MODIFY I/II; the reported improvements for LMN-201 versus SOC and versus bezlotoxumab were 35.7% (p<<0.001) and 26.3% (p<0.001), respectively. Within 28 days, recurrence occurred in 1/21 LMN-201 patients (4.76%; 95% CI 0.24%–23%), versus 161/621 (26%; 95% CI 23%–30%) after SOC alone and 87/625 (14%; 95% CI 11%–17%) after SOC plus bezlotoxumab in MODIFY I/II. No dose-related severe or serious adverse events were reported. The main cohort's LMN-201 dosing window was to increase from 7 days to approximately 70 days, until 8 weeks after initial clinical cure. [E3]\n\nNamed C. difficile infection pipeline agents include ADS024, DNV3837, ibezapolstat, ridinilazole, CP101, SER-109, VE303, MGB-BP-3, and CRS3123. Ibezapolstat is described in a Phase II interventional, open-label, single-arm study for treatment and reduction of recurrent infection in patients with multiple recurrences. VE303 is described in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III trial for prevention of recurrent infection. [E7] AZD5148 is a monoclonal antibody studied for prevention of recurrence in adults aged 18 or older; NCT07285213 is described as a randomized, quadruple-masked parallel study with a single dose administered by intramuscular injection or intravenous push, starting December 10, 2025, with primary completion April 20, 2027, and completion January 18, 2028. A separate pipeline report describes AZD5148 results from a Phase IIb randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled recurrence-prevention study announced in April 2026. [E1][E7] Bezlotoxumab is an intravenous monoclonal antibody used with SOC antibiotics in MODIFY I/II; the trial record also lists it and other antibodies against C. difficile toxins as excluded prior or current therapies. [E2][E3] A pipeline report says results from a Phase II randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled LMN-201 study were announced in February 2026, while a February 2026 listing describes NCT05330182 as recruiting. [E7][E6]", "confident_score": 0.65, "missing": "For the named competing agents ADS024, DNV3837, ibezapolstat, ridinilazole, CP101, SER-109, VE303, MGB-BP-3, and CRS3123: their individual mechanisms or classes (except the study descriptions stated for ibezapolstat and VE303), sponsors, trial identifiers, enrollment, detailed eligibility, and reported results are not supplied. AZD5148's trial identifier is supplied, but its phase-specific registry linkage, enrollment, efficacy results, and adverse-event results are not stated. The competitive set's other antibody-fragment, enzyme, biologic-cocktail, or TcdB-targeting agents and their trial profiles are not specified. For LMN-201, the trial record's enrollment is not linked to eligibility in the sources that provide the 375-participant figure; dose amount and schedule are not stated. The LMN-201 trial status and completion dates differ across listings, and the relationship between the February 2026 Phase II results announcement and the recruiting NCT05330182 listing is not specified.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E6", "E7", "E8"], "next_queries": ["LMN-201 competing C. difficile agents mechanisms pipeline", "LMN-201 AZD5148 NCT07285213 efficacy results enrollment", "LMN-201 NCT05330182 dose schedule enrollment", "LMN-201 NCT05330182 status completion date registry", "LMN-201 Phase II February 2026 results trial identifier"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 3960, "confident_score": 0.65, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 13, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 7, "cited_domains": ["clinicaltrials.gov", "delta.larvol.com", "deltatrials.com", "lumen.bio", "med.agiltrace.com", "openpr.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 80, "ungrounded_tokens": ["June", "December", "August", "Named", "January"], "n_ungrounded": 5, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q016", "objective": "PLM-103 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) animal models", "anchors": ["PLM-103"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 4901, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "title": "CCDI Hub", "date": null, "text": "This system is provided for Government-authorized use only. Unauthorized or improper use of this system is prohibited and may result in disciplinary action and/or civil and criminal penalties."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/clincancerres/article-pdf/23/3/623/2041560/623.pdf", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "title": "Cloudflare", "date": null, "text": "Cloudflare is currently unable to resolve your requested domain (aacrjournals.org)."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4167608/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Patient Derived Xenograft Models: An Emerging Platform for ...", "date": "2014-07-15", "text": "|(33)|PDAC|42|Primary|Surgery|Fresh tumor pieces in matrigel|Nude|s.c.|61 %| ... |PDAC (26)|T/C < 20 %|Gemcitabine Erlotinib Temsirolimus|14|36 0 0|NA| |PDAC (33)|TGI > 85 %|Gemcitabine|23|17 %|Response to Gemcitabine in the PDX model predicted longer time to progression in patients.| ... These experiments show that the response rates in PDX models correlate with those observed in the clinic, both for targeted agents and for classic cytotoxics. ... With regards to conventional chemotherapy, studies in NSCLC, HBC, CRC and PDAC demonstrated that response rates to clinically used agents such as paclitaxel, carboplatin, gemcitabine, 5-fluorouracil, irinotecan and adriamycin, among others, are comparable between PDX models and clinical data (Table 3). More recently, the role of PDX models as potential screening platforms for clinical trials has been also shown in a prospective study in PDAC. This work showed that the combination of *nab*-paclitaxel and gemcitabine is effective in PDX models of PDAC, a finding that correlated with the clinical efficacy of this combination. In fact, this regimen has recently been demonstrated to provide a survival benefit for patients with advanced PDAC in a randomized phase III study, and is likely to become a standard of care in this setting (38)."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://medschool.cuanschutz.edu/colorado-cancer-center/clinical-trials/214C19E7-3028-64D9-A5BF-FF0A001CE385", "domain": "medschool.cuanschutz.edu", "title": "Page not found", "date": null, "text": "303-724-4357 (4-HELP from a campus phone)"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://bio.tools/pdxnet_portal", "domain": "bio.tools", "title": "{{ngMeta['og:title']}}", "date": null, "text": "This is a development version of the registry. There is no guarantee that the data entered here will be preserved in the future."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://ngdc.cncb.ac.cn/openlb/publication/OLB-PM-37602328", "domain": "ngdc.cncb.ac.cn", "title": "OpenLB Open Library of Bioscience", "date": "2023-01-01", "text": "- Wafik S El-Deiry: Laboratory of Translational Oncology and Experimental Cancer Therapeutics, Warren Alpert Medical School, Brown University, Providence, RI, United States."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S266636432500061X", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "title": "Evaluation of the safety, pharmacokinetics, and antitumor activity of ...", "date": null, "text": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022356525397867", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "title": "A potential therapeutic target for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "date": null, "text": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/7b3e5bce8ca746e68b004c9c7068431f", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "PLM-103 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "date": null, "text": "Indication Phase ... Study Phase Population Analyzed Enrollment ... Results Evaluation Publication Date No Data"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://research-portal.uu.nl/ws/files/268222329/ijms-26-06388.pdf", "domain": "research-portal.uu.nl", "title": "Biomimetic Tumour Model Systems for Pancreatic Ductal ...", "date": null, "text": "It has been reported that cell line-derived xenografts have predicted the therapeutic response in various cancers, including PDAC, inconsistently [123–125]. ... The authors observed that there was a reduction in tumour volume with cetuximab compared to the untreated control group. Moreover, the reduction in tumour volume was more profound when treatment was combined with gemcitabine (~94.7% decrease in tumour cells for 32 ng/mL gemcitabine Int. J. Mol. Sci. 2025, 26, 6388 15 of 54 plus 2.5 µg/mL of cetuximab vs. a decrease of 10.5% in tumour cells without gemcitabine and only 2.5 µg/mL of cetuximab [126]). In contrast, it was found in a phase III clinical trial that patients who had advanced pancreatic cancer treated with gemcitabine and a placebo or gemcitabine with cetuximab experienced no benefit from treatment [127]. Other studies also further support the translational inconsistency between results seen with cell line-derived xenografts and clinical trials for various types of cancers [128,129]."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7359, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 245}, "timing": {"search_ms": 454.9, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 3753.8, "total_ms": 4208.7}, "luna_wall_ms": 3753.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "PLM-103 single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition, tumour regression, and tumour shrinkage results in PDAC animal models.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["PLM-103 PDAC animal model monotherapy tumour growth inhibition regression"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q014", "objective": "Sirpiglenastat in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["Sirpiglenastat", "DRP-104"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15288, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s43018-023-00647-3", "domain": "nature.com", "title": "Targeting pancreatic cancer metabolic dependencies through glutamine antagonism", "date": "2023-10-09", "text": "To overcome these limitations, recent efforts led to the creation of sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that circumvents the toxicities of DON while retaining the covalent and irreversible properties of DON 16, 17, 18, 19. ... In this study, we demonstrated that both DON and DRP-104 caused a metabolic crisis in PDAC by globally impairing Gln metabolism, resulting in a significant decrease in proliferation. In addition, we observed a significant reduction in tumor growth in several in vivo PDAC models using DRP-104 as a monotherapy. ... These preclinical results suggest that broadly targeting Gln metabolism could provide an alternative therapeutic avenue for PDAC. ... We first demonstrated that DON treatment led to a significant decrease in cellular proliferation across several PDAC tumors (Fig. 1b and Extended Data Fig. 1a–h). ... We first validated that DRP-104 caused a metabolic crisis in vitro similar to DON, impacting multiple critical metabolic pathways (Extended Data Fig. 7a). Most importantly, DRP-104 treatment led to an accumulation of tumor Gln levels in vivo (Fig. 2a), supporting its on-target activity as an inhibitor of Gln use. Remarkably, DRP-104 significantly reduced tumor growth in two independent orthotopic syngeneic PDAC models after two cycles of treatment (Fig. 2b). ... DRP-104 treatment significantly reduced liver colonization in two independent models (Fig. 2c,d). These data indicate that DRP-104 effectively delays tumor growth and prevents metastatic colonization in PDAC. ... To better understand the impact of Gln antagonism in other clinically relevant models, we tested Gln antagonism in a set of patient-derived organoids (PDOs) and patient-derived xenografts (PDXs) (Fig. 4a). DON demonstrated a reduction in proliferation in a panel of PDAC PDOs (Fig. 4b–f). Consistent with the PDO data and the other models tested (syngeneic, immune-deficient), we observed a significant decrease in tumor growth in multiple PDX models (Fig. 4g–j). Taken together, these data suggest that DON/DRP-104 is broadly effective in decreasing PDAC growth across multiple models. ... While there was substantial single-agent activity using DRP-104, residual tumors at the experimental end point displayed higher levels of proliferation markers compared to vehicle, suggesting that these tumors developed therapeutic resistance (Fig. 5a,b). ... While both DRP-104 and trametinib reduced tumor growth as single agents, we observed a more substantial decrease in tumor growth in the combination treatment (Fig. 6b). ... Indeed, combination treatment with DRP-104 and the MEK inhibitor trametinib reduced tumor growth and prolonged survival in syngeneic PDAC models. While survival was significantly improved in these proof-of-concept studies, we did not see evidence of complete tumor regression, suggesting that more potent combinations will be needed to ensure durable results."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.targetmol.com/compound/sirpiglenastat", "domain": "targetmol.com", "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) | Glutamine Antagonist", "date": null, "text": "|Description|Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) belongs to glutamine antagonist prodrugs and is a broad-spectrum glutamine metabolism inhibitor with tumor-selective activation, good tolerability, and oral activity. This compound is used in therapeutic research on various solid tumors.| ... |In vivo|Method: The in vivo activity of Sirpiglenastat was validated in a head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) subcutaneous xenograft model in SCID/NSG mice. Sirpiglenastat was administered by subcutaneous injection at a dose of 3 mg/kg on a 5 days on/2 days off schedule for 4 weeks, with vehicle diluent as the solvent control.Result: Sirpiglenastat significantly inhibited tumor growth, reduced tumor weight, and improved survival rates in the model mice. [3]| ... |In Vivo Formulation|10% DMSO+90% Saline: 9.05 mg/mL (20.5 mM), Suspension.Please add the solvents sequentially, clarifying the solution as much as possible before adding the next one. Dissolve by heating and/or sonication if necessary. Working solution is recommended to be prepared and used immediately. The formulation provided above is for reference purposes only. In vivo formulations may vary and should be modified based on specific experimental conditions.|"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36383674/?otool=icznmelib", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Discovery of DRP-104, a tumor-targeted metabolic inhibitor prodrug - PubMed", "date": "2022-11-18", "text": "Given its clinical potential, we designed DON peptide prodrugs and found DRP-104 [isopropyl(*S*)-2-((*S*)-2-acetamido-3-(1*H*-indol-3-yl)-propanamido)-6-diazo-5-oxo-hexanoate] that was preferentially bioactivated to DON in tumor while bioinactivated to an inert metabolite in GI tissues. In drug distribution studies, DRP-104 delivered a prodigious 11-fold greater exposure of DON to tumor versus GI tissues. DRP-104 affected multiple metabolic pathways in tumor, including decreased glutamine flux into the TCA cycle. In efficacy studies, both DRP-104 and DON caused complete tumor regression; however, DRP-104 had a markedly improved tolerability profile. DRP-104's effect was CD8^+^ T cell dependent and resulted in robust immunologic memory. ... DRP-104 is now in clinical trials under the FDA Fast Track designation."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9668306/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Discovery of DRP-104, a tumor-targeted metabolic inhibitor prodrug", "date": "2022-11-16", "text": "In efficacy studies, both DRP-104 and DON caused complete tumor regression; however, DRP-104 had a markedly improved tolerability profile. DRP-104’s effect was CD8^+^ T cell dependent and resulted in robust immunologic memory. ... DRP-104 is now in clinical trials under the FDA Fast Track designation. ... We report here that DRP-104 administration to CES1^−/−^ mice (a model recapitulating human prodrug metabolism) resulted in prodigious 6- and 11-fold greater exposures of DON to the tumor versus plasma and GI tissue, respectively, resulting in complete tumor regression without GI side effects. We further showed that DRP-104 enhanced the efficacy of anti-PD-1 immunotherapy and that its effect was CD8^+^ T cell dependent. Last, and most remarkably, we showed that mice previously cured with DRP-104 monotherapy completely rejected tumor rechallenge, indicating that glutamine antagonism therapy triggers immunologic memory. On the basis of these promising preclinical data, DRP-104 is currently in clinical trials (NCT04471415) for treatment of advanced-stage solid tumors as a single agent and in combination with immunotherapy. ... ### DRP-104 was similarly efficacious to DON in inhibiting tumor growth but with a marked reduction in toxicity ... Both DRP-104 and DON caused complete regression of the tumor (Fig. 4A). ... DRP-104 and anti-PD-1 monotherapies resulted in significant MC38 tumor growth inhibition. However, the combination of DRP-104 with anti-PD-1 therapy showed a significant improvement in efficacy compared to the monotherapies alone (Fig. 7A). ... In addition to tumor growth inhibition, both DRP-104 and anti-PD-1 monotherapy resulted in enhanced survival compared to vehicle. ... In all cases, mice that had previously been cured with DRP-104 monotherapy completely rejected tumor rechallenge (Fig. 7E), suggesting that immunologic memory had been established through glutamine blockade."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/7AEkDz37/", "domain": "ouci.dntb.gov.ua", "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) Induces Antitumor Efficacy through Direct ...", "date": null, "text": "DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) was designed as a novel prodrug of the broad-acting glutamine antagonist 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-norleucine (DON). DRP-104 is an inactive form that is preferentially converted to DON within tumors. ... Finally, DRP-104 demonstrated significant antitumor activity as a monotherapy, which was further enhanced in combination with checkpoint blockade therapies, leading to improved survival and long-term durable cures. In summary, DRP-104 broadly remodels the tumor microenvironment by inducing extensive tumor metabolism effects and enhancing the infiltration and function of multiple immune cells distinct from those obtained by checkpoint inhibitor therapy. This unique mechanism of action supports the ongoing clinical development of DRP-104 alone and in combination with checkpoint inhibitors. ... AbstractPancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cells use glutamine (Gln) to support proliferation and redox balance. ... In addition, we observed a profound decrease in tumor growth in several in vivo models using sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that was designed to circumvent DON-associated toxicity. ... Combinatorial treatment with DRP-104 and trametinib led to a significant increase in survival in a syngeneic model of PDAC. These proof-of-concept studies suggested that broadly targeting Gln metabolism could provide a therapeutic avenue for PDAC. The combination with an ERK signaling pathway inhibitor could further improve the therapeutic outcome."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.dracenpharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/DRACENposter_AACR2021_03252021-Final.pdf", "domain": "dracenpharma.com", "title": "Broad glutamine pathway inhibition by DRP-104 results in ...", "date": null, "text": "Here, we tested the novel broad acting glutamine antagonist DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) in mouse models of lung cancer as a single agent in OSI-resistant EGFR mutant LUAD or in combination with anti-PD-1 in CPI-resistant LUSC. ... DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) significantly inhibited tumor progression in the 3605 T1 (Lkb1-/-;Pten-/-;p53-/-) and 1969b (KRASG12D;p53-/-) genetically engineered mouse tumor models (GEMMs) but not in the related GEMM models 2785 2-B8 (KrasG12D;p53-/-;Lkb1-/l-) and L3161c (KrasG12D;p53-/-;Lkb1-/l-). Responsive tumors had a higher KEAP1/NRF2 pathway score compared to non-responsive tumors. Interestingly, single agent anti-tumor activity of DRP-104 was extended into several xenograft and PDX tumor models with KEAP1 mutations, including models co-harboring mutations in KRAS, PTEN, p53 and/or LKB1. In addition, we showed that DRP-104 induced a significant response in an OSI-resistant PDX model of EGFR mutant LUAD. Lastly, in the DRP-104 responsive but anti-PD-1 non-responsive GEMM tumors, the combination of DRP-104 and anti- ... Overall, DRP- 104 demonstrated potential to treat hypermetabolic therapy-resistant LUSC and LUAD as a single-agent therapy and in combination with immune CPI. ... Summary of DRP-104 Response in LUSC and LUAD PDX, xenograft, and syngeneic models tested. Responder defined as ≥80% tumor growth inhibition; partial responder defined as 30-80% tumor growth inhibition; non-responder defined as 0-30% tumor growth inhibition. ... Figure 3. DRP-104 is effective as single-agent therapy in LUSC and LUAD syngeneic xenograft models."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-026-02620-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=92febd2b-4845-46a6-b60e-41f1e6fa270d", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Metabolic plasticity in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma ...", "date": "2026-03-28", "text": "However, clinical use of DON is limited by systemic toxicity and poor pharmacokinetics. To overcome these limitations, a tumor-targeted prodrug, DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat), was developed. DRP-104 shows strong antitumor and anti-metastatic effects in syngeneic and immunocompromised PDAC mouse models by blocking multiple Gln-dependent pathways involved in central carbon, nucleotide, and lipid metabolism [375]. Th"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=e691bf1a-5906-401a-8e54-ede9c0957bd1", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "RMC-7977 / Revolution Medicines - Larvol Delta", "date": null, "text": "## Hypoxia enhances sensitivity to RAS inhibition in PDAC (AACR 2026) - \"The RAS(ON) multi-selective inhibitor daraxonrasib has demonstrated clinical activity in PDAC and is currently being evaluated in Phase 3 trials; however, monotherapy resistance still emerges underscoring the need to identify biological processes that enable tumor cell survival following RAS inhibition. Pharmacologic inhibition of mitochondrial respiration under normoxia also enhanced RMC-7977 sensitivity. Together, these findings reveal that RMC-7977 reduces glucose uptake and shifts cells toward oxidative metabolism and uncovers a potential metabolic vulnerability involving the PDAC tumor microenvironment that has the potential to enhance the impact of RAS inhibition.\" ... ## Defining a molecular signature for KEAP1-NRF2 mediated resistance to KRAS inhibition in KRAS-mutant pancreatic and lung cancer (AACR 2026) - \"We determined that loss of KEAP1 and activation of the transcription factor NRF2 drove resistance to the KRASG12D-selective inhibitor MRTX1133 and the RAS(ON) multi-selective inhibitor RMC-7977. Finally, we observed that KEAP1-deficient cells exhibited elevated glutamine metabolism, and combination treatment with the clinical candidate glutamine antagonist DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) strongly enhanced KRAS inhibitor-mediated growth suppression in KRAS-mutant PDAC and NSCLC tumors in vivo. In summary, our studies established a gene signature for KEAP1 loss-driven resistance and validated a therapeutic strategy to overcome KEAP1-NRF2-driven RAS inhibitor resistance.\""}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.prweb.com/releases/Dracen_Pharmaceuticals_announces_publication_in_Molecular_Cancer_Therapeutics/prweb18944062.htm", "domain": "prweb.com", "title": "Dracen Pharmaceuticals announces publication in Molecular Cancer Therapeutics", "date": "2022-10-10", "text": "\"By broadly targeting all 10 glutamine-metabolizing enzymes, DRP-104 leads to profound anti-tumor activity related to both direct effects on tumor metabolism as well as immune mediated activity via remodeling of the tumor microenvironment and induction of both innate and adaptive anti-tumor immunity. This unique mechanism of action shows promise treating various tumors including those resistant to IO checkpoint inhibitors. We are excited to continue the clinical development of this differentiated agent both as a monotherapy and in combination with immune checkpoint blockade.\" ... Our lead glutamine antagonist, DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat), is currently in clinical development. The mechanisms of action for DRP-104 include a) direct inhibition of tumor cell addiction to glutamine metabolism leading to substantial single agent activity and tumor regression; b) broad metabolic remodeling of the tumor microenvironment leading to enhanced anti-tumor immune activity; and c) stimulation of T effector, NK and NKT cells and inhibition of immunosuppressive MDSC and macrophage cells, leading to greater long-term durable responses and survival."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov/pdc/study/PDC000198", "domain": "proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov", "title": "PDC000198 - Proteomic Data Commons - National Cancer Institute", "date": null, "text": "This system is provided for Government-authorized use only. Unauthorized or improper use of this system is prohibited and may result in disciplinary action and/or civil and criminal penalties."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9672, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 895}, "timing": {"search_ms": 501.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 10834.4, "total_ms": 11336.1}, "luna_wall_ms": 10834.4, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) monotherapy significantly reduced tumour growth in two independent orthotopic syngeneic PDAC models after two treatment cycles and in multiple PDAC patient-derived xenograft models; it also showed antitumour effects in syngeneic and immunocompromised PDAC mouse models. [E1][E5][E7]", "confident_score": 0.78, "missing": "The quantitative tumour-growth inhibition values; whether sirpiglenastat monotherapy caused tumour regression or shrinkage in these PDAC models; the specific model identities and monotherapy dose and schedule.", "citations": ["E1", "E5", "E7"], "next_queries": ["Sirpiglenastat DRP-104 PDAC tumour growth inhibition percentage", "Sirpiglenastat DRP-104 PDAC monotherapy tumour regression shrinkage", "Sirpiglenastat DRP-104 PDAC orthotopic syngeneic model names", "Sirpiglenastat DRP-104 PDAC monotherapy dose schedule"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 307, "confident_score": 0.78, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 3, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 3, "cited_domains": ["link.springer.com", "nature.com", "ouci.dntb.gov.ua"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 4, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q007", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as FN1 IL-10RB IL10RA Interleukin-10 Antibody-Cytokine Conjugate Fusion Protein Biologic", "anchors": ["Dekavil"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15716, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.clinicaltrialsregister.eu/ctr-search/trial/2017-002108-28/BE", "domain": "clinicaltrialsregister.eu", "title": "Clinical trials", "date": null, "text": "|B.Sponsor: 1| |B.1.1|Name of Sponsor|Pfizer Inc, 235 East 42nd Street, New York, New York 10017| |B.1.3.4|Country|United States| |B.3.1 and B.3.2|Status of the sponsor|Commercial| ... |B.4.1|Name of organisation providing support|Pfizer Inc.| ... |D.3.1|Product name|Dekavil| |D.3.2|Product code|PF-06687234| ... |D.3.9.2|Current sponsor code|PF-06687234| |D.3.9.3|Other descriptive name|F8IL10| ... |D.3.11.13.1|Other medicinal product type|human monoclonal antibody-cytokine fusion protein F8IL10|"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c932383d8dcb4af180c9a51971a82032", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Dekavil - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Synapse", "date": null, "text": "# Dekavil ... |Drug Type Fusion protein| |--| |Synonyms F8-IL10, PF 06687234, PF-06687234| |Target EDA-FN x IL-10R| ... |Mechanism EDA-FN inhibitors(extra domain A of fibronectin inhibitors), IL-10R stimulants(Interleukin 10 receptor stimulants)| |Therapeutic Areas Immune System DiseasesDigestive System DisordersSkin and Musculoskeletal Diseases| |Active Indication-| |Inactive Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn DiseaseRheumatoid Arthritis| |Originator Organization Philogen SpA| ... |Inactive Organization Pfizer Inc.Philogen SpA| |License Organization Pfizer Inc.Philogen SpA| |Drug Highest PhaseDiscontinuedPhase 2| ... PHASE 1B, OPEN LABEL STUDY TO CHARACTERIZE THE DISTRIBUTION OF A SINGLE INTRAVENOUS DOSE OF [124I]-IODOBENZOYL (IB)-PF-06687234 WITH CONCURRENT ADMINISTRATION OF NON-RADIOLABELED PF-06687234 AS ASSESSED WITH POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY AND COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (PET-CT) IMAGING IN MODERATE TO SEVERE ULCERATIVE AND CROHN'S COLITIS SUBJECTS NCT03269695 / TerminatedPhase 2 A PHASE 2A, RANDOMIZED, DOUBLE-BLIND, PLACEBO-CONTROLLED STUDY TO EVALUATE THE EFFICACY, SAFETY, TOLERABILITY AND PHARMACOKINETICS OF PF-06687234 AS ADD-ON THERAPY TO INFLIXIMAB IN ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS SUBJECTS WHO ARE NOT IN REMISSION (BUILD UC) ... Efficacy and safety of the interleukin-10–fragment F8 fusion protein PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active ulcerative colitis: a randomized, phase IIa clinical trial ... Background:: PF-06687234 is a novel, human, single-chain variable fragment cytokine fusion protein comprising the antibody fragment F8 and the immunoregulatory cytokine interleukin-10. ... We evaluated the efficacy and safety of PF-06687234 as an add-on therapy to infliximab in participants with active ulcerative colitis (UC). ... This phase IIa, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, multicenter study randomized (1:1) participants with active UC to subcutaneous PF-06687234 20 mg or placebo once weekly, and participants continued intravenous background infliximab every 6 or 8 weeks, for 12 weeks. ... After 20 participants were randomized and treated, this study was stopped early for futility. There were no statistically significant differences between groups for any efficacy endpoint (all p > 0.05), although numerical trends towards efficacy were observed in some secondary endpoints. PF-06687234 was detected at a low concentration in only one colon tissue sample. There were no differences in AEs between the groups. The most frequently reported AE in the PF-06687234-treated group was injection site reaction. ... PF-06687234 was well tolerated, but when combined with background infliximab, did not meet the primary efficacy endpoint in this cohort of participants with active UC. Efficacious PF-06687234 tissue concentrations may not have been achieved at the dose tested. ... Efficacy, safety, and tolerability of PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in active UC subjects not in remission. ..."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5491079/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Updates on Molecular ...", "date": "2017-05-11", "text": "^37^ However, no clear therapeutic effect of IL-10 has yet been demonstrated in human clinical trials. However, newer formulations of IL-10 targeted at sites of inflammation, such as F8-IL-10 (Dekavil) may show promise for the treatment of IBD. Dekavil is an immunocytokine consisting of a targeting antibody fused to the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10. Dekavil has entered Phase II clinical trials in combination with methotrexate for the therapy of Rheumatoid Arthritis and investigations in IBD indications are being considered."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.sanofi.com/en/media-room/press-releases/2026/2026-02-17-11-00-00-3239014", "domain": "sanofi.com", "title": "Press Release: Sanofi and Teva's duvakitug phase 2b ...", "date": "2026-02-17", "text": "Press Release: Sanofi and Teva’s duvakitug phase 2b maintenance data demonstrated clinically meaningful durable efficacy in ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease ... - In the RELIEVE UCCD LTE phase 2b study, duvakitug showed robust, durable efficacy for an additional 44 weeks in UC and CD patients who had responded after 14 weeks of induction ... - Findings reinforce the potential of duvakitug which is in ongoing phase 3 programs in UC and CD **Paris and Parsippany, NJ, February 17, 2026.** Positive results from the RELIEVE UCCD long-term extension (LTE) study (clinical study identifier: NCT05668013) of duvakitug, an investigational human monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A, showed durable clinical and endoscopic efficacy maintained over 44 weeks in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) that initially responded to the induction phase. RELIEVE UCCD LTE is a double-blind randomized study evaluating the long-term efficacy, safety, and tolerability of duvakitug in UC and CD, the two most common forms of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). ... \"With phase 3 studies underway, we're committed to advancing duvakitug for patients who need new options, and it remains a key opportunity in our pipeline.\"* The study enrolled 130 patients who responded to duvakitug in the RELIEVE UCCD induction study and entered a 44-week maintenance period. Patients were re-randomized to receive either a 450 mg or 900 mg subcutaneous dose of duvakitug every four weeks for up to a total of 58 weeks of exposure. ... Both doses of duvakitug were well tolerated. ... The RELIEVE UCCD program is comprised of an induction study and a long-term extension. **RELIEVE UCCD**: A 14-week phase 2b, randomized, double-blinded, dose-ranging induction study to evaluate the efficacy, safety, pharmacokinetics, and tolerability of duvakitug in adults with moderate-to-severe UC or CD. ... **RELIEVE UCCD LTE**: An ongoing study to evaluate the long-term efficacy and safety of duvakitug. Patients who received duvakitug, completed the 14-week induction study, and were responders, entered a 44-week double-blind maintenance period and were re-randomized to receive either 450 mg or 900 mg of subcutaneous duvakitug every four weeks. Patients who completed the 44-week maintenance period may continue to receive duvakitug in an open-label extension. ... Duvakitug, a human monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A, is currently in phase 3 clinical studies for the treatment of UC and CD. ... The safety and efficacy of duvakitug have not been reviewed by any regulatory authority. ... Teva and Sanofi are collaborating to co-develop and co-commercialize duvakitug for the treatment of UC and CD. ... Sanofi is leading the phase 3 clinical development program. Teva will lead commercialization of the product in Europe, Israel, and specified other countries, and Sanofi will lead commercialization in North America, Japan, other parts of Asia, and the rest of the world."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=38609b33-9fa9-4d28-ad56-2b75573a091a", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "Dekavil (F8-IL10) / Pfizer, Philogen", "date": null, "text": "Dekavil (F8-IL10) / Pfizer, Philogen - LARVOL DELTA ... February 13, 2026 Efficacy and safety of the interleukin-10-fragment F8 fusion protein PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active ulcerative colitis: a randomized, phase IIa clinical trial. (PubMed, Therap Adv Gastroenterol) - P2a | \"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03269695. NCT03269695.\" ... March 08, 2025 EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE INTERLEUKIN-10-FRAGMENT F8 FUSION PROTEIN, PF06687234, AS ADD-ON THERAPY TO INFLIXIMAB IN PATIENTS WITH ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS: A RANDOMIZED, PHASE 2A CLINICAL TRIAL (DDW 2025) - P2a | \"PF06687234 was well tolerated, but when combined with background infliximab treatment, did not meet the efficacy endpoints in this small cohort of participants with active UC. The inability to detect high levels of PF06687234 in mucosal biopsies, suggests that efficacious tissue concentrations may not have been achieved at the dose tested. This therapeutic hypothesis should be investigated in larger studies and with higher doses than studied here.\""}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/95/NCT03269695/Prot_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "title": "a phase 2a, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled", "date": null, "text": "PHARMACOKINETICS OF PF-06687234 AS ADD-ON THERAPY TO INFLIXIMAB IN ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS SUBJECTS WHO ARE NOT IN REMISSION (BUILD UC) Investigational Product Number: PF-06687234 Investigational Product Name: Not Applicable (N/A) United States (US) Investigational New Drug Application (IND) Number: European Clinical Trials Database (EudraCT) Number: 2017-002108-28 Protocol Number: B7581002 Phase: 2A ... Pfizer has exclusively in-licensed F8IL10 (Dekavil) from Philogen S.pA., Siena, Italy, for development in inflammatory disorders. In nonclinical or clinical studies sponsored by Philogen or conducted using Philogen manufactured material, the product is identified as F8IL10. In nonclinical and clinical studies conducted by or for Pfizer using Pfizer manufactured material, the investigational product is identified as PF-06687234. PF-06687234 is comparable to F8IL10. ... PF-06687234 is a fully human single chain variable fragment (scFv)-cytokine fusion protein consisting of the scFv F8 (specific to the Extra-Domain A of fibronectin, selectively expressed in newly formed blood vessels during inflammatory responses) fused to the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10. PF-06687234 is designed to enrich IL-10 delivery to inflamed tissues, potentially leading to higher local concentrations, greater clinical efficacy, and decreased systemic toxicity and is currently being investigated in subjects with ulcerative colitis (UC). ... This is a Phase 2A, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel group, multicenter study in subjects with active UC and a non-remission response to infliximab. Each subject ... PF-06687234 is a fully human single chain variable fragment (scFv)-cytokine fusion protein comprised of the antibody fragment F8 and the anti-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-10 (IL-10). F8 is the scFv antibody fragment, which selectively binds to the splice variant of fibronectin Extra-Domain A (EDA), which is primarily expressed in newly formed blood vessels during inflammation."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21). ... |Combination|Infliximab + Ustekinumab|TNF antagonist + IL-12/23 p40 antibody|NCT06453317|Phase 2|Started Feb 2025; estimated completion June 2028| |Combination|AMT-101 + Adalimumab|IL-10 fusion protein + TNF antagonist|NCT04583358 (LOMBARD), NCT05372939 (MARKET)|Phase 2a|Both completed by July 2022; LOMBARD failed primary endpoint| ... Ravagalimab (ABBV-323) is a IgG1 monoclonal antibody that antagonizes CD40, a TNF receptor family member involved in lymphocyte activation and antigen-presenting cell function. ... Tralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC. ... A Phase 2a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study (NCT03269695) evaluated PF-06687234, an investigational IL-10 fusion protein, as weekly subcutaneous add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active UC who were not in remission despite stable infliximab treatment for at least 14 weeks."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6360861/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Targeting IL-10 Family Cytokines for the Treatment of Human ...", "date": null, "text": "TNF-blocking agents, including anti-TNF antibodies, such as infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab, have shown good clinical efficacy in the treatment of both CD and UC, and are currently the standard of care for patients with moderate-to-severe disease (Hanauer et al. 2002, 2006; Järnerot et al. 2005; Sandborn et al. 2007). ... |Dekavil (F8-IL-10)|Rheumatoid arthritis|Phase II NCT02076659|Philogen/Pfizer| | | |Phase II NCT02270632|Philogen/Pfizer| ... |AG011 (engineered *Lactococcus lactis* secreting human IL-10)|Ulcerative colitis|Phase I/II NCT00729872|ActoGeniX N.V.| ... Several local delivery strategies, such as ectopic expression of IL-10 by commensal bacteria or IL-10/antibody fusion proteins, have been devised and are being tested (Table 1). ... Four antibodies, L19 (binding the extra-domain B of fibronectin), F8 (binding the extra-domain A of fibronectin), G11 (binding the extra-domain C of tenascin-C), and F16 (binding the extra-domain A1 of tenascin-C), which specifically recognize angiogenic but not normal tissue, were considered as fusion partners for IL-10 (Trachsel et al. 2007; Schwager et al. 2009). ... More importantly, the repression of inflammation by L19-IL-10 is more profound than the effect mediated by a systemically distributed fusion protein of a control antibody single chain fused to IL-10 (Trachsel et al. 2007). ... The corresponding IL-10 immunocytokine, F8-IL-10 (Dekavil), also showed efficacy in the CIA model (Doll et al. 2013). The combination of a murine F8-mIL-10 and a TNF-blocking muTNFR-Fc fusion protein displayed superior efficacy compared with either agent alone (Doll et al."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7789055/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Research advances of vasoactive intestinal peptide in the ... - PMC", "date": "2020-12-28", "text": "The new immune cytokine, F8-IL-10 (Dekavil), a targeted antibody associated with IL-10, is currently being evaluated in phase 2 clinical trials in patients with rheumatoid arthritis, potentially paving the way for its use in IBD patients[12]."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.pfizer.com/science/focus-areas/immunology-inflammation/pipeline", "domain": "pfizer.com", "title": "Immunology & Inflammation: Autoimmune Pipeline & Trials - Pfizer", "date": null, "text": "anti-CXCR5 Dekavil | New project ##### Rheumatoid Arthritis (Biologic) - phase 1 - Inflammation & Immunology - New Molecular Entity - Biologic - Current IL-10 LITFULO (ritlecitinib) | New project ##### Crohn's Disease - phase 2 - Inflammation & Immunology - Product Enhancement - Small Molecule - Discontinued JAK3/TEC inhibitor LITFULO (ritlecitinib) | New project ##### Ulcerative Colitis - phase 2 - Inflammation & Immunology - Product Enhancement - Small Molecule - Discontinued JAK3/TEC inhibitor"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10802, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 5100}, "timing": {"search_ms": 713.1, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 52690.5, "total_ms": 53403.6}, "luna_wall_ms": 52690.5, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Dekavil (F8-IL10; PF-06687234; F8IL10) is a human antibody-fragment–IL-10 fusion protein: its F8 fragment binds fibronectin extra-domain A (EDA), which is expressed in newly formed vessels during inflammation, and the fusion is designed to enrich IL-10 delivery to inflamed tissue. Pfizer exclusively in-licensed F8IL10 from Philogen; Pfizer sponsored the UC study, while Philogen is listed as the originator. [E1][E2][E6] In the Phase 2A BUILD UC study, participants had active UC, were not in remission on infliximab, and continued background infliximab; PF-06687234 20 mg or placebo was given subcutaneously once weekly for 12 weeks, with infliximab given intravenously every 6 or 8 weeks. Twenty participants were randomized and treated before the study stopped early for futility. There were no statistically significant differences for any efficacy endpoint (all p > 0.05); the primary efficacy endpoint was not met. PF-06687234 was detected at low concentration in only one colon-tissue sample, there were no between-group differences in adverse events, and injection-site reaction was the most frequently reported adverse event in the PF-06687234 group. It was described as well tolerated. [E2][E5][E6] Synapse also associates NCT03269695 with a terminated Phase 1B PET-CT distribution study of a single intravenous dose of [124I]-iodobenzoyl PF-06687234 with concurrent non-radiolabeled PF-06687234 in moderate-to-severe UC and Crohn’s disease; other listings associate NCT03269695 with the Phase 2A BUILD UC study. [E2][E5][E6] Other UC agents in the evidence include AMT-101, an IL-10 fusion protein combined with the TNF antagonist adalimumab in Phase 2a studies LOMBARD (NCT04583358) and MARKET (NCT05372939); both were completed by July 2022, and LOMBARD failed its primary endpoint. [E7] AG011 is engineered Lactococcus lactis secreting human IL-10, studied in UC in Phase I/II under NCT00729872. [E8] Duvakitug is a human monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A; Sanofi and Teva said it was in ongoing Phase 3 UC and Crohn’s disease programs. In the RELIEVE UCCD LTE study (NCT05668013), 130 induction responders entered a 44-week maintenance period and were rerandomized to 450 mg or 900 mg subcutaneously every four weeks, for up to 58 weeks of total exposure. The release reported durable clinical and endoscopic efficacy over the additional 44 weeks and that both doses were well tolerated. [E4] Other UC treatments named are infliximab, adalimumab and golimumab (TNF-α-targeting biologics); vedolizumab (integrin α4β7-targeting); and ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab and risankizumab (IL-12/23-pathway therapies). Certolizumab is also named among TNF-blocking agents used for UC. [E7][E8] The evidence also names a Phase 2 combination of infliximab (TNF antagonist) and ustekinumab (IL-12/23 p40 antibody), NCT06453317, started February 2025 with estimated completion June 2028; and tralokinumab (CAT-354), a recombinant human IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13, tested as add-on therapy in a Phase IIa UC study, NCT01482884. [E7]", "confident_score": 0.78, "missing": "Numerical efficacy results and specified endpoint outcomes for duvakitug; AG011 trial results, enrollment and dosing; tralokinumab trial results and enrollment; the outcome of the AMT-101 MARKET study; and comparative head-to-head results across Dekavil and the other named agents.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8"], "next_queries": ["Dekavil PF-06687234 NCT03269695 Phase 2A results endpoints", "AG011 NCT00729872 ulcerative colitis results enrollment dose", "tralokinumab NCT01482884 ulcerative colitis results enrollment", "AMT-101 NCT05372939 MARKET ulcerative colitis primary endpoint results", "duvakitug NCT05668013 numerical clinical endoscopic efficacy results"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 3052, "confident_score": 0.78, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 15, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 7, "cited_domains": ["cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "clinicaltrialsregister.eu", "delta.larvol.com", "frontiersin.org", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "sanofi.com", "synapse.patsnap.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 60, "ungrounded_tokens": ["Other UC", "Phase IIa UC"], "n_ungrounded": 2, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q015", "objective": "GFH375/VS-7375 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in KRAS G12D-Mutated/MTAP-Deleted Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["GFH375 VS-7375", "GFH375", "VS-7375"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 16304, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/GFH375-poster-for-2024-AACR-Final-20240329.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "Selectivity over HUVEC (P value not updated) - verastem.com", "date": null, "text": "GFH375 potently and selectively inhibits p-ERK signaling and proliferation in KRAS G12D mutant tumor cells GFH375 accumulates in tumor tissue and elicits sustained inhibition of p-ERK signaling following a single oral administration GFH375 induces tumor regression in multiple KRAS G12D subcutaneous CDX tumor models via oral administration PK/PD study in Panc04.03 PDAC CDX mice ... The in vitro potency and selectivity, oral bioavailability, and in vivo efficacy of monotherapy and combination therapy with avutometinib, a unique RAF/MEK clamp, were evaluated in preclinical studies. ... • GFH375 (VS-7375) is a highly potent and selective inhibitor of KRAS G12D (ON/OFF). • GFH375 is orally bioavailable across preclinical species and holds promising therapeutic potential for treatment of KRAS G12D solid tumors according to its performance in multiple CDX tumor models."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/GFH375-poster-for-2024-AACR-Final-20240329.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "Selectivity over HUVEC （P value not updated)", "date": null, "text": "GFH375 potently and selectively inhibits p-ERK signaling and proliferation in KRAS G12D mutant tumor cells GFH375 accumulates in tumor tissue and elicits sustained inhibition of p-ERK signaling following a single oral administration GFH375 induces tumor regression in multiple KRAS G12D subcutaneous CDX tumor models via oral administration PK/PD study in Panc04.03 PDAC CDX mice ... The in vitro potency and selectivity, oral bioavailability, and in vivo efficacy of monotherapy and combination therapy with avutometinib, a unique RAF/MEK clamp, were evaluated in preclinical studies. ... • GFH375 (VS-7375) is a highly potent and selective inhibitor of KRAS G12D (ON/OFF). • GFH375 is orally bioavailable across preclinical species and holds promising therapeutic potential for treatment of KRAS G12D solid tumors according to its performance in multiple CDX tumor models."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.genfleet.com/Public/Uploads/uploadfile/files/20251207/Oct.pdf", "domain": "genfleet.com", "title": "GFH375/VS-7375 Program Update", "date": null, "text": "ORR [90%CI] 40.7% [30%, 52%] ... • ORR was 40.7% (24/59), 90%CI was [30%, 52%]. • DCR was 96.7% (57/59), 90%CI was [90%, 99%]. Majority (91.5%) had reduction in target lesions. ... • Median PFS was 5.52 months (90%CI: 4.27, 7.20), with a median follow-up time 5.65 months (90%CI: 4.96, 6.08). • 4-month PFS rate was 78.2% (90%CI: 69.8%, 87.5%). ... • The safety profile of GFH375 in KRAS G12D mutant PDAC patients is consistent with previous report.1,2 – Common TRAEs were gastrointestinal and hematological AEs; most were grade 1 or 2 and manageable with supportive treatment. – Most frequent TRAEs (≥20%) were diarrhea (56.1%), neutrophil count decreased (48.5%), vomiting (47.0%), nausea (47.0%), ... (N=59) 2L ... (N=47) ORR [90%CI] 40.68% [29.87%, 52.22%] 58.33% ... Population 2L PDAC with KRAS G12Dm 1 3L+ PDAC with KRAS G12Dm 1 2L PDAC with KRAS G12Xm 3 3L+ PDAC with KRAS G12Xm 4 Sample size 12 47 26 63 ORR 58.3% 36.2% 35% 22% DCR 100% 95.7% 92% 89% ... • No new safety signal has been found ... The overall safety/tolerability of GFH375 is better than that of daraxonrasib. ... The incidence of grade ≥3 TEAEs/TRAEs, TEAEs/TRAEs leading to treatment interruption, and SAEs/TRSAEs were higher in participants with prior ICI than in the ICI naïve participants (ESMO2025 data) ... Best-in-class as G12D monotherapy for NSCLC • Supporting single-arm accelerated approval for 2L+ NSCLC ... VS-7375-101 Study Schema, Efficiently Testing KRAS G12D+ Indications with Monotherapy and SOC Combinations ... • Efficacious starting dose: • 400 mg starting dose supported by monotherapy efficacy/safety in FIH study • Evaluating GI side effect mitigations: • Use prophylactic anti-emetics • Evaluate dosing with and without food ... FDA Engagement Plans • Granted Fast Track Designation for: • First-line in patients with KRAS G12D locally advanced or metastatic PDAC and • Patients with KRAS G12D locally advanced or mPDAC who received at least one prior line of standard systemic therapy in July 2025 • Plan to pursue Breakthrough Therapy Designation • Accelerated clinical development with FDA ... • First two monotherapy dose levels (400 mg QD and 600 mg QD) cleared, with no dose-limiting toxicities (DLT’s) reported • Promising anti-tumor activity observed in patients with various solid tumors, including advanced pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma • No nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea greater than Grade 1 was observed • Enrollment initiated for VS-7375 in combination with cetuximab in patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors, including colorectal cancer ... monotherapy dose"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/our-science/resources/", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "Resources | Verastem, Inc.", "date": "2026-05-05", "text": "### VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with potent anti-tumor activity as a single agent and in combination with other agents ... ### Strong durable tumor regressions with the KRASG12D ON/OFF inhibitor VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5inhibition in MTAP-deleted/KRASG12D-mutant pancreatic cancer"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "http://www.verastem.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/AACR-2025-VS-7375-poster-E-poster-4.8.26-printed.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "[PDF] VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with ...", "date": null, "text": "VS-7375 (GFH375) is an oral KRAS G12D-selective small molecule inhibitor that binds KRAS G12D in both the ON and OFF states. By blocking the activities of the ON and OFF states of KRAS G12D, VS-7375 has the potential to inhibit KRAS G12D signaling and tumor growth more completely than compounds that block KRAS G12D predominantly in the OFF state or only in the ON state. VS-7375 monotherapy and in combination with other agents is currently in clinical evaluation in the US (NCT07020221) and in China (NCT06500676; NCT07262567; NCT07259590). ... Mice bearing KRAS G12D-mutated tumors were treated with VS-7375 (KP4: 50 mg/kg orally twice daily; LS513 & LU0876: 30 mg/kg orally twice daily), RMC-9805 (KP4: 100 mg/kg orally once daily; LS513: 60 mg/kg orally once daily) or RMC-6236 (25 mg/kg orally once daily), N = 8 mice/group. Average tumor volumes over time are shown. PDAC = pancreatic cancer; CRC = colorectal cancer; NSLCL = non-small cell lung cancer. ... • VS-7375 is a potent KRAS G12D ON/OFF inhibitor with long residence time (18-24 hours) leading to more potent and durable suppression of pERK than the KRAS G12D ON-only inhibitor zoldonrasib (RMC-9805) • Across KRAS G12D-mutated tumor models in vivo, oral dosing of VS-7375 is more efficacious than KRAS G12D and pan-RAS ON-only inhibitors • In a KRAS G12D-mutated pancreatic cancer model, mechanisms of resistance observed with the tricomplex inhibitors zoldonrasib (RMC-9805) and daraxonrasib (RMC-6236) do not limit the durability of VS-7375 efficacy • Combinations of VS-7375 with other targeted therapies (e.g. cetuximab, FAK inhibitor, avutometinib, PRMT5i) confer durable tumor regressions • These results support the ongoing clinical evaluation of VS-7375 as monotherapy and in combinations for treatment of patients with KRAS G12D-mutated cancers (VS-7375-101; NCT06500676) ... Mice bearing KP4 KRAS G12D-mutated pancreatic tumors were treated with VS-7375 (50 mg/kg twice daily), RMC-9805 (100 mg/kg once ... Combination of a PRMT5 inhibitor with VS-7375 induces durable tumor regressions in a KRAS G12D-mutated;MTAP-deleted pancreatic cancer model ... Figure 8. Mice bearing KP4 KRAS G12D-mutated/MTAP-deleted pancreatic tumors were treated with VS-7375 (50 mg/kg orally, twice daily), a PRMT5 inhibitor (BMS-986504 & AMG193: 100 mg/kg orally, once daily; TNG462: 30 mg/kg orally, twice daily) or the combination. N = 8 mice/group. ... VS-7375 suppresses pERK, pAKT, pS6 and MYC levels more potently, rapidly, and durably than the KRAS G12D ON-only inhibitor zoldonrasib (RMC-9805)"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0820/2026082000047.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "[PDF] GenFleet Therapeutics (Shanghai) Inc. 勁方醫藥科技(上海)股份有限 ...", "date": null, "text": "The board of directors of the Company (the “Board”) is pleased to announce the initiation of a three-arm, parallel-controlled phase II study evaluating two novel combination regimens versus monotherapy of GFH375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor, among pretreated KRAS G12D-mutant patients with locally advanced unresectable or metastatic pancreatic cancer. ... GFH375/VS-7375 received China’s first Breakthrough Therapy Designation for monotherapy in KRAS G12D-mutant metastatic pancreatic cancer in February 2026, following Fast Track designation from the U.S. FDA for KRAS G12D-mutant metastatic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) granted in 2025. ... GFH375 is the world’s first oral KRAS G12D inhibitor that advanced into a registrational phase III monotherapy trial for metastatic pancreatic cancer. ... GFH375 is an orally active, potent, highly selective small-molecule KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor designed to bind to the protein of KRAS G12D non-covalently, thereby disrupting the activation of downstream pathways and effectively inhibiting tumor cell proliferation."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/pdf/AACR-VS7375-Poster-Presentation.pdf", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "VS-7375 (GFH375): An oral, selective KRAS G12D (ON/ ...", "date": null, "text": "VS-7375 (GFH375) is an oral KRAS G12D-selective small molecule inhibitor (G12Di) that binds KRAS G12D in both the active (ON; GTP-bound) and inactive (OFF; GDP-bound) states (Figure 1) (2). ... Furthermore, we have shown that oral administration of VS-7375 inhibits tumor growth in a dose-dependent manner in KRAS G12D-mutant human cancer models in vivo including pancreatic and colorectal cancers (Figure 2) (2). VS-7375 monotherapy is currently in phase 2 clinical evaluation (NCT06500676). ... • VS-7375 induces strong tumor regressions (>30% reduction) in contrast to G12D ON (RMC-9805) and pan-RAS ON (RMC-6236) inhibitors in a KRAS G12D-mutant xenograft model • VS-7375 monotherapy induces tumor regressions in multiple KRAS G12D-mutant cancer models and shows strong combinatorial anti-tumor efficacy with cetuximab • These results support the ongoing clinical evaluation of VS-7375 for treatment of patients with KRAS G12D-mutant cancers (NCT06500676)"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://investor.verastem.com/node/15761/pdf", "domain": "investor.verastem.com", "title": "[PDF] Verastem Oncology Announces Late Breaking and Regular ...", "date": null, "text": "Preclinical research with VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5 inhibitors demonstrated strong durable tumor regressions in KRAS G12D pancreatic cancer models In preclinical pancreatic, lung, and colorectal cancer models, VS-7375 (dual ON/OFF inhibitor) showed deeper and more sustained tumor regression compared to ON-only G12D or pan-RAS inhibitors ... Title: Strong durable tumor regressions with the KRAS G12D ON/OFF inhibitor VS-7375 in combination with PRMT5 inhibition in MTAP-deleted/KRAS G12D-mutant pancreatic cancer ... Title: VS-7375: An oral, selective KRAS G12D dual ON/OFF inhibitor with potent anti-tumor activity as a single agent and in combination with other ... In the KP4 KRAS G12D pancreatic cancer model, VS-7375 (50 mg/kg twice daily), zoldonrasib (100 mg/kg once daily) and daraxonrasib (25 mg/kg once daily), produced similar initial tumor regression through day nine. By approximately day 20, however, zoldonrasib and daraxonrasib lost anti-tumor activity with tumor outgrowth, (mean tumor volume >850 mm by day 30) whereas VS-7375 maintained sustained tumor regression (mean tumor volume ~80 mm by day 30), consistent with pharmacodynamic analyses showing durable pathway inhibition only with VS-7375. VS-7375 also showed deeper tumor regression compared to these RAS ON-only inhibitors in KRAS G12D-mutated lung and colorectal xenograft models. ... Verastem initiated VS-7375-101, an international Phase 1/2 clinical trial, in June of 2025 in the U.S., that is evaluating the safety and efficacy of VS-7375 in patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors. In July 2025, U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted Fast Track Designation (FTD) to VS-7375 for the first-line treatment of patients with KRAS G12D-mutated locally advanced or metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas (PDAC) and for the treatment of patients with KRAS G12D-mutated locally advanced or metastatic PDAC who have received at least one prior line of standard systemic therapy. ... Verastem selected VS-7375 (also known as GFH375), an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor, as its lead program in December 2023 and the license for VS-7375 that was exercised in January 2025 is the first one from this"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1526119/000110465925100537/tm2529024d1_ex99-1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "title": "Untitled", "date": null, "text": "*GFH375 resulted in a 41% ORR for patients with heavily pre-treated pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (68% had received 2 or more prior lines of therapy) at the 600 mg daily dose* ... *91.5% of patients experienced a reduction in tumors and a disease control rate of 96.7%; nearly half the patients evaluated remain on treatment* ... announced positive, updated efficacy and safety data from partner GenFleet Therapeutics’ Phase 1/2 monotherapy study in China of GFH375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor (VS-7375 outside of China) for patients with KRAS G12D mutant advanced pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC). Among 59 heavily pre-treated patients with advanced disease, who received two or more prior lines of therapy, an overall response rate (ORR) of 41% was achieved at the monotherapy recommended Phase 2 dose (RP2D) of 600 mg daily (QD). ... GenFleet reported that 66 patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant PDAC were treated with 600 mg QD of GFH375 monotherapy. ... As of the data cutoff of September 27, 2025, 59 efficacy-evaluable patients had at least one post-treatment tumor assessment and achieved an ORR of 40.7% (24/59) (confirmed and unconfirmed) and a disease control rate (DCR) of 96.7% (57/59) with the majority of patients (91.5%) experiencing a reduction in target lesions. Overall Survival (OS) observed at month four was 92.2%. The median OS was not reached as of the data cutoff, with a median follow-up time of 5.65 months. The median progression-free survival (PFS) was 5.52 months with a median follow-up time of 5.65 months and a 4-month PFS rate of 78.2%. At evaluation, 31 (47%) of patients were still on treatment with the longest duration of treatment eclipsing one year (367 days). ... Of the 66 patients in the safety population, four patients (6.1%) had a dose reduction and two patients (3%) discontinued due to TRAEs. No TRAE-related deaths were reported. ... The Phase 1/2a study will be conducted in the U.S., with the potential to expand globally, and will evaluate the safety and efficacy of VS-7375 in patients with advanced KRAS G12D mutant solid tumors."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.verastem.com/our-science/pipeline/", "domain": "verastem.com", "title": "Pipeline | Verastem, Inc.", "date": "2026-05-06", "text": "- **VS-7375, an oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor**: we are evaluating VS-7375 as monotherapy and in combinations for KRAS G12D-mutated PDAC, non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), colorectal cancer (CRC) and other solid tumors. ... |## KRAS G12D-mutated solid tumors (PDAC, NSCLC, CRC) **Regimen**: dose escalation and expansion trial of VS-7375 monotherapy + various combinations**Study Name**: TARGET-D 101|TARGET-D 101|Discovery|Preclinical|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|FDA Approved| |## KRAS G12D-mutated 2L metastatic PDAC **Regimen**: registration-directed trial of VS-7375 monotherapy + EGFR combination**Study Name**: TARGET-D 201|TARGET-D 201|Discovery|Preclinical|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|FDA Approved| ... VS-7375 (known as GFH375 in China), a potential best-in-class, potent and selective oral KRAS G12D (ON/OFF) inhibitor, has been identified as the lead discovery program. GenFleet’s IND for GFH375 (VS-7375) was approved in June 2024, and they are currently conducting Phase 1/2 and Phase 3 clinical trials in China."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10513, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 929}, "timing": {"search_ms": 542.1, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 11229.1, "total_ms": 11771.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 11229.1, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "The in vivo monotherapy TGI magnitude and whether VS-7375 caused tumour regression or tumour shrinkage in KRAS G12D-mutated/MTAP-deleted PDAC animal models.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["VS-7375 KP4 MTAP-deleted monotherapy tumor regression TGI"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q019", "objective": "Innovent Biologics Inc. descriptions of IBI-3001's advantage over existing treatments for Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma in investor materials, earnings calls, investor day presentations, and press releases", "anchors": ["IBI-3001"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 18602, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.takeda.com/newsroom/newsreleases/2025/innovent/", "domain": "takeda.com", "title": "Takeda Announces Oncology Partnership with Innovent", "date": "2025-10-21", "text": "**OSAKA, Japan and CAMBRIDGE, Massachusetts, October 21, 2025** – Takeda (TSE:4502/NYSE:TAK) today announced that it has entered into a license and collaboration agreement with Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) for the development, manufacturing and commercialization of two late-stage oncology medicines, IBI363 and IBI343, worldwide outside of Greater China. ... Takeda will also receive an exclusive option to license global rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine. ... IBI3001 is a potential first-in-class bispecific ADC designed to target both EGFR and B7H3. It is being studied in an ongoing Phase 1 clinical trial in patients with locally advanced or metastatic solid tumors in the U.S., China and Australia. As part of the agreement, Innovent will be solely responsible for clinical development of IBI3001 prior to potential exercise of the option to license. Should Takeda exercise the option, Takeda will develop, manufacture and commercialize IBI3001 worldwide, outside of Greater China. ... IBI3001 is a potential first-in-class bispecific antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) that comprises a bispecific antibody targeting EGFR and B7H3 antigens and an exatecan payload. ... Takeda will also receive an exclusive option to license global rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine. ... It is being studied in an ongoing Phase 1 clinical trial in patients with locally advanced or metastatic solid tumors in the U.S., China and Australia."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/InvestorsAndMedia/PressReleaseDetail?key=557", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "title": "Press Release", "date": null, "text": "Press Release Innovent Biologics Announces Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda to Bring Innovent’s Next Gen IO Backbone Therapy and ADC Molecules to the Global Market ... - Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option for the rights for IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC) outside Greater China. ... - Innovent to host conference calls and webcasts at 9:00 a.m. HKT (Chinese session) and 9:00 p.m. HKT (English session) on Wednesday, October 22, 2025. SAN FRANCISCO, U.S. and SUZHOU, China, October 22, 2025 — Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) announced a strategic global collaboration with Takeda (TSE:4502, NYSE:TAK) to advance next-generation IO and ADC cancer therapies, with the goal of developing potentially transformative cancer treatments to benefit patients worldwide. ... IBI343, a potentially best-in-class CLDN18.2 ADC currently in Phase 3 clinical stage; and IBI3001, a first-in-class EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC currently in Phase 1 clinical stage. ... IBI363 is now in registrational clinical development, including a global Phase 3 study in second line sqNSCLC that is expected to begin in the coming months; the China NMPA has granted Breakthrough Designation (BTD) and U.S. FDA has granted Fast Track Designation (FTD) for this indication.* ... Building on its already robust clinical data of over 1,200 treated patients, IBI363 will be initially developed globally in non-small cell lung cancer (“NSCLC”) and colorectal cancer (“CRC”), including in the first-line settings. ... It has also received Fast Track Designation from the U.S. FDA for the treatment of advanced unresectable or metastatic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) that has relapsed and/or is refractory to one prior line of therapy.* ... *IBI3001, currently in a Phase 1 clinical trial, is a first-in-class bispecific ADC targeting B7-H3 and EGFR. It combines multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, including enhanced EGFR blockade, receptor-mediated internalization, and strong ADC-mediated cytotoxicity, with a high safety margin demonstrated in preclinical models.* Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing, and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China. If exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to mid-teens. ... **Innovent will host conference calls and webcasts at 9:00 a.m. HKT (Chinese session) and 9:00 p.m. HKT (English session) on Wednesday, October 22, 2025.** *Details of the conference call dial-in and the webcast link will be provided on the company website at * https://investor.innoventbio.com/en/investors/webcasts-and-presentations/*. A replay will also be available on the website shortly after the event.*"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/innovent-to-present-new-clinical-data-of-next-gen-io-and-adc-pipeline-at-the-2026-esmo-annual-meeting-302887455.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "title": "Innovent to Present New Clinical Data of Next-gen IO and ...", "date": "2026-09-23", "text": "Sep 23, 2026, 04:48 ET SAN FRANCISCO and SUZHOU, China, Sept. 23, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Innovent Biologics, Inc. (\"Innovent\") (HKEX: 01801), a world-class biopharmaceutical company that develops, manufactures and commercializes high quality medicines for the treatment of oncology, cardiovascular and metabolic, autoimmune, ophthalmology and other major diseases, announces that clinical data for its IBI363/TAK-928 (PD ... In addition, we will unveil global multicenter Phase 1 data for the first-in-class IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC) for the first time, as well as Phase 2 data for the potentially best-in-class IBI354 (HER2 ADC) in first-line breast cancer. ... Presentation Title: IBI363 (TAK-928) plus chemotherapy as first line (1L) treatment for advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC): Update of a phase 1b study** ... **4.** **Presentation Title: IBI363 (TAK-928) plus bevacizumab (Bev) vs docetaxel (DTX) in patients (pts) with locally advanced or metastatic immunotherapy (IO)-resistant lung adenocarcinoma (LUAC) without actionable genomic alterations: a randomized phase Ib study** ... **5.** **Presentation Title: Safety and efficacy of IBI3001, an anti-EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC, in patients (pts) with advanced solid tumors: Results of a multi-regional phase 1 study** Final Publication Number: 1001O Session Type: Proffered Paper Session Title: Developmental Therapeutics Session Date & Time: October 24, 2026 14:45 PM - 16:15 PM CET Room: Cordoba Auditorium - Hall 4"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=9b437310-e3ab-4b3f-93e2-3f9bb486584c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "IBI3001 / Innovent Biologics, Takeda, Fortvita ...", "date": null, "text": "## Safety and efficacy of IBI3001, an anti-EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC, in patients (pts) with advanced solid tumors: Results of a multi-regional phase 1 study (ESMO 2026) - No abstract available ... ## Innovent Biologics Announces Closing of Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda for Next-Generation IO and ADC Therapies (PRNewswire) - \"Takeda will pay Innovent an upfront payment of US$1.2 billion, including a US$100 million equity investment in Innovent through new share issuance at premium, i.e., HK$112.56 per share. Furthermore, Innovent is eligible for development and sales milestone payments for IBI363, IBI343, and IBI3001 (if option exercised) totaling up to approximately $10.2 billion, for a total deal value of up to $11.4 billion. Innovent is also eligible to receive potential royalty payments for each molecule outside Greater China, except with respect to IBI363 in the U.S....\" ... ## IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC): Option to Global License for Development and Commercialization (PRNewswire) - \"Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing, and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China. If exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to mid-teens.\" ... ## IBI3001 in Participants With Unresectable, Locally Advanced or Metastatic Solid Tumors (clinicaltrials.gov) - P1 | N=250 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Innovent Biologics (Suzhou) Co. Ltd. | Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting | Phase classification: P1/2 ➔ P1 | N=180 ➔ 250 | Trial completion date: May 2027 ➔ Dec 2027 | Initiation date: May 2024 ➔ Dec 2024 | Trial primary completion date: May 2026 ➔ Dec 2026 ... ## Innovent Biologics’ new dual-antibody ADC cancer drug approved for clinical trials in China [Google translation] (vbdata.cn) - \"Innovent Biologics' new bispecific ADC drug IBI3001 has been approved for clinical trials in China. It targets B7-H3 and EGFR, has multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, shows good efficacy and safety, and has been conducting a Phase 1/2 study in Australia.\" ... ## Innovent to Present Preclinical Data of Multiple Novel Molecules at the 2024 AACR Annual Meeting (PRNewswire) - \"IBI3001 has multiple anti-tumor mechanisms of action: (1) enhanced EGFR signaling blockade; (2) EGFR- and B7-H3-aided payload internalization and cytotoxicity; and (3) potent bystander killing effects of ADC. The optimized B7-H3 arm not only enhances the EGFR signaling blockade but also reduces EGFR on-target toxicities. IBI3001 showed strong anti-tumor efficacy in vitro and in vivo across multiple solid tumors and is well tolerated with the therapeutic index at 40.\""}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/InvestorsAndMedia/PressReleaseDetail?key=566", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "title": "Press Release", "date": null, "text": "Press Release Innovent Biologics Announces Closing of Global Strategic Partnership with Takeda for Next-Generation IO and ADC Therapies ... SAN FRANCISCO and SUZHOU, China, Dec. 5, 2025 -- Innovent Biologics (HKEX: 01801) today announced that the global strategic collaboration with Takeda (TSE: 4502, NYSE: TAK) has closed and become effective following the satisfaction of all closing conditions. The collaboration, initially announced on October 22, 2025, aims to accelerate the global development and commercialization of Innovent's next-generation immuno-oncology (IO) and antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) therapies, including the global partnership on IBI363 (PD-1/IL-2^α-bias^) and IBI343 (CLDN18.2 ADC), and an option for an early-stage program IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC). ... - Additionally, Takeda receives an exclusive option to license global rights for IBI3001, a first-in-class EGFR/B7H3 bispecific ADC in Phase 1 stage, outside Greater China. Takeda will pay Innovent an upfront payment of US$1.2 billion, including a US$100 million equity investment in Innovent through new share issuance at premium, i.e., HK$112.56 per share. Furthermore, Innovent is eligible for development and sales milestone payments for IBI363, IBI343, and IBI3001 (if option exercised) totaling up to approximately $10.2 billion, for a total deal value of up to $11.4 billion."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://en.innoventbio.com/", "domain": "en.innoventbio.com", "title": "Innovent", "date": null, "text": "Innovent Announces Expansion of Global Phase 3 MarsLight-11 Study of IBI363/TAK-928 (Alpha-biased IL-2/PD-1 Bispecific Fusion Protein) in Immunotherapy-Resistant NSCLC to Include Patients with Non-Squamous NSCLC"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/65944053/innovent-bio-1801-hk-six-innovative-drugs-from-innovent-bio", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "title": "Innovent Bio (1801.HK): Innovent Bio's 6 innovative drugs have positive TCE data for new medical insurance", "date": "2025-12-09", "text": "Aoyixin (liotinib tablets, EGFR TKI). This time, it has been added to the new national medical insurance drug catalogue: 1. First-line treatment for adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) exon 19 deficiency or exon 21 (L858R) replacement mutation; 2. Adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC who have previously been treated with EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKI) and have been tested to have positive EGFR T790M mutations. Liotinib introduces a unique naphthylamine group structure, which is more lipophilic, which ensures that the drug can effectively pass through the blood-brain barrier, thereby significantly reducing the risk of disease progression in patients with brain metastases and the risk of progression of intracranial disease, bringing a more effective choice for patients with EGFR mutations. ... 5. Dabrol (tarretinib adipate capsules, ROS1 TKI). This time, it was added to the new national medical insurance drug catalogue: adult patients with locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) positive for ROS1. ... On December 8, Innovent Bio first announced preliminary data from the first human trial of its self-developed anti-GPRC5D/BCMA/CD3 tri-specific antibody IBI3003 for patients with recurrent or refractory multiple myeloma (R/R MM) in the form of an oral report at the 2025 American Society of Hematology Annual Meeting (ASH). IBI3003 has shown good tolerability and controllable safety characteristics. Despite the current short follow-up time, IBI3003 has shown encouraging initial efficacy signals, particularly in high-risk patients with extramedullary lesions or previous single or dual targeted treatments against BCMA and GPRC5D. ... IBI3003 has shown encouraging efficacy and depth of relief at doses of ≥120ug/kg. ① Encouraging outcomes were observed during median follow-up of 3.25 months: patients treated at least 120 μg/kg (n=24) had an ORR of 83.3%, including 4 CRs, 7 VGPR, and 9 PR cases. ... IBI3003 has controlled safety in R/R MM patients. Hematologic toxicity is the most common grade ≥3 TEA Es. It mainly occurs during the dose escalation phase, and the extent is controllable and recoverable. The incidence of CRS and immune-effector cell-related neurotoxicity syndrome (ICA NS) was 64.1% and 6.1%, respectively, both grade 1-2, and recovered after treatment; prophylactic use of tocilizumab can reduce the risk of CRS."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2025/1022/2025102200031.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "信達生物製藥 INNOVENT BIOLOGICS, INC. - hkexnews.hk", "date": null, "text": "The Board is pleased to announce that on October 22, 2025 (Hong Kong time), Innovent and Takeda (through its wholly-owned subsidiary, Takeda Pharmaceuticals International AG) have established a global strategic collaboration to accelerate the development of Innovent’s next- generation IO and ADC cancer therapies to the global market. The collaboration includes two late-stage investigational medicines IBI363 (PD-1/IL-2α-bias) and IBI343 (CLDN18.2 ADC), as well as an option for an early-stage program IBI3001 (EGFR/B7H3 ADC). ... Additionally, Takeda will receive an exclusive option for the rights outside of Greater China for IBI3001 (EGFR/ B7H3 ADC). ... This collaboration aims to explore and maximize IBI363’s potential as a new IO backbone therapy through aligned co-development plans. Building on its already robust clinical data of over 1,200 treated patients, IBI363 will be initially developed globally in non-small cell lung cancer (“NSCLC”) and colorectal cancer (“CRC”), including in the first-line settings. ... The Company has shown that IBI363, with an IL-2 receptor alpha focused approach, selectively expands tumor-specific CD8+ T cells that increase tumor cell killing efficiency without activating or expanding the toxicity related peripheral T cells, which results in a better safety profile than what is seen with traditional IL-2s. Phase 1b/2 results presented at ASCO 2025 have demonstrated outstanding tumor responses and preliminary survival benefits of IBI363 across immunotherapy-resistant lung cancer, “cold tumors” such as acral and mucosal melanoma, and microsatellite stable (“MSS”) colorectal cancer. IBI363 is now in registrational clinical development, including a global Phase 3 study in second line sqNSCLC that is expected to begin in the coming months; the China NMPA has granted Breakthrough Designation (“BTD”) and the U.S. FDA has granted Fast Track Designation (“FTD”) for this indication. ... Innovent will grant Takeda an exclusive option to license global development, manufacturing and commercialization rights for IBI3001 outside of Greater China. If exercised, Takeda will pay Innovent an exercise fee, potential milestone payments, and tiered royalties on net sales up to the mid-teens. ... IBI3001, currently in a Phase 1 clinical trial, is the Company’s first-in-class bispecific ADC targeting B7-H3 and EGFR. It combines multiple anti-tumor mechanisms, including enhanced EGFR blockade, receptor-mediated internalization, and strong ADC-mediated cytotoxicity, with a high safety margin demonstrated in preclinical models."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.takeda.com/newsroom/newsreleases/2025/closing-partnership-innovent/", "domain": "takeda.com", "title": "Takeda's Strategic Partnership with Innovent Closes", "date": "2025-12-04", "text": "As part of the agreement, Takeda also has an exclusive option to license global rights to IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine, outside of Greater China. ... As part of the agreement, Takeda also has an exclusive option to license global rights to IBI3001, an early-stage investigational medicine, outside of Greater China. Takeda will pay Innovent US$1.2 billion upfront per the terms of the agreement before the end of Takeda’s fiscal year, ending March 31, 2026."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pro.boehringer-ingelheim.com/us/inoncology/tumor-types/hnscc", "domain": "pro.boehringer-ingelheim.com", "title": "Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "date": "2022-04-22", "text": "For localized HNSCC, the standard of care primarily consists of a surgical approach followed by radiation and adjuvant chemotherapy.^1,4 ^This is often the procedure recommended for tumors of the oral cavity and advanced tumors of the larynx and hypopharynx when functional preservation is not feasible.^1 ^In patients with localized disease with no clinical node involvement, or with only a single node involved, cure rates of over 80% have been achieved by surgery alone.^4^ Despite the high cure rate for standard of care surgery, about 17-31% of these patients eventually undergo further radiation therapy and/or adjuvant chemotherapy due to disease recurrence.^1^ For high-risk patients with post-surgical extra-nodal extension, close surgical margins, perineural invasion, or poor candidates for surgical management, chemoradiotherapy with single-agent cisplatin is recommended. ^1,4 ^For recurrent, unresectable, or metastatic disease with no surgical or radiotherapy options, the preferred first-line regimens include pembrolizumab for tumors that express PD-L1 with combined positive score ≥1, and pembrolizumab with platinum (cisplatin or carboplatin) and 5-fluorouracil."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11217, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 598}, "timing": {"search_ms": 530.8, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 7572.4, "total_ms": 8103.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 7572.4, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "IBI3001’s development or use in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma; Innovent’s claims of advantage over existing treatments for that disease, including any named comparators and supporting evidence.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["IBI3001 head and neck squamous cell carcinoma", "IBI3001 HNSCC investor presentation", "IBI3001 HNSCC standard of care comparison", "IBI3001 HNSCC clinical trial results"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q017", "objective": "SW-682 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["SW-682"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 13121, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://springworkstx.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Schirmer_TEAD_Head-Neck-SCC_ENA-2024.pdf", "domain": "springworkstx.com", "title": "Genigraphics Research Poster Template 42x72", "date": null, "text": "Rational combination of pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 and MEK inhibitor mirdametinib in head and neck squamous cell carcinomas leads to synergistic response ... ▪The pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 modulates tumor growth in HNSCC models harboring aberrant Hippo pathway signaling both in vitro and in vivo. ▪Addition of SW-682 synergized with the anti-proliferative effects of mirdametinib in vitro in HNSCC models ▪Combination with SW-682 enhanced efficacy and response durability of MEK targeted therapies in preclinical HNSCC models, supporting investigation of SW-682 combinations in clinical studies. ... SW-682 decreases colony formation in HNSCC FAT1 loss models • Treatment with SW-682 results in a dose dependent decrease in CAL-33 and SCC-25 colony formation after 14 days. Combination of mirdametinib and SW-682 synergized to reduce proliferation of HNSCC in vitro ... Combination of mirdametinib and SW-682 further reduced colony formation when combined in HNSCC • Given the evidence of cooperative dependence between MAPK and Hippo pathways and the variable monotherapy response, in vitro combination effects of SW-682 with mirdametinib were evaluated. • Higher synergy was observed in the SCC-25 model, with an average Bliss score of 24.4. CAL-33 showed a reduced Bliss score of 10.7. • CAL-33 and SCC-25 cell lines harboring biallelic loss of function ... with 100 nM SW-682 • Addition of SW-682 to mirdametinib reduced clonogenic capacity of SCC-25 and CAL-33 cells. Combination of SW-682 and mirdametinib demonstrated increased anti-tumor activity in CAL-33 xenograft model A. Combination of SW-682 and mirdametinib significantly increased tumor growth inhibition (%TGI) over monotherapy in the CAL-33 model. B. On day 47 post-treatment, SW-682 plus mirdametinib resulted in significantly smaller tumor volumes compared to mirdametinib alone. This study is ongoing. ... Gr. 2: 44% Gr. 4: 80% Gr. 6: 93%*"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=d78df0cf-33d8-4282-974c-3719ae487f81", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "SW-682 / EMD Serono", "date": null, "text": "## Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD signaling as a therapeutic approach in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma. (PubMed, Cancer Lett) - \"Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC. A HNSCC-specific TEADi target gene set was defined from RNA-seq data, which is highly expressed in HNSCC tissues and predicts poor prognosis of HPV-negative HNSCC patients. Our results underscore that YAP/TAZ-TEAD-mediated growth-promoting programs represent a vulnerability in HPV-negative HNSCC, thus providing a pre-clinical rationale for the future evaluation of YAP/TAZ-TEAD targeting strategies as a therapeutic approach for HPV-negative HNSCC patients.\" ... ## Rational combination of pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 and MEK inhibitor mirdametinib in head and neck squamous cell carcinomas leads to synergistic response (EORTC-NCI-AACR 2024) - \"The pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 modulates tumor growth in HNSCC models harboring aberrant Hippo pathway signaling both in vitro and in vivo. The combination of SW-682 with mirdametinib resulted in enhanced anti-tumor responses in HNSCC models. These observations may provide a rational combination approach for clinical studies.\" ... ## SW-682: A novel TEAD inhibitor for the treatment of cancers bearing mutations in the Hippo signaling pathway (AACR 2023) - \"Moreover, to identify new indications that may benefit from TEAD inhibition, we screened patient-derived 3D organoid tumor cells and matching patient-derived xenograft models that have been molecularly profiled. In summary, SW-682 is a potent and selective investigational TEAD inhibitor which demonstrated anti-tumor effects in models harboring aberrant expression of the Hippo pathway, suggesting therapeutic potential in multiple Hippo-mutant solid tumors.\""}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://springworkstx.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TEAD-AACR-2023-Poster_FINAL.pdf", "domain": "springworkstx.com", "title": "[PDF] SW-682: a novel TEAD inhibitor for the treatment of cancers bearing ...", "date": null, "text": "In vivo, daily oral administration of SW-682 in tumor-bearing mice resulted in tumor regression in Hippo-mutant mesothelioma models and caused decreased expression of the TEAD- dependent genes CCN1 and CCN2 and a YAP gene signature4, as measured by RNA-seq analysis. ... SW-682 is efficacious in vivo in two mesothelioma models and caused dose- dependent tumor regression in xenograft model ... opportunities guided by a biomarker-driven development approach ... Tumor regressions observed with SW-682 at well-tolerated daily oral doses with no effects on body weight"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12044704/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD signaling as a therapeutic approach in ...", "date": "2025-01-16", "text": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC. By the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors. HNSCC cells with genetic alterations in *FAT1* were more sensitive to TEADi compared to *FAT1-* wild type cells. Mechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition. ... Finally, we provide pre-clinical evidence for the efficacy of novel smTEADi, SW-682, in HNSCC. ... Strikingly, pTEADi significantly suppressed tumor growth of Cal33 and Cal27, whereas failed to exhibit growth inhibition in HN12 that carries wild type *FAT1* (Figs. 2I–2L; Supplementary Figs. S2E and S2F), suggesting that these *FAT1*-mutant HNSCC cells are more sensitive to TEAD inhibition. ... We then examined the antitumor activities of SW-682, the investigational smTEADi being tested clinically, in Cal33, Cal27 and HN12 HNSCC xenograft models. Tumor-bearing NSG mice were treated once daily with SW-682 (30mg/kg) or vehicle. Notably, we observed significant growth inhibition in Cal33 and Cal27 tumors (Figs. 3F and 3G), but more moderate effects in HN12, similar to the observations in the pTEADi-expressing HN12 xenograft models (Figs. 3H and 3I). We did not observe a significant difference in body weight between vehicle- and SW-682-treated groups, suggesting minimal toxicity *in vivo* (Supplementary Figs. S3C and S3D). ... Of interest, the novel pan-TEAD inhibitor SW-682 showed a higher antitumor effect in *FAT1-* mutant HNSCC xenograft models than *FAT1*-wild type counterparts, which was supported by the results with pTEADi that closely phenocopied the TEAD pharmacological blockade. ... The emerging picture from our studies is that both a smTEADi, SW-682, and pTEADi promote the transcriptional reprogramming of HNSCC cells, promoting concomitantly cell cycle arrest and terminal differentiation, thereby providing a novel mechanistic framework for the potential antitumor activity of TEADi."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12672301/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Targeting TEAD in cancer - PMC - NIH", "date": "2025-11-19", "text": "In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), use of SW-682 to block YAP/TAZ-TEAD binding led to an antitumor response in FAT1-mutant HNSCC xenograft models (85). ... SW-682 (NCT06251310) is a pan-TEAD small molecule inhibitor which blocks palmitoylation of TEAD to inhibit all TEAD-dependent transcription (161). It was reported that SW-682 inhibited proliferation of Hippo-mutant mesothelioma cells *in vitro* and tumor regression in murine mesothelioma models. Like other TEAD inhibitors, SW-682 synergized with EGFR and KRAS^G12C^ inhibitors for NSCLC PDX models *in vivo (* 161). SW-682 was also shown to decrease cellular proliferation and downregulated YAP/TAZ-TEAD target genes in HNSCC cell lines Cal27 and Cal33 and in tumor xenograft models (85). It was also reported that SW-682 has good oral bioavailability in mice and did not lead to weight loss during treatment. However, there were no reports on the potential effects of SW-682 on renal function and urine biomarkers which have been reported with use of other TEAD inhibitors which could indicate a better side effect profile clinically."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/375454a460c05815e86c5f64e4da29bb", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "SW-682 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "date": null, "text": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC. By the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors. HNSCC cells with genetic alterations in FAT1 were more sensitive to TEADi compared to FAT1-wild type cells. Mechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition. ... |Advanced Malignant Solid Neoplasm|Phase 1|United States|Springworks Therapeutics, Inc.|31 Jul 2024| ... |Solid tumor|IND Application|United States|SpringWorks Therapeutics LLC|09 Jan 2024| ... |Squamous Cell Carcinoma of Head and Neck|Preclinical|United States|Springworks Therapeutics, Inc.|29 Apr 2025|"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "title": "CCDI Hub", "date": null, "text": "This system is provided for Government-authorized use only. ... At any time, and for any lawful Government purpose, the government may monitor, intercept, and search and seize any communication or data transiting or stored on this system. - Any communication or data transiting or stored on this system may be disclosed or used for any lawful Government purpose."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/130c14f677be7b74c390c1511f90bc6e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Springworks Therapeutics, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "date": null, "text": "Here, we provide the pre-clinical evidence for the antitumor activity of novel smTEADi, SW-682 in HPV-negative HNSCC. By the use of multiple complementary experimental approaches, including siRNA knockdown, expression of a genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi), and SW-682, we revealed that disruption of YAP/TAZ-TEAD interaction suppresses YAP/TAZ-TEAD-dependent target gene transcription and growth of HNSCC tumors. HNSCC cells with genetic alterations in FAT1 were more sensitive to TEADi compared to FAT1-wild type cells. Mechanistically, TEADi suppressed cell cycle progression and promoted the expression of terminal differentiation gene programs, resulting in tumor growth inhibition. ... STRIKE-001 (NCT07103018) is a multi-center, open-label dose escalation evaluating KTX-2001 as a monotherapy (Part A) and in combination with darolutamide (Part B). Part A is designed to evaluate the safety, tolerability, maximum tolerated dose, and recommended Phase 2 dose(s) of KTX-2001 monotherapy. ... |SW-682 ( TEAD )|Advanced Malignant Solid Neoplasm More|Phase 1|"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE277859", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "GEO Accession viewer", "date": "2024-10-01", "text": "Targeting YAP/TAZ-TEAD as a precision therapy approach in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma ... In this regard, HPV-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) harbor multiple genetic alterations that promote YAP/TAZ hyperactivation, raising the possibility that HNSCC cells might be dependent on YAP/TAZ-TEAD driven oncogenic transcriptional programs. To test this hypothesis, we examined the antitumor activity of the novel smTEADi, SW-682 and genetically encoded TEAD inhibitor peptide (pTEADi) in Cal33 HPV-negative HNSCC cell line-derived xenograft model. To elucidate the transcriptomic changes upon YAP/TAZ-TEAD inhibition, RNA extracted from xenograft tumors treated with SW-682 or pTEADi, as well as control, was subjected to RNA sequencing."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/12/12/3743", "domain": "mdpi.com", "title": "Patient-Derived Xenograft and Organoid Models for Precision Medicine Targeting of the Tumour Microenvironment in Head and Neck Cancer", "date": "2020-12-12", "text": "A matched PDX model and cell line developed from the resistant tumour remained sensitive to gefitinib, so the patient was switched to gefitinib monotherapy and demonstrated remarkable regression within six weeks [88]. A second patient that progressed on cytotoxic chemotherapy was switched to erlotinib after the patient’s PDX showed a robust response, leading to tumour regression in the patient [88]."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9344, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 1216}, "timing": {"search_ms": 553.6, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 14027.5, "total_ms": 14581.1}, "luna_wall_ms": 14027.5, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "In HPV-negative HNSCC xenograft models, mice received SW-682 monotherapy at 30 mg/kg once daily or vehicle. Tumor growth inhibition was significant in Cal33 and Cal27 tumors and more moderate in HN12 tumors. SW-682 showed greater antitumor activity in FAT1-mutant than in FAT1-wild-type HNSCC xenograft models. [E4][E5]", "confident_score": 0.8, "missing": "Quantitative TGI values and evidence of actual tumor regression or shrinkage in the HNSCC animal models.", "citations": ["E4", "E5"], "next_queries": ["SW-682 HNSCC xenograft Cal33 Cal27 TGI percentage", "SW-682 HNSCC xenograft tumor regression shrinkage"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 319, "confident_score": 0.8, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 2, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 2, "cited_domains": ["pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 10, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 2, "next_queries_subject_first": 2}}
{"row_id": "q018", "objective": "SPR2015 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft CDX, patient-derived xenograft PDX, syngeneic, orthotopic, or genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["SPR2015"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15107, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260928125137/en/Merck-Enters-into-Exclusive-Global-License-Agreement-with-SciBrunch-Therapeutics-for-SPR2015-an-Investigational-Oral-KRAS-G12D-ON-Inhibitor", "domain": "businesswire.com", "title": "Merck Enters into Exclusive Global License Agreement ...", "date": "2026-09-28", "text": "SPR2015 is a potent and selective novel investigational preclinical molecular glue KRAS G12D (ON) inhibitor that has demonstrated nanomolar antiproliferative activities in various KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines while maintaining good selectivity over KRAS wildtype cells. ... In addition, SPR2015 has demonstrated compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX/PDX) models, as reported in preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/news/msd-takes-400m-bite-scibrunchs-kras-drug", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "title": "MSD takes a $400m bite of SciBrunch's KRAS drug", "date": "2026-10-02", "text": "The company reported studies at this year's American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) congress which showed antitumour efficacy for SPR2015 as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models. ... KRAS G12D mutations are found in 37% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cancers, 13% of colorectal cancer cases, and 4% of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) cases."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://2024.sci-hub.se/4365/612c5cc4af0de1f0fbfa4f70d2e69969/ponz-sarvise2015.pdf", "domain": "2024.sci-hub.se", "title": "Mouse Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "date": null, "text": "�New genetically engineered mouse models are needed to address the genetic heteroge- neity seen in human patients. ... Transplantation of human cancer cell lines or primary human tumors into a host mouse to create a xenograft was one of the first models developed to study PDAC. ... Xenografts can be derived either from established cell lines or directly from human tumors. Cells or tumor tissue can be implanted subcutaneously (heterotopically) or into the mouse pancreas (orthotopically). Each approach has certain advantages and disadvantages. ... Patient derived xenografts (PDXs) are generated by transplantation of PDAC tumor tissue directly into mice, either subcutaneously or in the subrenal capsule. PDX tumors may reflect more of the genetic heterogeneity and stromal abundance present in the original tumor.11 Despite serial passaging in mice, PDX tumors have been shown to maintain some of these features, as well as responses to cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agents.12,13 Like standard xenografts, the PDX approach can be used to generate large numbers of mice, serving as a platform upon which therapeutic candidates can be screened. ... GENETICALLY ENGINEERED MOUSE MODELS The genetically engineered mouse model (GEMM) was developed to address many of the key weaknesses of the xenograft approach. The GEMM attempts to recapitulate the PDAC phenotype by introducing into the mouse pancreas the specific genetic mutations present in human PDAC. The most common of these are activating mutations in KRAS, followed by inactivating mutations in CDKN2A (P16/INK4A), TP53, and SMAD4.8 Successful engineering of the LSL-KrasG12D and the LSL-KrasG12V-bGeo mouse was a key development that launched the field.16–18 LSL-KrasG12D Model The original GEMM, the LSL-KrasG12D mouse, was generated by targeting expression of the endogenous oncogenic LSL-KrasG12D allele to the developing pancreas through the use of pancreas-specific Cre recombinase alleles.17 Such PDX1-Cre, LSL-KrasG12D, or P481/�Cre, LSL-KrasG12D (KC) GEMMs develop a spectrum of pre- invasive ductal lesions that mirror human pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN), and upon aging, KC mice stochastically develop primary and metastatic PDAC. The KC GEMM develops PanIN with 100% penetrance. The median survival of this model is around 1 year, and not all KC mice develop PDAC. The KC GEMM represents a suitable platform for the study of PanIN biology and the development of diagnostic and therapeutic approaches. ... Trp53R172H/- (KPC) GEMM incorporates inactivation of the TP53 suppressor gene, and consequently, rapidly develops PDA. The median survival is approximately 5 months, and most mice (96%) develop PDAC at the time of death."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.pharmtech.com/view/kras-g12d-inhibitor-s-2-13b-deal-tests-cross-border-tech-transfer", "domain": "pharmtech.com", "title": "KRAS G12D Inhibitor's $2.13B Deal Tests Cross-Border Tech Transfer", "date": "2026-09-28", "text": "- SPR2015 functions as an ON-state molecular glue, showing nanomolar antiproliferative activity in KRAS G12D cell lines with wild-type selectivity and tumor regression in xenograft models. ... SPR2015 is described as a molecular glue inhibitor that engages the ON form of the mutant protein.^1^ It showed nanomolar antiproliferative activity across multiple KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines while maintaining selectivity over wild-type cells, and it demonstrated monotherapy antitumor efficacy in cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.pharmexec.com/view/merck-licenses-preclinical-kras-g12d-inhibitor-scibrunch", "domain": "pharmexec.com", "title": "Merck Licenses Preclinical KRAS G12D Inhibitor from SciBrunch", "date": "2026-09-28", "text": "- Preclinical monotherapy produced compelling tumor regressions across CDX and PDX models, with AACR 2026 data emphasizing activity in colorectal cancer models where prior G12D agents have struggled. ... KRAS G12D is the most common oncogenic RAS mutation found in human tumors, arising from a substitution of glycine with aspartate at position 12, a change that leaves KRAS active and continuously signaling for cell proliferation and survival.^1^ SPR2015 has also shown compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models, according to preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.^2^"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://mdanderson.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/current-methods-in-mouse-models-of-pancreatic-cancer-3/", "domain": "mdanderson.elsevierpure.com", "title": "Current methods in mouse models of pancreatic cancer", "date": null, "text": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is the fourth leading cause of cancer death in the Western world. ... In the past several years, there has been an explosion of genetically engineered mouse models (GEMM) and patient-derived xenografts (PDX) that recapitulate both genetic and morphological alterations that lead to the development of PDAC. Both models are increasingly used for characterization and validation of diagnostic and therapeutic strategies. In this chapter we will discuss state-of-the-art models to consider when selecting an appropriate in vivo system to study disease etiology, cell signaling, and drug development. ... |Pages (from-to)|185-211| |Number of pages|27| |Journal|Methods in Molecular Biology| |Volume|1267| |DOIs|- https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4939-2297-0_9| |State|Published - 2015| ... Mazur, P. K., Herner, A., Neff, F., & Siveke, J. T. (2015). Current methods in mouse models of pancreatic cancer. *Methods in Molecular Biology*, *1267*, 185-211."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "title": "CCDI Hub", "date": null, "text": "## Warning ... This system is provided for Government-authorized use only."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.genengnews.com/topics/cancer/merck-scibrunch-launch-up-to-2-13b-collaboration-to-develop-preclinical-kras-inhibiting-molecular-glue/", "domain": "genengnews.com", "title": "Merck, SciBrunch Launch Up-to-$2.13B Collaboration ...", "date": "2026-09-29", "text": "SPR2015 is a preclinical molecular glue, more specifically an oral KRAS G12D (ON) inhibitor that SciBrunch is developing for forms of cancer that include colorectal cancer (CRC), non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)—the most common and aggressive type of pancreatic cancer. ... At the 2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting, a team of SciBrunch researchers that included founder, chairman, and CEO Tao Hu, PhD, presented preclinical data showing SPR2015 to have demonstrated what it termed compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX and PDX) models. “SPR2015 is a highly potent and selective KRAS G12D (on) inhibitor with nanomolar proliferation inhibitory activities in various KRAS G12D cell lines while with good selectivity over KRAS wild type,” Hu and SciBrunch researcher colleagues reported in a poster presentation during AACR 2026. ... In a head-to-head mouse study comparing a single oral once-daily dose at 100 mg/kg of SPR2015 to another unspecified G12D (ON) inhibitor in over 15 CRC CDX or PDX models, SPR2015 showed significantly superior efficacy in the *in vivo* CRC xenograft models, achieving a 64.7% objective response rate (ORR) and 94.1% disease control rate (DCR) as monotherapy. ... In their announcement, Merck and SciBrunch did not disclose a current timeframe for taking SPR2015 into Phase I."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3594661/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Genetically Engineered Mouse Models of Pancreatic Cancer", "date": null, "text": "While different genetic and chemical approaches to generate pancreatic cancer in mice and other rodents date back to the early 1980s^6-8^, it was the generation of the K-ras^LSL.G12D^ mouse^9, 10^ in 2001 that allowed tissue-specific expression of mutant K-ras under physiological control from the endogenous mouse locus. The backbone of this mouse is a targeting construct containing a transcriptional and translational silencing cassette (STOP) flanked by two loxP sites, residing upstream of exon 1 in the K-ras gene. This “LSL cassette” prevents expression of the allele, which also contains an additional G-A transition that yields a glycine to aspartic acid substitution in codon 12, the most commonly mutated residue K-ras in human PDAC. This mutation alters the intrinsic GTPase activity of K-ras,^11-13^ as well as its affinity for GAPs, locking the protein into the inactive, GTP-bound configuration.^14^ Expression of the bacterial recombinase *cre* results in removal of the LSL cassette, expression of mutant K-ras^G12D^ from its endogenous locus, and thereby activation of the Ras pathway. ... ^17^ By crossing PDX-Cre^18, 19^ or p48-Cre^20^mice to K-ras^LSL.G12D^ mice, the authors targeted expression of K-ras^G12D^specifically to the pancreas. Surprisingly, the mice were born with normal pancreatic histology and architecture. ... Aguirre et al. found that homozygous deletion of cdkn2a (which encodes the p16^Ink4a^ and p19^Arf^ tumor suppressors) in the context of K-ras mutation in the pancreas led to the rapid development of highly aggressive and metastatic ductal adenocarcinoma, causing 100% lethality at eleven weeks. ... Similarly, the conditional expression of the tumor-associated p53^R172H^ mutation also accelerated K-ras^G12D^ pancreatic tumorigenesis^21^. While, born with histologically normal pancreata, K-ras^LSL.G12D/+^; p53^R172H/+^; PdxCre mice (now referred to as KPC mice) developed PanIN lesions on an accelerated schedule, and died of PDAC with a median survival of 5.5 months. ... Ijichi and colleagues crossed the K-ras^LSL.G12D^ model with a knockout of the TGF-β2-receptor (Tgfbr2), resulting in rapid onset of PanIn formation and progression to PDAC with a median survival of eight weeks. ... A list of genetic combinations that have been reported to develop PDAC (or premalignancies) ... |^78^Elastase-CreERT;K-ras^G12D^|mPanIN|No|<18 months|Acinar derived mPanINs| ... |^80^Elastase-tTA TRE-Cre;K-ras^G12V^|mPanINs, PDAC|No|18 months|PDAC development in adult mice through pancreatitis, inducible| ... |^18^Pdx1-Cre;K-ras^G12D^|mPanIN to PDAC progression|Yes|>12 months|Slow progression|"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528418/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Genetically engineered mouse models of pancreatic ... - PMC", "date": "2013-02-11", "text": "Over the last decade, several models, primarily based on the genetic activation of resident KRas oncogenes knocked‐in within the endogenous KRas locus have been generated. These models faithfully reproduce the histological lesions that characterize human pancreatic tumors. Decoration of these models with additional mutations, primarily involving tumor suppressor loci known to be also mutated in human PDAC tumors, results in accelerated tumor progression and in the induction of invasive and metastatic malignancies. ... Genetically engineered mouse models of cancer faithfully reproduce the histological changes characteristic of human PDAC tumors. ... These mice do not develop any lesions unless they undergo pancreatitis. ... ## 2. Genetically engineered mouse (GEM) models of PDAC: prenatal models ... The first model to faithfully reproduce the natural history of human PDAC in mice involved expression of a resident (knocked‐in) KRasG12D oncogene in all pancreatic lineages during early embryonic development (Hingorani et al., 2003) (Table 1). ... The resulting strains, designated as Pdx1‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D (from now on KC, according to the nomenclature given in the original publication) and Ptf1a‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D, express the KRasG12D oncogene in all pancreatic lineages from early embryonic development (E8.5 in KC mice and E9.5 in Ptf1a‐Cre; LSL‐KRasG12D animals). ... In addition, these mice display ADM, a benign lesion that precedes the appearance and possibly be a precursor of PanIN lesions (Zhu et al., 2007). PanIN lesions progress with long latencies to PDAC in a percentage of mice (Table 1). Addition of mutations in loci encoding tumor suppressors known to be mutated or inactivated in human PDAC such as INK4A, TP53, LKB1 or SMAD4 accelerate the progression of these PanIN lesions leading to the generation of invasive PDAC with complete penetrance (Table 2). ... List of current GEM models of PDAC. |GEM model|Lesions|Metastasis|Survival|References| |--|--|--|--|--| |Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; Ink4a/Arflox/lox|PanIN and PDAC|Yes|2 m|Aguirre et al., 2003| |Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; p53R172H/+(KPC)|PanIN and PDAC|Yes|5 m|Hingorani et al., 2005| ... |Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; Brca2Tr/Δ11|PanIN and PDAC|NA|NA|Skoulidis et al., 2010| |Pdx1‐Cre; K‐Ras+/LSLG12D; p53R270H/+; Brca2Tr/+|PanIN and PDAC|NA|<5 m|Skoulidis et al., 2010| ... As indicated above, addition of mutations in Ink4a and TP53 tumor suppressors increase the penetrance of PDAC to 100% of the animals and significantly reduce tumor latency, inducing death of all animals by 6–8 month of age (Guerra et al., 2011) (Table 2). ... These GEM models illustrate that the alteration in the TGF‐α and TGF‐β pathways result in generation of cystic neoplasms and PanINs, which progress to PDAC. ... In GEM models of PDAC, pancreatitis appears to be an essential component for tumor development in adult mice. ... GEM models available to date faithfully reproduce the histological changes that lead to the ..."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9971, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 1423}, "timing": {"search_ms": 605.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 14341.5, "total_ms": 14947.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 14341.5, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "**AACR 2026** SPR2015 showed monotherapy antitumor efficacy and tumor regression across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX/PDX) models. In a head-to-head mouse study spanning over 15 colorectal cancer CDX or PDX models, a single oral once-daily dose of 100 mg/kg SPR2015 had significantly superior efficacy to an unspecified KRAS G12D (ON) inhibitor, with a 64.7% objective response rate and 94.1% disease control rate. AACR 2026 data emphasized activity in colorectal cancer models where prior G12D agents had struggled. [E1][E2][E4][E5][E8]", "confident_score": 0.82, "missing": "SPR2015-specific results in syngeneic, orthotopic, and genetically engineered models; animal species and identities or characteristics of the individual CDX/PDX models, including model-by-model results and the allocation of the over 15 models between CDX and PDX; identity of the comparator inhibitor.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E4", "E5", "E8"], "next_queries": ["SPR2015 syngeneic model efficacy", "SPR2015 orthotopic model results", "SPR2015 genetically engineered mouse model", "SPR2015 CDX PDX model identities responses", "SPR2015 AACR 2026 comparator inhibitor"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 572, "confident_score": 0.82, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 5, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 5, "cited_domains": ["businesswire.com", "genengnews.com", "pharmaphorum.com", "pharmexec.com", "pharmtech.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 11, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q026", "objective": "BM-013 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BM-013"], "facet": "catalysts", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 4372, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://biminibiotech.nl/technology-2/", "domain": "biminibiotech.nl", "title": "Portfolio - BIMINI Biotech", "date": "2025-01-01", "text": "Our WASp inhibitor (BM-013) downregulates the WASp concentration and function by enhancing degradation of the protein, thereby inhibiting the proliferation specifically of leukemic cells. Target indications include Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and T-Cell Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL)."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://biminibiotech.nl/category/news/", "domain": "biminibiotech.nl", "title": "News – BIMINI Biotech", "date": "2026-09-18", "text": "maart 18, 2026 Leiden, 18 March 2026 – BIMINI Biotech is pleased to announce that it has been awarded funding through the Health~Holland International Kickstarter Call for Public-Private Partnerships 2025 for a collaborative project focused on advancing first-in-class therapeutics for acute myeloid leukemia (AML)."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "date": "2026-09-24", "text": "venetoclax for newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia|On May 13, 2026, the Food and Drug Administration approved an oral combination of decitabine and cedazuridine tablets (Inqovi, Taiho Oncology, Inc.) with venetoclax for the treatment of newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults 75 years or older, or who have comorbidities that preclude the use of intensive induction chemotherapy.|5/13/2026|"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/b09b26122a623602b4fd5d207414aef3", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Bimini Biotech B.V. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials - Synapse", "date": null, "text": "Pipeline Snapshot as of 16 Apr 2026 No data posted"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://biobase.whitefordresearch.com/companies/bimini-biotech", "domain": "biobase.whitefordresearch.com", "title": "BIMINI Biotech", "date": null, "text": "Its public materials describe the company as advancing lead programs toward first-in-human studies, with current work centered on translational preclinical development and patient-stratification biomarkers. ... The company's lead emphasis is oncology, particularly aggressive hematological cancers including lymphomas, leukemias, mantle cell lymphoma, and acute myeloid leukemia (AML). In 2026, BIMINI announced the MYELO-WASP Eurostars project, a EUR 2.2 million international consortium intended to complete translational preclinical development of proprietary WASp modulators for myeloid and lymphoid leukemias and generate a preclinical package for CTA-enabling studies and first-in-human trials. It also announced a Health~Holland-backed AML-WASP collaboration with a EUR 440,000 budget to optimize small-molecule WASp activators, validate activity in relapse/refractory AML models, and identify predictive biomarkers for patient stratification."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://bimini-biotech.com/", "domain": "bimini-biotech.com", "title": "Bimini Biotech | Longevity Solutions", "date": null, "text": "Email info@bimini-biotech.com LinkedIn linkedin.com/in/fxpellay"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://app.dealroom.co/companies/bimini_biotech", "domain": "app.dealroom.co", "title": "Bimini Biotech company information, funding & investors", "date": null, "text": "BIMINI Biotech's business model revolves around the development and commercialization of its proprietary portfolio of products, including BM-011, a WASp activator, and BM-013, a WASp inhibitor. These products have shown promising results in treating difficult-to-treat non-Hodgkin lymphomas and leukemias, respectively."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://aml-hub.com/drug-updates/1996251866145952164", "domain": "aml-hub.com", "title": "Home | AML Hub", "date": "2025-12-03", "text": "China’s NMPA has approved quizartinib in combination with standard induction and consolidation chemotherapy and as maintenance monotherapy for the treatment of ND FLT3-ITD AML, based on results from the phase III QuANTUM-First trial."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://biomediaproject.com/bmp/story/", "domain": "biomediaproject.com", "title": "Story", "date": null, "text": "Switch browsers to one of the latest available browsers for the full experience! ... Enable JavaScript in your browser for the full experience."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://informaconnect.com/bioeurope-spring/sponsors/bimini-biotech/", "domain": "informaconnect.com", "title": "BIMINI Biotech | BIO-Europe Spring", "date": "2025-07-03", "text": "2026 Presenting Companies This site is part of the Informa Connect Division of Informa PLC"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 6704, "cached_input_tokens": 5547, "output_tokens": 212}, "timing": {"search_ms": 831.6, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 4289.6, "total_ms": 5121.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 4289.6, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "BM-013’s first regulatory approval year or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["BM-013 acute myeloid leukemia approval launch year"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q022", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication Novel UC Treatment, Cosmo Health, Distal Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Novel UC Treatment"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 19226, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists. ... The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21). More recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26). ... The therapeutic landscape for UC is rapidly evolving, with an unprecedented number of investigational agents currently in clinical development. This pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms. ... Integrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium. ... These investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds; ... TL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jcag/article/7/4/282/7674170", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "Practical guidance for managing patients with moderate-to-severe ...", "date": "2024-08-12", "text": "The emergence of small molecule therapies offers novel treatment options that target mechanisms central to the immunopathology of UC. Sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase inhibitors are 2 classes of therapies that target unique pathways to attenuate inflammation and modulate the immune response characteristic of UC. ... Advanced small molecule therapies, such as sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase (JAK) inhibitors, are among the emerging UC treatment options designed to provide patients with alternatives to support long-term treatment goals (Figure 1). These treatment options have novel mechanisms of action for patients to manage symptoms effectively and relatively safely. ... Etrasimod functions by selectively activating S1P receptor subtypes 1, 4, and 5.^26^ The blockade of S1P receptors prevents lymphocytes from being recruited into the colon, allowing the mucosa to heal.^27^"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists. ... More recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26). ... This pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms. ... |JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown| ... |TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027| ... Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC. ... Upon binding to S1PR1 on lymphocyte surfaces, these agents cause persistent receptor internalization and proteasome-mediated degradation, resulting in functional downregulation (43). ... These investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds; ... Several mechanistic classes demonstrate particular promise. TL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations. Next-generation selective JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitors aim to preserve efficacy while mitigating safety concerns through enhanced selectivity."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "date": "2023-08-17", "text": "There are currently multiple emerging agents in the pipeline for the management of UC. This includes agents with alternative routes of administration such as oral or subcutaneous tumor necrosis factor inhibitors or novel mechanisms of action such as toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist cobitolimod and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor apremilast. ... These small molecules include Janus Kinase (JAK) inhibitors such as tofacitinib, upadacitinib and filgotinib; ... |Drug Class|Agent|Target|Mode of Delivery|Stage| |--|--|--|--|--| |**Anti-TNF**|CT-P13|TNF|SC|Phase III completed| |**Anti-TNF**|OPRX-106|TNF|Oral|Phase II completed| |**JAK inhibitors**|Ivarmacitinib|JAK1|Oral|Phase II completed/Phase III recruiting| |**JAK inhibitors**|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|JAK1/TYK2|Oral|Phase IIb completed| ... |**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|PTG-100|α4β7 integrin|Oral|Phase IIa completed (primary endpoint not met)| |**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Mirikizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed| |**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Risankizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed (results pending)| |**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Brazikumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Development Halted| ... |**S1P Receptor Modulators**|Amiselimod (MT-1303)|S1PR1|Oral|Phase II recruiting| |**PDE4 Inhibitors**|Apremilast|PDE4|Oral|Phase II completed| |**TLR9 Agonist**|Cobitolimod|TLR9|Topical (enema)|Phase IIb completed/Phase III recruiting| ... Etrolizumab is an SC humanized monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity.25 It blocks binding to their ligands MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin resulting in inhibition of leukocyte trafficking to the gut and inhibition of retention of leukocytes, respectively.26 Studies on animal models indicate that the medication selectively targets lymphocyte homing on mucosal surfaces with no impact on migration to non-mucosal tissues.26 ... HCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, selectively inhibits binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.31 It shares the same mechanism of action as natalizumab. ... Abrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183) is a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody which targets α4β7 integrin on gut-tropic lymphocytes. ... Mirikizumab is an IgG4-variant monoclonal antibody that binds to the p19 subunit of IL23. ... Cobitolimod is a topical toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist. It results in the activation of TLR9 on lymphocytes and antigen presenting cells which lead to induction of regulatory T-cells, eventual production of IL-10 and suppression of TH-17 cells."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.emjreviews.com/gastroenterology/symposium/ulcerative-colitis-today-tomorrow-and-the-future/", "domain": "emjreviews.com", "title": "Ulcerative Colitis: Today, Tomorrow, and the Future", "date": "2020-12-01", "text": "Emerging therapies for UC currently undergoing Phase III development fall under four main mechanisms of action: leukocyte retention in lymphoid organs by S1P receptor modulators (etrasimod and ozanimod), IL-23 inhibitors (mirikizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab, and risankizumab), JAK/TYK2 inhibitors (filgotinib and upadacitinib), and leukocyte adhesion inhibitors (integrin blockers and etrolizumab).^35^ ... The leukocyte adhesion inhibitor etrolizumab selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 and E-cadherin, to reduce gut-specific lymphocyte trafficking to the inflamed colon."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12897911/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "date": "2026-02-04", "text": "Mesalazine is an anti-inflammatory agent with a complex, yet not fully elucidated mechanism of action in UC [20]. Its therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory pathways [21]. Mesalazine is thought to inhibit the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways, thereby reducing the synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes [22]. A key component of its activity involves agonism of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-), which plays a critical role in regulating intestinal inflammation. Additionally, mesalazine may exert antioxidant effects and contribute to the suppression of T-cell activation and proliferation, further supporting its anti-inflammatory properties [20,22]. ... By increasing the expression of anti-inflammatory genes while simultaneously reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory ones, the GR/GC complex is responsible for anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects, it also reduces the functioning of pro-inflammatory factors, including nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells (NF-κB) and activator protein 1 (AP-1) [25,26]. ... Overall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response. In the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27]. ... The immunosuppressive effects attributed to thiopurines occur through several mechanisms, the most common of which include inhibition of the expression of proinflammatory genes, such as *ITGA4* and tumor necrosis factors [38]. ... The mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues. TNF is extensively produced by immune cells during the inflammatory process and then participates in their migration from blood vessels to diseased sites. By inhibiting TNF with IFX, it is possible to control the symptoms of UC [43]. ... Adalimumab (ADA) is another drug belonging to the TNF inhibitor class, this fully human recombinant IgG1 antibody is involved in inhibiting neutrophil activation and leukocyte migration to sites of inflammation. By interacting with TNF, adalimumab induces antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, stimulates the complement system, and induces T-cell apoptosis [47]. ... Vedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes. ... Vedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls. ... This antibody can inhibit leukocyte ..."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402463/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Current Pharmacologic Options and Emerging Therapeutic ... - PMC", "date": "2024-09-09", "text": "Now, a wide range of therapies has emerged, including alpha-4-integrin blockade agents, anti-CD3 antibodies, probiotic bacterial therapy, epidermal growth factors, etc., which offer the prospect of improved dosing and compliance with traditional molecules. ... ^26^ Mesalamine acts on colonic mucosa and reduces inflammation through a diverse process which includes inhibiting the mediators of lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase, interleukin-1, interleukin-2, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha. Also, it activates the Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR)-gamma, which helps in the transcription of a few key target genes such as nuclear factor B, signal transducers, and activators of transcription that help in the control of intestinal inflammation.^27^ ... These drugs are monoclonal antibodies that act against the TNF-alpha either by neutralizing its biological activity or by interfering with the TNF-alpha attachment to its receptor site. ... This drug acts by inhibiting the action of integrin, which is a protein involved in neutrophil function and migration by interacting with cellular adhesion molecules to allow for leukocyte adhesion to that vessel wall. ... Tofacitibin and Upadacitinib are JAK inhibitors, which act by inhibiting the JAK kinase, thereby inhibiting the cytokine and growth factor receptor signaling."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/75a0/e296f6c60ceffcd0af16826ef7f1d11558b9.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "title": "[PDF] Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "date": null, "text": "Its therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory path- ways [21]. ... Overall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response. In the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27]. ... The mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues. ... Vedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes. ... Vedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls. ... The main target in the mechanism of action of golimumab is binding to human TNF and neutralizing the expression of adhesion molecules, including E-selectin, VCAM-1, and ICAM-1, on the surface of vascular endothelium. ... Its mechanism of action is based on binding to the p40 subunit of IL-12 and IL-23, which inhibits interaction with the IL-12 receptor, its signaling, and subsequent activation of Th1 cells."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12428888/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Emerging Therapies in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC - NIH", "date": "2025-08-29", "text": "Anti-TNF agents, including infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab pegol, represent the most extensively studied biologic class in IBD, targeting TNF-α, a key cytokine in the pathogenesis of both Crohn’s disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC). ... Anti-integrin therapy includes vedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks α4β7 integrin, thereby preventing lymphocyte trafficking to the gut mucosa through a mechanism that specifically targets gut-homing immune cells while preserving systemic immune function. ... Anti-IL-12/23 therapy is represented by ustekinumab, which targets the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, modulating Th1 and Th17 pathways implicated in both CD and UC through a mechanism that addresses key cytokine networks central to IBD pathogenesis. ... By inhibiting the JAK-STAT signaling pathway, tofacitinib blocks the transmission of signals from pro-inflammatory cytokines, including interleukins (IL-2, IL-4, IL-6) and interferons (IFN-γ) [49]. ... **Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators** such as ozanimod and etrasimod selectively prevent the egress of lymphocytes from lymph nodes, thereby reducing the inflammatory response in the gut. ... By selectively inhibiting IL-23, these agents reduce the inflammatory activity of Th17 cells, providing a novel mechanism of action distinct from anti-TNF agents [59]."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2024.1394124/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Frontiers | Harnessing nature’s pharmacy: investigating natural compounds as novel therapeutics for ulcerative colitis", "date": "2024-08-14", "text": "**Results:** The results showed that 279 natural compounds treat UC through four main mechanisms, including regulating gut microbiota and metabolites (Mechanism I), protecting the intestinal mucosal barrier (Mechanism II), regulating intestinal mucosal immune response (Mechanism III), as well as regulating other mechanisms (Mechanism Ⅳ) such as cellular autophagy modulation and ferroptosis inhibition. ... These compounds have been found to have an impact on the makeup of the microbiota and its metabolites, as well as the prevention of colonization by intestinal pathogens and the reduction of the risk of recurrence of ulcerative colitis, as illustrated in Figure 3. ... As shown in Supplementary Table S1, natural compounds can improve the barrier function of the UC mucosa through multiple perspectives, these include upregulation of the expression of tight junction protein, reduction in the intestinal mucosal, permeability, regulation of the intestinal mucus function, reduction of oxidative stress, and protection of the intestinal epithelial cells (Figure 4)."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11160, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 1068}, "timing": {"search_ms": 578.8, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 12643.2, "total_ms": 13222.0}, "luna_wall_ms": 12643.2, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "Novel UC Treatment’s mechanism or mechanistic class; which agents share that class and compete for distal ulcerative colitis; and the development stage and reported results of any such agents.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["Novel UC Treatment Cosmo Health mechanism of action", "Novel UC Treatment Cosmo Health distal ulcerative colitis same class agents", "Novel UC Treatment Cosmo Health development stage trial results"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 3, "next_queries_subject_first": 3}}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 17421, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1e53ce24-191f-48ed-a22e-45f1dec47600", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "date": "2025-12-05", "text": "To reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23]. Pharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium. ... Etrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7. It works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium. Mouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23]."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3711369/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "A randomised phase I study of etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7) in ...", "date": "2012-06-20", "text": "Etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7, anti-β7, PRO145223) is a humanised monoclonal antibody targeting the β7 subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α4β7 and αEβ7, which are implicated in leucocyte migration and retention in ulcerative colitis (UC). ... ### What are the new findings? - Etrolizumab (antibodies to β7) does inhibit the migration of lymphocytes to the gut and antagonises the retention of lymphocytes in the mucosa by blocking the αEβ7–E-cadherin interaction. ... Etrolizumab is a humanised IgG1 monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7^29^ ^30^ and αEβ7^15^ integrins with high affinity, blocking binding to their ligands, MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin, respectively. ... Etrolizumab provides a dual approach mechanism of action for the treatment of IBD that involves inhibition of leucocyte trafficking to the gut (through blocking of α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interactions) as well as inhibition of retention of leucocytes in the intraepithelial lining of the gut (through blocking αEβ7–E-cadherin interactions), which may provide improved efficacy over currently available therapies. ... First, as discussed above, it could block α4β7-expressing leucocytes from entering the gut by inhibiting their extravasation through MAdCAM-1-expressing mucosal endothelial venules."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12189", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "title": "Etrolizumab", "date": "2012-02-12", "text": "**Etrolizumab**is a humanized IgG1κ anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody under investigation for use in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. ... Etrolizumab is a humanized IgG1κ monoclonal antibody directed against the β7 subunit of gastrointestinal α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins that, due to its target specificity, appears as or more efficacious than vedolizumab and without the CNS effects of natalizumab. ... Etrolizumab is a humanized anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody that impairs the targeting and retention of gut-specific lymphocytes contributing to an inflammatory microenvironment in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. ... Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, is characterized by chronic inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. This inflammation is driven, at least in part, by excessive infiltration and retention of intestinal-homing lymphocytes. An adhesion cascade drives the infiltration of immune cells. The binding of immune cell glycoproteins to their corresponding epithelial cell selectins results in partial retention and the \"rolling\" of immune cells along the endothelium. A stable arrest is mediated by stronger interactions between immune cell integrins and cell adhesion molecules (CAMs) on the endothelial cell; once arrested, immune cells will extravasate and enter the target tissue. ... Of primary importance to intestinal homing is the mucosal addressin cellular adhesion molecule (MAdCAM-1), which interacts strongly with α4β7 integrins, and vascular cell adhesion molecule (VCAM-1), which binds with lower affinity. ... α4β7 integrin is expressed at high levels on intestinal-homing lymphocytes but can also be found on natural killer cells, basophils, eosinophils, macrophages, activated monocytes, and mast cells. ... The infiltration and retention of immune cells as a mechanism of IBD is suggested by observations across multiple studies of an increase in the number of, and alteration in the secreted cytokine profile of, α4β7 +and αEβ7 +immune cells in the gastrointestinal tract of IBD patients. ... Etrolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody specific for the β7 integrin subunit; it has a K dof 116 ± 11 pmol/L against human α4β7 and 1800 ± 170 pmol/L against human αEβ7, as assessed by transfection in HEK293 cells. This strong binding provides efficient inhibition of integrin/CAM interactions. Etrolizumab has an IC 50for the α4β7 interactions with MAdCAM-1, VCAM-1, and fibronectin of 0.075 ± 0.034, 0.089 ± 0.009, and 0.119 ± 0.056 nmol/L, respectively. Etrolizumab also inhibits the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, albeit less effectively (IC 50of 3.96 ± 1.78 nmol/L). While etrolizumab does not exhibit antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, it disrupts the integrin-CAM interactions involved in gut immune cell residence, in part by inducing the endocytic uptake of bound integrin receptors, and therefore reduces the inflammatory burden in IBD. ... Integrin ITGA4/ITGB7 ..."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=0c8fefc4-9b58-46fd-9932-8178652bd368", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for ...", "date": null, "text": "In IBD (CD, UC, pediatric CD), TNFR1/TNFR2 blockade dampens NF‑κB/MAPK activation in gut‑homing lymphocytes, restores epithelial barrier integrity, and limits mucosal infiltration. ... To reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23]. Pharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium. ... Etrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7. It works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium. Mouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23]."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6001693/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Review article: nonclinical and clinical pharmacology ... - PMC", "date": "2018-03-30", "text": "Etrolizumab exerts its effect via a unique dual MOA that inhibits both leucocyte trafficking to the intestinal mucosa and retention within the intestinal epithelial layer. The gut‐selectivity of etrolizumab results from its specific targeting of the β7 subunit of α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins. Etrolizumab does not bind to α4β1 integrin, which mediates lymphocyte trafficking to tissues including the central nervous system, a characteristic underlying its favourable safety with regard to progressive multifocal leucoencephalopathy. ... Binding to β7 integrin enables etrolizumab to block both leucocyte trafficking to the gut via α4β7 interactions with its endothelial ligand mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 (MAdCAM‐1),17, 18 expressed primarily on endothelium of venules of intestinal lamina propria and associated lymphoid tissues19, 20 as well as the retention of lymphocyte subsets within the intestinal mucosa through the interaction of αEβ7 with its ligand E‐cadherin, expressed on the gut epithelium21 (Figure 1, Table 1).22, 23, 24, 25 ... α4β7 integrin plays a crucial role in leucocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa and associated lymphoid tissues. α4β7 integrin is constitutively expressed at a modest level on naive T and B cells and at higher levels on intestinal‐homing effector/memory T and B cells."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367223/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Cellular Mechanisms of Etrolizumab Treatment in Inflammatory ...", "date": "2019-02-01", "text": "Mechanistically, etrolizumab is known to block β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces intestinal trafficking of β7-expressing cells. Etrolizumab blocks β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces β7-positive lymphocyte migration and retention in the inflamed gut mucosa, but the exact mechanisms by which this inhibition occurs are not fully understood. ... **Results:** As demonstrated by several different approaches, etrolizumab and etrolizumab-s treatment led to internalization of β7 integrin. This resulted in impaired dynamic adhesion to MAdCAM-1. ... The anti-β7 antibody etrolizumab is currently being investigated in several phase III trials in IBD patients and blocks both α4β7-mediated gut homing as well as αEβ7-controlled retention (Vermeire et al., 2014; Zundler et al., 2017c). ... We show with complementary techniques including molecular microscopy with stimulated emission depletion (STED) imaging and ImageStream^®^ analyses that etrolizumab leads to internalization of α4β7 integrin, that this functionally impairs β7-dependent adhesion to MAdCAM-1, and that internalization of α4β7 integrin is higher with etrolizumab compared with vedolizumab."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6973243/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "2.4. Integrin αeβ7 (cd103)", "date": "2019-06-19", "text": "Vedolizumab targets the α4β7 subunit and inhibits its interactions with MAdCAM‐1. Etrolizumab targets the β7 integrin and inhibits the interaction of α4β7 and αEβ7 with MAdCAM‐1 or E‐cadherin, respectively. ... |Vedolizumab|α4β7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|In the market|Takeda|109, 110| |Etrolizumab|α4β7; αEβ7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|Phase 3|Genentech|130, 131, 132, 133| ... |Abrilumab|α4β7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|Phase 2b|Amgen, AstraZeneca|123, 144| ... Vedolizumab is a humanized antibody which binds to α4β7 and was shown to specifically inhibit the interaction between α4β*7* integrin and its ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and fibronectin (Table 1).106 Given that MAdCAM‐1 expression is largely restricted to the intestine, the inhibitory effects of vedolizumab are regarded as gut selective. ... Vedolizumab was shown to be safe and effective for the treatment of IBD in humans in phase 3 studies. ... Abrilumab (AMG181/MEDI7183) is a fully human monoclonal antibody directed against α4β7.121, 122 In a phase 2b multicenter, randomized double‐blind study in patients with moderate to severe UC who were refractory to aTNF or immunomodulator therapy , abrilumab demonstrated evidence of efficacy and an acceptable safety profile, with no reported cases of PML.123 Although there was some evidence for efficacy of abrilumab in CD, the primary end point, CDAI remission (score < 150) at week 8 was not met in the phase 2b, multicenter, randomized double‐blind study in patients with moderate to severe disease.123 ... Etrolizumab is a humanized immunoglobulin (Ig)G1 monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and E‐cadherin (Table 1). ... Phase 3 studies for etrolizumab are ongoing in both UC and CD. ... Current clinical research suggests that inhibition of α4β7 via vedolizumab or other small molecules, or dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 via etrolizumab should not lead to any significant side effects outside the gut, including the risk of PML.142 In terms of α4β7 blockade, although it has been shown that homing of both T~eff~ and T~reg~ cells can be impacted,69 clinical studies do not support the idea that β7 blockade may lead to any worsening of inflammation."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2147/CEG.S293272", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "title": "Anti-Integrins for the Treatment of Inflammatory Bowel ...", "date": "2021-08-24", "text": "Vedolizumab (anti-α4β7) is approved for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC) and CD. It is the most widely used anti-integrin therapy in IBD and has been shown to be effective in both induction and maintenance therapy, with a favorable safety profile. ... Etrolizumab (anti-β7) blocks leukocyte trafficking via α4β7 and cell adhesion via αEβ7 integrins. Large phase 3 clinical trials evaluating efficacy of etrolizumab in the induction and maintenance of patients with IBD are underway. ... Vedolizumab (Takeda) is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer. The antibody selectively blocks α4β7:MAdCAM-1 interactions—preventing leukocyte adhesion to intestinal endothelium—but does not affect α4β1:VCAM-1 interactions. ^Citation36^ Vedolizumab is FDA-approved for induction and maintenance therapy in patients with CD and CD. ... Etrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks cellular adhesion by binding the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins. The α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins function as trafficking molecules that localize leukocytes to inflammatory sites in the gut.^Citation56^ Etrolizumab binds α4β7 and prevents binding to MAdCAM-1, a receptor that is highly upregulated in the intestinal venules of patients with UC and CD. Etrolizumab also binds αEβ7 and prevents interaction with E-cadherin found on intestinal mucosal cells. ... ^Citation59^ Currently, there are several phase 3 clinical trials evaluating etrolizumab in IBD (UC: HIBISCUS I and II, GARDENIA, LAUREL, HICKORY; CD: BERGAMOT)^Citation60^ and two open-label extension trials (UC: COTTONWOOD; CD: JUNIPER). ... Antibody blockade of leukocyte trafficking to the gastrointestinal tract with anti-integrin therapy (anti-α4β7) is effective for patients with IBD and is associated with favorable safety profiles."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/cei/article/215/1/15/7239982?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "Targeting T-cell integrins in autoimmune and inflammatory diseases", "date": null, "text": "MAdCAM-1 is expressed in the HEVs of the small intestine, Peyer’s patches, and large intestine, and interacts with l-selectin and α4β7 to mediate rolling and firm adhesion of T cells, respectively [14]. ... Thus, drugs that inhibit the action of α4β7, α4β1, and αEβ7 have been developed, trialled, and authorized for the treatment of IBD (Fig. 1) [27]. ... Vedolizumab binding to α4β7 inhibits the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and fibronectin, but not VCAM-1, as VCAM-1 binds to α4β7 without clashing with vedolizumab [48]. Vedolizumab binding to α4β7 induces the internalization of α4β7, and removal of vedolizumab restores α4β7 expression and MAdCAM-1 binding to normal within 4 days, in vitro. ... Etrolizumab is a humanised anti-β7 monoclonal antibody and, as such, binds to α4β7 and αEβ7 to inhibit their binding to MAdCAM-1 and VCAM-1, and E-cadherin, respectively. T cells expressing αEβ7 bind to E-cadherin that is expressed by epithelial cells on their basolateral surface, causing retention of these T cells in the lamina propria [30]. ... Another mechanism of action of etrolizumab may be the inhibition of αEβ7 on colonic DCs. ... In IBD, targeting the α4β7-MAdCAM-1 interaction with vedolizumab has shown efficacy in clinical trials and possesses an advantage with regard to safety due to the selectivity of said interaction to the gut, as does targeting β7-chain on its own with etrolizumab."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5901649/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Next generation therapeutics for inflammatory bowel disease", "date": "2014-01-01", "text": "Vedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that targets the α4β7 integrin, was approved for use in UC and CD, and is now widely used in routine practice.[10, 11] (Tables 1 and 2) For UC, treatment outcomes in clinical practice have mirrored those seen in the RCT with rates of clinical remission after induction ranging from 24% - 36%.[ ... Etrolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that selectively binds the β7 subunit of the α4β7 and the αEβ7 integrins, has recently completed a phase 2 trial in UC.[19] In this small study of 124 UC patients etrolizumab was demonstrated to be considerably more efficacious as compared to placebo for achieving clinical remission with induction therapy. ... Nonetheless, these data are quite promising and phase 3 trials in UC and CD are currently underway, including a head to head comparison against adalimumab. ... Tofacitinib, an oral small molecule that inhibits JAK 1 and 3 (and JAK 2 at higher doses), has undergone phase 2 and 3 studies in UC."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11195, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 5646}, "timing": {"search_ms": 577.4, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 59130.3, "total_ms": 59707.7}, "luna_wall_ms": 59130.3, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Vedolizumab (Takeda) is an anti-integrin monoclonal antibody targeting α4β7; it blocks α4β7 interactions with MAdCAM-1 and fibronectin, and is approved for ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD), including induction and maintenance therapy. Phase 3 studies found it safe and effective in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD); UC clinical-practice remission after induction ranged from 24%–36%. Pivotal GEMINI trials had no observed PML cases, and pharmacokinetic studies found minimal systemic immunosuppression. [E7][E8][E9][E10][E1]\n\nEtrolizumab (rhuMAb β7; PRO145223) is a humanized IgG1κ monoclonal antibody against β7, targeting both α4β7 and αEβ7. It blocks α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin interactions, inhibiting gut-directed leukocyte trafficking and mucosal retention; it does not bind α4β1 and does not exhibit antibody-dependent cytotoxicity. Its reported Kd values are 116 ± 11 pmol/L for human α4β7 and 1800 ± 170 pmol/L for human αEβ7. Reported IC50 values are 0.075 ± 0.034 nmol/L for α4β7–MAdCAM-1, 0.089 ± 0.009 nmol/L for α4β7–VCAM-1, 0.119 ± 0.056 nmol/L for α4β7–fibronectin, and 3.96 ± 1.78 nmol/L for αEβ7–E-cadherin. It induces integrin internalization; one study found greater α4β7 internalization with etrolizumab than with vedolizumab. [E2][E3][E5][E6][E9] A randomized Phase I study is identified, and a Phase 2 UC study enrolled 124 patients and found greater clinical-remission efficacy with induction treatment than placebo. Phase 3 trials are named as HIBISCUS I and II, GARDENIA, LAUREL and HICKORY in UC, and BERGAMOT in CD; the open-label extension trials are COTTONWOOD in UC and JUNIPER in CD. Adalimumab is named as a head-to-head comparator in Phase 3 trials. [E2][E8][E10] Mouse models and genetic analyses found that dual α4β7/αEβ7 blockade could more effectively reduce accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8+ T cells in intestinal mucosa in CD. [E1] DrugBank reports Kd and IC50 measurements for etrolizumab as above. [E3]\n\nAbrilumab (AMG181/MEDI7183; Amgen and AstraZeneca) is a fully human monoclonal antibody directed against α4β7. In a randomized, double-blind, multicenter Phase 2b study in moderate-to-severe UC refractory to anti-TNF or immunomodulator therapy, it showed evidence of efficacy and an acceptable safety profile, with no reported PML cases. In a separate randomized, double-blind, multicenter Phase 2b study in moderate-to-severe CD, the week-8 primary endpoint of CDAI remission (score <150) was not met. [E7]", "confident_score": 0.77, "missing": "The evidence does not identify a specific subject molecule or sponsor, or state its route, dose, schedule, development stage, trial and registry ID, enrolment or eligibility, regulatory designations, or results. For the named competing agents, routes, doses and schedules are not stated; trial registry IDs and enrolments are not stated. Etrolizumab’s Phase 3 trial results are not stated. Whether any specifically named small-molecule α4β7 inhibitor is a competitor is not established by these items.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8", "E9", "E10"], "next_queries": ["α4β7 inhibitor molecule sponsor IBD pipeline", "α4β7 inhibitor clinical trial registry enrollment eligibility", "α4β7 inhibitor regulatory designation results IBD", "α4β7 inhibitor small molecule IBD development", "α4β7 inhibitor route dose schedule"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 2518, "confident_score": 0.77, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 16, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 9, "cited_domains": ["academic.oup.com", "go.drugbank.com", "link.springer.com", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "tandfonline.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 47, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q028", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 13116, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "date": null, "text": "Significant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression. ... humans."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.perspectivetherapeutics.com/newsroom/press-release?i=159323", "domain": "perspectivetherapeutics.com", "title": "Press Release", "date": null, "text": "For more information, activate the button labeled \"Explore your accessibility options\". Newsroom"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov/studies/phs003215", "domain": "moleculartargets.ccdi.cancer.gov", "title": "CCDI Hub", "date": null, "text": "This system is provided for Government-authorized use only. ... At any time, and for any lawful Government purpose, the government may monitor, intercept, and search and seize any communication or data transiting or stored on this system. - Any communication or data transiting or stored on this system may be disclosed or used for any lawful Government purpose."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2589004220301905", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "title": "Article Rapid and Selective Targeting of Heterogeneous Pancreatic ...", "date": null, "text": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.scienc