{"row_id": "q027", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 10086, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ptr.70430", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "title": "Phytochemicals as Multitarget Therapeutics in Pancreatic Cancer: Mechanisms, Clinical Evidence, and Translational Challenges", "date": "2026-08-16", "text": "Quercetin has shown potential to inhibit tumor cell growth, but its effectiveness is limited by low bioavailability and poor solubility. HQL's ability to degrade HA in the tumor microenvironment facilitated NP penetration and contributed to its antitumor efficacy (Sun et al. 2025). Quercetin-3-*O*-glucoside may enhance gemcitabine's chemotherapeutic effects and inhibit metastasis, determined by various growth factors in PC (Lee et al. 2016)."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "date": "2025-06-10", "text": "Hence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL. In the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect. Treatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice. HQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids. Mechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2. In summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy. Thereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Activated T Cells Break Tumor Immunosuppression by Macrophage ...", "date": "2025-04-07", "text": "To translate our genetic results in a clinically relevant setting, we used the HPGDS inhibitor HQL-79 (38) in YUMM 1.7 melanoma-bearing mice. Systemic administration of HQL-79 (15 mg/kg twice daily) recapitulated the antitumor phenotype observed in *Hpgds*^ΔMø^ mice (Fig. 6A–C; Supplementary Fig. S9A and S9B), achieving 78% of tumor growth inhibition with one of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Supplementary Fig. S9B). The impairment in tumor growth was accompanied by a shift in TAMs toward antitumoral markers (Fig. 6D–F) and robust infiltration of CD8^+^ T cells (Supplementary Fig. S9C–S9E). Like macrophage-specific *Hpgds* deletion, HQL-79 strongly affected the tumor vasculature by decreasing vessel density and size and by improving pericyte coverage and perfusion (Supplementary Fig. S9F–S9L), together with reduced hypoxia (Supplementary Fig. S9M and S9N), cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream (Fig. 6G), and lung colonization (Fig. 6H). ... Lastly, we extended our findings to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC). This tumor type was selected due to the higher expression of HPGDS in the malignant tissue compared with the healthy counterpart (Supplementary Fig. S11F) and its colocalization with macrophage markers only in both murine and human tumors (Supplementary Fig. S11G). In mice with orthotopic KPC tumors (carrying the two most common mutations found in patients with PDAC, i.e., *KRAS* and *TP53*), αPD1 was ineffective, and HQL-79 displayed a mild inhibitory effect on tumor growth, but their combination was synergic (Fig. 7G and H)."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "title": "www.creative-biolabs.com › blog › adcHyaluronidase-Conjugated Liposomes May Effectively Target ...", "date": "2025-08-14", "text": "The research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes. This novel liposome was named HQL. ... In vitro experiments showed that HQL could stably exist in 10% fetal bovine serum and plasma for 15 days. ... In vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls. It had no obvious inhibitory effect on the proliferation of normal pancreatic ductal epithelial cells (HPNE), showing good tumor cell specificity. HQL had stronger penetration ability in HA-rich agarose gels. In 2D and 3D cell culture models, it was more effectively taken up by pancreatic cancer cells, promoted cancer cell apoptosis, and induced cell cycle arrest at the G2/M phase. In experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups. In in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model. Results showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%. Fluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver. The drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours. Furthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours. Safety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects. Mechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2. In summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety. It provides a new and promising drug delivery method for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer, which is expected to bring new hope to pancreatic cancer patients in the future and promote further development in the field of pancreatic cancer treatment."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig7/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Figure 7.", "date": "2025-01-01", "text": "HPGDS inhibition overcomes immunotherapy resistance and is effective in human settings. Tumor growth (), tumor weight (), and representative images () of ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ genetically engineered mice (GEM) treated with vehicle, HQL-79 15 mg/kg twice daily, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6). ... Percentage of CD11c^+^ macrophages in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle, HQL-79, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6). Flow cytometric quantification of PD1 expressing CD8^+^ T cells in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination and αPD1 ( = 6). Histologic quantification of CD8^+^ T cells at the tumor margin vs. core in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 6 ). and Tumor weight () and representative pictures () of KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mice treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 11–16)."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig6/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Figure 6.", "date": "2025-01-01", "text": "Tumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79, 15 mg/kg BID or with vehicle control ( = 6)."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8578999/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "In Vivo Preclinical Models", "date": "2021-10-27", "text": "Zebrafish is a useful and economical *in vivo* animal model for speedy analysis of invasion and metastatic behavior. ... *In vivo* models are useful to model the TME and immune response, key players in PDAC transformation and progression. Murine models are ideal platforms for understanding PDAC progression, pathophysiology and testing therapeutic modalities."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00151-6", "domain": "cell.com", "title": "Cloudflare", "date": null, "text": "If you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00001-8", "domain": "cell.com", "title": "Cloudflare", "date": null, "text": "If you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5718030/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine as anti-cancer agents", "date": "2017-11-23", "text": "* A similar observation can be made when comparing CQ efficacy in pancreatic cancer mouse models and a lung cancer mouse model: CQ significantly slowed down tumour growth and increased survival in the first, but not in the latter [27]. ... |Carew *et al* [171]|CQ|Vorinostat|Female nude BALB/c mice|HCT8 colon cancer cells|Significantly enhanced tumour reduction| ... Next, CQ and HCQ were tested via *in vitro* assays, in which they were found to suppress pancreatic cancer cell proliferation [82, 83]."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8617, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 1068}, "timing": {"search_ms": 545.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 11874.3, "total_ms": 12420.0}, "luna_wall_ms": 11874.3, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "In mice with orthotopic KPC tumors carrying KRAS and TP53 mutations, HQL-79 monotherapy had a mild inhibitory effect on tumor growth. A KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mouse figure includes vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, and combination groups, with 11–16 mice stated for the groups. [E3][E5]", "confident_score": 0.65, "missing": "The numerical TGI for HQL-79 monotherapy; whether tumor regression or shrinkage occurred and its magnitude; HQL-79 monotherapy dose and schedule in the PDAC model; results for any other PDAC animal models.", "citations": ["E3", "E5"], "next_queries": ["HQL-79 KPC FC1245 tumor growth inhibition TGI", "HQL-79 KPC tumor regression shrinkage", "HQL-79 orthotopic KPC dose schedule", "HQL-79 PDAC animal model monotherapy efficacy"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 280, "confident_score": 0.