onfirm the \"Catalog Number\" and the \"Sample Lot No.\" and then enter the following information."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7014, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 627}, "timing": {"search_ms": 568.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 6819.3, "total_ms": 7387.8}, "luna_wall_ms": 6819.3, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "IPS-06061 showed strong anti-tumour efficacy in a xenograft animal model. [E1][E4][E5]", "confident_score": 0.55, "missing": "Whether the xenograft model was KRAS G12D-mutant pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma; whether IPS-06061 was tested as single-agent monotherapy; and the tumour growth inhibition results, including any quantitative TGI, tumour regression or tumour shrinkage findings.", "citations": ["E1", "E4", "E5"], "next_queries": ["IPS-06061 KRAS G12D pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma xenograft", "IPS-06061 single-agent monotherapy xenograft", "IPS-06061 tumour growth inhibition TGI results", "IPS-06061 tumour regression shrinkage animal model"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 86, "confident_score": 0.55, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 3, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 3, "cited_domains": ["delta.larvol.com", "linkedin.com", "semanticscholar.org"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 2, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q024", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7 Integrin α4β7 Small Molecule therapies", "anchors": ["emvistegrast"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 16343, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=43b8710c-7d26-400d-99a6-57e1ce79a7ba", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "title": "emvistegrast (GS-1427) / Gilead - LARVOL DELTA", "date": null, "text": "## EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF EMVISTEGRAST (FORMERLY GS-1427), AN ORAL Α4Β7 INTEGRIN INHIBITOR, FOR MODERATE TO SEVERE ULCERATIVE COLITIS: RESULTS OF THE INDUCTION PHASE OF THE PHASE 2 SWIFT TRIAL (UEGW 2026) - \"Full abstract will be published on the day of presentation\" ... ## Pharmacokinetics, Safety, and Tolerability of GS-1427, an Oral Prodrug of a Potent and Selective α4β7 Integrin Inhibitor, in Healthy Participants. (PubMed, J Clin Pharmacol) - \"The bioavailability of tablet versus capsule formulations and the effect of food and an acid-reducing agent (omeprazole) on exposure were also assessed. All AEs were grade 1 in severity and no serious AEs or deaths were reported. The pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability results from this study support further evaluation of GS-1427 in a phase 2 trial in patients with UC.\" ... ## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Active, not recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Trial completion date: Jul 2027 ➔ Apr 2028 ... ## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | N=423 ➔ 228 | Trial completion date: Mar 2028 ➔ Feb 2027 | Trial primary completion date: May 2027 ➔ Oct 2025 ... ## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=423 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/SYRE/drug/SPY001", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "title": "SPY001 (Ph 2) for Ulcerative Colitis | Spyre Therapeutics, Inc", "date": "2026-09-16", "text": "Phase 2 ... Highest phase Phase 2 Registered trials 2 across 1 sponsor since Jun 2025 ... Molecular targetα4β7 ... NCT07652294Long-term Safety and Efficacy of Multiple Long-acting Antibodies Alone and in Combinations for IBDUlcerative Colitis ... NCT07012395A Study of Long-acting Antibodies Alone and in Combinations for Moderate to Severe Ulcerative ColitisUlcerative Colitis ... |Gilead Sciences, Inc.|GILD|3|PHASE3|Filgotinib, Tilpisertib Fosmecarbil, emvistegrast| ... |Spyre Therapeutics, Inc|SYRE|1|PHASE2|SPY001, SPY002, SPY003| ... SPY001 is an investigational small molecule being developed for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It is currently in Phase 2 clinical development and has not been approved by regulatory authorities. ... SPY001 targets the α4β7 integrin, a protein involved in immune cell trafficking to the gut. By targeting this integrin, the drug aims to modulate the inflammatory response associated with ulcerative colitis. ... SPY001 is being studied in two Phase 2 clinical trials. The first, NCT07012395, is recruiting participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. The second, NCT07652294, is enrolling by invitation to evaluate long-term safety and efficacy. Both trials are randomized, double-blind, and controlled, with a combined enrollment of 1290 participants."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Table 1.", "date": null, "text": "|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown| ... |JAK Inhibitor|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|Oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; advanced to alopecia areata trials| ... |α4β7 antagonist|PTG-100|Oral α4β7 integrin antagonist peptide|NCT02895100|Phase 2b|Terminated early for futility March 2018| |α4β7 antagonist|PN-943|Oral gastrointestinal-restricted cyclic peptide targeting α4β7|NCT04504383|Phase 2|Completed Feb 2023; primary endpoint not met for 450mg dose| |α4β7 antagonist|Abrilumab|Human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01694485, NCT01959165|Phase 2b|Global study completed; 13.3% remission (70mg) vs 4.3% placebo| |α4β7 antagonist|Vatelizumab (SAR339658)|Monoclonal antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01659138, NCT01861249|Phase 2|Terminated early due to recruitment challenges| ... |α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027| |α4β7 antagonist|MORF-057|Oral small-molecule inhibitor of α4β7 integrin|NCT05291689 (EMERALD-1), NCT05611671 (EMERALD-2)|Phase 2a, Phase 2b|EMERALD-1 completed Nov 2023; EMERALD-2 primary completion Nov 2024| ... |TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029| ... |Combination|Mirikizumab + Tirzepatide|IL-23p19 antibody + dual GLP-1/GIP receptor agonist|NCT06937086|Phase 3b|Started June 2025; estimated completion April 2028| ... |Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "title": "UCSF Inflammatory Bowel Disease Clinical Trials for 2026 ...", "date": "2026-09-24", "text": "The goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. The study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo. The primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "|α4β7 antagonist|PTG-100|Oral α4β7 integrin antagonist peptide|NCT02895100|Phase 2b|Terminated early for futility March 2018| |α4β7 antagonist|PN-943|Oral gastrointestinal-restricted cyclic peptide targeting α4β7|NCT04504383|Phase 2|Completed Feb 2023; primary endpoint not met for 450mg dose| |α4β7 antagonist|Abrilumab|Human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01694485, NCT01959165|Phase 2b|Global study completed; 13.3% remission (70mg) vs 4.3% placebo| ... |α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027| |α4β7 antagonist|MORF-057|Oral small-molecule inhibitor of α4β7 integrin|NCT05291689 (EMERALD-1), NCT05611671 (EMERALD-2)|Phase 2a, Phase 2b|EMERALD-1 completed Nov 2023; EMERALD-2 primary completion Nov 2024| ... Abrilumab, a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, was evaluated in two Phase 2 studies for moderate-to-severe UC. ... Emvistegrast is an oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor designed to block immune cell migration to the gastrointestinal tract, reducing intestinal inflammation in UC patients. A Phase 2 double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter RCT evaluates three different doses of GS-1427 against placebo over 76 weeks, with the primary endpoint measuring clinical response at week 12 defined as a decrease in mMS of at least 2 points with 30% reduction from baseline and improved RBS (NCT06290934). Participants must be adults aged 18–75 with moderately to severely active UC confirmed by endoscopy and histology, having at least 15 cm disease extent from anal verge, modified MS of 5–9 points with MES of at least 2, and inadequate response or intolerance to prior treatments including corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapies, while excluding those with CD, toxic megacolon, prior vedolizumab exposure, or contraindicated medications, with the study completing primary outcomes in October 2025 and final completion estimated for February 2027."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "title": "UCSF Ulcerative Colitis Clinical Trials for 2026", "date": "2026-09-24", "text": "The goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. The study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo. The primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://x.com/drughunter_com/status/1970510609163723171", "domain": "x.com", "title": "drug hunter on X: \"Gilead's Recently Disclosed, Selective α4β7 ...", "date": "2025-09-23", "text": "Gilead’s Recently Disclosed, Selective α4β7 Inhibitor Emvistegrast Advances to Phase 2 for UC | https://drughunters.com/42ESG2P Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is a selective oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Ph. 2 for UC, first disclosed by Gilead’s Leena Patel at the ACS Fall 2025 meeting in Washington, DC. It targets the same clinically validated pathway as vedolizumab (Entyvio®), aiming to deliver gut-selective efficacy in an orally administered drug. This ester prodrug was rationally designed from known α4β7/α4β1 dual inhibitors with picomolar potency in cellular and whole blood assays, 482-fold selectivity over α4β1, and shows on-target engagement comparable to antibody controls in multiple preclinical models. GS-1427 is now in Ph. 2 clinical trials for UC, a welcome advance for the estimated 2 million patients living with recurrent UC."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/trial/NCT06290934", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "title": "GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active ...", "date": "2024-03-04", "text": "UCSF Inflammatory Bowel Disease Trial → GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis 1. Inflammatory Bowel Disease Studies # Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis ... at Oakland, California and other locations ... study started March 2024 study ends around April 2028 ... The goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. The study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo. The primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12. ... A Phase 2, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Multicenter, Dose-Ranging Study Evaluating the Efficacy and Safety of GS-1427 in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (UC) ... **Open to people ages 18-75** - Individuals have Ulcerative Colitis (UC) with symptoms of at least 90 days duration before randomization, with the diagnosis confirmed by endoscopy and histology at any time prior to randomization. Documentation of endoscopy and histology consistent with the diagnosis of UC must be available in the source documents. - Individuals have UC with minimum disease extent of 15 cm from the anal verge. - Individuals have moderately to severely active UC as determined by endoscopy occurring during screening with a total modified Mayo Clinic Score (mMCS) of 5 to 9 points, including a centrally read endoscopic subscore of at least 2. - Individuals have an inadequate response or loss of response or are intolerant to at least 1 of the following UC treatments: corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapy. - Individuals have an inadequate response or loss of response or are intolerant to < 3 AT mechanisms of action for UC (use of 2 or more AT with the same mechanism of action, eg, 2 TNF-α inhibitors, counts as 1 mechanism of action) ... - Have any history of exposure to vedolizumab or other integrin antagonists ... ## Locations - UCSF Benioff Children's Hospital Oakland Oakland California 94609 United States - University Of California, Davis Sacramento California 95817 United States ## Details - **Status** in progress, not accepting new patients - **Start Date** March 2024 - **Completion Date** April 2028 (estimated) - **Sponsor** Gilead Sciences ... ** Gilead Clinical Trials Website - **ID** NCT06290934 - **Phase** Phase 2 research study - **Study Type** Interventional - **Participants** Expecting 228 study participants - **Last Updated**"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development. ... |JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown| ... |α4-integrin antagonist|Carotegrast methyl (AJM300)|Oral small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist|NCT03531892|Phase 3|Completed; 45% clinical response vs 21% placebo at week 8| ... |α4β7 antagonist|PTG-100|Oral α4β7 integrin antagonist peptide|NCT02895100|Phase 2b|Terminated early for futility March 2018| ... |α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027| |α4β7 antagonist|MORF-057|Oral small-molecule inhibitor of α4β7 integrin|NCT05291689 (EMERALD-1), NCT05611671 (EMERALD-2)|Phase 2a, Phase 2b|EMERALD-1 completed Nov 2023; EMERALD-2 primary completion Nov 2024| ... The study led to the current Phase 3 trial (NCT05181137)) which began in November 2021 and, based on its estimated completion date, is anticipated to be ongoing; however, no interim results have been publicly reported to date. ... Carotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist tested in this multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III RCT (NCT03531892) involving 203 patients with moderately active UC who received either 960mg three times daily or placebo for 8 weeks (46). ... Emvistegrast is an oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor designed to block immune cell migration to the gastrointestinal tract, reducing intestinal inflammation in UC patients. A Phase 2 double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter RCT evaluates three different doses of GS-1427 against placebo over 76 weeks, with the primary endpoint measuring clinical response at week 12 defined as a decrease in mMS of at least 2 points with 30% reduction from baseline and improved RBS (NCT06290934). Participants must be adults aged 18–75 with moderately to severely active UC confirmed by endoscopy and histology, having at least 15 cm disease extent from anal verge, modified MS of 5–9 points with MES of at least 2, and inadequate response or intolerance to prior treatments including corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapies, while excluding those with CD, toxic megacolon, prior vedolizumab exposure, or contraindicated medications, with the study completing primary outcomes in October 2025 and final completion estimated for February 2027."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.guidetopharmacology.org/GRAC/LigandDisplayForward?tab=clinical&ligandId=14189", "domain": "guidetopharmacology.org", "title": "emvistegrast | Ligand page", "date": null, "text": "|**Synonyms:** example 356 [WO2020092375] | GS-1427 | GS1427 **Compound class:** Synthetic organic **Comment:** Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is an oral α4/β7 integrin inhibitor. It was designed to reduce α4β7 integrin-mediated recruitment of α4β7-expressing lymphocytes to the gut, as disease-modifying mechanism for inflammatory bowel disease. GS-1427 is one of the inhibitors claimed in patent WO2020092375A1 [1]. ... |Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is a clinical lead candidate. It is being developed (by Gilead Sciences) for immunomodulatory potential in ulcerative colitis.| ... |NCT06290934|Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis|Phase 2 Interventional|Gilead Sciences| | |"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11110, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 4718}, "timing": {"search_ms": 1131.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 47411.8, "total_ms": 48543.5}, "luna_wall_ms": 47411.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Emvistegrast (GS-1427), developed by Gilead Sciences, is an oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Phase 2 for moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis. Its Phase 2 study, NCT06290934, is randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter and dose-ranging; it evaluates three doses against placebo over 76 weeks, with clinical response at Week 12 as the primary endpoint. The stated response definition is a decrease in modified Mayo score of at least 2 points and 30% from baseline, with improved rectal bleeding score. Eligibility includes adults aged 18–75 with UC symptoms for at least 90 days, disease extent of at least 15 cm from the anal verge, modified Mayo score 5–9 with endoscopic subscore at least 2, and inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance to at least one listed UC treatment; the record also specifies inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance to fewer than three advanced-therapy mechanisms. Prior vedolizumab or other integrin-antagonist exposure is excluded. The study expects 228 participants; one listing gives a prior enrollment estimate of 423 revised to 228. LARVOL lists the study as active, not recruiting in one entry and recruiting in another. The Frontiers pipeline table gives primary completion in October 2025 and final completion in February 2027, whereas a UCSF listing estimates completion in April 2028; LARVOL also shows completion-date revisions including April 2028 and February 2027, and primary-completion revisions from May 2027 to October 2025. [E1][E3][E5][E8][E9][E10]\n\nOther UC agents identified in the α4β7/integrin competitive landscape include MORF-057, an oral small-molecule α4β7 inhibitor in Phase 2a and Phase 2b: EMERALD-1 (NCT05291689) completed in November 2023, and EMERALD-2 (NCT05611671) had primary completion in November 2024. [E3][E5][E9] SPY001, developed by Spyre Therapeutics, is described as an investigational small molecule targeting α4β7 for moderate-to-severe UC and is in Phase 2. Its two listed trials are NCT07012395 and NCT07652294; the source describes both as randomized, double-blind and controlled, with combined enrollment of 1,290, and says the latter evaluates long-term safety and efficacy. [E2] PTG-100 is an oral α4β7-antagonist peptide; its Phase 2b study, NCT02895100, terminated early for futility in March 2018. PN-943 is an oral gastrointestinal-restricted cyclic peptide targeting α4β7; its Phase 2 study, NCT04504383, completed in February 2023 and did not meet the primary endpoint for the 450 mg dose. [E3][E5][E9] Abrilumab is a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7; its Phase 2b studies were NCT01694485 and NCT01959165, and the reported remission rates were 13.3% for 70 mg versus 4.3% for placebo. Vatelizumab (SAR339658) is a monoclonal antibody targeting α4β7; its Phase 2 studies were NCT01659138 and NCT01861249 and were terminated early due to recruitment challenges. [E3][E5]\n\nCarotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist, rather than an α4β7-specific agent. Its Phase 3 study, NCT03531892, was completed and involved 203 patients with moderately active UC receiving 960 mg three times daily or placebo for 8 weeks; clinical response at Week 8 was 45% versus 21%, respectively. [E9] The pipeline table also lists OD-07656 plus vedolizumab as a combination of an IRAK4 inhibitor and an α4β7 integrin antagonist in Phase 2a, with a start in June 2025 and estimated completion in November 2026. [E3] Emvistegrast is described as targeting the same clinically validated pathway as vedolizumab (Entyvio). [E7]", "confident_score": 0.76, "missing": "Emvistegrast’s actual dose amounts and dosing schedule, regulatory designations, and clinical efficacy results are not stated. The conflicting completion estimates and trial-status listings are not resolved. For SPY001, trial-specific enrollment and reported efficacy or safety results are not stated. For MORF-057, the sponsor and reported trial outcomes are not stated. Sponsors are not stated for PTG-100, PN-943, Abrilumab, Vatelizumab, carotegrast methyl, or the OD-07656-plus-vedolizumab combination. For PTG-100, PN-943, Abrilumab, Vatelizumab and carotegrast methyl, the evidence does not give a sponsor; for the OD-07656-plus-vedolizumab combination it does not give trial results. Vedolizumab’s development stage and UC trial results are not stated in the pathway comparison. The evidence does not establish whether any of these agents is currently being developed or marketed as a competing therapy as of 2026-10-06.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E5", "E7", "E8", "E9", "E10"], "next_queries": ["emvistegrast GS-1427 UC dose schedule", "emvistegrast GS-1427 UEGW 2026 induction results", "emvistegrast GS-1427 regulatory designations", "emvistegrast GS-1427 α4β7 UC pipeline MORF-057 sponsor results", "emvistegrast GS-1427 UC competitor development status"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 3572, "confident_score": 0.76, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 18, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 8, "cited_domains": ["biopharmawatch.com", "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "delta.larvol.com", "frontiersin.org", "guidetopharmacology.org", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "x.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 76, "ungrounded_tokens": ["Other UC"], "n_ungrounded": 1, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q021", "objective": "Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as S1PR S1PR1 Sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor, including Small Molecule", "anchors": ["Mocravimod"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15173, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12057646/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Targeting the Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Pathway - PMC - NIH", "date": "2024-09-26", "text": "In this review, we revisit the role of the S1P/S1PR axis in the pathogenesis of IBD and discuss efficacy and safety data from clinical trials and real-world reports on the two S1PR modulators, ozanimod and etrasimod, that are currently approved for IBD treatment, and comment on their potential positioning in the IBD day-to-day management. ... Sphingosine-1-phosphate modulators are an emerging promising therapeutic target in the field of IBD. To date, two such drugs, specifically ozanimod and etrasimod have been approved for use in moderate to severe UC treatment. ... |NCT01375179 [83]|UC|Phase 2|18–65 years old, biologic naïve and exposed|27 participants 14% clinical remission for patients in 1.2 mg mocravimod compared to 0% for placebo at week 8 Terminated for not reaching the minimal clinically relevant threshold of 20%|No difference in the distribution of adverse events between mocravimod and placebo groups 1 case of lymphopenia. 1 case of infectious diarrhea| ... ### 4.1. Approved Agents ... Ozanimod (RPC1063) is an orally administered prodrug that is phosphorylated by SphK, and its active metabolite selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5. While ongoing clinical trials (NCT03915769, NCT05644665, NCT05076175) continue to explore the efficacy of ozanimod in treating UC, it has already garnered approval for the management of moderate to severe cases, largely due to the conclusive findings from the TOUCHSTONE and True North studies [54, 55]. ... A Phase 2, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled dose-ranging trial has been conducted to evaluate the safety and efficacy of icanbelimod (CBP-307) a next-generation selective S1PR1 modulator in moderate to severe UC, where 145 participants were randomized (1:1:1) to receive icanbelimod of 0.1 mg (*n* = 39), 0.2 mg (*n* = 53) or placebo treatment (*n* = 53) for 12 weeks of induction and 36 weeks for maintenance [72]. ... Amiselimod (MT-1303) is an orally administered prodrug that functions after its conversion to its active metabolite, amiselimod-P (MT-1303-P), by SphKs and demonstrates increased selectivity for S1PR1, indicating favorable safety features, especially regarding cardiac side effects [76]. ... Mocravimod (KRP-203) is an orally administered prodrug, the active metabolite of which, mocravimod-P (KRP-203-P) is an agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5 and only partially antagonizes S1PR3, leading to reduced risk of bradycardia development, in contrast to the first generation of S1P modulators. ... In a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, randomized (1:1) trial assessing the safety and efficacy of mocravimod in 27 patients with moderately active 5-ASA-resistant UC, mocravimod did not meet the minimum clinical efficacy criteria, i.e., clinical remission defined as partial Mayo Score of 0 or 1 and a modified Baron Score of 0 or 1 with no macroscopic bleeding (partial Mayo sub-score of 0 for rectal bleeding) at a statistically significant threshold compared to placebo ..."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/3YH0N35CE3", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "title": "Mocravimod Hydrochloride - Inxight Drugs", "date": null, "text": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation. This drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated. In addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed. ... ### Approval Year Unknown 149124 ... |Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 7 Target ID:&nbspP21453 Gene ID:&nbsp1901.0 Gene Symbol:&nbspS1PR1 Target Organism:&nbspHomo sapiens (Human) Sources:&nbsphttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|Agonist 2752| | | ... |Ulcerative colitis 125 Sources:&nbsphttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01375179|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown #### Approved Use Unknown| |--|--|--|--|--| ... |Crohn's disease 64 Sources:&nbsphttp://www.clinicaltrials.jp/user/showCteDetailE.jsp?japicId=JapicCTI-132108|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown #### Approved Use Unknown| ... |<table><tr><td> 2-amino-2-[2-(2-chloro-4-{[3-(phenylmethoxy)phenyl]sulfanyl}phenyl)ethyl]propane-1,3-diol hydrochloride</td><td> </td></tr></table>|Systematic Name|English| |<table><tr><td> 1,3-Propanediol, 2-amino-2-[2-[2-chloro-4-[[3-(phenylmethoxy)phenyl]thio]phenyl]ethyl]-, hydrochloride (1:1)</td><td> </td></tr></table>|Systematic Name|English|"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://ctis.eu/trial/2024-512752-38-00", "domain": "ctis.eu", "title": "Adjunctive and… · Phase III (2024-512752-38-00) - CTIS.eu", "date": "2025-09-09", "text": "2024-512752-38-00 ... - **ClinicalTrials.gov** NCT05429632 ... ### Test 1 Mocravimod (KRP203) PRD9710650 · Product - **Active substance** Mocravimod - **Pharmaceutical form** CAPSULES - **Route of administration** ORAL USE - **Max daily dose** 3.00 mg milligram(s) - **Max total dose** 1095.00 mg milligram(s) - **Max treatment duration** 12 Month(s) - **Authorisation status** Not Authorised - **MA holder** PRIOTHERA S.A.S."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/X71GCJ0HLI", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "title": "Mocravimod - Inxight Drugs", "date": null, "text": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation. This drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated. In addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed. ... ### Approval Year Unknown 149124 ... |Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 7 Target ID:&nbspP21453 Gene ID:&nbsp1901.0 Gene Symbol:&nbspS1PR1 Target Organism:&nbspHomo sapiens (Human) Sources:&nbsphttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|Agonist 2752| | | ... |Ulcerative colitis 125 Sources:&nbsphttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01375179|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown #### Approved Use Unknown| |--|--|--|--|--| ... |Crohn's disease 64 Sources:&nbsphttp://www.clinicaltrials.jp/user/showCteDetailE.jsp?japicId=JapicCTI-132108|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown #### Approved Use Unknown|"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12421927/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Efficacy and safety of sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor ...", "date": null, "text": "This meta-analysis supports S1P receptor modulators, particularly ozanimod and etrasimod, as effective and safe treatments for UC. ... Several S1PrMs, including Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have demonstrated efficacy in managing moderate to severe IBD, significantly improving patient health and quality of life. Notably, Ozanimod has been approved in both the United States and the European Union for the treatment of UC, underscoring its therapeutic potential. Its high-affinity binding to S1P receptor subtypes, particularly S1P1 and S1P5, further strengthens its role as a viable treatment option for UC and Crohn’s disease (14). ... Inclusion criteria comprised of randomized controlled trials (RCTs), following the PICO framework: Population—patients diagnosed with ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn’s disease (CD); Intervention—treatment with S1P1 receptor modulators, including Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod; Comparator—placebo or standard treatments for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD); and Outcomes—efficacy (clinical, endoscopic, and histologic remission) and safety (adverse events, serious adverse events, and drug discontinuation rates). ... This meta-analysis highlights the potential role of S1P receptor modulators as an emerging treatment option for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), particularly in ulcerative colitis (UC)."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11383267/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Safety and efficacy of S1P receptor modulators for the ... - PMC", "date": "2024-09-06", "text": "S1PrMs show promising potential for establishing histologic remission, endoscopic improvement, clinical remission, and corticosteroid-free clinical remission. ... Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulators (S1PrMs) are emerging as a effective class of drugs for IBD. These compounds target the S1P receptor, a key regulator of inflammation and immune responses, offering a new approach to cope with the disease mechanism. The S1PrMs such as Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have been effective in treating IBD, from moderate to severe patients and improving their health and lifestyle. ^[6]^ Also, Ozanimod use has been approved in the US and European Union for the UC, a type of IBD, treatment. It shows high affinity binding to the subtypes of S1P receptors like S1P and S5P, proving it a potential therapeutic option for the various forms of IBD, including UC and Crohn’s disease.^[7]^ ... Studies meeting the following criteria were included in this meta-analysis, ... 2. Target population comprising IBD patients, either ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease. ... | |Mocravimod (1.2 mg; n = 17)|N/A|N/A|8 (57.1)|2 (14.28)|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A|N/A| ... Ozanimod and Etrasimod are considered more effective S1P modulators for Ulcerative Colitis than Mocravimod and Amiselimod due to their efficacy, safety profiles, and mechanisms of action. They have demonstrated improved results in clinical trials, including lowering inflammation and improving mucosal repair, with potentially fewer adverse effects."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/mocravimod", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Mocravimod", "date": "2025-06-14", "text": "Mocravimod is a sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor agonist, with potential immunosuppressive activity. Upon administration of mocravimod, this agent binds to S1P receptors on lymphocytes, which prevents binding of serum S1P to S1P receptors and leads to S1P receptor internalization. ... Status of Orphan Designation Positive Decision Date"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8116828/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Targeting Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Signaling in Immune ...", "date": "2021-05-13", "text": "| |S1PR1|S1PR2|S1PR3|S1PR4|S1PR5| |--|--|--|--|--|--| |S1PR agonists|AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, AUY954, cenerimod. ceralifimod, CS-077, CS-2100, CYM544, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK1842799, GSK2018682, mocravimod, ozanimod, Ponesimod, RP-001, SEW2871, siponimod, ST-968, ST-1071, Syl948, TC-G 1006, TC-SP14, amgen 8,14| |Fingolimod, ST-968, ST-1071|Amiselimod, etrasimod, fingolimod|A-971432, AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, cerafimod, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK2018682, ozanimod, siponimod| ... Over the past few years, the S1P/S1PRs axis has been identified as a potential therapeutic target for immune-mediated diseases, and the safety and efficacy of several S1PRs modulators have been explored in MS, RA, SLE, psoriasis, atopic dermatitis (AD), ulcerative colitis (UC), and Crohn’s disease (CD) [23, 36]. ... However, all available agents in use today require parenteral administration. Small molecules that could be orally administered such as S1PR1 agonist/modulators could be a valuable addition. ... Mocravimod/KRP-203 is a prodrug agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5 and partial agonist of S1PR3. Despite attenuating colitis in mouse models [2, 54], it was minimally effective in patients with UC (NCT01375179) [55]. A multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group study evaluated the efficacy, safety, and tolerability of KRP203 in moderately active UC refractory to 5-aminosalicylates over 8 weeks [55]. ... Clinical remission was achieved in 2/14 patients (14%) receiving KRP203 compared with none in the placebo group. This study did not meet the minimum clinically relevant threshold for efficacy [55] and was terminated (NCT01375179); however, its small sample size was a major limitation. Overall, KRP203 was well tolerated and safe, and no adverse cardiac events were reported [55]. ... Ozanimod/RPC1063/Zeposia is an oral S1PR modulator that selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5 [58, 59], reducing concerns around S1PR3 engagement. ... Currently, there are three active clinical trials for moderate-severe UC: NCT02531126, NCT01647516, NCT03915769, three of which are in phase 3. STEPSTONE, a phase 2, uncontrolled multicenter trial, recruited 69 patients with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease (CD), to examine endoscopic and clinical outcomes following treatment with ozanimod for 12 weeks [65]. ... Currently four phase 3 ozanimod clinical trials in CD are ongoing: NCT03467958, NCT03440385, NCT03440372, and NCT03464097. ... Etrasimod**/** APD334, an oral selective modulator of S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5, has shown efficacy in the CD4+CD45RBhi T-cell transfer mouse model of colitis [71]. Recently, the results from a phase 2, double-blind, parallel-group study in patients with moderate to severe UC (*n* = 156) have been published (NCT02447302) [72]. ... Currently, Arena Pharma is evaluating etrasimod’s use in other trials such as a phase 3 extension study for the treatment of moderate to severely active UC (ELEVATE OLE UC; NCT03950232) ..."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.guidetopharmacology.org/GRAC/LigandDisplayForward?tab=clinical&ligandId=9727", "domain": "guidetopharmacology.org", "title": "mocravimod | Ligand page", "date": "2023-01-01", "text": "|Synonyms: compound 2 [PMID: 23124563] | KRP-203 | KRP203 Compound class: Synthetic organic Comment: Mocravimod (KRP203) is a sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor type 1 (S1P 1R) agonist with immunomodulatory potential [6]. Structurally mocravimod is a highly modified analogue of the approved multiple sclerosis drug fingolimod. ... Mocravimod was also tested in autoimmune indications (ulcerative colitis, subacute cutaneous Lupus erythematosus and Crohn's disease).| ... |NCT01375179|Efficacy & Safety in Moderately Active Refractory Ulcerative Colitis Patients|Phase 2 Interventional|Novartis|"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB18151", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "title": "Mocravimod", "date": "2023-09-12", "text": "## Identification - Generic Name - Mocravimod ... Target Actions Organism USphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1agonist Humans ... **Drug product information from 10+ global regions**Our datasets provide approved product information including: **dosage, form, labeller, route of administration, and marketing period.**Access drug product information from over 10 global regions. - Product Ingredients ... **1. Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1** - Kind - Protein - Organism - Humans - Pharmacological action - Unknown - Actions - Agonist - General Function ... G-protein coupled receptor for the bioactive lysosphingolipid sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) that seems to be coupled to the G(i) subclass of heteromeric G proteins. Signaling leads to the activation of RAC1, SRC, PTK2/FAK1 and MAP kinases."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10940, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 5164}, "timing": {"search_ms": 1139.8, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 53348.5, "total_ms": 54488.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 53348.5, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "Mocravimod (KRP-203) is an orally administered prodrug; its active metabolite is an agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5. One review says it partially antagonizes S1PR3, while another says it is a partial S1PR3 agonist. Mocravimod is also listed as a synthetic organic compound and an S1PR1 agonist. [E1][E8][E9] For ulcerative colitis (UC), its Phase 2 study NCT01375179 enrolled 27 participants aged 18–65 who were biologic-naïve or biologic-exposed and had moderately active, 5-ASA-resistant disease. The multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group trial randomized participants 1:1; at week 8, clinical remission was 14% with mocravimod 1.2 mg versus 0% with placebo. Remission was defined as partial Mayo Score 0 or 1 and modified Baron Score 0 or 1, with no macroscopic bleeding and a partial Mayo rectal-bleeding sub-score of 0. The study did not reach its minimum clinically relevant threshold of 20% and was terminated. A separate meta-analysis table lists mocravimod 1.2 mg with n=17 and the figures 8 (57.1) and 2 (14.28), without outcome labels in the excerpt. Another review reports remission in 2/14 patients receiving KRP-203 versus none on placebo, says the study was terminated for not meeting the minimum efficacy threshold, notes small sample size as a limitation, and reports that it was well tolerated with no adverse cardiac events. Mocravimod has also been investigated in Phase 2 studies for Crohn’s disease, which are listed as successfully completed. [E1][E2][E4][E6][E8][E9] Other agents listed as S1PR1 agonists are AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, AUY954, cenerimod, ceralifimod, CS-077, CS-2100, CYM544, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK1842799, GSK2018682, ozanimod, Ponesimod, RP-001, SEW2871, siponimod, ST-968, ST-1071, Syl948, TC-G 1006, TC-SP14 and amgen 8,14. [E8] For UC, ozanimod (RPC1063) is an oral prodrug phosphorylated by SphK; its active metabolite selectively binds S1PR1 and S1PR5, and it is approved for moderate-to-severe UC. The review lists UC trials NCT03915769, NCT05644665 and NCT05076175. It also describes a Phase 2 uncontrolled multicenter study of ozanimod in 69 people with moderate-to-severe Crohn’s disease, examining endoscopic and clinical outcomes after 12 weeks, and lists four ongoing Phase 3 Crohn’s disease trials: NCT03467958, NCT03440385, NCT03440372 and NCT03464097. [E1][E8] Etrasimod (APD334) is an oral selective modulator of S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5 and is approved for moderate-to-severe UC. A Phase 2 double-blind, parallel-group UC study enrolled 156 participants (NCT02447302); the review also names a Phase 3 extension study, ELEVATE OLE UC (NCT03950232), and says Arena Pharma was evaluating etrasimod in trials including that extension. [E1][E8] Icanbelimod (CBP-307) is a next-generation selective S1PR1 modulator studied in a Phase 2 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled dose-ranging trial in moderate-to-severe UC. Of 145 randomized participants, 39 received 0.1 mg, 53 received 0.2 mg and 53 received placebo; the study included 12 weeks of induction and 36 weeks of maintenance. [E1] Amiselimod (MT-1303) is an orally administered prodrug converted by SphKs to amiselimod-P (MT-1303-P), with increased selectivity for S1PR1; the review notes favorable safety features, especially regarding cardiac side effects. It is also included among S1P receptor modulators considered in reviews of IBD. [E1][E5][E6]", "confident_score": 0.74, "missing": "For the additional S1PR1 agonists listed in the 2021 review, the evidence does not establish which are competing agents for Crohn’s disease or UC, their sponsors, development stages, disease-specific trials or results, or routes, doses and schedules. For amiselimod, evidence does not give Crohn’s disease/UC-specific trial identifiers, enrolment, stage or results. For icanbelimod, evidence does not give a trial identifier, sponsor or results. For ozanimod and etrasimod, the evidence does not provide the enrolment, eligibility, dose, schedule or results for the listed disease trials; it also does not give ozanimod’s Crohn’s-trial results or etrasimod’s Crohn’s disease trial details. For mocravimod’s Crohn’s disease Phase 2 studies, the evidence does not give trial identifiers, enrolment, eligibility, dose, results or sponsor; the UC study sponsor is also not consistently established. The UC study’s reported mocravimod treatment-group size differs across sources (14 versus 17), and the 17-participant table’s outcome labels are not provided. The S1PR3 action is stated differently across reviews: partial antagonism versus partial agonism.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E8", "E9"], "next_queries": ["Mocravimod Crohn's disease Phase 2 trial registry results", "Mocravimod NCT01375179 treatment group enrollment outcome", "Mocravimod S1PR1 agonist Crohn's UC pipeline competitors", "Mocravimod amiselimod UC Crohn's trial registry", "Mocravimod S1PR3 partial agonist antagonist mechanism"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 3411, "confident_score": 0.74, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 18, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 7, "cited_domains": ["drugs.ncats.io", "guidetopharmacology.org", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 84, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q041", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication DB-3Q, Direct Biologics LLC, Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease", "anchors": ["DB-3Q"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 16428, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07625293", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "title": "Study Details | NCT07625293 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "date": "2026-06-12", "text": "## DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease ClinicalTrials.gov ID ** NCT07625293 Sponsor ** Direct Biologics, LLC ... Last Update Posted ** 2026-06-12 **Not yet recruiting** ** ... This research study is studying DB-3Q as a possible treatment for medically refractory Crohn's disease. The purpose of this study is to research and evaluate safety and effectiveness of the administration of bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular vesicles product, DB-3Q, the study drug for Crohn's disease. Official Title A Phase 2a Study of DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease Conditions ** Crohn Disease (CD) Crohn Disease (CD) Crohn Disease (CD) Crohn Disease (CD) Intervention/Treatment ** - Biological: DB-3Q - Other: Placebo Other Study ID Numbers ** - 3Q-CD-201 Study Start (Estimated) ** 2026-06 Primary Completion (Estimated) ** 2027-06 Study Completion (Estimated) ** 2027-12 Enrollment (Estimated) ** 36 Study Type ** Interventional Phase ** Phase 2 ... Study Contact ** **Name: ** Bill Arana **Phone Number: ** 15123547124 **Email: ** barana@directbiologics.com This study has 1 location United States Missouri Locations **St Louis, Missouri, United States, 63110** Washington University, St. Louis Contact : Study Coordinator 314-273-0301 luluhuang@wustl.edu ... Inclusion Criteria: 1. Written informed consent from participant 2. Males and females 18-75 years of age 3. Diagnosed with Crohn's disease of at least 6 months duration with medically refractory symptoms that failed to respond or responded but recurred after one advanced immunologic therapy (must have been receiving at least one advanced immunological therapy for 14 weeks duration prior to screening, including, but not limited to, adalimumab, certolizumab, , infliximab, risankizumab, upadacitinib, ustekinumab and vedolizumab), or is intolerant, or has a contraindication to advanced immunological therapy with a next step of subtotal colectomy or escalation in medical management 4. Active CD as defined by a CDAI score ≥ 220 and/or SES-CD score ≥ 4 5. Exposure to corticosteroids, 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) drugs, thiopurines, methotrexate, anti-TNF therapy, anti-integrin and anti-interleukin in the past are permitted but a washout period of 8 weeks for any monoclonal antibody is necessary ... Exclusion Criteria: 1. Lack of informed consent 2. Pregnant woman, woman of childbearing potential without a documented negative urine or serum pregnancy test, or woman who is breast feeding 3. Clinically significant medical conditions within the six months before initial administration of IMP: e.g., myocardial infarction, active angina, congestive heart failure or other conditions that would, in the opinion of the investigators, compromise the safety of the participant 4. Confirmed Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV), Hepatitis B, or Hepatitis C infections ... 12. Microscopic, ischemic or infectious colitis ... |Participant Group/Arm ..."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT06918808", "domain": "ichgcp.net", "title": "DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease", "date": "2025-06-13", "text": "# DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease ... ## A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease This is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease. ... #### Status Recruiting ... #### Study Type Interventional #### Enrollment (Estimated) 36 #### Phase - Phase 2 ... New York New York, New York, United States, 10032 - Recruiting - Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NYPH Contact: - Claudia Musat ... #### Ages Eligible for Study ... Inclusion Criteria: - Written informed consent from participant - Men and women 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn's Disease for at least six months duration prior to Day 1 - Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively draining - Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year including, but not limited to, antibiotics, immunomodulators (6-MP, methotrexate, azathioprine), monoclonal antibodies (adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab, guselkumab, infliximab, risankizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab), or small molecule inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) - Previous failed surgical intervention, including seton placement at least two weeks prior to screening, or are not candidates for surgical intervention or are not willing to undergo surgical intervention for the management of their fistula - Medical therapy for CD stable for at least 2 months prior to Day 1 (Changes in dosing or dosing intervals related to serum drug levels are not permitted) ... **Primary Purpose**: Treatment **Allocation**: Randomized **Interventional Model**: Sequential Assignment **Masking**: Quadruple ... |--|--| |Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.| |Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl| |Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.| |Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl| |Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 ml IV and DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.| |Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 ml IV and Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl| ... |## Outcome ..."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/news/columbia-enrolls-nations-first-patient-direct-biologics-phase-2-clinical-trial-fistulizing", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "title": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 ...", "date": null, "text": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 Clinical Trial for Fistulizing Crohn's Disease | Columbia Surgery ... Columbia University Irving Medical Center has enrolled the first patient in a multicenter Phase 2 clinical trial investigating the effectiveness of Direct Biologics' proprietary extracellular vesicle technology (DB-3Q, extracellular vesicles (EVs) secreted from bone marrow-derived mesenchymal stem cells) for the treatment of perianal fistulizing Crohn's Disease. ... \"This study aims to determine if this approach is a viable option to treat anal fistula in patients with Crohn's disease specifically.\" ... EVs may offer a game-changing avenue for treating autoimmune diseases, with their ability to repair damaged tissues thanks to their anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties. \"The advantages of EVs over traditional stem cell therapies are multiple”, notes Dr. Zoccali. \"While derived from stem cells, it is a much more stable product, hence easier to store and administer. It's also less immunogenic, which can mitigate some of the problems associated with stem cell treatment due to the body's response to foreign DNA.\" This phase 2 multicenter randomized study will have multiple cohorts with three different doses and administration routes. The first uses an initial dose administered in the operating room via direct injection near the fistula. The second phase will double that dose. The third phase will include local injections and add an IV infusion of the medication. ... We are very optimistic that this trial will determine the appropriate dosage to help patients with this novel modality.\""}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/sites/default/files/2025-03/colorectal-ibd-trials-2025.pdf", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "title": "[PDF] Claudia Musat, MD - Columbia Surgery", "date": null, "text": "DB-3Q: Bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular vesicles product in Patients with Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease (CD) PI: Marco Zoccali, MD IRB: AAAV6645 Disease: Perianal Fistulas due to Crohn's Disease (CD) Treatment: DB-3Q (a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell product) or Placebo The purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of ascending doses of DB-3Q, a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived secretome with extracellular vesicles (EVs) isolate product, via direct injection and intravenous (IV) administration, as treatment for participants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease (PFCD). The investigational product is experimental and has not been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The experimental study drug is a biologic product which is purified from the bone marrow of a healthy well-screened individual. 36 participants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease, randomized 3:1 to IMP or placebo in blocks of 4 participants (3 randomized to IMP, and 1 randomized to placebo) such that each cohort will have 12 participants (9 randomized to IMP and 3 randomized to placebo). Your participation in this study will be approximately 24 weeks long. Eligibility: 1. 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn’s Disease for at least six months duration prior to Day 1 2. Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively draining 3. Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year 4. Previous failed surgical intervention or not interested in surgical intervention 5. NO ileal anal pouch and/or history of proctectomy and severe proctitis"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/7ab73070909872e45913d775d2ff7416", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Direct Biologics LLC - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "date": null, "text": "|Active Indication Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Acute [+1]| |Inactive Indication Colitis, Ulcerative [+7]| |Drug Highest PhasePhase 3| ... |Active Indication Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease| |Inactive Indication-| |Drug Highest PhasePhase 2| ... 12 Clinical Trials associated with Direct Biologics LLC NCT06918808 / Not yet recruitingPhase 2 A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease This is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease. |Start Date15 Apr 2025| |--| |Sponsor / Collaborator Direct Biologics LLC| NCT05127122 / WithdrawnPhase 1/2 Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles Infusion Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome: A Phase I/II Clinical Trial ... |Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles (Direct Biologics)|Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Adult More|Phase 3| |Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)|Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease More|Phase 2|"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://fdaaa.trialstracker.net/trial/NCT06918808/", "domain": "fdaaa.trialstracker.net", "title": "NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC", "date": null, "text": "## NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC This trial is ongoing. It must report results 1 year, 10 months from now. ... |Full entry on ClinicalTrials.gov|NCT06918808| ... |Title|**A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease**| |Results Status|Ongoing| |ACT or pACT?|This is what FDAAA officially calls an \"Applicable Clinical Trial\"| |Start date|May 19, 2025| |Completion date|July 30, 2027| |Required reporting date|July 29, 2028, midnight| |Actual reporting date|None|"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/76f5bfcac1da44b8ac9629daf1034a48", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived ...", "date": null, "text": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics) ... Drug Type Exosomes, Mesenchymal stem cell therapy Synonyms DB-3Q ... Active Indication Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease Inactive Indication Originator Organization Direct Biologics LLC ... Clinical Trials associated with Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics) NCT06918808 Recruiting Phase 2 A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease This is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease. Start Date 19 May 2025 Sponsor / Collaborator Direct Biologics LLC 100 ... Indication Highest Phase Country/Location Organization Date Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease Phase 2 United States Direct Biologics LLC 19 May 2025 ... No Data"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://mtec-sc.org/life-sciences/direct-biologics", "domain": "mtec-sc.org", "title": "Direct Biologics | MTEC", "date": "2025-02-13", "text": "Developer of investigational secretome-based biologics derived from adult bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (MSC). The company focuses on cGMP manufacturing of extracellular vesicle (EV)-enriched secretome products, clinical trial execution across phases, and multi-omics characterization using AI-enabled analytics to support mechanism-of-action and potency assay development. Programs target conditions with unmet needs including ARDS and inflammatory bowel disease. ... ### DB-3Q — Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease Investigational secretome-based product candidate being evaluated in clinical trials for perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease (Phase II noted). ### DB-3Q — Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease Investigational secretome-based product candidate under clinical evaluation for medically refractory Crohn's disease. ... ### DB-3Q An investigational secretome-based product being evaluated in clinical trials for Perianal Fistulizing Crohn’s Disease and Medically Refractory Crohn’s Disease."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/direct-biologics_our-clinical-development-programs-span-multiple-activity-7490811433820266496-1073", "domain": "linkedin.com", "title": "Direct Biologics Clinical Research Programs", "date": "2026-08-05", "text": "Our clinical development programs span multiple investigational products, indications, and stages of research. As shown in our pipeline, Direct Biologics currently has Phase 2 through Phase 3 clinical research programs underway. ... Here are our current trials: Extracellular Vesicle Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) (EXTINGUISH ARDS) https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05354141?intr=exoflo&rank=7 DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06918808?lead=Direct%20Biologics DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease Like"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.clareohealth.com/clinical-trials/NCT02914561", "domain": "clareohealth.com", "title": "Filgotinib in the Induction and Maintenance of Remission in Adults ...", "date": "2016-10-31", "text": "RECRUITINGPhase 2 ### DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease NCT06918808 · Perianal Fistula Due to Crohn's Disease ... ### Trial snapshot StatusCOMPLETED PhasePHASE3 Enrollment1,372 Start dateOct 2016 Last updatedDec 18, 2023 ### Condition Crohn's Disease ### Locations shown Digestive Health Specialists of the Southeast Dothan, Alabama Arizona Digestive Health - Sun City Sun City, Arizona Southern California Research Center, Inc. Coronado, California"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10646, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 2355}, "timing": {"search_ms": 785.7, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 25316.1, "total_ms": 26101.8}, "luna_wall_ms": 25316.1, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "DB-3Q is a bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell-derived extracellular vesicle product being studied for medically refractory Crohn’s disease in Phase 2a trial NCT07625293 (3Q-CD-201), with estimated enrollment of 36. ClinicalTrials.gov lists the trial as not yet recruiting; estimated study start is 2026-06, estimated primary completion is 2027-06, and estimated study completion is 2027-12. Eligibility includes males and females aged 18–75 with Crohn’s disease for at least 6 months and active disease defined by CDAI ≥220 and/or SES-CD ≥4; participants must have failed or relapsed after one advanced immunologic therapy, or be intolerant of or contraindicated for such therapy with subtotal colectomy or escalation of medical management as the next step. Named qualifying therapies include adalimumab, certolizumab, infliximab, risankizumab, upadacitinib, ustekinumab, and vedolizumab. Prior exposure to corticosteroids, 5-aminosalicylic acid drugs, thiopurines, methotrexate, anti-TNF therapy, anti-integrin therapy, and anti-interleukin therapy is permitted; monoclonal antibodies require an 8-week washout. [E1]", "confident_score": 0.55, "missing": "Named competing agents in the same mechanistic class for medically refractory Crohn’s disease; DB-3Q’s specific target, route, dose, and schedule in this indication; and DB-3Q trial results.", "citations": ["E1"], "next_queries": ["DB-3Q same-class competitors medically refractory Crohn's disease", "DB-3Q mechanism target extracellular vesicles Crohn's disease", "DB-3Q NCT07625293 dose route schedule", "DB-3Q NCT07625293 results efficacy safety"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 1116, "confident_score": 0.55, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 1, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 1, "cited_domains": ["clinicaltrials.gov"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 16, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q025", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanism of action as HEMAY007, targeting TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha", "anchors": ["HEMAY007"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 15296, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Table 1.", "date": null, "text": "|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown| |JAK Inhibitor|Itacitinib (INCB039110)|JAK1 inhibitor|NCT03627052|Phase 2|Withdrawn Nov 2019 due to lack of recruitment| ... |JAK Inhibitor|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|Oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; advanced to alopecia areata trials| ... |TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026| ... |TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties| |TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026| ... |PDE4 inhibitor|Mufemilast (Hemay005)|Selective phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor|NCT05486104|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2025|"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100830.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "This summary aims to give you an overview of the information contained in this", "date": null, "text": "Phase II trials have been completed for ankylosing spondylitis (“AS”), with additional trials ongoing or planned for ulcerative colitis (“UC”), atopic dermatitis (“AD”), psoriatic arthritis (“PsA”), Crohn’s disease (“CD”), and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (“COPD”). ... Hemay007*(10)(11) ... Mufemilast** ... For Hemay007, both Phase II clinical trials were approved based on results derived from the same Phase I clinical trial, Hemay007AU01. ... Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator. In the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the testing subjects, and pathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue almost returned to a normal state. As of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed the Phase II clinical trial for monotherapy for UC. A Phase II clinical trial for rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is ongoing. For phase II clinical trial of Hemay007 for UC at week 12, clinical response rates, as evaluated by both central laboratory and principal investigator (PI) assessments, there was no statistically significant differences across all groups in both the FAS and PPS populations (P > 0.05 for all comparisons). Clinical remission rates at Week 12 were also comparable among the treatment and placebo groups, with no statistically significant differences observed. For the development of Hemay007, we will prioritize the clinical trial for RA. Currently, we do not have a definitive timetable for the next steps in UC."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108622/a133802/sehk26060801670_c.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "[PDF] 概覽", "date": null, "text": "強直性脊柱炎（「AS」）的II期試驗，針對潰瘍性結腸炎（「UC」）、特應性皮 ... Hemay007*(10)(11) ... III期 ... (10) Hemay007的兩項II期臨床試驗均基於同一項I期臨床試驗(Hemay007AU01)所得的結果而獲得批准。 ... 我們僅針對Hemay007、Hemay808及Hemay022的開發進行研究。 ... Hemay007是通過多種藥理功能亞結構組合化合物庫篩選而來，是一種TNF-α小 分子調節劑。在自發性UC猴模型中，Hemay007減少了受試者結腸中的潰瘍數量，治 療後對腸道進行病理檢查發現，結腸組織幾乎恢復到正常狀態。截至最後實際可行日 期，我們已完成單藥治療UC的II期臨床試驗。我們正在進行類風濕關節炎(RA)的II期 臨床試驗。就Hemay007用於UC的II期臨床試驗第12週而言，無論是透過中央實驗室 評估還是主要研究者(PI)評估，所有組別在FAS及PPS人群中的臨床反應率均未顯示出 統計學上的顯著差異（所有比較P>0.05）。第12週的臨床緩解率在治療組與安慰劑組之 間亦相當，未觀察到統計學上的顯著差異。目前，我們並無就UC的下一步制定正式"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21). ... |TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties| |TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026| ... A Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension. The primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents. The study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov. ... Mufemilast (Hemay005) is a selective phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor being tested in a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II RCT (NCT05486104) with three arms comparing 45mg twice daily, 60mg twice daily, and placebo over 12 weeks of treatment followed by 4 weeks of observation. ... The study started in November 2022 with an estimated completion date of December 2025, and results are not yet available as the trial is ongoing but no longer recruiting participants."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c6cef31360b74c8b9fd3d4c563740a9e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "title": "Hemay007 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "date": null, "text": "Synonyms Target TNF-α Action inhibitors Mechanism TNF-α inhibitors(Tumor necrosis factor α inhibitors) ... Colitis, Ulcerative Inflammatory Bowel Diseases ... Active Organization Xiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. Tianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Technology Co., Ltd. Inactive Organization Hemay Pharmaceutical Pty Ltd License Organization Drug Highest Phase Phase 2 ... A Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind Phase 2 Study to Evaluate the Safety, Efficacy, Pharmacokinetics (PK) and Pharmacodynamics (PD) of Hemay007 in Patients With Moderate to Severe Rheumatoid Arthritis ... NCT03977480 Terminated Phase 2 A Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo Parallel Controlled Clinical Study on the Effecacy and Safety of Different Dosing Regimens of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis This study adopts a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, low-medium-high dose group and placebo parallel controlled clinical study design. ... CTR20182150 Terminated Phase 2 Hemay007不同剂量给药方案用于活动性溃疡性结肠炎患者有效性和安全性多中心、随机、双盲、安慰剂平行对照临床研究 ... A multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study on the efficacy and safety of different dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with active ulcerative colitis"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/category/target-protein/tnf-alpha", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "title": "TNF-Alpha Proteins, Antibodies & ELISA Kits", "date": "2024-09-23", "text": "|Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase 2 Clinical|Tianjin Hemay Bio-Tech Co Ltd|Arthritis, Rheu … ... |Balinatunfib|SAR-441566; SAR441566|Phase 2 Clinical|Sanofi|Arthritis, Rheumatoid; Colitis, Ulcerative; Crohn Disease; Plaque psoriasis; Psoriasis|Details| |Brivekimig|SAR-442970; SAR442970|Phase 2 Clinical|Sanofi|Colitis, Ulcerative; Crohn Disease; Diabetes Mellitus, Type 1; Glomerulonephritis; Glomerulosclerosis, Focal Segmental; Hidradenitis Suppurativa; Inflammation; Nephrosis|Details|"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100842.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "OVERVIEW", "date": null, "text": "commence Phase II clinical trials in China, with first patient enrolment expected in 2026. We have obtained orphan drug designation in the United States from the FDA ... Our key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator. ... Hemay007 Hemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator. In the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys. ... UC ������������ ... Supplemental IND approval obtained (200mg) ... 2021 Phase II completed ... Phase II: – Initiated: April 2021 – Expected completion: 2026(1) ... For the development of Hemay007, we will prioritize the clinical trial for RA. Currently, we do not have a definitive timetable for the next steps in UC. We do not currently have plans to initiate any additional clinical trials overseas in respect of Hemay007. ... By inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly TNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation. ... UC (Phase II) Design. The clinical trial was a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo- controlled, parallel-group study designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and dose-response relationship of various dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with UC. A total of 70 patients with UC were enrolled and randomized into four parallel groups: 16 patients received Hemay007 400 mg twice daily (BID), 18 patients received 800 mg once daily (QD), 17 patients received 600 mg BID, and 19 patients received a placebo. 63 subjects (90.0%) completed the double-blind induction treatment phase, with 13, 18, 15, and 17 subjects in the respective groups. Following the double-blind period, all patients entered a 12-week open-label extension phase with Hemay007 at 600 mg twice daily. ... applications (20mg tablets and 100mg tablets) with"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://assetmemo.bio/drug/hemay007", "domain": "assetmemo.bio", "title": "Hemay007: TNF-alpha, Phase 2 in Ulcerative Colitis", "date": null, "text": "Discontinuedsponsor decision ... Discontinued, for reasons the sponsor has not stated Hemay007 is Tianjin Hemay's oral candidate. Its only ulcerative colitis study, the Phase 2 NCT03977480, is TERMINATED on ClinicalTrials.gov for failure to recruit. Scoped to the ulcerative colitis market only: the molecule continued elsewhere, with a Phase 2 rheumatoid arthritis study (NCT05247216) completed and last updated 2024-03, and no Crohn's disease study was ever registered. The trials below are preserved as the program's evidence history; no evidence signal is assigned to a program the sponsor has stopped. ... 107 competitors in this universe: 22 direct rivals (same mechanism and indication), 5 same-class, 80 same-indication; 76 further along in development. |Drug|Stage|Modality|Mechanism|Evidence signal|Reported efficacygraded featured trials| |--|--|--|--|--|--| |Hemay007 — this drug|Phase 2|Small molecule|TNF-alpha| | | |Adalimumab (Humira)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Aacf (Idacio)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Aaty (Yuflyma)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Adaz (Hyrimoz)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Adbm (Cyltezo)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Afzb (Abrilada)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Aqvh (Yusimry)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Atto (Amjevita)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Bwwd (Hadlima)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Fkjp (Hulio)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Adalimumab-Ryvk (Simlandi)|Approved|—|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Certolizumab Pegol (Cimzia)|Approved|Antibody fragment|**TNF-alpha**| | | |CT-P13|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Golimumab (Simponi)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | | |Infliximab (Remicade)|Approved|Monoclonal antibody|**TNF-alpha**| | |"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/A1171-TNF-alpha.html", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "title": "TNF-alpha - ACROBiosystems", "date": "2018-01-19", "text": "|Infliximab|TA-650|Approved|Janssen, Merck Sharp & Dohme, Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma|REMICADE|fda|Ulcerative colitis, Psoriatic arthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), Crohn's disease|CENTOCOR INC|1998-08-24|Pediatric ulcerative colitis, Psoriasis vulgaris, Crohn's disease, Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki syndrome), Psoriatic erythroderma, Ankylosing Spondylitis (AS), Uveitis, Pustular psoriasis, Ulcerative colitis, Psoriatic arthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), Behcet's disease, Pediatric Crohn's disease|Details| ... |Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase Ⅱ|Tianjin Hemay Biotech|Ulcerative colitis|Details|"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "The introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21). ... |JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown| ... |TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties| ... |Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination. DUET-UC primary completion May 2025| ... Despite positive Phase 3 results demonstrating a favorable benefit-risk profile, ontamalimab was not submitted for regulatory approval. Following Takeda’s acquisition of Shire, development was discontinued due to inability to find a licensee for the compound, rather than efficacy or safety concerns. ... The following section reviews investigational TNF pathway inhibitors currently in Phase II and III clinical development for moderate to severe UC. #### 2.3.1. Hemay007 A Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension. The primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents. The study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11095, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 6186}, "timing": {"search_ms": 548.1, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 64623.6, "total_ms": 65171.7}, "luna_wall_ms": 64623.6, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "TNF-α-targeting UC competitors include infliximab (Remicade), adalimumab (Humira), and golimumab (Simponi), which are biologic therapies targeting TNF-α for moderate to severe UC [E4]. The UC-market listing also identifies approved TNF-α agents adalimumab-Aacf (Idacio), adalimumab-Aaty (Yuflyma), adalimumab-Adaz (Hyrimoz), adalimumab-Adbm (Cyltezo), adalimumab-Afzb (Abrilada), adalimumab-Aqvh (Yusimry), adalimumab-Atto (Amjevita), adalimumab-Bwwd (Hadlima), adalimumab-Fkjp (Hulio), adalimumab-Ryvk (Simlandi), certolizumab pegol (Cimzia), and CT-P13; the listing specifies monoclonal antibodies for adalimumab and its Adbm form, an antibody fragment for certolizumab pegol, and TNF-α as the target for these agents [E8]. Infliximab is also listed as approved for UC [E9]. Brivekimig (SAR442970) is a Sanofi Phase 2 clinical agent listed for UC [E6]; it is a bispecific pentavalent nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L, with trial NCT06975722 listed as Phase 2b, started July 2025, with estimated completion in December 2026 [E1][E4]. ACROBiosystems lists SAR-442970 as Phase 2 clinical, while the later-dated pipeline article lists Phase 2b [E1][E4][E6]. The source also lists balinatunfib (SAR-441566) as a Sanofi Phase 2 clinical agent for UC, but does not state its mechanism [E6]. Other UC agents named as competitive contrasts include vedolizumab, targeting integrin α4β7, and ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, and risankizumab, targeting IL-12/23 pathways [E4]; ivarmactinib (SHR0302), a selective JAK1 inhibitor, with NCT03675477 and NCT05181137, Phase 2 and Phase 3 development, Phase 2 completed in February 2021 and Phase 3 status unknown; itacitinib (INCB039110), a JAK1 inhibitor, NCT03627052, Phase 2, withdrawn in November 2019 due to recruitment difficulties; brepocitinib (PF-06700841), an oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor, NCT02958865 (VIBRATO), Phase 2b, completed in May 2021 and advanced to alopecia areata trials; and D-2570, a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor, NCT07035041, Phase 2, recruiting, started May 2025 with estimated completion in December 2026 [E1]. Mufemilast (Hemay005) is a selective PDE4 inhibitor in Phase 2, NCT05486104; its UC study compares 45 mg twice daily, 60 mg twice daily, and placebo over 12 weeks, followed by 4 weeks of observation, started in November 2022, with estimated completion in December 2025, and is no longer recruiting [E1][E4]. The guselkumab-plus-golimumab combination is described as an IL-23p19 antibody plus TNF antagonist; VEGA (NCT03662542) is Phase 2 and completed in November 2021 with an 83% response, while DUET-UC (NCT05242484) is Phase 2b with primary completion in May 2025 [E10]. Ontamalimab had positive Phase 3 results but was not submitted for regulatory approval; its development was discontinued after the acquisition of Shire because a licensee could not be found, rather than for efficacy or safety concerns [E10]. Assetmemo counts 107 competitors in its UC universe, including 22 direct rivals, 5 same-class agents, and 80 same-indication agents, and states that 76 are further along in development [E8]. HEMAY007 is a small-molecule TNF-α regulator and oral candidate; its Phase 2 UC study NCT03977480 was multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, and terminated early in August 2022 due to recruitment difficulties [E1][E4][E8]. The 12-week study used 400 mg twice daily, 800 mg once daily, 600 mg twice daily, or placebo, followed by a 12-week open-label extension in which patients received 600 mg twice daily [E4][E7]. It enrolled 70 of a planned 288 patients: 16 received 400 mg twice daily, 18 received 800 mg once daily, 17 received 600 mg twice daily, and 19 received placebo; 63 (90.0%) completed the double-blind induction phase, with 13, 18, 15, and 17 completing in those respective groups [E7][E10]. Eligibility required age 18–70 years, active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2), and treatment failure or intolerance to 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents; the primary outcome was clinical response defined by an MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement [E10]. At week 12, clinical response rates by both central laboratory and principal investigator assessment showed no statistically significant differences across groups in the FAS or PPS populations (P>0.05 for all comparisons); clinical remission rates were also comparable between treatment and placebo groups, with no statistically significant differences [E2][E3]. The company prioritizes HEMAY007 development for RA and has no definitive timetable for the next UC steps [E2][E7].", "confident_score": 0.74, "missing": "For the named UC competitors, the evidence does not provide competitor-specific trial registry IDs, enrolment, endpoints, outcomes, doses or routes for the approved TNF-α agents; it also does not provide these clinical details for balinatunfib or most of the named cross-class UC agents. The mechanism for balinatunfib is not stated. HEMAY007 regulatory designations are not stated. Direct comparative results between HEMAY007 and the named competitors are not stated.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E6", "E7", "E8", "E9", "E10"], "next_queries": ["HEMAY007 NCT03977480 trial results enrollment endpoints", "HEMAY007 regulatory designation FDA", "HEMAY007 UC TNF-alpha competitors trial registry outcomes", "HEMAY007 UC dose route schedule clinical study", "HEMAY007 balinatunfib SAR-441566 mechanism UC"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 4591, "confident_score": 0.74, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 29, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 9, "cited_domains": ["acrobiosystems.com", "assetmemo.bio", "frontiersin.org", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "www1.hkexnews.hk"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 101, "ungrounded_tokens": ["Sanofi Phase", "February"], "n_ungrounded": 2, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 5, "next_queries_subject_first": 5}}
{"row_id": "q038", "objective": "KRAS G12D drugs approved against the target and their regulatory approval status", "anchors": ["ZE980277"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": "target", "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 7219, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=1124625", "domain": "accessdata.fda.gov", "title": "Search Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals - FDA", "date": null, "text": "|Generic Name:|KRAS G12D inhibitor| |--|--| |Date Designated:|05/01/2026| |Orphan Designation:|treatment of pancreatic cancer| |Orphan Designation Status:|Designated| |FDA Orphan Approval Status:|Not FDA Approved for Orphan Indication|"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "date": "2026-09-24", "text": "|FDA approves sotorasib with panitumumab for KRAS G12C-mutated colorectal cancer|On January 16, 2025, the Food and Drug Administration approved sotorasib (Lumakras, Amgen Inc.) with panitumumab (Vectibix, Amgen Inc.) for adult patients with KRAS G12C-mutated metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC), as determined by an FDA-approved test, who have receive"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.cancer.columbia.edu/news/targeting-kras-promising-new-therapy-hard-treat-lung-cancer-subtype", "domain": "cancer.columbia.edu", "title": "Targeting KRAS: Promising New Therapy for Hard-to-Treat Lung ...", "date": "2026-01-15", "text": "However, other KRAS mutations—including KRAS G12D—have remained without approved targeted options. Now, a new investigational therapy designed to selectively target KRAS G12D is being evaluated in a global registrational clinical trial, with the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center (HICCC) at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia serving as one of the major academic centers participating in the study. ... In January 2026, zoldonrasib received Breakthrough Therapy Designation from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of KRAS G12D–mutant NSCLC. ... At present, there are no approved targeted therapies for KRAS G12D–mutant NSCLC."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/ongoing-cancer-accelerated-approvals", "domain": "fda.gov", "title": "Ongoing | Cancer Accelerated Approvals", "date": "2026-09-17", "text": "|Krazati (adagrasib)|Adult patients with KRAS G12C-mutated locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), as determined by an FDA-approved test, who have received at least one prior systemic therapy.|12/12/2022|4378-1: Conduct a randomized comparative clinical trial of adagrasib in adult patients with KRAS G12"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.aacr.org/about-the-aacr/newsroom/news-releases/oral-investigational-agent-zoldonrasib-elicits-objective-responses-in-patients-with-kras-g12d-mutated-lung-cancer/", "domain": "aacr.org", "title": "Oral Investigational Agent Zoldonrasib Elicits Objective Responses ...", "date": "2025-04-27", "text": "However, around 4% of NSCLC cases harbor KRAS G12D mutations, a mutation against which there are no approved targeted therapies, explained Kathryn C. Arbour, MD, an assistant attending and thoracic medical oncologist at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, who presented the study. ... Zoldonrasib is a novel RAS(ON) tri-complex inhibitor that selectively targets the G12D mutation. ... The efficacy analysis included 18 patients with NSCLC who enrolled at least eight weeks before the data cutoff. Among these patients, 61% had an objective response, and 89% experienced disease control."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2026.1817943/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Therapeutic inhibition of RAS in non-small cell lung cancer - Frontiers", "date": "2026-06-30", "text": "As of this writing, both drugs retain their accelerated approvals, but neither have received full or standard FDA approvals based on these results, and neither study has easily interpretable OS results, limited by both sample size and crossover. ... |Zoldonrasib (RMC-9805)|KRAS G12D (ON)|Molecular glue / tri-complex inhibitor (RAS(ON))|NCT06040541|RMC-9805-001 (phase 1/1b)| |ASP3082|KRAS G12D|PROTAC / targeted degrader|NCT05382559|ASP3082 first-in-human (phase 1)|"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com/2026/04/kras-inhibitors-2026-complete-guide-to.html", "domain": "cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com", "title": "cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com · 2026 · 04KRAS Inhibitors (2026): The Complete Guide to Targeted Therapy...", "date": "2026-09-07", "text": "Zoldonrasib is a covalent, G12D-selective RAS(ON) inhibitor and holds FDA Breakthrough Therapy Designation for KRAS G12D-mutated disease. ... No targeted therapy is currently FDA-approved specifically for RAS G12D-mutant cancers; zoldonrasib and related G12D-directed programs (including earlier-stage compounds such as MRTX1133) remain under active clinical investigation. ... Not yet as a G12D-selective agent. Daraxonrasib's pancreatic cancer approval covers RAS G12-mutant disease broadly (including G12D and G12V) rather than being G12D-specific. Zoldonrasib, a G12D-selective RAS(ON) inhibitor, remains investigational, with Phase 3 trials ongoing. ... - No KRAS G12D-selective drug is yet approved anywhere; zoldonrasib is the most clinically advanced candidate, in Phase 3 development."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://datalookout.com/kras-inhibitor-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "title": "KRAS Inhibitor Clinical Trial Landscape, September 2026: 115 ...", "date": "2026-10-01", "text": "Revolution Medicines is leading the development of pan-RAS inhibitors that target multiple KRAS mutations simultaneously, including G12D, the dominant mutation in pancreatic cancer: ... - **RMC-9805 (Revolution Medicines)**: KRAS G12D-selective. Phase 1 in KRAS G12D-mutant solid tumors, particularly PDAC. ... The broader KRAS-mutant population (G12D, G12V) remains without an approved targeted therapy."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.clintrialfinder.info/mechanisms/kras-inhibitors", "domain": "clintrialfinder.info", "title": "www.clintrialfinder.info › mechanisms › kras-inhibitors100 KRAS Inhibitor Trials Recruiting (September 2026 ...", "date": "2026-09-14", "text": "Mirati / Bristol Myers Squibb's KRAS G12C covalent inhibitor. FDA-approved December 2022 for KRAS G12C NSCLC (post-platinum) based on KRYSTAL-1, and FDA accelerated approval June 2024 for KRAS G12C metastatic CRC in combination with cetuximab (KRYSTAL-1 CRC cohort)."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13362513/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Precision Targeting of KRAS-Mutant Cancers: Beyond G12C ... - PMC", "date": "2026-06-26", "text": "At present, the clinical use of approved KRAS inhibitors is largely limited to KRAS G12C-mutated advanced cancers. In NSCLC, sotorasib and adagrasib are approved as single agents for patients with locally advanced or metastatic KRAS G12C-mutated disease who have received at least one prior systemic therapy. Thus, these agents are primarily used in the second-line or later setting rather than as first-line standard therapy. ... In contrast, KRAS G12D inhibitors, pan-RAS/RAS(ON) inhibitors, and KRAS degraders remain investigational and are not yet established as routine clinical therapies. ... Among these, zoldonrasib (RMC-9805) has emerged as an investigational RAS(ON)-selective KRAS G12D inhibitor currently under early clinical evaluation, with preliminary activity reported in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma [55] (NCT06040541)."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8696, "cached_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 3273}, "timing": {"search_ms": 657.4, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 33140.8, "total_ms": 33798.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 33140.8, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "KRAS G12D-selective targeted therapies are not FDA-approved; sources state there are no approved targeted therapies for KRAS G12D-mutant NSCLC or RAS G12D-mutant cancers. [E3][E5][E7] An FDA listing for a “KRAS G12D inhibitor” gives an orphan designation for pancreatic cancer dated 05/01/2026, with status “Designated” and “Not FDA Approved for Orphan Indication.” [E1] Zoldonrasib received Breakthrough Therapy Designation from the FDA in January 2026 for KRAS G12D-mutant NSCLC. [E3] A separate source says daraxonrasib has a pancreatic cancer approval covering broader RAS G12-mutant disease, including G12D and G12V, but is not G12D-specific; Datalookout says the broader G12D/G12V population remains without an approved targeted therapy. [E7][E8]", "confident_score": 0.68, "missing": "The FDA listing does not identify the “KRAS G12D inhibitor” by drug name or sponsor. The source describing daraxonrasib’s pancreatic cancer approval does not specify its approving authority or approval date, and the sources disagree on whether broader G12D/G12V disease has an approved targeted therapy.", "citations": ["E1", "E3", "E5", "E7", "E8"], "next_queries": ["ZE980277 KRAS G12D FDA approval status", "ZE980277 KRAS G12D inhibitor orphan designation drug name sponsor", "ZE980277 pancreatic cancer approval authority date"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 752, "confident_score": 0.68, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 7, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 5, "cited_domains": ["aacr.org", "accessdata.fda.gov", "cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com", "cancer.columbia.edu", "datalookout.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 20, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 3, "next_queries_subject_first": 3}}
