{"row_id": "q044", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as GPR52 Prostaglandin E2 Receptor EP4, including Small Molecule therapies", "anchors": ["NXE0033744", "NXE744"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 8076, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2026Apr_EN_vF.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "title": "Presentation title - investors.nxera.life", "date": null, "text": "NXE’744 IBD Phase1 Under development in-house Licensing activities in progress In discussions with multiple partners for a 2026 license deal Phase 1 completed successfully Phase 2 ready to initiate ... NXE’744 showed early efficacy signals in an indomethacin challenge study ... Phase1 completed ✓ Phase 2 ready ... IBD Advanced solid tumors Discovery Phase 2 Phase 2 ready ... A first-in-class GI-targeted agent to promote mucosal healing in IBD • All elements of the first-in-human study have now completed dosing in the clinic ➢ SAD/MAD studies are complete with no concerning adverse events and no systemic exposure observed ➢ Gut restricted profile confirmed by high gut tissue concentrations measured following oral dosing ➢ UC patient cohort has completed dosing (n=6) with interim analysis confirming high gut tissue concentration. ➢ Indomethacin challenge cohort complete, interim analysis complete with no need to increase subjects and final data read-out by March 2026 ➢ Preliminary data analysis demonstrates a highly significant ~50% reduction in indomethacin induced permeability in the NXE’744 treatment group; these data confirm target engagement in the small intestine 27 NXE’744: EP4 agonist for IBD* – target engagement & Phase 2 ready ... Modulation of barrier homeostasis and inflammatory axis positions EP4 as an attractive MOA for IBD therapy ... NXE0033744 EP4 agonist SME Inflammatory bowel disease ... NXE0033744 EP4 agonist Inflammatory bowel diseases Ph1 Up to 220 - 2023-11-24 2026-06-30 2024-05-02 ISRCTN70080074"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/20250829_ESG_Report_2024_EN.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "title": "[PDF] Environment, Social & Governance Report - Investors", "date": null, "text": "During 2024, we have progressed our EP4 agonist, NXE0033744, into a Phase 1 clinical trial for the treatment of patients with Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD). Although we didn’t progress any new assets into preclinical development during the year, our discovery pipeline continues to grow and we are constantly evaluating exciting targets where we believe we can"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.daiwair.co.jp/td_download.cgi?c=4565&i=2938504", "domain": "daiwair.co.jp", "title": "Nxera Pharma Operational Highlights and Consolidated Results ...", "date": null, "text": "Progress with in-house programs ... • First subject dosed in a Phase 1 trial with NXE0033744, a novel, potent, selective and gut-restricted EP4 receptor agonist in development for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://finance.stockweather.co.jp/contents/dispPDF.aspx?disclosure=20241101509584", "domain": "finance.stockweather.co.jp", "title": "Consolidated Financial Results for the Nine months Ended September 30, 2024", "date": null, "text": "Biosciences (“Neurocrine”) that NBI-1117568, an oral selective muscarinic M4 receptor agonist then being advanced in Phase 2 clinical trials by Neurocrine for the treatment of schizophrenia and other neuropsychiatric disorders, had successfully completed a long-term preclinical toxicity program that meets US Food & Drug Administration (“FDA\") requirements to allow for safe, chronic ... On August 28, 2024, the Group noted the announcement by Neurocrine that NBI-1117568 had delivered positive topline results from its Phase 2 clinical study in adults with schizophrenia. The NBI-1117568-SCZ2028 dose-finding study met its primary endpoint for the once-daily 20 mg dose. ... On March 21, 2024, the Group announced it had dosed the first subject in a Phase 1 trial evaluating its novel EP4 receptor agonist, NXE0033744 for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (“IBD”), a therapeutic area where there remains significant unmet need for millions of people worldwide. NXE0033744 is a potent, selective and gut-restricted prostaglandin EP4 receptor agonist that has been uniquely designed to bring clinical benefit by accelerating the healing of damaged epithelial mucosa and suppressing exaggerated gut inflammation, with minimal systemic exposure to avoid adverse events. This approach is widely recognized to promote deeper remission and offer better long-term clinical outcomes. NXE0033744 aims to address the significant unmet need of people with IBD that do not achieve satisfactory disease control. Current treatments for IBD typically achieve remission rates of less than 25%, and the safety profiles of these drugs mean that they require careful monitoring. The Phase 1 trial is a first-in-human randomized, double-blind study to assess the safety, pharmacokinetics and effects on pharmacodynamic biomarkers of single and multiple ascending doses of NXE0033744 in adult healthy volunteers and people with Crohn’s disease to generate proof of mechanism. The trial will be conducted in the UK and initial data read-outs are anticipated from 2025."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.nxera.life/pipeline", "domain": "nxera.life", "title": "Pipeline - Nxera Pharma", "date": null, "text": "### EP4 agonist Phase 1 Read More Indication Inflammatory Bowel Disease Modality Small molecule Candidate NXE0033744"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/jp/common/pdf/20250829_Annual_Report_2024_JP.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "title": "アニュアルレポート", "date": null, "text": "められています。NXE-744は、炎症性腸疾患の治療における新たなメカニズムを 提供する可能性があります。 ... 階、3品目がフェーズ2試験段階、1品目がフェーズ3段階にあります。 ... 消化管で局所作用する炎症性腸疾患（以下「IBD」）の治療薬候補であるEP4受容体作動薬 NXE0033744のフェーズ1試験で、最初の被験者への投与を実施。NXE0033744は、全身へ の影響を最小限に抑えながら、損傷した粘膜上皮の治癒を促進し、腸菅の炎症亢進を抑制 することで安全性および有効性を大幅に高め、治療効果を発揮するように設計されている"}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/nxera-pharma-annual-report-year-ended-31-december-2024-ce7c50dcd18dff20", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "title": "Nxera Pharma : Annual Report Year ended 31 December 2024", "date": "2025-08-29", "text": "EP4 ag. NXE'744 Inflammatory Bowel Disease ... Our three wholly owned clinical programs are also advancing as planned in Phase 1 development, with several important milestones expected over the next 12-18 months. These programs are: NXE-149, which is targeting unmet needs in schizophrenia and is the subject of an option-to-license agreement with Boehringer Ingelheim; NXE-732, which is being investigated in solid tumors in collaboration with Cancer Research UK; and NXE-744, which offers a possible novel mechanism to treat inflammatory bowel diseases. ... EP4 agonist NXE0033744 The first subject was dosed in a Phase 1 trial of NXE'744, an oral, gut-restricted EP4 agonist with potential to treat Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD). NXE'744 has been designed to bring clinical benefit by accelerating the healing of damaged epithelial mucosa and suppressing exaggerated gut inflammation, with minimal systemic exposure thereby providing significantly enhanced safety and efficacy."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/nxera-pharma-operational-highlights-and-consolidated-results-for-the-first-quarter-2024", "domain": "biospace.com", "title": "Nxera Pharma Operational Highlights and Consolidated Results for ...", "date": "2024-05-09", "text": "**Progress with in-house programs** - First subject dosed in a Phase 1 trial with NXE0033744, a novel, potent, selective and gut-restricted EP4 receptor agonist discovered by Nxera in development for the treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2025Jan_EN_v2.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "title": "[PDF] Presentation title - Nxera Pharma", "date": null, "text": "EP4 ag. NXE’744 IBD ... NXE’744 (EP4 agonist) ... Immuno-oncology NXE0033744 EP4 agonist SME Inflammatory bowel disease ... NXE0033744 EP4 agonist Inflammatory bowel diseases Ph1 -"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://investors.nxera.life/common/pdf/Corporate_Presentation_2025Feb_EN_vF.pdf", "domain": "investors.nxera.life", "title": "[PDF] Corporate Presentation - Investors - Nxera Pharma", "date": null, "text": "NXE’744 (EP4 agonist) ... Phase 2 study start in IBD ... Immuno-oncology NXE0033744 EP4 agonist SME Inflammatory bowel disease ... - NXE0033744 EP4 agonist Inflammatory bowel diseases Ph1 Up to 220 - 2023-11-24 2026-06-30 2024-02-15 ISRCTN70080074 -"}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8769, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 4295}, "timing": {"search_ms": 572.2, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 46303.0, "total_ms": 46875.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 46303.0, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "NXE0033744, also called NXE’744 and NXE-744, is Nxera’s oral, gut-restricted, small-molecule prostaglandin EP4 receptor agonist for IBD; Nxera calls it a first-in-class GI-targeted agent intended to promote mucosal healing. [E1][E5][E7] Its first-in-human Phase 1 trial was described as randomized and double-blind, assessing safety, pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamic biomarkers after single and multiple ascending doses in adult healthy volunteers and people with Crohn’s disease, and was to be conducted in the UK. The first subject was dosed on March 21, 2024. [E4] The trial listing gives enrolment of up to 220 and registry identifier ISRCTN70080074. [E1] Nxera’s presentation says Phase 1 completed successfully and Phase 2 was ready to initiate, while its pipeline page lists the candidate at Phase 1. [E1][E5] Nxera reported that the single- and multiple-ascending-dose studies were complete, with no concerning adverse events and no systemic exposure; high gut-tissue concentrations following oral dosing supported the gut-restricted profile. Its ulcerative-colitis cohort completed dosing (n=6), with interim analysis confirming high gut-tissue concentration. [E1] The indomethacin-challenge cohort and interim analysis were complete; the treatment group showed a highly significant approximately 50% reduction in indomethacin-induced permeability, which Nxera said confirmed target engagement in the small intestine. The presentation stated that final data read-out was due by March 2026. [E1] The March 2024 announcement anticipated initial data read-outs from 2025. [E4]", "confident_score": 0.68, "missing": "Other named IBD competing agents, including other EP4 agonists or small-molecule therapies, and their mechanisms/classes, indications, stages, trials, enrolment and results; NXE0033744’s dose and schedule; its regulatory designations; and final trial results and endpoint status beyond the interim findings stated above.", "citations": ["E1", "E4", "E5", "E7"], "next_queries": ["NXE0033744 IBD EP4 agonist competing agents", "NXE0033744 SAD MAD dose schedule", "NXE0033744 IBD regulatory designation", "NXE0033744 ISRCTN70080074 final results endpoints"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 1586, "confident_score": 0.68, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 10, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 4, "cited_domains": ["finance.stockweather.co.jp", "investors.nxera.life", "marketscreener.com", "nxera.life"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 17, "ungrounded_tokens": ["The March"], "n_ungrounded": 1, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q040", "objective": "Active Ulcerative Colitis Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHST15 Oligonucleotide Small Interfering RNA therapies", "anchors": ["GUT-1"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 18043, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/18/3/406/7287358?guestAccessKey=", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active ...", "date": "2024-03-01", "text": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 [CHST15] biosynthesizes sulphated matrix glycosaminoglycans and is implicated in intestinal inflammation and fibrosis. Here, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC]. ... In this randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2a study, we enrolled endoscopically active UC patients, refractory to conventional therapy, in five hospital centres across Germany. Patients were randomized 1:1:1 using a block randomized technique to receive a single dosing of 25 nM GUT-1, 250 nM GUT-1, or placebo by endoscopic submucosal injections. ... GUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis. GUT-1 application was well tolerated. ... Single dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC. ... GUT-1 is a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of CHST15.^15–17^ Previous *in vitro* studies demonstrated that GUT-1 suppresses CHST15 mRNA in a dose-dependent manner as well as the mRNA expression of vimentin, α-SMA and Wnt3 in a TGF-β-stimulated human fibroblast cell line. ... In this Phase 2a induction trial, we found that CHST15 was highly expressed in the inflamed colon at baseline in UC patients who did not respond sufficiently to prior conventional treatment including biologics and had active endoscopic lesions with respect to an MES of ≥2. Single dosing of 250 nM GUT-1, an oligonucleotide that specifically suppresses CHST15, revealed 71.4% achieved endoscopic improvement at the end of this induction trial [at week 2 or week 4]. ... In conclusion, the present study shows that local application of GUT-1 could be a novel option for induction therapy in active UC. The effects may be due to a reduced sulphated matrix and CS-E generation, by targeting CHST15, which simultaneously acts to repress neutrophil-based mucosal inflammation and accelerate degradation of established fibrosis, leading to tissue remodelling in UC."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://d-nb.info/1335375937/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "title": "Add-on multiple submucosal injections of the RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1 to anti-TNF antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis: an open-label, proof-of concept study", "date": null, "text": "Background Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) is an enzyme biosynthesizing matrix glycosaminoglycan that modulates tissue remodeling. We evaluated the efficacy of add-on submucosal injections of GUT-1, the RNA oligonucleotide inhibitor of CHST15, to ongoing anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis (UC). Methods This was an open-label study of 250 nM of GUT-1 by endoscopic submucosal injections at weeks 0, 2, 4 in five UC patients who lost response during maintenance treatment to anti-TNF antibodies. ... Results At week 6, rates of endoscopic improvement and clinical remission were 80% (n = 4/5) and 60% (n = 3/5), respectively. ... GUT-1 was well tolerated. Three patients did not show clinical recurrence for 52 weeks. All three corticosteroid-dependent patients showed no corticosteroid exposure for at least 24 weeks after achieving clinical remission. Multiple dosing was also well tolerated. Conclusions Add-on multiple injections of GUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody was able to induce rapid and dura- ble clinical responses in UC patients who lost response to anti-TNF therapy. ... GUT-1 is a synthesized double stranded RNA oli- gonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of the CHST15 gene through siRNA mechanisms [9, 14, 15]. CHST15 siRNA suppressed CHST15 mRNA in a dose dependent manner and demonstrated repression of fibrosis-related genes in human fibroblast cell lines [11, 13]. ... In a phase IIa induction trial, one-time submu- cosal injection of 250 nM of GUT-1 demonstrated 71.4% of endoscopic improvement (vs. 28.6% for placebo) and 57.1% of clinical remission (vs. 14.3% for placebo) at week 4 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) refractory to conventional treatment [16]. In histological evaluation, reduction of mucosal target CHST15 protein expression and reversal of tissue remodeling was shown [16]. ... Add-on multiple injections of 250 nM of GUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody treatment reduced SFS at week 2, reduced RBS and Endoscopy Subscore till week 4 and induced 80% of endoscopic improvement, 100% of clinical response and 60% of clinical remission at week 6."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article-abstract/18/3/406/7287358?redirectedFrom=fulltext", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active Ulcerative Colitis Patients: A Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Phase 2a Induction Trial", "date": null, "text": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 [CHST15] biosynthesizes sulphated matrix glycosaminoglycans and is implicated in intestinal inflammation and fibrosis. Here, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC]. In this randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2a study, we enrolled endoscopically active UC patients, refractory to conventional therapy, in five hospital centres across Germany. Patients were randomized 1:1:1 using a block randomized technique to receive a single dosing of 25 nM GUT-1, 250 nM GUT-1, or placebo by endoscopic submucosal injections. ... Endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 or 4 was achieved by 71.4% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 28.6% in the placebo group. Clinical remission was shown by 57.1% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 14.3% in the placebo groups. Histological improvement was shown by 42.9% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 0% in the placebo groups. GUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis. GUT-1 application was well tolerated. Single dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1e53ce24-191f-48ed-a22e-45f1dec47600", "domain": "link.springer.com", "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "date": "2025-12-05", "text": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15(CHST15) is involved in biosynthesis of sulfated glycosaminoglycans and is associated with intestinal inflammation and fibrosis. GUT-1, a double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide targeting CHST15, was evaluated in a Phase IIa study of 28 UC patients unresponsive to conventional treatments. Single-dose administration (25 nM, 250 nM) was compared with placebo; the primary endpoint was endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4. In the 250 nM group, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1%, and histological improvement 42.9%, significantly higher than the 25 nM group (0%) and placebo (28.6%, 14.3%, 0%). GUT-1 reduces CHST15 expression, inhibiting mucosal inflammation and fibrosis, and may serve as an inducible therapy to regulate tissue remodeling in UC [55]. ... The VARSITY study demonstrated that VDZ showed higher clinical remission, endoscopic improvement, and histological remission than adalimumab in UC."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://ecco-ibd.eu/publications/news/issues/2023/volume-18-issue-4/y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay - ECCO - European Crohn’s and Colitis Organisation", "date": "2023-12-18", "text": "One such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15). This is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms. ... Based on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01). A prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6]. Accordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial. ... Adult patients aged 18–65 years with moderate-to-severe left-sided UC were recruited from five centres around Germany. Twenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo. ... Endoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm. ... The mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4. The values in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.6% (95% CI 15.2%–24.1%) at baseline, 14.7% (95% CI 10.6%–18.7%) at week 2 and 14.0% (95% CI 7.7%–20.2%) at week 4, suggesting an element of target engagement with the lower dose of GUT-1 although this was seemingly insufficient to enact an effect on the outcome measures described above. ... At two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated. ... Atreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.ecco-ibd.eu/publications/ecco-news/item/2023-4-y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay.html", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay", "date": "2024-09-27", "text": "### Submucosal injection of the RNA nucleotide GUT-1 in active ulcerative colitis patients: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 2a induction trial. ... One such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15). This is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms. Targeting this molecule in mouse models has previously been shown to offer promising signals for ameliorating colitis [5]. Based on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01). A prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6]. Accordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial. ... Adult patients aged 18–65 years with moderate-to-severe left-sided UC were recruited from five centres around Germany. Twenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo. ... Endoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm. ... The mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4. The values in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.6% (95% CI 15.2%–24.1%) at baseline, 14.7% (95% CI 10.6%–18.7%) at week 2 and 14.0% (95% CI 7.7%–20.2%) at week 4, suggesting an element of target engagement with the lower dose of GUT-1 although this was seemingly insufficient to enact an effect on the outcome measures described above. ... At two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated. ... Atreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC. Important steps going forward will be to determine whether this treatment can also be used in pancolitis and proctitis and to further elucidate the biological basis as part of mechanistic work in future clinical trial phases."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12797379/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "date": "2025-12-05", "text": "Tofacitinib (JAK1/JAK3 inhibitor) demonstrated efficacy comparable to that of biologics in TNF-α inhibitor–naive UC and maintains superiority over placebo; it is approved for moderate to severe UC. Trials in CD did not meet primary endpoints and development was halted [42, 43]. ... Tocilizumab and PF-04236921 have completed clinical trials, while other programs have been terminated due to insufficient efficacy or safety concerns [45]. ... PF-00547659, a fully human monoclonal antibody targeting MAdCAM-1, selectively reduces lymphocyte gut homing for moderate to severe UC. ... Ozanimod is approved by the FDA for moderate to severe UC. ... Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15(CHST15) is involved in biosynthesis of sulfated glycosaminoglycans and is associated with intestinal inflammation and fibrosis. GUT-1, a double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide targeting CHST15, was evaluated in a Phase IIa study of 28 UC patients unresponsive to conventional treatments. Single-dose administration (25 nM, 250 nM) was compared with placebo; the primary endpoint was endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4. In the 250 nM group, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1%, and histological improvement 42.9%, significantly higher than the 25 nM group (0%) and placebo (28.6%, 14.3%, 0%). GUT-1 reduces CHST15 expression, inhibiting mucosal inflammation and fibrosis, and may serve as an inducible therapy to regulate tissue remodeling in UC [55]. ... Systematic reviews and network meta-analyses indicate that infliximab has the highest clinical response and remission rates for induction in moderate to severe UC, while ozanimod is most effective for inducing mucosal healing. ... After anti TNF failure or intolerance, vedolizumab and ustekinumab are favored for their safety and gut selectivity, whereas JAK and S1P modulators provide oral options for patients preferring non injectable therapy or requiring systemic anti-inflammatory potency. ... Current therapeutic classes—including anti‑TNF agents, integrin antagonists, IL‑12/23 and IL‑23 inhibitors, Janus kinase inhibitors, and S1P modulators—have substantially improved remission rates and durability in moderate to severe disease, particularly for patients unresponsive to conventional treatments."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13525582/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Identification of Ferro‐Aging‐Related Biomarkers for Ulcerative ...", "date": "2026-08-29", "text": "Collectively, these findings identify CHST15 and MSN as ferro‐aging‐related biomarkers linking mucosal inflammation, immune‐stromal remodeling, and disease‐associated tissue organization in UC."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10394652/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Unveiling the key genes, environmental toxins, and drug exposures in modulating the severity of ulcerative colitis: a comprehensive analysis", "date": "2023-07-19", "text": "Via WGCNA, a total 77 high Mayo score-associated genes specific in UC were identified. Subsequently, the 9 gene signatures of the exacerbation of UC was screened out by random forest algorithm and Limma analysis, including BGN,CHST15,CYYR1,GPR137B,GPR4,ITGA5,LILRB1,SLFN11 and ST3GAL2."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://scispace.com/pdf/current-and-emerging-therapeutic-targets-for-ibd-191591vd96.pdf", "domain": "scispace.com", "title": "[PDF] Current and emerging therapeutic targets for IBD - SciSpace", "date": null, "text": "Specifically, a study has examined carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) in gut inflammation78. ... In experimental acute dextran sulfate sodium colitis, small-interfering RNA (siRNA)-based silencing of CHST15 reduced colitis activity and intestinal accumu­ lation of F4/80+ macrophages and ER‑TR7+ fibroblasts. ... Furthermore, a phase I study in 18 patients with Crohn’s disease published in 2016 determined the safety of STNM01, a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide directed against CHST15, in Crohn’s disease79. The majority of individuals who received STNM01 showed a reduction of endoscopic inflam­ mation as compared with placebo therapy. Moreover, histo­logical analyses revealed that STNM01 reduces ­tissue fibrosis in Crohn’s disease. ... 79. Suzuki, K. et al. Phase 1 clinical study of siRNA targeting carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 in Crohn’s disease patients with active mucosal lesions. J. Crohns Colitis http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/ecco-jcc/ jjw143 (2016)."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11308, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 5981}, "timing": {"search_ms": 474.5, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 59494.7, "total_ms": 59969.2}, "luna_wall_ms": 59494.7, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "GUT-1, previously called STNM01, is a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide that selectively inhibits CHST15 through siRNA mechanisms. [E1][E2][E5][E6] In vitro, it suppressed CHST15 mRNA dose-dependently and reduced vimentin, α-SMA and Wnt3 mRNA in a TGF-β-stimulated human fibroblast cell line. [E1] A Phase I study of STNM01 in 18 patients with Crohn’s disease found that most recipients showed reduced endoscopic inflammation versus placebo, and histological analyses showed reduced tissue fibrosis; the study demonstrated safety. [E5][E10]\n\nIn active UC, the randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase 2a induction study enrolled adults aged 18–65 with moderate-to-severe left-sided disease refractory to conventional treatment, including patients with prior biologic treatment and endoscopic lesions with MES ≥2. It screened 28 patients and randomized 24 in a 1:1:1 allocation to a single 25 nM or 250 nM dose of GUT-1 or placebo, delivered by endoscopic submucosal injection at five centres in Germany; endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4 was the primary endpoint. [E1][E3][E5][E6] At 250 nM, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1% and histological improvement 42.9%; at 25 nM, each was 0%; with placebo, the respective rates were 28.6%, 14.3% and 0%. [E3][E5][E6] In the 250 nM arm, CHST15-positive area was 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4. At 25 nM, it was 19.6% (95% CI 15.2%–24.1%), 14.7% (95% CI 10.6%–18.7%) and 14.0% (95% CI 7.7%–20.2%) at those timepoints. Two hours after injection, plasma GUT-1 was below the 0.5 ng/ml lower limit of quantification in all treatment groups; GUT-1 was well tolerated. [E1][E3][E5][E6]\n\nA separate open-label study gave 250 nM GUT-1 by submucosal injection at weeks 0, 2 and 4 to five patients with moderate-to-severe UC who had lost response to maintenance anti-TNF antibody treatment. At week 6, endoscopic improvement was 80% (4/5), clinical response 100% and clinical remission 60% (3/5). Three patients had no clinical recurrence for 52 weeks, and all three corticosteroid-dependent patients had no corticosteroid exposure for at least 24 weeks after achieving remission. [E2]\n\nOther named UC treatments in the evidence include tofacitinib, a JAK1/JAK3 inhibitor approved for moderate-to-severe UC, with efficacy described as comparable to biologics in TNF-α-inhibitor-naive UC and superior to placebo; ozanimod, approved for moderate-to-severe UC and described as most effective for inducing mucosal healing; and PF-00547659, a fully human monoclonal antibody targeting MAdCAM-1 that selectively reduces lymphocyte gut homing in moderate-to-severe UC. [E7] Infliximab is described as having the highest clinical response and remission rates for induction in moderate-to-severe UC. Vedolizumab and ustekinumab are described as favored for safety and gut selectivity after anti-TNF failure or intolerance; in UC, vedolizumab showed higher clinical remission, endoscopic improvement and histological remission than adalimumab in the VARSITY study. [E4][E7]", "confident_score": 0.65, "missing": "No distinct competing CHST15-targeting siRNA or oligonucleotide agent is named beyond GUT-1/STNM01; GUT-1's Phase 2a registry identifier and regulatory designations; development stage and agent-specific trial, enrolment and results details for the named non-CHST15 UC alternatives where not stated.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E10"], "next_queries": ["GUT-1 CHST15 siRNA competitor pipeline", "GUT-1 Phase 2a trial registry identifier", "GUT-1 regulatory designations", "GUT-1 UC competitor development stages"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 3130, "confident_score": 0.65, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 22, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 8, "cited_domains": ["academic.oup.com", "d-nb.info", "ecco-ibd.eu", "link.springer.com", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "scispace.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 57, "ungrounded_tokens": ["CHST15-positive"], "n_ungrounded": 1, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q061", "objective": "FG-3149 sponsor development stage indication public source", "anchors": ["FG-3149"], "facet": null, "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 5972, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://oamonitor.ireland.openaire.eu/national/search/publication?pid=10.1016/j.bpg.2025.102030", "domain": "oamonitor.ireland.openaire.eu", "title": "New weapons for the management of perianal Crohn's disease", "date": null, "text": "Researcher Monitors ... We’re experiencing a temporary issue. Please refresh the page"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/1751-2980.12913", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "title": "Serum biomarkers of fibrostenotic Crohn's disease", "date": "2020-06-01", "text": "Opens an external website Opens an external website in a new window ... Reject Non-Essential"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyntra_Bio", "domain": "en.wikipedia.org", "title": "Kyntra Bio - Wikipedia", "date": "2025-09-10", "text": "By 2026, Kyntra was mainly reduced to a single early-stage oncology drug candidate. ... Over the next few years, multiple late-stage clinical trials across its pipeline failed, leading the company to significantly reduce its workforce in 2023 and 2024. ... Following the sale of its Chinese subsidiary, FibroGen is now left primarily with FG-3246, an asset it acquired from Fortis Therapeutics, a defunct biotech company, at no upfront cost with a large milestone due only if the drug advances into Phase 3. ... |Drug / Program|Indication|Phase 3 Trial(s)|Outcome|Notes| |--|--|--|--|--| |Roxadustat|Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)|ANDES, OLYMPUS, ALPS, ROCKIES, SIERRAS, HIMALAYAS, PYRENEES|FDA rejected August 2021|Company changed prespecified statistical criteria for cardiovascular safety analysis and FDA raised concerns about data integrity| |Roxadustat|Anemia in Myelodysplastic Syndromes (MDS)|MATTERHORN|Failed May 2023|Did not meet primary endpoint| |Pamrevlumab|Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF)|ZEPHYRUS-1, ZEPHYRUS-2|Failed June 2023|ZEPHYRUS-1 missed primary endpoint; ZEPHYRUS-2 terminated after interim review| |Pamrevlumab|Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)|LELANTOS-1, LELANTOS-2|Failed June–August 2023|Both trials failed to meet primary endpoints on motor function| ... All of its US Phase 3 trials for both roxadustat and pamrevlumab including indications for anemia, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, and pancreatic cancer ultimately failed or were rejected. In 2025, the only significant remaining asset is FG-3246. ... FibroGen is also developing FG-3180, a PET imaging agent, to help track the cancer and predict responses to FG-3246. A Phase 2 trial of FG-3246 began in September 2025, with full completion expected in March 2028."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.law360.co.uk/companies/fibrogen-inc/ptab_cases", "domain": "law360.co.uk", "title": "FibroGen Inc. (NASDAQ:FGEN) : Ptab Cases :: Law360", "date": "2026-10-03", "text": "## PTAB Cases (74) ... et al. ... 2. Filed: September 12, 2025 | IPR2025-01531 Apotex Inc."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://investor.kyntrabio.com/node/13811/pdf", "domain": "investor.kyntrabio.com", "title": "FibroGen Reports First Quarter 2024 Financial Results", "date": null, "text": "FG-3246 (also known as FOR46), a first-in-class antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) targeting CD46 is in development for the treatment of metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.fibrogen.com/our-partners", "domain": "fibrogen.com", "title": "Our Partners - FibroGen", "date": "2016-01-01", "text": "FibroGen anticipates the initiation of a Phase II trial in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, or mCRPC, in the second half of 2024 with the potential for additional trials targeting other CD46 expressing cancers."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/fibrogen-corporate-presentation-september-2025-ce7d58d9db80f422", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "title": "FibroGen : Corporate Presentation - September 2025", "date": "2025-09-18", "text": "Completed sale of FibroGen China to AstraZeneca for approximately $220 million"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://reportify-1252068037.cos.ap-beijing.myqcloud.com/media/production/s_4689968_a2b4c0ac65184dcf6818814db38a7a6b.pdf", "domain": "reportify-1252068037.cos.ap-beijing.myqcloud.com", "title": "FibroGen, Inc. (NASDAQ:FGEN) Q1 2024 Earnings Conference Call ...", "date": null, "text": "Lastly, in April, we announced compelling top line results from our Phase 1 monotherapy study of FG-3246 in patients with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. ... FG-3246 is a first-in-class ADC for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, colorectal cancer, and other tumor types. ... FG-3246 is comprised of an anti CD46 antibody, YS5 linked to an antimitotic agent, MMAE, which is clinically validated and FDA-approved ADC payload. ... FG-3246 has demonstrated monotherapy clinical efficacy in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. ... The completed Phase 1 trial includes a total of 56 metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer patients who are ... The radiopharmaceutical biomarker will be part of our Phase 2 dose optimization monotherapy trial sponsored by FibroGen. ... Lastly, we are planning a meeting with the FDA in the third quarter to discuss the details of the FG-3246 development program, contingent upon reaching agreement with the FDA on the trial design, we anticipate the initiation of a Phase 2/3 dose optimization and registration-enabling study in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer in the fourth quarter of 2024."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.pharmacompass.com/pipeline-prospector-drugs-in-development/fibrogen", "domain": "pharmacompass.com", "title": "FibroGen | Drug Developments | Pipeline Prospector", "date": "2025-02-20", "text": "**Study Phase : ** Preclinical **Sponsor : ** Regeneron Pharmaceuticals"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/langas/article/PIIS2468-1253(24)00272-3/fulltext", "domain": "thelancet.com", "title": "Efficacy and safety of filgotinib as induction and maintenance therapy for Crohn's disease (DIVERSITY): a phase 3, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled trial", "date": "2025-02-01", "text": "The most frequently reported serious TEAE (≥2% of patients in any filgotinib or associated placebo group) was an exacerbation, flare, or worsening of Crohn's disease (appendix p 39)."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 6217, "cached_input_tokens": 4548, "output_tokens": 183}, "timing": {"search_ms": 553.6, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 3355.7, "total_ms": 3909.3}, "luna_wall_ms": 3355.7, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "no", "summarized_answer": "", "missing": "FG-3149’s sponsor, development stage, indication, and a public source identifying these details are not stated.", "citations": [], "next_queries": ["FG-3149 sponsor development stage indication"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": false, "answer_chars": 0, "confident_score": null, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 0, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 0, "cited_domains": [], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 0, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": false, "subject_in_cited": false, "next_queries": 1, "next_queries_subject_first": 1}}
{"row_id": "q042", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis, Duration \u00143 Months, Modified Mayo Score 5 to 9, With Inadequate Response, Loss of Response, or Intolerance to Prior Standard-of-Care Medications competing agents in the same mechanistic class as TYK2 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["D-2570"], "facet": "commercial", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 12228, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026| ... It is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950). ... #### 2.1.9 D-2570 D-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks. ... The study began May 2025 with estimated primary completion July 2026 and final completion December 2026, currently in the recruiting Phase with no results yet available as the trial is ongoing."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "date": "2026-01-30", "text": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027| ... |TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026| ... It is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950). ... #### 2.1.9. D-2570 D-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100538.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "SUMMARY", "date": null, "text": "Within our clinical-stage pipeline, our Core Products tyrosine kinase 2 (“TYK2”) inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial. ... D-2570 TYK2 Autoimmune diseases Submit NDA in H2 2027 Global Initiate Phase III trial in H1 2026 Complete Phase II trial in H2 2026 Complete Phase I trial in H1 2026 Initiate Phase II trial in H1 2026 Initiate Phase II trial in H1 2026 Submit IND in H1 2026 ... Our Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases. Our Phase II clinical trial in moderate-to-severe psoriasis delivered outstanding results, with efficacy significantly superior to both placebo and approved products of the same class (based on published clinical data), and comparable to that of biologics, while maintaining strong safety and tolerability. We have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025. In parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China. ... D-2570 is a TYK2 allosteric inhibitor under development primarily for the treatment of plaque psoriasis and UC."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/78219536/yifang-biopharma-u-688382-2026-interim-report-review-core-pipeline", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "title": "Yifang Bio-U (688382) 2026 H1 Report Commentary: Multiple Core Pipeline Programs Show Strong Progress; Optimistic About the Realization of Innovation", "date": "2026-08-25", "text": "D-2570 is demonstrating broad therapeutic potential, with the value of its TYK2 inhibitor pipeline continuing to stand out. In the first half of 2026, D-2570 has made significant progress: (1) enrollment and dosing have been initiated for the first patient in a head-to-head Phase III trial in psoriasis; (2) the first patient has been enrolled in a Phase II trial for psoriatic arthritis; (3) the first patient has been enrolled in a Phase II trial for systemic lupus erythematosus; (4) all participants have been enrolled in a pivotal Phase III trial for moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis; (5) all participants have been enrolled in a Phase II trial for moderate-to-severe active ulcerative colitis; and (6) the U.S. FDA has formally approved D-2570 for a Phase II monotherapy study in psoriasis. In the Phase II psoriasis trial, D-2570 demonstrated superior efficacy compared to both placebo and currently marketed TYK2 inhibitors, highlighting its \"best-in-class\" potential. Its indications are expanding into multiple autoimmune diseases, including psoriatic arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, and ulcerative colitis, with substantial market opportunities."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07265570", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "Study Evaluating ISM5411 Administered Orally to Subjects With Active Ulcerative Colitis (BETHESDA)", "date": null, "text": "NCT02368717 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3 ... - A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis NCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12942054/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Interleukin-23p19 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "date": "2026-02-14", "text": "In the ANTHEM-UC trial, a phase 2b study involving 252 adults with moderate-to-severe UC, preliminary results reported dose-dependent improvements in clinical response rates. ... In addition, small-molecule inhibitors targeting JAK1 and TYK2, which act downstream of the IL-23 receptor, are currently under clinical development. Deucr avacitinib is a selective, allosteric inhibitor of TYK2, an intracellular signaling mediator of IL-23. However, in three phase 2 clinical trials in patients with moderately to severely active CD and UC (LATTICE-CD, LATTICE-UC, and IM011-127), deucravacitinib demonstrated modest signals of efficacy, but failed to achieve the primary or secondary efficacy endpoints after 12 weeks of treatment across multiple dosing regimens [48]. ... Of interest, a next-generation allosteric TYK2 inhibitor, TAK-279 (zasocitinib), is currently in phase 2 evaluation for both UC and CD [51,52]. TAK-279 is designed for sustained 24 h pathway suppression and these trials will help understand if deeper TYK2 target engagement can translate to mucosal efficacy in IBD."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT06254950", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "A Study on the Safety of TAK-279 and Whether it Can Reduce Inflammation in the Bowel of Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis", "date": null, "text": "NCT03943446 ·Status: TERMINATED ·Phase: PHASE2 - A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis NCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "date": "2023-08-17", "text": "OST-122 is a gut selective oral JAK inhibitor with selectivity for JAK3, TYK2 and AMPK-related protein kinase 5 (ARK5). The phase Ib/IIa trial which enrolled 32 patients with moderate-to-severe UC has been recently completed and results are yet to be published (NCT04353791).84 ... Deucravacitinib is an oral TYK2 inhibitor in development for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC. In a phase II, double-blind trial (LATTICE-UC), 131 patients were randomized to deucravacitinib 6 mg twice daily vs placebo.89 The trial failed to meet its primary endpoint of clinical remission at 12 weeks defined by the modified Mayo score (stool frequency sub-score ≤1 with a decrease from baseline of at least 1 point, a rectal bleeding sub-score of 0, and an endoscopy sub-score of ≤1)."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07257588", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "title": "The Efficacy of Hyperbaric Oxygen-assisted Treatment for ASUC and Refractory IBD", "date": null, "text": "- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis NCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2 - A Study to Evaluate IBI112 in the Treatment of Moderate to Severe Active Ulcerative Colitis ... - Clinical Response and Safety Following FMT for UC"}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100550.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "BUSINESS", "date": null, "text": "inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial. ... Our Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases. ... We have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025. In parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China. ... We have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 for moderate-to- severe plaque psoriasis in China and are actively preparing for expansion into additional ... Meanwhile, we will continue with the China Phase II clinical trial targeting UC and the U.S. ... D-2570 (TYK2 inhibitor): The U.S. Phase I clinical trial has been initiated. The clinical data are expected in the first half of 2026. ... D-2570 is an oral selective allosteric inhibitor of TYK2 under clinical development for the treatment of autoimmune diseases, including psoriasis and UC. ... Currently, a registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 targeting moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis is ongoing. We expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027 and thereafter submit to the NMPA an NDA for D-2570. Meanwhile, we are exploring the potential of D-2570 as an induction therapy for moderately to severely active UC. A Phase II clinical trial is ongoing for that purpose in China, and we expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026. We expect to conduct other Phase II clinical trials targeting PsA and SLE in China in the first half of 2026. We have also initiated Phase I clinical trial in U.S., with completion expected in the first half of 2026. ... We initiated the Phase II clinical trial in January 2024 and completed the trial in April 2025. ... We initiated the trial in September 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027. A Phase II clinical trial to evaluate the safety and efficacy of D-2570 as induction therapy in subjects with moderately to severely active UC in China sponsored by us Overview. This is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II clinical trial. The target population is the patients with moderately to severely active UC. The primary objective is to conduct a preliminary evaluation of the efficacy of different doses of D-2570 in the treatment of moderately to severely active UC. ... Trial design. A total of 120 subjects are planned to be included. The trial is divided into two phases: the induction treatment phase and the extended treatment phase. ... Trial status. We initiated the Phase II clinical trial in May 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026. ... In March 2025, ..."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9748, "cached_input_tokens": 6546, "output_tokens": 5600}, "timing": {"search_ms": 483.3, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 56976.2, "total_ms": 57459.5}, "luna_wall_ms": 56976.2, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "D-2570 is an oral, highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor in a China Phase II study of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis, designed as a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study with induction and extended-treatment phases. The planned 120 participants were assigned 1:1:1 to two D-2570 dose arms or placebo for 12 weeks; the primary objective is a preliminary evaluation of efficacy at different doses. The study began in May 2025. All participants had been enrolled by the first half of 2026; listings label the study active, not recruiting. The January 2026 review listed it as recruiting and said results were not yet available. The company filing expected completion in the second half of 2026; the review estimated primary completion in July 2026 and final completion in December 2026. [E1][E4][E5][E7][E9][E10]\n\nOther TYK2-related agents identified for UC include deucravacitinib, an oral selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor. In the Phase II LATTICE-UC trial, 131 patients were randomized to 6 mg twice daily or placebo; the trial failed its primary endpoint of clinical remission at week 12, defined using the modified Mayo score. Across the Phase II LATTICE-CD, LATTICE-UC and IM011-127 trials in moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease and UC, it showed modest efficacy signals but failed primary or secondary efficacy endpoints after 12 weeks across multiple dosing regimens. [E6][E8] TAK-279, also called zasocitinib, is an allosteric TYK2 inhibitor in Phase II evaluation for UC and Crohn’s disease, designed for sustained 24-hour pathway suppression. Its UC study, NCT06254950, is a Phase II multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind trial in 207 participants aged 18–75 with inadequate response to conventional, biologic or advanced therapies; the primary outcome is clinical remission at week 12 using modified Mayo score, and estimated completion is August 2027. [E1][E2][E6] OST-122 is a gut-selective oral JAK inhibitor selective for JAK3, TYK2 and ARK5. Its Phase Ib/IIa UC trial, NCT04353791, enrolled 32 patients with moderate-to-severe UC and was completed; results were yet to be published in the August 2023 review. [E8]", "confident_score": 0.76, "missing": "Whether D-2570 or the competing programmes specifically match the stated disease-duration limit (≤3 months) and modified Mayo score range (5–9); D-2570’s specific prior-treatment eligibility criteria, including inadequate response, loss of response or intolerance to prior standard-of-care medications; whether the competitor trials meet those same prior-treatment criteria beyond the stated inadequate response for TAK-279; D-2570’s numeric dose amounts; updated D-2570 trial outcomes and results for TAK-279 and OST-122; and UC-specific regulatory designations for D-2570.", "citations": ["E1", "E2", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8", "E9", "E10"], "next_queries": ["D-2570 NCT07035041 eligibility modified Mayo duration", "D-2570 NCT07035041 dose arms regimen", "D-2570 NCT07035041 results clinical remission", "D-2570 ulcerative colitis regulatory designation"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 2196, "confident_score": 0.76, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 12, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 9, "cited_domains": ["frontiersin.org", "med.agiltrace.com", "news.futunn.com", "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "www1.hkexnews.hk"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 44, "ungrounded_tokens": ["2023", "TYK2-related", "Phase II LATTICE-UC", "Phase II LATTICE-CD", "Crohn", "IIa UC"], "n_ungrounded": 6, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 4, "next_queries_subject_first": 4}}
{"row_id": "q055", "objective": "DB-1305/Anti-PD-L1/VEGF Bispecific Antibody patients studied in Recurrent or Disseminated HNSCC (Oral Cavity/Oropharynx/Hypopharynx/Larynx), Incurable by Local Therapies trials: biomarker selection, disease severity, line of therapy, and required prior treatment", "anchors": ["DB-1305"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 18868, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.uclahealth.org/clinical-trials/db-1311-combination-with-bnt327-or-db-1305", "domain": "uclahealth.org", "title": "DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 ...", "date": null, "text": "- For HNSCC patients: Histologically or cytologically confirmed recurrent (recurrent disease that is not amendable to curative treatment with local/ or systemic therapies)/ (disseminated) HNSCC of the oral cavity, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx that is considered incurable by local therapies. ... - 1. Prior treatment with B7H3 targeted therapy. - Prior treatment with antibody-drug conjugate with topoisomerase inhibitor. ... - Has an uncontrolled concomitant or intercurrent illness, that in the opinion of the investigator, contra-indicates trial participation, limits compliance with trial procedures or substantially increases the risk of incurring AEs. - Has uncontrolled or significant cardiovascular disease. Has clinically uncontrolled pleural effusion, ascites or pericardial effusion requiring drainage, peritoneal shunt, or cell-free concentrated ascites reinfusion therapy. - Has a history of (non-infectious) ILD/pneumonitis. - Any autoimmune, connective tissue or inflammatory disorders. - Has spinal cord compression or clinically active central nervous system (CNS) metastases. - Has unresolved toxicities from previous anticancer therapy, defined as toxicities (other than alopecia) not yet resolved to Grade ≤1 or baseline."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/pdfdirect/10.1200/JCO.22.02328", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "title": "Immunotherapy and Biomarker Testing in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancers: ASCO Guideline", "date": null, "text": "Patients with HNSCC commonly present with locoregionally advanced disease and often receive multimodality treatments, including surgery, radiation therapy, and/or chemotherapy. Although cisplatin is the most commonly used chemotherapy, the choice of chemotherapy depends on several factors, including prior use of platinum-based chemotherapy and primary tumor site. Recurrent or metastatic HNSCC is typically treated with systemic therapy involving chemotherapy and/or immunotherapy. ... Recommendation 2.1. Pembrolizumab monotherapy or pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil should be offered as first- line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS $ 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High; Strength of recommendation: Strong). Recommendation 2.2. Pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil may be offered as first-line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS , 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: Moderate; Strength of recommendation: Strong). ... Pembrolizumab or nivolumab should be offered to patients with platinum-refractory recurrent or metastatic HNSCC, regardless of CPS status (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High; ... Recommendation 2.1. Pembrolizumab monotherapy or pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil (5-FU) should be offered as first-line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS $ 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High;"}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com/test/test-guides/TG_PD-L1/pd-l1-test-selection-guide", "domain": "testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com", "title": "PD-L1: Test Selection Guide", "date": "2026-07-12", "text": "|HNSCC|Keytruda® (pembrolizumab),6 see test code 36260 in **Table 2** for test information|*PD-L1 testing required (companion diagnostic)*- 1st-line treatment for PD-L1+ (CPS ≥1) metastatic or unresectable recurrent disease *PD-L1 testing indicated but not required*- 1st-line treatment (combo) for metastatic or unresectable recurrent disease - Treatment for metastatic or recurrent disease with progression on or after platinum-based therapy| ... |HNSCC|Opdivo® (nivolumab),8 see test code 93793 in **Table 2** for test information|*PD-L1 testing optional (complementary diagnostic)*- Treatment for recurrent or metastatic disease with progression on or after platinum-based therapy|"}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.mypathologyreport.ca/biomarkers/pd-l1-testing-in-cancer/", "domain": "mypathologyreport.ca", "title": "PD-L1 Testing in Cancer - MyPathologyReport", "date": null, "text": "PD-L1 testing is mandatory for all patients with advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) because the result directly determines first-line treatment. ... - **TPS ≥ 50% (PD-L1 high).** Pembrolizumab monotherapy is approved as a first-line treatment for advanced NSCLC without actionable driver mutations (EGFR, ALK, and others) when TPS is ≥ 50%. ... ### Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma PD-L1 testing is standard for all patients with recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). The 22C3 assay with CPS scoring is used, and two cut-offs are clinically meaningful: - **CPS ≥ 1.** Pembrolizumab combined with platinum-based chemotherapy is approved for first-line treatment of recurrent/metastatic HNSCC with CPS ≥ 1, based on the KEYNOTE-048 trial. - **CPS ≥ 20.** Pembrolizumab monotherapy (without chemotherapy) is approved as first-line treatment for CPS ≥ 20, based on demonstrated non-inferiority to chemotherapy with a more favorable tolerability profile. This higher threshold identifies patients most likely to achieve durable responses from immunotherapy alone. ... In most cancer types with established PD-L1 thresholds, particularly NSCLC with TPS ≥ 50%, HNSCC with CPS ≥ 20, and cervical cancer with CPS ≥ 1, a high result supports the use of checkpoint inhibitor monotherapy or combination therapy."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12715102/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Immune biomarkers for head and neck cancer - PMC", "date": "2025-12-18", "text": "These therapies have shown better outcomes in terms of overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival (PFS) relative to standard chemotherapy, with the greatest benefits observed in HNSCC cases with elevated levels of PD-L1 [7, 12]. ... PD-L1 level, TMB, and microsatellites are the only approved biomarkers for ICI responses [41]. In 2016, FDA approval was granted to two PD-1 inhibitors in patients with relapsed or metastatic HNSCC. These two monoclonal antibodies were pembrolizumab and nivolumab. ... Furthermore, atezolizumab and durvalumab are monoclonal antibodies targeting PD-L1. Serving as a response biomarker, patient biomarkers disease status or clinical state are important to consider when selecting therapies against HNC. ... PD-L1 expression levels could be used as biomarkers for anti-PD-L1 drugs. Therefore, PD-L1 is a static predictive biomarker for relapsed or metastatic HNSCC exhibiting a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1 [41]. Compared with standard-of-care chemotherapy, treatment of HNSCC with pembrolizumab or nivolumab resulted in better OS. ... In KEYNOTE-012, a phase 1b clinical trial of 188 HNSCC patients, a CPS of 1 or higher was considered a significant predictor of HNSCC’s response to pembrolizumab. CPS-positive patients accounted for 81% of the cohort and had better ORRs, PFS, and OS than CPS-negative patients. ... Consequently, following the findings of the randomized phase 3 KEYNOTE-040 trial demonstrating that the CPS demonstrated greater sensitivity than TPS at lower threshold values in predicting pembrolizumab efficacy, CPS developed as the preferred scoring method for evaluating PD-L1 expression HNSCC [119]. ... Accordingly, the anti-PD-1 antibodies pembrolizumab and nivolumab received FDA approval to treat HNSCC recurring after the use of platinum-based regimens. Pembrolizumab also was approved alongside chemotherapy (platinum and fluorouracil) for all HNSCC patients and received approval as a single-agent therapy for patients PD-L1-positive patients with CPS ≥ 1 [123]. ... For HNSCC that is recurring or metastatic, a CPS ≥ 1 is the FDA-approved threshold that justifies the using pembrolizumab in the first-line setting either as single agent, or alongside chemotherapy. ... PD-L1 remains a key predictive biomarker, but inconsistencies in testing methods and thresholds hinder its clinical utility [42, 43, 175]. Recent evidence suggests that analyzing PD-L1 at relapse may yield more accurate predictions than using archival samples. ... Currently, HPV status and PD-L1 CPS remain the most widely established biomarkers with direct implications for clinical decision-making in head and neck cancer."}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13101959/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Driving Progress in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer", "date": "2026-07-01", "text": "Anti-programmed death 1 (PD-1) agents such as nivolumab^4^ and pembrolizumab^5,6,7^ in pretreated patients with R/M HNSCC and their subsequent incorporation in the first-line advanced-disease setting^8^, has provided clinical benefits offering durable responses in a subset of patients. ... The KEYNOTE-048 study^8^ established the use of pembrolizumab in the first-line setting, either as monotherapy (limited to patients with PD-L1 ≥ 1 expression) or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU). As a result, immunotherapy was established as the core element of standard-of-care in the first-line treatment of platinum-sensitive R/M HNSCC. ... Subsequently, other 5-FU free regimens based on platinum plus taxane combination with immunotherapy have also proven be beneficial in the platinum-sensitive population. ... Based on available evidence, current guidelines recommend the use of immunotherapy as monotherapy in this setting^23^. ... Despite biological^10^ and technical limitations, PD-L1 remains a dominant factor to guiding first-line treatment decision. ... The treatment of recurrent/metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) remains heavily reliant on chemotherapy-based regimens. However, immunotherapy and targeted therapy approaches offer the opportunity to introduce chemotherapy-sparing strategies. ... Immune checkpoint blockade with different agents such as nivolumab, pembrolizumab or toripalimab paired with EGFR inhibitors have demonstrated overall response rates (ORR) ranging from 30% to 55% in immune-naïve populations^86,87,88,89^. ... Petosentamab (MCLA-158), a bispecific antibody that targets the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) and the leucine-rich repeat containing G-protein-coupled receptor 5 (LGR5), currently holds two breakthrough therapy designations by the FDA for patients with R/M HNSCC who have experienced disease progression following treatment with platinum-based chemotherapy and a PD-L1/PD-1 inhibitor^91^ and also in the first-line setting in combination with pembrolizumab for patients with PD-L1 (CPS≥1)^92^. The combination of petosentamab with pembrolizumab continues to be investigated in the front-line setting (NCT03526835). ... BCA101 in combination with pembrolizumab in R/M HNSCC for patients who had received no previous systemic therapy in the R/M disease setting showed ORR of 46% in the whole population and 57% in th HPV- subgroup^93,94^. The phase II/III FORTIFI-HN01 study (NCT06788990) is currently evaluating the combination of pembrolizumab with ficerafusp alfa (BCA101) vs placebo in the first-line setting for PD-L1-positive R/M HNSCC^95^. ... Previous data suggest that second-line treatment should be considered after IO progression in patients with HNSCC, even in cases with poor PS^98^. ... Single agent regimens such as docetaxel, fluoropidimidines or cetuximab are commonly used but offer modest benefit and often come with substantial toxicity, particularly in patients with ..."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org/squamous-cell-carcinoma", "domain": "ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org", "title": "UCLA Squamous Cell Carcinoma Clinical Trials — Los Angeles", "date": "2026-09-24", "text": "The purpose of this study is to compare anti-tumor activity of amivantamab in addition to pembrolizumab and carboplatin versus pembrolizumab, 5-fluorouracil (FU), and platinum therapy (carboplatin or cisplatin) in participants with refractory/metastatic (R/M) head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). HNSCC is a type of cancer that develops in the head and neck regions, including the outer tissue layer of the mouth and throat. This study will focus on participants with HNSCC who are treatment-naive (have not received prior treatment) in the R/M setting. ... - ## DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 in Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors open to eligible people ages 18 years and up A Phase II, Multicenter, Open-Label Trial of DB-1311 in combination with BNT327 or DB-1305 in Participants with Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors"}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8440813/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Immunotherapy in Recurrent/Metastatic Squamous Cell ...", "date": "2021-09-01", "text": "Prior to immunotherapy, platinum-based chemotherapy with or without cetuximab had been the standard systemic treatment for R/M HNSCC for over a decade (61). ... Pembrolizumab plus platinum/5-FU for all patients 2. Pembrolizumab monotherapy for CPS ≥1| ... This led to the FDA approval in 2019 of platinum/5FU plus pembrolizumab for all patients and pembrolizumab monotherapy only for patients with a PD-L1 CPS ≥1. ... In practice, the decision to choose pembrolizumab monotherapy *versus* chemotherapy plus pembrolizumab for a patient with CPS 1-19 depends on multiple patient and disease factors, such as tumor and symptom burden, comorbidity, and performance status. In patients with a PD-L1 CPS 1-19 with high tumor burden and/or significant symptoms that can tolerate chemotherapy, we favor chemotherapy plus pembrolizumab as a standard of care treatment, to maximize potential response, which can translate directly into a quality-of-life benefit. ... In summary, current frontline standard of care systemic therapy options for R/M HNSCC of the oral cavity, oropharynx, larynx, and hypopharynx include pembrolizumab monotherapy or platinum/5FU plus pembrolizumab for PD-L1 expressers by CPS or platinum/5FU plus pembrolizumab for all patients."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12142288/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Immunotherapy in recurrent/metastatic head and neck ... - PMC", "date": "2025-05-31", "text": "The immunohistochemical assessment of PD-L1 expression was initially identified as a powerful and easily accessible predictive tool, and gained its place as the current standard for patient selection, but it has clear limitations. ... Before the advent of immunotherapy, first-line treatment options included combination regimens of cytotoxic agents in combination with cetuximab, a chimeric human anti-epidermal growth factor receptor monoclonal antibody ^7,8^. ... A few years later, in 2019, after the result of the KEYNOTE 048 trial ^18^ the FDA granted approval for PD-1 inhibition as first-line treatment for patients with metastatic or unresectable, recurrent HNSCC, approving pembrolizumab in combination with platinum and fluorouracil for all patients with HNSCC and pembrolizumab as a single agent for patients with HNSCC whose tumors express a PD-L1 combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1. The expression of PD-L1 on tumor cells and infiltrating immune cells is currently assessed by immunohistochemistry on formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue to identify patients eligible for ICI therapy. The CPS is calculated as the number of PD-L1 positive invasive cancer cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages, divided by the number of viable tumor cells and multiplied by 100. ... In general, tumor PD-L1 expression is associated with improved efficacy with anti-PD-1/PD-L1 therapy in R/M HNSCC, with its predictive value being enriched by the consideration of PD-L1 expression on both tumor cells and tumor-infiltrating immune cells (CPS) ^21^. In particular, post hoc analysis of data from the KEYNOTE-040 trial showed CPS and TPS to have equivalent performances at a cutoff of 50, but CPS is more sensitive than TPS at a lower cutoff of ≥ 1 ^24^. Therefore, CPS emerged as the best scoring method and is currently recommended, with evidence pointing at the thresholds of CPS ≥20 and ≥ 1 as clinically significant, with improved overall response rate, overall survival and progression-free survival in this population when treated with ICIs ^18,19^. ... Concerning the issue of the several immunohistochemical assays available for the evaluation of PD-L1, in the US pembrolizumab was approved by the FDA exclusively for patients with CPS ≥ 1 assessed with the 22C3 PharmDx assay, while in Europe EMA stated that pembrolizumab could be used as first-line treatment for R/M HNSCC in patients with CPS ≥ 1 as assessed using any validated antibody and IHC platform. ... Currently, no recommendation exists on whether PD-L1 should be preferably tested on the primary tumor, lymph node metastasis, or distant metastasis when these options are simultaneously available. ... Consequently, a positive CPS should be regarded as reliable, while a negative test should prompt further investigation if feasible. ... In 2023, the American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO) published guidelines for immunotherapy and biomarker testing in R/M HNSCC stating that TMB testing may be performed in patients ..."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org/head-and-neck-squamous-cell-carcinoma", "domain": "ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org", "title": "Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma clinical trials at UCLA", "date": "2026-09-18", "text": "This study will focus on participants with HNSCC who are treatment-naive (have not received prior treatment) in the R/M setting. ... - ## DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 in Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors open to eligible people ages 18 years and up A Phase II, Multicenter, Open-Label Trial of DB-1311 in combination with BNT327 or DB-1305 in Participants with Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors ... - ## FORTIFI-HN01: A Study of Ficerafusp Alfa (BCA101) or Placebo in Combination With Pembrolizumab in First-Line PD-L1-pos, R or M HNSCC open to eligible people ages 18 years and up Ficerafusp alfa is directed against two targets, Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR) and Transforming Growth Factor beta (TGF-β). This study intends to evaluate the safety and efficacy of ficerafusp alfa in combination with pembrolizumab versus placebo with pembrolizumab in 1L PD-L1-positive, recurrent or metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma (HNSCC). ... An open-label, controlled, multi-site, interventional, 2-arm, Phase II/III trial of BNT113 in combination with pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab monotherapy as first line treatment in patients with unresectable recurrent or metastatic HPV16+ HNSCC expressing programmed cell death ligand-1 (PD-L1) with combined positive score (CPS) ≥1."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10555, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 1514}, "timing": {"search_ms": 533.4, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 15450.4, "total_ms": 15983.8}, "luna_wall_ms": 15450.4, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "In the DB-1311 combination trial that includes DB-1305, HNSCC participants had to have histologically or cytologically confirmed recurrent disease not amenable to curative treatment with local or systemic therapies, or disseminated disease considered incurable by local therapies, in the oral cavity, oropharynx, hypopharynx, or larynx. [E1] Prior B7H3-targeted therapy and prior antibody-drug conjugate treatment with a topoisomerase inhibitor are listed as exclusions. Participants also could not have unresolved prior anticancer-therapy toxicities above Grade ≤1 or baseline, except alopecia. [E1]", "confident_score": 0.7, "missing": "Biomarker selection criteria; line of therapy; whether any specific prior treatment is required, including its regimen or timing (the stated prior-treatment details are exclusions and a toxicity criterion).", "citations": ["E1"], "next_queries": ["DB-1305 HNSCC biomarker eligibility criteria", "DB-1305 HNSCC line of therapy eligibility", "DB-1305 HNSCC required prior treatment criteria"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 600, "confident_score": 0.7, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 2, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 1, "cited_domains": ["uclahealth.org"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 7, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 3, "next_queries_subject_first": 3}}
{"row_id": "q058", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 5882, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301900_c.pdf", "domain": "hkexnews.hk", "title": "C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "date": null, "text": "本集團欣然宣佈，於2026 年4 月17 日至22 日在美國加利福尼亞州聖地亞哥舉行的 ... 二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘 要號#2634） ... 的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型 ... 亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活 性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯 示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。 ... 顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、 肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導 腫瘤消退。 ... 廣泛的聯合用藥潛力：與化療藥物(5 -FU) 、抗VEGF 抗體、VEGFR 抑制劑 及KRAS G12C抑制劑聯合給藥，均表現出顯著的協同增效作用。"}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/sunshine-lake-pharma-co-ltd-showcases-three-innovative-oncology-drug-candidates-at-2026-aacr-annu-ce7f58d8dd8ffe27", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "title": "Sunshine Lake Pharma Co., Ltd. Showcases Three Innovative ...", "date": "2026-04-23", "text": "At the 2026 Annual Meeting of the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR), held from 17 to 22 April 2026 in San Diego, California, USA, the Group presented the latest preclinical research results of three oncology pipeline candidates through three poster presentations. ... HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications. ... Significant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression. ... The preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials in humans."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.aacrmeetingnews.org/closing-plenary-session-examined-highlights-from-the-aacr-annual-meeting-2026/", "domain": "aacrmeetingnews.org", "title": "Closing Plenary Session examined highlights from the ...", "date": "2026-04-30", "text": "Daraxonrasib, a multi-RAS-ON inhibitor recently reported to be effective against pretreated, metastatic pancreatic cancers, was found to also be effective in the first-line setting, either as monotherapy or in combination with chemotherapy."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.insights.bio/bioinsights/journal/article/3821/aacr-annual-meeting-2026-postevent-summary-clinical-data-readouts-and-emerging-themes", "domain": "insights.bio", "title": "AACR Annual Meeting 2026 – Post-event summary", "date": "2026-05-14", "text": "TNG961 caused complete tumor regression in pancreatic and lung cancer models and demonstrated combinatorial activity with inhibitors targeting MTAP deletion, which frequently co-occurs."}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://rpubs.com/Pancreatic-Ductal-Cancer/1459637", "domain": "rpubs.com", "title": "RPubs", "date": null, "text": "Objective 1: Crosswalk ID harmonization by Judy Ngobeni"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13332605/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Advances in antibody-drug conjugates in cancer - PMC - NIH", "date": "2026-07-04", "text": "Across multiple preclinical and early clinical studies, emerging ADC technologies demonstrated improved precision, enhanced efficacy, and the ability to overcome resistance mechanisms that have historically limited this class of therapeutics."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.adcreview.com/conference-coverage/aacr/aacr-2026-emerging-antibody-drug-conjugates-for-the-treatment-of-solid-tumors-part-1/", "domain": "adcreview.com", "title": "AACR 2026: Emerging Antibody-Drug Conjugates for the ...", "date": "2026-05-05", "text": "IN30758, a novel ADC being developed by InxMed (Ying Shi Bio), targets integrins such as *alphaVbeta6*, an epithelial-specific transmembrane receptor that is typically absent in healthy adult tissues but significantly upregulated during wound healing, chronic inflammation, and in various solid epithelial cancers, including pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), and ovarian cancer."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://medical.emdserono.com/en_US/medinfo/therapeutic-areas/oncology/congress-presentations/aacr-2026.html", "domain": "medical.emdserono.com", "title": "AACR 2026", "date": "2026-05-11", "text": "2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting – April 17-22, 2026 / San Diego, CA"}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33199632/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies effective therapy for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma - PubMed", "date": "2020-12-01", "text": "To identify effective PDAC therapies, we leveraged a syngeneic orthotopic PDAC transplant mouse model to perform a large-scale, in vivo screen of 16 single-agent and 41 two-drug targeted therapy combinations in mice. Among 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity. Mechanistically, we observed that single-agent MEK inhibition led to compensatory activation of resistance pathways, including components of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling axis, which was overcome with the addition of HSP90 inhibition. The combination of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) was also active in vitro in established human PDAC cell lines and in vivo in patient-derived organoid PDAC transplant models. These findings encourage the clinical development of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) combination therapy and highlight the power of clinically relevant in vivo model systems for identifying cancer therapies."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://www.aacr.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/D32026_AbstractTitlesOnly.pdf", "domain": "aacr.org", "title": "[PDF] AACR DRUG DISCOVERY AND DEVELOPMENT (AACR D3)", "date": null, "text": "B068 A sphingolipid-derived paclitaxel nanovesicle enhances efficacy of combination therapies in triple-negative breast cancer and pancreatic cancer."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7742, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "output_tokens": 1252}, "timing": {"search_ms": 523.2, "jev_ms": 0.0, "luna_ms": 13766.2, "total_ms": 14289.4}, "luna_wall_ms": 13766.2, "cost_usd": {"search": 0.001, "jev": 0.0, "luna": 0.0}, "output": {"has_answer": "yes", "summarized_answer": "**AACR 2026** In multiple KRAS-mutant xenograft models, including pancreatic cancer, oral HEC234055 produced dose-dependent tumor-growth inhibition and tumor regression. Separately, HEC234055 showed significant synergistic effects in combination with 5-FU, an anti-VEGF antibody, a VEGFR inhibitor, and a KRAS G12C inhibitor. [E1][E2]", "confident_score": 0.58, "missing": "The evidence does not establish that any of the listed combinations was tested in vivo in a pancreatic cancer model. It does not identify which combination partner or partners were used in pancreatic cancer, or give combination-specific efficacy results, doses, or model details.", "citations": ["E1", "E2"], "next_queries": ["HEC234055 PDAC combination xenograft", "HEC234055 pancreatic cancer combination partner in vivo", "HEC234055 AACR 2026 abstract 2634 combination dose tumor growth"]}, "raw_text": null, "metrics": {"json_valid": true, "has_answer": true, "answer_chars": 334, "confident_score": 0.58, "missing_nothing": false, "missing_empty": false, "n_tags": 2, "invalid_tags": [], "uncited_answer": false, "n_cited_items": 2, "cited_domains": ["hkexnews.hk", "marketscreener.com"], "url_in_answer": false, "search_talk": [], "nonempty_when_no": false, "claim_tokens": 7, "ungrounded_tokens": [], "n_ungrounded": 0, "names_subject": true, "subject_in_cited": true, "next_queries": 3, "next_queries_subject_first": 3}}
{"row_id": "q059", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "facet": "readout", "scope": null, "skip": null, "abstained": false, "n_items": 10, "evidence_chars": 5127, "evidence": [{"id": "E1", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "date": null, "text": "HEC234055 — Orally Available High-Activity Pan-RAS Molecular Glue Inhibitor Targeting the ‘‘Undruggable’’ (Abstract #2634) RAS gene mutations drive approximately one-quarter of human cancers, with common mutation subtypes such as KRAS G12D and G12V still representing significant unmet clinical needs. The drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications. ... Broad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (‘‘GTP’’)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification. ... Significant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression. ... Excellent in vivo efficacy: In MC38-hPDL1 homology models and multiple PBMC humanized xenograft models, the tumor growth inhibition rate (TGI) of single- agent administration can reach up to approximately 70%. In particular, in PD-L1 antibody-insensitive models such as NCI-H358 (non-small cell lung cancer) and MDA-MB-231 (triple-negative breast cancer), the single-agent efficacy of HEC201625 is significantly superior to that of PD-L1 antibodies, demonstrating a differentiated advantage. ... The preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials in humans."}, {"id": "E2", "url": "https://en.gempharmatech.com/product/search_608.html", "domain": "en.gempharmatech.com", "title": "Find Your Research Mouse Model 丨 GemPharmatech", "date": null, "text": "Use this search tool to find knockout, knock-in, transgenic, humanized mouse models and more by gene, disease area, or model type."}, {"id": "E3", "url": "https://www.findmice.org/", "domain": "findmice.org", "title": "International Mouse Strain Resource", "date": null, "text": "The IMSR is a searchable online database of mouse strains, stocks, and mutant ES cell lines available worldwide, including inbred, mutant, and genetically engineered strains."}, {"id": "E4", "url": "https://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301900_c.pdf", "domain": "hkexnews.hk", "title": "C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "date": null, "text": "二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘 要號#2634） ... 亞型仍存在巨大未滿足的臨床需求。在研產品HEC234055 為本集團自主研發 的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型 擴增。此次公佈的臨床前數據亮點如下： ... 廣譜覆蓋與高活性：可同時作用於KRAS 的活性（三磷酸鳥苷（「GTP 」）結 合）與非活性（GDP 結合）狀態，並覆蓋G12D 、G12C 、G12V 、G13D 、 Q61H等多種突變類型及野生型擴增。 ... 亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活 性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯 示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。 ... 顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、 肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導 腫瘤消退。"}, {"id": "E5", "url": "https://www.crownbio.com/databases/pdx-models", "domain": "crownbio.com", "title": "PDX Model Database Registration", "date": "2025-09-26", "text": "Our online, searchable patient-derived xenograft (PDX) database enables scientists around the world to quickly and easily search for models that meet their specific research needs. ... **Browse the collection to:** - Access a diverse collection of over 2,400 well-characterized and ready-for-service PDX models from US, European, and Asian populations - Evaluate and filter models based on indication, target, growth characteristics, standard of care treatment, and ethnicity"}, {"id": "E6", "url": "https://www.mmrrc.org/", "domain": "mmrrc.org", "title": "MMRRC Repository", "date": null, "text": "STOCK Tg(Cxcr4-EGFP)CD73Gsat/Mmucd is available from the MMRRC and contains multiple copies of a modified BAC in which EGFP reporter gene is inserted immediately upstream of the coding sequence of the targeted gene."}, {"id": "E7", "url": "https://www.wagnerlab.net/resource-page", "domain": "wagnerlab.net", "title": "Resource Page", "date": null, "text": "This page lists genetically engineered mouse models generated and published by our team and how to obtain them from public repositories (links to repositories not shown on the mobile page)."}, {"id": "E8", "url": "https://en.gempharmatech.com/product/searchByNcbi_18783_1.html", "domain": "en.gempharmatech.com", "title": "Find Your Research Mouse Model 丨 ...", "date": null, "text": "Use this search tool to find knockout, knock-in, transgenic, humanized mouse models and more by gene, disease area, or model type."}, {"id": "E9", "url": "https://www.pdxnetwork.org/models", "domain": "pdxnetwork.org", "title": "PDX Models - PDXNet", "date": null, "text": "Models available for research are listed in CancerModels.org. ... PDXNet researchers generated 520 new PDX models across 56 cancer types. The PDXNet Portal can be referenced for an up-to-date list of PDXNet-generated models."}, {"id": "E10", "url": "https://bio.tools/pdxnet_portal", "domain": "bio.tools", "title": "{{ngMeta['og:title']}}", "date": null, "text": "This is a development version of the registry. There is no guarantee that the data entered here will be preserved in the future."}], "ok": true, "error": null, "status": 200, "attempts": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7608, "cached_input_tokens": 5945, "